window.BANK=[{"id":"BM32|Q1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q1","t":"Cardinal signs of acute inflammation","s":"Which of the following is <b>NOT</b> a characteristic of <b>acute</b> inflammation?","lec":"L10","sl":6,"c":"The five cardinal signs of acute inflammation: <b>rubor</b> (redness), <b>calor</b> (heat), <b>tumor</b> (swelling), <b>dolor</b> (pain), <b>functio laesa</b> (loss of function). <b>Fibrosis, scarring, lymphocyte/plasma-cell infiltration and angiogenesis</b> belong to chronic inflammation. Any answer choice naming a repair/remodelling process is the odd one out on an 'acute' stem.","o":{"A":"Loss of function","B":"Pain","C":"Redness","D":"Swelling","E":"Fibrosis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"<i>Functio laesa</i> (loss of function) is one of the five cardinal signs of acute inflammation.","B":"<i>Dolor</i> (pain) — from bradykinin and PGE₂ acting on nerve endings.","C":"<i>Rubor</i> (redness) — from vasodilation and increased blood flow.","D":"<i>Tumor</i> (swelling) — from exudate accumulating after increased vascular permeability.","E":"Correct — <b>fibrosis</b> is a feature of <b>chronic</b> inflammation and scar formation, not acute. Acute inflammation is rapid in onset (minutes) and short-lived (hours to a few days); fibrosis takes weeks."},"also":"BM31 Q11, BM30 Q11","img":"slides/L10_6.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q2","t":"Cell-derived inflammatory mediators","s":"Which of the following inflammatory mediators is <b>cell-derived</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":25,"c":"<b>Cell-derived</b> mediators: vasoactive amines (histamine, serotonin), <b>arachidonic acid metabolites</b> (prostaglandins, leukotrienes), <b>PAF</b>, <b>ROS</b>, NO, lysosomal enzymes, and cytokines (TNF, IL-1, IL-6, chemokines). <b>Plasma-derived</b>: complement (C3a, C5a, MAC), coagulation/fibrinolysis products, and kinins (bradykinin). The exam repeatedly tests this split.","o":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Bradykinin","C":"C3a","D":"Fibrin split products","E":"C5a"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>histamine</b> is a preformed vasoactive amine stored in the granules of <b>mast cells, basophils and platelets</b>; it is the prototype cell-derived mediator.","B":"Bradykinin comes from the <b>kinin system</b> in plasma — plasma-derived.","C":"C3a is a complement anaphylatoxin — the complement system is <b>plasma-derived</b>.","D":"Fibrin split products come from the plasma <b>coagulation</b> cascade — plasma-derived.","E":"C5a is also a complement product — plasma-derived."},"f":["completed"],"img":"slides/L10_25.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q3","t":"Granulomatous inflammation","s":"A 43-year-old woman has a history of <b>pulmonary tuberculosis</b>. Which pattern of inflammation would you most likely find in her lung?","lec":"L10","sl":42,"c":"<b>Granuloma</b> = focus of chronic inflammation with <b>epithelioid macrophages</b> ± <b>multinucleated giant cells</b>, rimmed by lymphocytes. Causes: <b>TB</b> (caseating), sarcoidosis (non-caseating), fungal infection, leprosy, syphilis, Crohn's, and <b>foreign body</b> (splinter, suture). Persistent, poorly degradable stimulus + Th1/IFN-γ macrophage activation is the recipe.","o":{"A":"Purulent inflammation","B":"Granulomatous inflammation","C":"Fibrinous inflammation","D":"Serous inflammation","E":"Pseudomembranous inflammation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Purulent (suppurative) inflammation = pus, driven by pyogenic bacteria such as <i>Staphylococcus</i>; not the TB pattern.","B":"Correct — <b>TB is the classic granulomatous inflammation</b>: epithelioid macrophages, Langhans multinucleated giant cells and lymphocytes surrounding <b>central caseous necrosis</b>.","C":"Fibrinous inflammation follows fibrinogen leakage onto serosal surfaces (pericarditis, pleuritis).","D":"Serous inflammation is a protein-poor effusion, e.g. a skin blister.","E":"Pseudomembranous inflammation is the <i>C. difficile</i> / diphtheria pattern on mucosal surfaces."},"also":"BM31 Q16, BM30 Q16","img":"slides/L10_42.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q5","t":"Proliferative phase of tissue repair","s":"Which immune cell plays the major role in the <b>initial proliferative phase</b> of tissue repair?","lec":"L10","sl":70,"c":"Wound-healing phases: <b>haemostasis → inflammation</b> (neutrophils then M1 macrophages) <b>→ proliferation</b> (M2 macrophages, angiogenesis, fibroblasts/fibroplasia, granulation tissue, re-epithelialisation) <b>→ maturation/remodelling</b> (MMPs, collagen III → I). The <b>M1 → M2 switch</b> is the hinge between inflammation and repair.","o":{"A":"Neutrophil","B":"Classical (M1) macrophage","C":"M2 macrophage","D":"Lymphocyte","E":"Fibroblast"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Neutrophils dominate the earlier <b>inflammatory</b> phase, clearing debris and microbes; they do not drive proliferation.","B":"Classical <b>M1</b> macrophages are pro-inflammatory (ROS, NO, IL-1, TNF) — they belong to the inflammatory phase.","C":"Correct — <b>M2 (alternatively activated) macrophages</b> switch the wound into the proliferative phase: they secrete <b>TGF-β, VEGF and PDGF</b>, driving angiogenesis, fibroblast recruitment and ECM deposition.","D":"Lymphocytes modulate the response but are not the principal proliferative-phase driver.","E":"Fibroblasts are the <b>effector</b> of the proliferative phase, but the question asks for the <b>immune</b> cell that drives it — that is the M2 macrophage."},"img":"slides/L10_70.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q6","t":"Cells in rheumatoid joint inflammation","s":"A patient presents with joint swelling and inflammation consistent with <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>. Which cell contributes most to these joint findings?","lec":"L19","sl":47,"c":"RA pathogenesis: smoking/<i>P. gingivalis</i> + <b>HLA-DRB1</b> → <b>citrullination (PAD enzyme)</b> → DC presents citrullinated peptide → <b>Th1/Th17</b> (IFN-γ, IL-17) → macrophage TNF/IL-1/IL-6 + B cell <b>RF / anti-CCP</b> → pannus, erosion. Treatment targets follow the mechanism: anti-TNF, IL-6R, methotrexate.","o":{"A":"Lymphocyte","B":"Monocyte","C":"Mast cell","D":"Eosinophil","E":"Erythrocyte"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — RA synovitis is driven by <b>lymphocytes</b>: CD4⁺ Th1/Th17 cells secreting IFN-γ and IL-17, plus B cells/plasma cells producing <b>RF and anti-CCP</b>. The lymphoid infiltrate in the pannus is the hallmark.","B":"Monocytes/macrophages amplify the damage (TNF, IL-1, IL-6) but act downstream of the T-cell response.","C":"Mast cells are the type I hypersensitivity cell — not the RA mechanism.","D":"Eosinophils belong to parasite/allergy responses.","E":"Erythrocytes have no role in the inflammatory infiltrate."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM32 Q22, BM31 Q18","img":"slides/L19_47.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q7","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q7","t":"Excessive tissue repair after burn","s":"A patient develops an abnormality after a severe burn, characterised by <b>excessive tissue repair</b> extending beyond the original wound margins. What is it?","lec":"L10","sl":74,"c":"Repair abnormalities: <b>deficient</b> → dehiscence, ulceration, chronic wound (infection, poor nutrition/vitamin C, steroids, diabetes, ischaemia, foreign body); <b>excessive</b> → <b>hypertrophic scar</b> (within margins, may regress), <b>keloid</b> (beyond margins, persists), <b>exuberant granulation</b> ('proud flesh'), <b>contracture</b> (burns).","o":{"A":"Hypertrophic scar","B":"Keloid","C":"Desmoid tumour","D":"Wound contracture","E":"Dehiscence"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"A <b>hypertrophic scar</b> is also excessive collagen, but it stays <b>within</b> the original wound boundary and often regresses — the classic distinguishing point.","B":"Correct — a <b>keloid</b> is excessive collagen deposition that grows <b>beyond the original wound margins</b> and does not regress. Burns and dark-skinned patients are classic settings.","C":"Desmoid (aggressive fibromatosis) is a fibroblastic neoplasm, not a repair abnormality.","D":"Contracture is exaggerated wound <b>contraction</b> causing deformity and restricted movement — common after burns, but it is over-<i>contraction</i>, not over-<i>deposition</i>.","E":"Dehiscence is <b>deficient</b> repair — the wound bursts open."},"f":["completed"],"img":"slides/L10_74.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q8","t":"Angiogenesis in tissue repair","s":"In tissue repair, which cytokine/growth factor is most important for <b>angiogenesis</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":67,"c":"<b>Angiogenesis = VEGF.</b> Steps: vasodilation (NO) + permeability (VEGF) → pericyte detachment and basement-membrane degradation (MMPs) → endothelial <b>migration and proliferation</b> → tube formation → pericyte recruitment (PDGF) and maturation. Other repair factors worth knowing: <b>PDGF</b> (fibroblast/smooth-muscle recruitment), <b>TGF-β</b> (fibrosis, the main fibrogenic cytokine), <b>EGF/FGF</b> (re-epithelialisation).","o":{"A":"FGF-2","B":"FGF-10","C":"TNF-α","D":"VEGF","E":"BMP-1"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"FGF-2 does contribute to angiogenesis and fibroblast proliferation, but it is a supporting factor — VEGF is <i>the</i> angiogenic factor.","B":"FGF-10 acts mainly in epithelial–mesenchymal signalling and lung/limb development.","C":"TNF-α is a pro-inflammatory cytokine (fever, endothelial activation), not the angiogenic driver.","D":"Correct — <b>VEGF (vascular endothelial growth factor)</b> is the principal driver of angiogenesis: it induces endothelial proliferation, migration and increased vascular permeability, sprouting new vessels from existing venules.","E":"BMP-1 is a procollagen C-proteinase involved in collagen maturation, not angiogenesis."},"also":"BM30 Q8","img":"slides/L10_67.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q10","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q10","t":"Role of M cells (mucosal immunity)","s":"Describe the <b>role of M cells</b> in mucosal immunity.","lec":"L11","sl":15,"c":"Gut epithelium's specialised cells: <b>M cells</b> = antigen sampling; <b>goblet cells</b> = mucus; <b>Paneth cells</b> = antimicrobial peptides (α-defensins/cryptdins, lysozyme) at the crypt base; <b>enterocytes</b> = barrier + pIgR-mediated IgA transport. <b>Inductive site</b> = Peyer's patch (where lymphocytes are activated); <b>effector site</b> = lamina propria (where plasma cells secrete IgA).","ans":"<b>M (microfold) cells</b> are specialised epithelial cells that <b>overlie Peyer's patches and other mucosal lymphoid follicles</b>.<br><br><b>Function — antigen sampling / transcytosis:</b><ul><li>They <b>take up antigens, particles and whole microbes from the gut lumen</b> and <b>transcytose</b> them across the epithelium to the underlying lymphoid tissue.</li><li>They deliver that antigen to <b>dendritic cells and macrophages</b> in the subepithelial dome pocket, which then prime <b>T and B cells</b> in the Peyer's patch — making the Peyer's patch the <b>inductive site</b> of gut mucosal immunity.</li><li>Structural adaptations that let them do this: <b>reduced mucin secretion</b>, <b>short irregular microvilli (microfolds)</b> instead of a dense brush border, a <b>thin glycocalyx</b>, and a deep <b>basolateral pocket</b> holding lymphocytes and DCs.</li></ul><b>Clinical catch:</b> because they are a doorway through the barrier, M cells are also the <b>entry route exploited by pathogens</b> such as <i>Salmonella</i>, <i>Shigella</i>, <i>Yersinia</i> and poliovirus.","img":"slides/L11_15.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q12","t":"β-defensin deficiency","s":"A patient has a defect in <b>β-defensin</b> production. What is the most likely consequence?","lec":"L11","sl":16,"c":"<b>Antimicrobial peptides</b> of the gut: <b>α-defensins (cryptdins)</b> from <b>Paneth cells</b>, <b>β-defensins</b> and <b>cathelicidin (LL-37)</b> from enterocytes, plus <b>lysozyme</b> and <b>RegIIIγ</b>. They punch holes in microbial membranes and shape the microbiota. Note the direction of the Th17 link: <b>IL-17/IL-22 → epithelium makes more defensins</b>.","o":{"A":"Impaired epithelial antimicrobial defence and increased mucosal infection","B":"Excessive activation of Th17 cells","C":"Increased mucus production","D":"Reduced antibody isotype switching","E":"Loss of oral tolerance to food antigens"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — defensins are <b>antimicrobial peptides</b> that disrupt the phospholipid membranes of microbes. Losing them removes a first-line chemical barrier, so the mucosa becomes <b>susceptible to bacterial and fungal colonisation and infection</b>.","B":"Th17 responses would if anything be <b>driven up</b> by the resulting microbial load, not caused directly; Th17 in fact <i>induces</i> defensin production.","C":"Mucus is a goblet-cell product — unrelated to defensin genes.","D":"Isotype switching depends on CD40–CD40L and cytokines (TGF-β for IgA), not defensins.","E":"Oral tolerance is a Treg/dendritic-cell function, not a defensin function."},"f":["completed"],"img":"slides/L11_16.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q14","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q14","t":"Anti-tumour effector mechanism","s":"What is the <b>most important</b> mechanism for eliminating tumour cells?","lec":"L12","sl":19,"c":"The anti-tumour hierarchy: <b>CTL (MHC I–restricted, perforin/granzyme + Fas)</b> is primary → <b>NK</b> covers MHC-I-loss variants (missing self) → <b>M1 macrophages</b> (ROS/NO, TNF) → <b>antibodies</b> (complement, ADCC). This CTL/NK complementarity is a favourite exam pairing: 'tumour loses MHC I, which cell kills it?' → NK.","ans":"<b>CD8⁺ cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs)</b> are the principal anti-tumour effector.<br><br><ul><li>CTLs recognise <b>tumour antigen peptides presented on MHC class I</b> on the tumour cell surface — every nucleated cell displays its intracellular proteins this way, so mutated/overexpressed tumour proteins become visible.</li><li>Killing is by the <b>perforin/granzyme</b> pathway and by <b>Fas–FasL</b> engagement, both triggering apoptosis.</li><li>They need help: <b>CD4⁺ Th1</b> cells license dendritic cells and supply IL-2/IFN-γ.</li></ul><b>Backup mechanism worth naming:</b> <b>NK cells</b> cover the escape route — when a tumour <b>down-regulates MHC class I</b> to hide from CTLs, it loses the inhibitory 'self' signal and NK cells kill it by <b>missing-self</b> recognition, plus <b>ADCC</b>. Macrophages (M1) and antibodies (complement, ADCC) contribute but are secondary.","also":"BM31 Q19-related, AX 59m Q1","img":"slides/L12_19.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q17","t":"AIRE defect","s":"A defect in <b>AIRE</b> causes autoimmune disease. What is the underlying mechanism?","lec":"L13A","sl":13,"c":"Central tolerance geography: <b>thymus cortex → positive selection</b>; <b>thymus medulla → negative selection</b>, powered by <b>AIRE</b> in mTECs presenting insulin, thyroglobulin and other tissue antigens. Mendelian tolerance genes worth memorising: <b>AIRE</b> → APS-1/APECED; <b>FOXP3</b> → IPEX; <b>CTLA4</b> → impaired checkpoint/Treg; <b>FAS</b> → ALPS.","o":{"A":"Defective peripheral tolerance","B":"MHC class I deficiency","C":"Failure to delete autoreactive T cells in the thymus","D":"Failure of receptor editing in the bone marrow","E":"Loss of regulatory T cell suppression in lymph nodes"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"AIRE acts in the <b>thymus</b> — that is <b>central</b>, not peripheral, tolerance.","B":"AIRE is a transcriptional regulator, not an MHC gene.","C":"Correct — <b>AIRE (AutoImmune Regulator)</b> in <b>medullary thymic epithelial cells</b> drives ectopic expression of peripheral tissue-restricted self antigens so that autoreactive thymocytes can be shown their targets and deleted. Losing AIRE means <b>negative selection fails</b> and self-reactive T cells escape to the periphery → <b>APS-1/APECED</b>.","D":"Receptor editing is a <b>B-cell</b> central tolerance mechanism in the bone marrow — unrelated to AIRE.","E":"Treg dysfunction is the <b>FOXP3</b>/IPEX mechanism, not AIRE (though AIRE also contributes to Treg generation)."},"also":"BM31 Q17, BM30 Q17, AX 59f 2f","img":"slides/L13A_13.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q18","t":"Treg suppressive cytokine","s":"Which cytokine secreted by <b>CD4⁺ T cells expressing CD25 and FOXP3</b> mediates immunosuppression?","lec":"L13A","sl":29,"c":"<b>Treg identity: CD4⁺, CD25(IL-2Rα)<sup>high</sup>, CD127<sup>low</sup>, FOXP3⁺.</b> Four suppression mechanisms: (1) secrete <b>IL-10 and TGF-β</b>; (2) <b>CTLA-4</b> strips B7 off APCs; (3) <b>IL-2 consumption</b> via CD25; (4) direct cytolysis. FOXP3 mutation → <b>IPEX</b>. This item recurs almost every cohort.","o":{"A":"IL-2","B":"IL-4","C":"IL-10","D":"IL-17","E":"TNF-α"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"IL-2 is what Tregs <b>consume</b> — they use high-affinity CD25 to soak IL-2 away from effector T cells. They depend on it for survival but do not secrete it as their suppressive mediator.","B":"IL-4 is the Th2 cytokine driving IgE class switching.","C":"Correct — CD4⁺<b>CD25</b><sup>high</sup><b>FOXP3</b>⁺ cells are <b>regulatory T cells</b>, and their principal suppressive cytokines are <b>IL-10</b> and <b>TGF-β</b>.","D":"IL-17 is the Th17 cytokine — pro-inflammatory, recruits neutrophils.","E":"TNF-α is pro-inflammatory, the opposite of a suppressive mediator."},"also":"BM31 Q18, BM30 Q18","img":"slides/L13A_29.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q20","t":"Successful Treg immunotherapy","s":"A patient with autoimmune disease receives <b>regulatory T-cell therapy</b>. If it works, which outcome indicates an effective immunomodulatory effect?","lec":"L13A","sl":29,"c":"Read the <i>direction</i> of every immunotherapy stem. <b>Immunosuppression working</b> = ↓ effector T proliferation, ↑ IL-10/TGF-β, ↑ Treg, ↓ autoantibody. <b>Immune activation working</b> (cancer) = ↑ T-cell proliferation, ↑ IFN-γ, tumour shrinkage. The same molecule can appear on both sides — CTLA-4-Ig suppresses (transplant), <b>anti</b>-CTLA-4 activates (cancer).","o":{"A":"Enhanced macrophage activation by IFN-γ","B":"Up-regulation of IgE production","C":"Suppression of effector T-cell proliferation","D":"Increased Th17 cell numbers","E":"Increased autoantibody titre"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"IFN-γ-driven macrophage activation is a <b>pro</b>-inflammatory Th1 readout — the wrong direction.","B":"More IgE means more allergic/Th2 activity, not successful suppression.","C":"Correct — the whole point of Treg therapy is to <b>dampen effector T-cell responses</b>, so reduced effector T-cell proliferation (with more IL-10/TGF-β) is the success signal.","D":"Th17 cells are strongly pathogenic in autoimmunity; a rise means the therapy failed.","E":"Rising autoantibody indicates continuing loss of tolerance."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM32 Q53","img":"slides/L13A_29.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q22","t":"Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","s":"A 45-year-old woman has <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>. Which best describes the pathogenesis?","lec":"L19","sl":45,"c":"RA snapshot: <b>symmetrical polyarthritis</b> of MCP/PIP/wrist, <b>morning stiffness > 1 hour</b>, F:M = 4:1, peak 30–50 y. Investigations: <b>RF</b>, <b>anti-CCP</b> (more specific), ESR/CRP, joint X-ray. Deformities: ulnar deviation, swan-neck, boutonnière. Treatment: DMARDs (<b>methotrexate</b> first line), then biologics (<b>anti-TNF</b>, anti-IL-6R). Hypersensitivity classification: predominantly <b>type IV</b> with a type III component.","o":{"A":"It is autoantibody-related","B":"It is a type I (IgE-mediated) hypersensitivity","C":"It is a direct joint infection of large joints","D":"It is caused by uric acid crystal deposition","E":"It is unrelated to autoantibodies"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — RA is an <b>autoantibody-associated systemic autoimmune disease</b>: <b>rheumatoid factor</b> (anti-IgG Fc) and <b>anti-CCP (ACPA)</b> against citrullinated peptides, together with T-cell-driven synovitis.","B":"Type I hypersensitivity is IgE/mast cell — the allergy mechanism, not RA.","C":"Septic arthritis is a single large joint with acute infection; RA is a <b>symmetrical small-joint polyarthritis</b>.","D":"Uric acid crystals cause <b>gout</b>, a distinct crystal arthropathy.","E":"The opposite of the truth — autoantibodies are central to RA and to its diagnosis."},"also":"BM32 Q6, BM31 Q18, AX 59f Q6","img":"slides/L19_45.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q23","t":"Immediate skin test reaction","s":"A 20-year-old woman undergoes a <b>skin prick test</b>. After 15 minutes a <b>wheal and flare</b> appear. What is the primary mechanism of this immediate reaction?","lec":"L15P1","sl":22,"c":"<b>Skin prick test</b> = type I. Read at <b>15–20 min</b>; positive = wheal ≥3 mm larger than the negative control. Stop antihistamines first. Contrast with the <b>patch test</b> (type IV, read at <b>48–72 h</b>) and <b>specific IgE</b> blood testing. Timing is the single most reliable discriminator in these stems: <b>minutes = I, hours = III, 2–3 days = IV.</b>","o":{"A":"Histamine release from mast cells","B":"Deposition of immune complexes","C":"Activation of the complement cascade","D":"Neutrophil recruitment","E":"T cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — allergen cross-links <b>IgE bound to FcεRI</b> on dermal mast cells → immediate degranulation → <b>histamine</b> → vasodilation and increased permeability = the <b>wheal (swelling)</b> and <b>flare (axon-reflex erythema)</b> within 15–20 minutes.","B":"Immune complex deposition is <b>type III</b> and takes hours (Arthus reaction ~4–8 h), not 15 minutes.","C":"Complement is not the primary driver of the wheal-and-flare; anaphylatoxins can degranulate mast cells but that is the anaphylactoid, non-IgE route.","D":"Neutrophils arrive in the <b>late-phase</b> reaction (6–12 h), along with eosinophils.","E":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity peaks at <b>48–72 hours</b> and is read as induration, not a 15-minute wheal."},"also":"BM32 Q26, BM31 Q29","img":"slides/L15P1_22.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q24","t":"Cytokine in allergic rhinitis","s":"A 5-year-old boy with allergic rhinitis has nasal congestion and itchy, watery eyes. Symptoms improve with antihistamines and worsen on pollen exposure. Which cytokine is primarily responsible?","lec":"L15P1","sl":13,"c":"Th2 cytokine division of labour: <b>IL-4</b> → Th2 differentiation + <b>IgE class switch</b>; <b>IL-5</b> → <b>eosinophil</b> growth/activation; <b>IL-13</b> → mucus hypersecretion, airway hyperreactivity, also IgE; <b>IL-9</b> → mast cells. Biologics map onto these: omalizumab (anti-IgE), mepolizumab (anti-IL-5), dupilumab (anti-IL-4Rα).","o":{"A":"IL-2","B":"IL-6","C":"IL-12","D":"IL-4","E":"IFN-γ"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"IL-2 is a T-cell growth factor — not the allergy switch.","B":"IL-6 is a general acute-phase/pro-inflammatory cytokine.","C":"IL-12 drives the <b>Th1</b> response — the opposite pole to allergy.","D":"Correct — <b>IL-4</b> is the master Th2 cytokine: it induces Th2 differentiation and directs B-cell <b>class switching to IgE</b>, the basis of type I hypersensitivity.","E":"IFN-γ is the Th1 effector cytokine and actually <b>suppresses</b> IgE switching."},"also":"BM30 Q29","img":"slides/L15P1_13.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q25","t":"Nickel contact dermatitis — mechanism","s":"A patient wears a <b>nickel necklace</b> and develops a red, itchy rash after <b>48 hours</b>. Which cell mediates this?","lec":"L15P1","sl":37,"c":"<b>Contact dermatitis = type IV, hapten-driven.</b> Classic haptens: <b>nickel</b> (earrings, necklaces, watch straps), poison ivy urushiol, chromate, latex additives, topical drugs. Diagnosis = <b>patch test at 48–72 h</b>. Treatment: avoid the allergen + topical corticosteroid. Note the exam's other type IV set: <b>tuberculin/Mantoux</b>, TB granuloma, chronic transplant rejection, type 1 diabetes, MS.","o":{"A":"B cell","B":"Mast cell","C":"T cell","D":"Eosinophil","E":"Neutrophil"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"B cells/antibody mediate types II and III, not contact dermatitis.","B":"Mast cells give the immediate type I reaction in minutes — the 48-hour delay excludes this.","C":"Correct — nickel contact dermatitis is <b>type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity</b>: nickel acts as a <b>hapten</b> binding skin proteins, Langerhans cells present it, and <b>CD4⁺ T cells (and CD8⁺ CTLs)</b> drive cytokine-mediated inflammation peaking at <b>48–72 hours</b>.","D":"Eosinophils belong to Th2/parasite and late-phase allergic responses.","E":"Neutrophils are the acute pyogenic/type III cell."},"also":"BM32 Q26, BM31 Q30, BM30 Q30, BM29 Q35, BM28 Q54","img":"slides/L15P1_37.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q26","t":"Nickel contact dermatitis — investigation","s":"Following on from the previous item (nickel-induced rash at 48 hours), which test should be performed?","lec":"L15P1","sl":42,"c":"Match the test to the mechanism: <b>type I</b> → skin prick (15–20 min), specific IgE, tryptase in anaphylaxis; <b>type II</b> → direct/indirect Coombs (DAT/IAT); <b>type III</b> → complement C3/C4 (low), immune complexes, ANA/anti-dsDNA; <b>type IV</b> → <b>patch test (48–72 h)</b>, tuberculin/Mantoux, interferon-γ release assay.","o":{"A":"Skin patch test","B":"Skin prick test","C":"Serum total IgE","D":"Allergen-specific IgE","E":"Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) test"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>patch test</b> applies the suspected allergen under occlusion and is read at <b>48 and 72 hours</b>; it is the diagnostic test for <b>type IV</b> contact hypersensitivity.","B":"The skin <b>prick</b> test detects <b>type I</b> IgE-mediated sensitivity, read at 15–20 minutes.","C":"Total IgE is a crude atopy marker for type I disease.","D":"Allergen-specific IgE also tests type I, not type IV.","E":"DHR assesses the neutrophil <b>respiratory burst</b> — the test for chronic granulomatous disease, unrelated here."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 Q31, BM30 Q31, BM29 Q36, BM28 Q55","img":"slides/L15P1_42.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q30","t":"Reaction after rabies vaccine","s":"A boy receives the <b>rabies vaccine</b> and afterwards develops raised red lesions and fever. What is the mechanism?","lec":"L15P2","sl":26,"c":"<b>Type III after vaccines/antisera:</b> <b>serum sickness</b> is the <i>systemic</i> form (fever, urticaria, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, 7–10 days after foreign protein — antivenom, ATG, chimeric antibodies, rabies antiserum); the <b>Arthus reaction</b> is the <i>local</i> form (painful oedematous swelling at the injection site 4–12 h after a booster in someone with high pre-existing antibody, e.g. tetanus toxoid). ⚠ If a stem instead stresses onset within minutes with wheeze and hypotension, switch to <b>type I</b>.","o":{"A":"Type I hypersensitivity, IgE-mediated","B":"Type II, IgG-mediated","C":"Type II, IgM-mediated","D":"Type IV, T cell-mediated","E":"Type III, immune complex-mediated"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"IgE anaphylaxis occurs within <b>minutes</b> of the injection with urticaria, bronchospasm and hypotension — not a delayed fever with lesions.","B":"Type II targets <b>cell-surface</b> antigens (RBCs, basement membrane) — a vaccine does not present those.","C":"Same objection as B; IgM type II is the ABO transfusion reaction pattern.","D":"Type IV is a localised, cell-mediated induration without the systemic fever/urticaria/arthralgia picture.","E":"Correct — a soluble foreign antigen given repeatedly forms <b>antigen–antibody immune complexes</b> that deposit in vessels and tissue, activating complement → the <b>serum-sickness-like reaction</b> (fever, urticarial rash, arthralgia) classically described after <b>rabies vaccine/antiserum</b>. Locally this is the <b>Arthus reaction</b>."},"f":["keydiff"],"img":"slides/L15P2_26.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q31","t":"Graft type — identical twin","s":"A 45-year-old man receives a kidney transplant from his <b>identical twin</b>. What type of transplantation is this?","lec":"L14","sl":7,"c":"The four graft types, memorised as a set: <b>Auto</b> = self → self · <b>Iso/syn</b> = identical twin, genetically identical · <b>Allo</b> = same species, different genes (most transplants) · <b>Xeno</b> = different species. Read the relationship carefully — <b>'identical twin' → iso</b>, but <b>'his brother/sister' → allo</b>. Both wordings appear in these papers and the answer changes.","o":{"A":"Isograft","B":"Xenograft","C":"Allograft","D":"Autograft","E":"Twinograft"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — an <b>isograft (syngeneic graft)</b> is between <b>genetically identical</b> individuals: identical twins, or inbred animals of the same strain. No rejection occurs, so no immunosuppression is needed.","B":"A <b>xenograft</b> crosses species (e.g. the 2022 pig heart transplant).","C":"An <b>allograft</b> is between <b>genetically different members of the same species</b> — the usual clinical transplant, e.g. from a sibling or unrelated donor.","D":"An <b>autograft</b> moves tissue from one site to another <b>in the same person</b> (skin graft for burns, CABG vein graft).","E":"'Twinograft' is not a term — it is the standard distractor planted in this question."},"also":"BM31 Q35, BM30 Q35","img":"slides/L14_7.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q32","t":"Hyperacute rejection","s":"A 65-year-old patient receives a transplant. <b>Minutes later</b> there is thrombotic occlusion and vessel damage. What causes this type of rejection?","lec":"L14","sl":27,"c":"<b>Rejection timeline — learn it as a table.</b> <b>Hyperacute</b>: minutes–hours · <b>recipient pre-formed antibody</b> + complement · thrombotic occlusion · prevented by ABO matching and crossmatch. <b>Acute</b>: days–months · recipient <b>T cells and antibodies</b> · parenchymal/endothelial injury · treated with immunosuppression. <b>Chronic</b>: months–years · <b>CD4⁺ T cells and cytokines</b> · <b>concentric intimal hyperplasia</b>, fibrosis, graft vasculopathy · largely untreatable.","o":{"A":"Recipient pre-formed antibodies against donor antigens","B":"Donor T cells attacking recipient tissue","C":"Recipient CD8⁺ T-cell cytotoxicity","D":"CD4⁺ T cells and cytokines causing intimal hyperplasia","E":"NK cell missing-self recognition"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>hyperacute rejection</b> occurs within <b>minutes to hours</b> of anastomosis. <b>Pre-formed antibodies</b> in the <b>recipient</b> (from prior transfusion, pregnancy, previous transplant, or ABO mismatch) bind graft endothelium → complement activation → neutrophil margination → <b>thrombosis and infarction</b>.","B":"Donor T cells attacking the host is <b>GVHD</b>, seen after HSC/bone-marrow transplant.","C":"CD8⁺ T-cell-mediated parenchymal injury is <b>acute</b> rejection — days to weeks, not minutes.","D":"CD4⁺/cytokine-driven concentric intimal hyperplasia is <b>chronic</b> rejection — months to years.","E":"NK missing-self is a tumour/viral surveillance mechanism, not the classical rejection pathway."},"also":"BM32 Q33, BM31 Q36, BM30 Q37, BM28 Q42","img":"slides/L14_27.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q33","t":"Chronic rejection","s":"A 50-year-old man received a <b>heart transplant 10 years ago</b> and now has <b>hyperplasia of the coronary arteries</b>. Which mechanism explains this?","lec":"L14","sl":32,"c":"<b>Chronic rejection = graft vasculopathy.</b> Signature histology: <b>concentric intimal hyperplasia</b> narrowing arterial lumina, with interstitial fibrosis and progressive loss of function. Organ-specific names: cardiac allograft vasculopathy (heart), bronchiolitis obliterans (lung), vanishing bile duct syndrome (liver), chronic allograft nephropathy (kidney). Slow, insidious, and the main limit on long-term graft survival.","o":{"A":"Acute rejection","B":"Hyperacute rejection","C":"Graft-versus-host disease","D":"Chronic rejection","E":"Ischaemia–reperfusion injury"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Acute rejection occurs within the first weeks to months and shows lymphocytic parenchymal infiltration, not a decade-later vasculopathy.","B":"Hyperacute rejection destroys the graft within minutes to hours of implantation.","C":"GVHD is a complication of <b>haematopoietic stem-cell</b> grafts, where donor T cells attack the host (skin, gut, liver) — not a coronary lesion in a heart recipient.","D":"Correct — <b>chronic rejection</b> presents months to years out with <b>concentric medial/intimal hyperplasia</b> of graft arteries (graft arteriosclerosis), driven by <b>CD4⁺ T cells and cytokines</b> causing smooth-muscle proliferation, luminal narrowing, ischaemia and fibrosis. In a heart this is <b>cardiac allograft vasculopathy</b>.","E":"Ischaemia–reperfusion injury occurs peri-operatively, not after 10 years."},"also":"BM32 Q32, BM31 Q37","img":"slides/L14_32.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q34","t":"Graft-versus-host disease","s":"A 42-year-old man with AML undergoes <b>allogeneic bone marrow transplantation</b> from an HLA-matched sibling. Three weeks later he develops jaundice, raised liver enzymes, watery diarrhoea and a generalised maculopapular rash. Liver biopsy shows lymphocytic infiltration and bile duct damage. Which mechanism BEST explains this?","lec":"L14","sl":37,"c":"<b>GVHD = donor T cells vs host.</b> Requirements: graft contains immunocompetent T cells, host is immunocompromised (so cannot reject them), and host expresses alloantigens the donor lacks. <b>Acute GVHD</b> (&lt;100 days): epithelial cell death in <b>skin, gut, liver</b>. <b>Chronic GVHD</b>: scleroderma-like fibrosis, sicca. Prevention: HLA matching, T-cell depletion, <b>cyclosporine/methotrexate</b> prophylaxis. Even HLA-identical siblings get GVHD because of <b>minor histocompatibility antigens</b>.","o":{"A":"Host T lymphocytes recognise donor MHC and mount a cytotoxic response","B":"Donor B lymphocytes produce alloantibodies against host HLA, activating complement","C":"Host NK cells lyse donor haematopoietic cells by missing-self recognition of MHC class I","D":"Donor T lymphocytes recognise host MHC as foreign and mount a cell-mediated response","E":"Donor macrophages release IL-1 and TNF-α, activating host endothelium"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"That is <b>graft rejection</b> (host-versus-graft) — the opposite direction. In GVHD the graft attacks the host.","B":"GVHD is <b>T-cell</b> mediated, not alloantibody mediated.","C":"Missing-self NK killing would cause graft failure, not the skin/gut/liver triad.","D":"Correct — <b>GVHD</b>: mature <b>donor T cells</b> in the marrow inoculum recognise <b>host alloantigens</b> as foreign and attack. The classic triad of target organs is <b>skin (maculopapular rash), gut (watery diarrhoea) and liver (jaundice, bile duct damage)</b>, appearing within the first ~100 days = acute GVHD.","E":"Cytokine release contributes to the 'cytokine storm' phase but is not the primary recognition mechanism the question asks for."},"also":"BM31 Q38, BM30 Q38, BM29 Q42","img":"slides/L14_37.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q35","t":"Hyper-IgM syndrome","s":"A 28-year-old with recurrent sinopulmonary infections and neutropenia is found to have a mutated <b>CD40L</b> gene. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L16","sl":26,"c":"<b>Antibody-defect gene map:</b> <b>BTK</b> → XLA (no B cells, all Ig low, absent tonsils) · <b>CD40L/CD40</b> → hyper-IgM (<b>IgM ↑/normal, IgG/IgA/IgE ↓</b>, neutropenia, <i>Pneumocystis</i>) · <b>IL2RG (common γ chain)</b> → X-linked SCID · <b>RAG1/2</b> → SCID · unknown/polygenic → CVID · <b>AIRE/FOXP3</b> → autoimmune, not infection-first. Onset 7–9 months, as maternal IgG wanes, is the tell-tale for B-cell defects.","o":{"A":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","B":"Common variable immunodeficiency","C":"Hyper-IgM syndrome","D":"Selective IgA deficiency","E":"Severe combined immunodeficiency"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"XLA is a <b>BTK</b> mutation: B cells are absent (CD19⁻), <b>all</b> immunoglobulin classes are low, and tonsils/lymph nodes are absent.","B":"CVID presents later with low IgG ± IgA/IgM but normal B-cell numbers and no single classic gene.","C":"Correct — <b>X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome</b> is caused by <b>CD40L (CD154)</b> mutation on T cells. Without CD40L–CD40 signalling B cells cannot <b>class switch</b>, so <b>IgM is normal or high while IgG, IgA and IgE are low</b>. <b>Neutropenia</b> is a characteristic accompanying feature, as is <i>Pneumocystis</i> and <i>Cryptosporidium</i> susceptibility.","D":"Selective IgA deficiency is the commonest PID, usually asymptomatic or with mucosal infections — no CD40L defect.","E":"SCID has profoundly low T <b>and</b> B function from the first months of life."},"also":"BM29 Q47, AX 59f Q11","img":"slides/L16_26.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q39","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q39","t":"Immunoglobulin replacement therapy","s":"What is the purpose of <b>immunoglobulin replacement therapy</b> in primary immunodeficiency?","lec":"L16","sl":30,"c":"<b>Passive vs active immunisation</b> is the concept being tested. <b>Passive</b> = receive <b>antibodies</b> → immediate protection, <b>no memory</b>, short-lived (IVIG, antivenom, rabies immunoglobulin, maternal IgG across the placenta / IgA in breast milk). <b>Active</b> = receive <b>antigen</b> → slower onset but <b>memory</b> and long-lasting (vaccines, natural infection). Treatment of B-cell PID = <b>IVIG + prophylactic antibiotics</b>; T-cell/SCID = <b>haematopoietic stem cell transplant</b>.","o":{"A":"To enhance T cell function","B":"To provide passive antibody protection","C":"To replace complement proteins","D":"To activate the patient's own B cells","E":"To suppress autoimmunity"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"IVIG supplies antibody; it does not reconstitute T-cell immunity (that needs stem-cell transplantation).","B":"Correct — IVIG/SCIG gives <b>pre-formed, pooled donor IgG</b>: immediate but temporary <b>passive immunity</b> against a broad range of pathogens. It must be repeated (typically 400–600 mg/kg every 3–4 weeks) because it confers <b>no memory</b>.","C":"It contains immunoglobulin, not complement components.","D":"It cannot make a patient with absent or non-functional B cells produce their own antibody.","E":"High-dose IVIG <i>is</i> used as an immunomodulator in some autoimmune disease (ITP, Kawasaki), but that is a different indication from replacement in PID."},"also":"AX 58/60 11f","img":"slides/L16_30.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q40","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q40","t":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","s":"A 7-month-old boy has recurrent otitis media and sinopulmonary infections. Examination shows <b>absent tonsils and lymph nodes</b>. All immunoglobulin classes are markedly decreased and flow cytometry shows <b>absence of CD19</b>. What is the most likely underlying defect?","lec":"L16","sl":25,"c":"The flow-cytometry shortcut for antibody defects: <b>CD19/CD20 absent</b> → XLA (BTK). <b>CD19 present but Ig low</b> → CVID or a class-switch defect. <b>IgM high with IgG/IgA low</b> → hyper-IgM (CD40L). Timing: symptoms begin at <b>7–9 months</b> as transplacental maternal IgG disappears. Physical clue unique to XLA: <b>no palpable tonsils or lymph nodes</b> because germinal centres never form.","o":{"A":"Thymic atrophy","B":"BTK mutation","C":"IL2RG (common γ chain) mutation","D":"RAG1/2 mutation","E":"CD40 ligand mutation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Thymic aplasia (DiGeorge) gives a <b>T-cell</b> defect with hypocalcaemia, cardiac anomalies and facial dysmorphism — and CD19⁺ B cells would be present.","B":"Correct — <b>absent CD19⁺ B cells</b> plus <b>all Ig classes low</b> plus <b>absent tonsils/lymph nodes</b> in a boy from ~6–9 months of age is <b>X-linked agammaglobulinaemia (Bruton)</b>, caused by <b>BTK</b> mutation blocking pre-B → immature B cell maturation.","C":"IL2RG causes <b>X-linked SCID</b>: T⁻B⁺NK⁻, with severe infection from the first weeks and failure to thrive — B cells would be present in number.","D":"RAG mutation causes SCID with absent T <b>and</b> B cells and profound early illness.","E":"CD40L gives hyper-IgM: <b>IgM normal/high</b>, B cells present. Here <i>all</i> classes are low and B cells are absent."},"also":"BM29 Q45, BM28 Q59, AX 59f/60 1f","img":"slides/L16_25.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q41","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q41","t":"Contraindication to live vaccines","s":"In which patient would a <b>live vaccine</b> be contraindicated?","lec":"L16","sl":40,"c":"<b>Live attenuated vaccines: BCG, MMR, OPV (Sabin), varicella, rotavirus, live attenuated influenza, yellow fever, JE (live).</b> Contraindicated in <b>severe immunodeficiency (SCID, advanced HIV), pregnancy, and high-dose immunosuppression/chemotherapy</b>. Killed/subunit/toxoid/conjugate/mRNA vaccines are safe in all these groups. Every cohort asks a version of 'which vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy?' → the live one, usually <b>MMR</b>.","o":{"A":"Patient with selective IgA deficiency","B":"HIV patient with controlled disease (CD4 > 500)","C":"Healthy adult taking a monoclonal antibody for migraine","D":"Child with SCID","E":"Patient with selective IgG subclass deficiency"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Selective IgA deficiency is usually mild; live vaccines are generally acceptable (caution mainly with blood products because of anti-IgA antibodies).","B":"HIV with <b>CD4 > 200–500</b> and controlled viraemia may receive MMR and varicella — live vaccines are contraindicated only in <b>severe</b> immunosuppression.","C":"An unrelated monoclonal antibody in a healthy adult is not a contraindication (unlike immunosuppressive biologics such as anti-TNF).","D":"Correct — <b>SCID</b> is the absolute contraindication. With no functional T or B cells the attenuated organism replicates unchecked and causes <b>disseminated disease</b> — the classic tragedy being <b>disseminated BCG</b> or vaccine-strain rotavirus/polio after routine immunisation of an undiagnosed SCID infant.","E":"IgG subclass deficiency is a partial antibody defect; live vaccines are not absolutely contraindicated."},"also":"BM31 Q40, BM30 Q40, BM29 Q51","img":"slides/L16_40.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q44","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q44","t":"Vaccines requiring an adjuvant","s":"Which of the following vaccines requires an <b>adjuvant</b> to boost effectiveness?","lec":"L17","sl":28,"c":"<b>Which vaccines need adjuvants? The non-living ones.</b> Inactivated, <b>subunit</b>, <b>conjugate</b> and toxoid vaccines need <b>adjuvants</b>; live attenuated, mRNA and viral-vector vaccines do not. Adjuvant functions: <b>depot effect</b> (slow antigen release), enhanced APC uptake, innate activation (TLR agonists). Examples: <b>alum</b>, MF59 oil-in-water, AS04. Distinguish <b>adjuvant</b> (boosts immunogenicity of a given antigen) from <b>carrier protein</b> (converts a T-independent polysaccharide into a T-dependent antigen — the conjugate-vaccine trick).","o":{"A":"MMR vaccine","B":"BCG","C":"Oral polio vaccine","D":"HPV vaccine","E":"Rotavirus vaccine"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"MMR is <b>live attenuated</b> — it replicates in the host and is intrinsically immunogenic, so no adjuvant is needed.","B":"BCG is live attenuated <i>Mycobacterium bovis</i>; no adjuvant.","C":"OPV (Sabin) is live attenuated; no adjuvant.","D":"Correct — <b>HPV vaccine is a protein subunit (virus-like particle) vaccine</b>. Non-replicating vaccines are weakly immunogenic on their own and require an <b>adjuvant</b> (aluminium salts, AS04) to generate a durable response.","E":"Rotavirus vaccine is live attenuated and given orally; no adjuvant."},"also":"BM31 Q41, BM30 Q41","img":"slides/L17_28.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q50","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q50","t":"'Living drug' immunotherapy","s":"Which of the following represents a form of <b>'living drug'</b>?","lec":"L18","sl":7,"c":"<b>Eras of cancer immunotherapy (L18's spine):</b> (1) <b>Coley's toxin</b> — bacterial infection causing tumour regression; (2) <b>Cytokine era</b> — high-dose <b>IL-2</b>, IFN-α (durable remissions in a minority, severe toxicity: flu-like syndrome, <b>capillary leak syndrome</b>); (3) <b>Checkpoint inhibitor revolution</b> (2011–) — anti-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab), anti-PD-1 (nivolumab, pembrolizumab), anti-PD-L1; (4) <b>'Living drugs'</b> (2017–) — <b>CAR-T</b>, adoptive cell transfer. Know one example per era.","o":{"A":"CAR-T cell therapy","B":"Checkpoint inhibitor","C":"High-dose IL-2","D":"BCG instillation","E":"Monoclonal anti-HER2 antibody"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>CAR-T cells</b> are the archetypal 'living drug': the patient's own T cells are harvested, <b>genetically engineered</b> to express a chimeric antigen receptor, expanded and re-infused. They then proliferate, persist and keep killing inside the patient — a drug that is alive and self-amplifying.","B":"Checkpoint inhibitors are <b>monoclonal antibodies</b> — conventional protein drugs.","C":"High-dose IL-2 is a recombinant <b>cytokine</b>, again a conventional biologic.","D":"BCG is a live organism used as a non-specific immune stimulant, but it is not an engineered cellular therapy — the lecture reserves 'living drug' for cellular therapies.","E":"Trastuzumab is a monoclonal antibody."},"also":"BM32 Q51, BM32 Q52","img":"slides/L18_7.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q51","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q51","t":"Nobel-prize checkpoint discovery","s":"<b>Tasuku Honjo</b> (with James Allison, honoured at the Vivian L. Smith Department) received the Nobel Prize for discovering what?","lec":"L18","sl":22,"c":"Names the lecture explicitly flags: <b>Allison → CTLA-4</b>, <b>Honjo → PD-1</b>, Nobel 2018, checkpoint blockade. <b>Coley</b> → the 19th-century observation that infection could shrink tumours. <b>Jenner</b> → smallpox vaccination (L17). Expect at least one 'who discovered what' item.","o":{"A":"Checkpoint blockade for cancer immunotherapy","B":"Living drugs and cellular therapy","C":"The cytokine era and BCG","D":"Coley's toxin","E":"CAR-T cell therapy"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>2018 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine</b> went to <b>James P. Allison</b> (CTLA-4) and <b>Tasuku Honjo</b> (PD-1) for 'cancer therapy by inhibition of negative immune regulation' — i.e. <b>immune checkpoint blockade</b>.","B":"Cellular therapy/CAR-T was developed by others (Rosenberg, June, Eshhar) and was not the subject of this prize.","C":"IL-2 cytokine therapy is associated with Steven Rosenberg; BCG for bladder cancer with Morales.","D":"Coley's toxin dates from the 1890s (William Coley).","E":"CAR-T is a 'living drug', a separate era from checkpoint blockade."},"also":"BM32 Q53","img":"slides/L18_22.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q52","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q52","t":"High-dose IL-2 in renal cell carcinoma","s":"A patient with metastatic renal cell carcinoma in the 1990s is treated with an agent that acts as a potent <b>T-cell growth factor</b>, producing durable remission in a subset of patients despite significant toxicity. Which molecule is responsible?","lec":"L18","sl":5,"c":"IL-2 toxicity is a two-part exam trick. Asked for the <b>most common</b> side effect → <b>flu-like syndrome</b>. Asked for the <b>most serious</b> side effect → <b>capillary leak syndrome</b> (hypotension, oedema, shock). Read the adjective. The same distinction appears in the AX bank twice with different answers.","o":{"A":"IFN-γ","B":"Ipilimumab","C":"Nivolumab","D":"High-dose IL-2","E":"BCG"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"IFN-γ is a macrophage-activating Th1 cytokine; IFN-<b>α</b> (not γ) had a role in RCC/melanoma, but it is not the classic 'potent T-cell growth factor'.","B":"Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) was approved in <b>2011</b> — the wrong era for a 1990s treatment.","C":"Nivolumab (anti-PD-1) came even later, around 2014.","D":"Correct — <b>high-dose IL-2</b> defined the <b>cytokine era</b> of the 1990s. As a T-cell growth factor it produced durable complete remissions in a small subset of metastatic <b>melanoma and renal cell carcinoma</b> patients, at the price of severe toxicity — flu-like syndrome and, seriously, <b>capillary leak syndrome</b> with shock.","E":"BCG is used intravesically for <b>non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer</b>, not metastatic RCC."},"also":"AX 60 Q62, AX 59m Q5","img":"slides/L18_5.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q53","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q53","t":"PD-1/PD-L1 blockade","s":"What is the primary therapeutic goal of blocking <b>PD-1/PD-L1</b>?","lec":"L18","sl":24,"c":"<b>Checkpoints are brakes; inhibitors cut the brake line.</b> <b>CTLA-4</b> acts <b>early, in lymph nodes</b>, competing with CD28 for B7 (priming phase) → blocked by <b>ipilimumab</b>. <b>PD-1</b> acts <b>late, in peripheral tissue/tumour microenvironment</b>, on already-activated T cells → blocked by <b>nivolumab, pembrolizumab</b> (or anti-PD-L1: atezolizumab). Cost of releasing the brake: <b>irAEs</b> — colitis, thyroiditis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, dermatitis — treated with corticosteroids. Contrast with transplant, where you <b>add</b> a brake: <b>CTLA-4-Ig (abatacept/belatacept)</b>.","o":{"A":"To prevent T cells from being inactivated","B":"To increase the total production of T cells","C":"To increase non-specific inflammatory responses","D":"To deliver a radioactive payload to the tumour","E":"To enhance NK cell degranulation directly"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — tumours express <b>PD-L1</b>, which engages <b>PD-1</b> on tumour-infiltrating T cells and switches them off (exhaustion). Blocking that interaction <b>releases the brake</b>, so already-primed T cells stay active and can kill the tumour.","B":"Checkpoint inhibitors do not increase thymic output or T-cell numbers overall — they restore the function of existing tumour-specific T cells.","C":"The effect is antigen-specific reactivation, not non-specific inflammation (though <b>irAEs</b> are the unwanted consequence).","D":"Radioimmunotherapy is a different modality entirely.","E":"NK enhancement is not the mechanism of PD-1 blockade."},"also":"BM32 Q20, BM31 Q19, BM30 Q20","img":"slides/L18_24.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q54","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q54","t":"Personalised mRNA cancer vaccines","s":"Personalised <b>mRNA cancer vaccines</b> are in development. What do these next-generation vaccines target?","lec":"L12","sl":13,"c":"<b>Tumour antigen classification.</b> <b>TSA / neoantigens</b> — expressed <b>only</b> on tumour cells, arising from mutations; truly tumour-specific, the ideal vaccine and CTL target, and individual to each patient. <b>TAA</b> — normal cellular proteins <b>overexpressed</b> or aberrantly expressed on tumours (oncofetal antigens <b>AFP, CEA</b>; differentiation antigens <b>PSA</b>, tyrosinase; overexpressed <b>HER2</b>) — useful as tumour <b>markers</b> but risk on-target off-tumour toxicity.","o":{"A":"Neoantigens specific to the individual's cancer","B":"Immune checkpoints such as CTLA-4","C":"Circulating cytokines","D":"Normal antigens shared by all cell types","E":"Viral capsid proteins common to all tumours"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — a personalised mRNA cancer vaccine sequences the patient's own tumour, identifies its unique mutation-derived <b>neoantigens (tumour-specific antigens)</b>, and encodes them in mRNA so the patient's APCs express and present them, priming a tumour-specific T-cell response.","B":"Blocking CTLA-4 is what checkpoint inhibitor <b>antibodies</b> do; a vaccine supplies antigen instead.","C":"Cytokines are not the target of a vaccine.","D":"Targeting antigens shared with normal cells would cause autoimmunity and is exactly what neoantigen targeting avoids.","E":"Only a minority of cancers are virus-driven (HBV→HCC, HPV→cervical), so viral proteins cannot be a universal target."},"img":"slides/L12_13.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q49","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q49","t":"BCG for non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer","s":"A patient with <b>non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer</b> is treated with intravesical <b>BCG</b>. What is the principle behind this — how does it reduce the tumour?","lec":"L18","sl":8,"c":"BCG is the surviving descendant of <b>Coley's toxin</b> — the observation that infection can shrink tumours. Classify it as <b>non-specific active immunotherapy</b>, contrasted with <b>specific active</b> (tumour vaccines, dendritic-cell vaccines), <b>passive</b> (monoclonal antibodies such as trastuzumab, rituximab), and <b>adoptive cellular</b> (CAR-T, TILs). Its two classic uses: <b>non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer</b> (intravesical) and historically cutaneous melanoma.","ans":"<b>BCG is non-specific active immunotherapy — it turns the bladder wall into an inflamed, immunologically hostile environment.</b><br><br><b>Mechanism, step by step:</b><ul><li>Live attenuated <i>Mycobacterium bovis</i> (BCG) is instilled <b>directly into the bladder</b>, where it <b>attaches to and is internalised by urothelial and tumour cells</b> (via fibronectin/integrin).</li><li>Its <b>PAMPs engage TLRs</b> on urothelium and resident antigen-presenting cells → massive innate activation and release of pro-inflammatory cytokines (<b>IL-1, IL-6, IL-8, IL-12, TNF-α</b>).</li><li>This recruits <b>neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells and T cells</b> into the bladder wall and drives a strong <b>Th1</b> response with <b>IFN-γ</b>.</li><li>The activated cytotoxic effectors (<b>CTLs, NK cells, activated macrophages</b>) then kill the tumour cells as bystanders of this local inflammatory storm; a BCG-specific delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction also develops.</li></ul><b>In one line:</b> BCG does not target a tumour antigen — it provides a <b>powerful non-specific local immune stimulus</b> that breaks tumour-induced immune tolerance in the bladder and lets anti-tumour effectors do the killing. It reduces both recurrence and progression in NMIBC.","also":"AX 60 Q59","img":"slides/L18_8.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q58","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q58","t":"Rheumatoid factor by sandwich ELISA","s":"A patient is suspected of having <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>, so <b>rheumatoid factor</b> is requested.<br>(1) Identify the technique shown.<br>(2) Read and interpret the graph: the standard curve is <b>y = 0.1x + 0.2</b> and the patient's <b>OD = 0.8</b>.","lec":"L19","sl":56,"c":"<b>Sandwich vs competitive ELISA — the direction of the signal is the whole trick.</b> <b>Sandwich (non-competitive):</b> signal ∝ analyte, <b>more analyte = more colour</b>; needs two epitopes so it suits larger antigens; high sensitivity and specificity. <b>Competitive:</b> patient analyte competes with labelled analyte, so <b>signal is inversely proportional</b> — more analyte = <b>less</b> colour; used for small molecules (haptens, drugs, hormones) with only one epitope. If a graph question gives you a line, always state the equation, substitute, and then interpret against a cut-off.","ans":"<b>(1) Technique — Sandwich ELISA</b> (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, non-competitive/two-site format).<br><ul><li>A <b>capture antibody/antigen</b> is adsorbed to the microplate well.</li><li>Patient serum is added; the analyte (<b>rheumatoid factor</b>) binds and is 'sandwiched'.</li><li>An <b>enzyme-conjugated detection antibody</b> is added (HRP or alkaline phosphatase), then substrate.</li><li>The enzyme converts substrate to a coloured product; <b>optical density (OD) is directly proportional to analyte concentration</b>. Washing steps between each stage remove unbound reagent.</li></ul><b>(2) Calculation</b><br>Standard curve: <b>y = 0.1x + 0.2</b>, where y = OD and x = concentration (IU/mL).<br>Substitute the patient's OD: <b>0.8 = 0.1x + 0.2</b><br>→ 0.1x = 0.8 − 0.2 = <b>0.6</b><br>→ <b>x = 6</b><br><br><b>Answer: the rheumatoid factor concentration is 6 units/mL (in the units of the standard curve).</b><br><br><b>Interpretation:</b> compare against the assay cut-off (commonly ~14 IU/mL for RF) — a value of 6 would be <b>below the cut-off, i.e. a negative/normal RF</b>. A negative RF does <b>not</b> exclude rheumatoid arthritis (seronegative RA occurs in roughly 20–30%); <b>anti-CCP</b> is the more specific test and should be added, alongside the clinical picture of symmetrical small-joint polyarthritis with morning stiffness &gt;1 hour.","f":["image"],"also":"BM31 written Q, BM30 written","img":"slides/L19_56.webp"},{"id":"BM32|Q60","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q60","t":"Specific IgE interpretation in asthma","s":"A 22-year-old man presents with dyspnoea and is diagnosed with <b>asthma</b>. <b>Allergen-specific IgE</b> testing is performed. Read and interpret the result.","lec":"L15P1","sl":24,"c":"<b>Specific IgE interpretation is worth guaranteed marks because the rule is mechanical:</b> (1) indicator ≥ class 3 → valid, otherwise uninterpretable; (2) patient allergens at ≥ class 3 are positive; (3) conclude type I IgE-mediated allergy to those allergens. The BM29, BM30 and BM31 written sections all use this exact format, including the deliberately failed-indicator version.","ans":"<b>How to read a specific-IgE (immunoblot/ImmunoCAP) panel — always in this order:</b><br><br><b>Step 1 — check the indicator/control first.</b> The panel carries a <b>standard indicator band</b>. If the indicator is <b>class 3 or above</b>, the strip has developed properly and the result is <b>valid and interpretable</b>. If the indicator is <b>below class 3 (class 0–2)</b>, the run has failed and <b>you must not interpret any of the patient bands</b> — report it as invalid and repeat. This is the trap the examiners plant repeatedly.<br><br><b>Step 2 — grade each allergen band.</b> Results are reported as <b>classes 0–6</b>:<br><table><tr><th>Class</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>0 / I</td><td>Negative — no detectable specific IgE</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Borderline/equivocal</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Low positive</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Positive (clinically significant)</td></tr><tr><td>4–6</td><td>Strongly positive — high specific IgE</td></tr></table><br><b>Step 3 — report the positives and link them to the history.</b> Name each allergen at <b>class 3 or higher</b> as a sensitising agent, and state that the patient's asthma is <b>allergic (atopic, extrinsic) asthma</b> — a <b>type I, IgE-mediated hypersensitivity</b> — triggered by those allergens. Typical culprits on a Thai inhalation panel: <b>house dust mite (<i>D. pteronyssinus</i>, <i>D. farinae</i>), cockroach, cat and dog epithelium, grass/weed pollen, <i>Aspergillus</i></b>.<br><br><b>Step 4 — management follows the reading:</b> allergen avoidance for the identified triggers, inhaled corticosteroid ± bronchodilator, and consider <b>allergen-specific immunotherapy</b> or <b>omalizumab (anti-IgE)</b> for severe allergic asthma.<br><br><b>Key caveat to write down:</b> sensitisation is not the same as clinical allergy — a positive specific IgE must correlate with the patient's symptom history before you call it the cause.","f":["image"],"also":"BM31 written Q9/Q10, BM30 written Q9/Q10, BM29 SG Q12","img":"slides/L15P1_24.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q7","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q7","t":"Vitamin C and wound healing","s":"A patient's wound fails to heal for 6 weeks. After a further 6 weeks taking <b>ascorbic acid</b> the wound closes. Which process did the ascorbic acid support?","lec":"L10","sl":73,"c":"<b>Factors influencing tissue repair</b> — a guaranteed exam list: <b>infection</b> (the most important cause of delay), <b>nutrition</b> (protein and especially <b>vitamin C</b> — collagen cross-linking), <b>glucocorticoids</b> (inhibit collagen synthesis), <b>diabetes/ischaemia/venous insufficiency</b>, <b>foreign body</b>, mechanical stress, and the type/extent of tissue involved. A non-healing wound that responds dramatically to vitamin C is always pointing at <b>collagen synthesis/fibroplasia</b>.","o":{"A":"Migration","B":"Fibroplasia","C":"Haemostasis","D":"Inflammation","E":"Epithelialisation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Cell migration into the wound depends on chemotaxis and integrins, not on vitamin C.","B":"Correct — <b>ascorbic acid (vitamin C) is an essential cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase</b>, the enzymes that hydroxylate proline and lysine in procollagen. Without it collagen cannot cross-link properly, so <b>fibroplasia</b> (fibroblast proliferation + collagen deposition) fails and the wound will not close — the mechanism of poor healing in scurvy.","C":"Haemostasis happens in the first minutes via platelets and the clotting cascade; it was clearly not the block here.","D":"Vitamin C deficiency does not prevent inflammation — the wound was inflamed and still not healing.","E":"Epithelialisation is driven by EGF/keratinocyte migration; the rate-limiting defect in scurvy is collagen synthesis."},"img":"slides/L10_73.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q8","t":"Inflammation phase of tissue repair","s":"Which finding indicates the <b>inflammation phase</b> of tissue repair?","lec":"L10","sl":70,"c":"Four phases with one signature each: <b>haemostasis</b> → platelet plug/fibrin · <b>inflammation</b> → <b>neutrophils then macrophages</b> · <b>proliferation</b> → angiogenesis, granulation tissue, fibroplasia, re-epithelialisation · <b>maturation/remodelling</b> → MMPs, collagen III→I, scar contraction. ⚠ The BM32 handwritten key marked several options here; the BM33 deck supports <b>macrophage infiltration</b> as the inflammation-phase indicator.","o":{"A":"Epithelialisation","B":"Neovascularisation","C":"Collagen deposition","D":"Macrophage infiltration","E":"Endothelial cell activation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Epithelialisation belongs to the <b>proliferative</b> phase.","B":"Neovascularisation (angiogenesis) is also <b>proliferative</b> phase.","C":"Collagen deposition is <b>proliferative → maturation</b>.","D":"Correct — <b>macrophage infiltration</b> is the hallmark of the inflammatory phase: neutrophils arrive first (24 h), then macrophages (2–3 days) clear debris, kill microbes and then switch phenotype to orchestrate repair.","E":"Endothelial activation happens at the very start of the vascular response, but the cellular marker the question wants — and the one the BM33 deck labels — is the macrophage infiltrate."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM30 Q7","img":"slides/L10_70.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q9","t":"Cardinal signs and their causes","s":"Which pairing of an inflammatory sign with its cause is <b>correct</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":11,"c":"Lock these five pairings in: <b>Rubor (redness) = vasodilation</b> · <b>Calor (heat) = vasodilation / increased blood flow</b> · <b>Tumor (swelling) = increased vascular permeability → exudate</b> · <b>Dolor (pain) = bradykinin + PGE₂</b> · <b>Functio laesa = the combined effect</b>. This exact matching item appears in BM31, BM30 and the AX bank.","o":{"A":"Redness — vasodilation","B":"Loss of function — increased vascular permeability","C":"Oedema — leukocyte transmigration","D":"Heat — leukocyte transmigration","E":"Pain — increased permeability"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>rubor (redness)</b> results from <b>vasodilation</b> and the consequent increase in blood flow to the injured area, mediated by histamine, NO and prostaglandins.","B":"Loss of function results from pain and swelling together, not directly from permeability.","C":"Oedema is caused by <b>increased vascular permeability</b> with exudate escaping into tissue — not by leukocyte transmigration.","D":"Heat, like redness, is caused by <b>vasodilation</b> and increased blood flow, not transmigration.","E":"Pain is caused by <b>bradykinin and prostaglandin E₂</b> acting on nerve endings (plus pressure from swelling), not by permeability itself."},"also":"BM30 Q9","img":"slides/L10_11.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q10","t":"Acute inflammation in pneumonia","s":"A 28-year-old healthy man has had fever with a productive cough for 2 days. Chest X-ray suggests pneumonia and lung biopsy reveals oedema. Which mechanism is most associated with this condition?","lec":"L10","sl":10,"c":"The acute inflammatory response has two arms, and the exam wants both named: <b>vascular events</b> — transient vasoconstriction → <b>vasodilation</b> (heat, redness) → <b>increased permeability</b> → exudate, stasis and congestion; <b>cellular events</b> — margination and rolling (selectins) → firm adhesion (integrins/ICAM) → <b>transmigration</b> → chemotaxis → phagocytosis. <b>Neutrophil</b> is the acute cell; <b>macrophage/lymphocyte/plasma cell</b> is the chronic set.","o":{"A":"Granulomatous inflammation","B":"Lung haemorrhage","C":"Abundant lymphocyte infiltration","D":"Vascular congestion and neutrophil infiltration","E":"Lung fibrosis"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Granulomatous inflammation is chronic and implies TB, fungal infection or sarcoidosis — not a 2-day bacterial pneumonia.","B":"Haemorrhage is not the defining mechanism of acute bacterial pneumonia.","C":"Lymphocyte infiltration indicates a <b>chronic</b> or viral process; 2 days of productive cough is acute and pyogenic.","D":"Correct — <b>2 days</b> of fever with a productive cough is <b>acute inflammation</b>. The vascular event is <b>vasodilation and congestion</b> with exudate (oedema); the cellular event is <b>neutrophil</b> margination, transmigration and alveolar accumulation.","E":"Fibrosis is a late outcome of chronic injury, taking weeks to months."},"also":"BM30 Q10, BM29 Q20","img":"slides/L10_10.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q11","t":"Acute inflammation of the appendix","s":"Biopsy shows <b>several neutrophils in the muscularis propria with an oedematous appendix</b>. What is the abnormality?","lec":"L10","sl":37,"c":"<b>Read the cell, name the process.</b> <b>Neutrophil</b> → acute. <b>Lymphocyte + plasma cell + macrophage (± fibrosis)</b> → chronic. <b>Epithelioid macrophage + multinucleated giant cell (± caseation)</b> → granulomatous. <b>Eosinophil</b> → parasite or allergy. This single rule answers a large fraction of the L10 items across every cohort.","o":{"A":"Scar formation","B":"No inflammation","C":"Acute inflammation","D":"Chronic inflammation","E":"Granulomatous inflammation"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Scar formation is the end-stage of repair — fibroblasts and collagen, not neutrophils.","B":"Neutrophil infiltration plus oedema is by definition inflammation.","C":"Correct — <b>neutrophils + oedema</b> is the textbook signature of <b>acute inflammation</b>. In the appendix this is acute appendicitis; transmural neutrophil infiltration into the <b>muscularis propria</b> is the diagnostic criterion.","D":"Chronic inflammation shows <b>lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages</b> with fibrosis.","E":"Granulomatous inflammation requires <b>epithelioid macrophages and giant cells</b>."},"also":"BM32 Q1, BM30 Q11, BM29 Q21","img":"slides/L10_37.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q12","t":"Endogenous pyrogen","s":"Following on from the previous item, which mediator causes the <b>fever</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":31,"c":"<b>Fever pathway, worth writing out once:</b> LPS/PAMP → macrophage releases <b>TNF, IL-1, IL-6</b> (endogenous pyrogens) → act on <b>hypothalamic vascular organ of the lamina terminalis</b> → <b>COX-2</b> → <b>PGE₂</b> → raises the thermoregulatory set-point → fever. This is exactly why <b>NSAIDs/aspirin (COX inhibitors) are antipyretic</b>. The systemic acute-phase effects of TNF/IL-1/IL-6 also include leukocytosis, CRP production and, at extreme levels, septic shock.","o":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Complement","C":"Leukotriene","D":"Lysosomal enzyme","E":"TNF"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Histamine causes vasodilation, increased permeability and itch — not fever.","B":"Complement fragments are anaphylatoxins and opsonins; C5a is chemotactic, but complement is not the pyrogen.","C":"Leukotrienes cause bronchoconstriction, increased permeability and chemotaxis (LTB₄).","D":"Lysosomal enzymes cause tissue damage during degranulation.","E":"Correct — <b>TNF (with IL-1 and IL-6) are the endogenous pyrogens</b>. They act on the hypothalamus to induce <b>COX-2 → PGE₂</b>, which resets the thermoregulatory set-point upward → fever."},"also":"BM30 Q12, BM29 Q22, AX 57m Q39","img":"slides/L10_31.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q13","t":"Exudate vs transudate","s":"A 50-year-old man has skin lesions on his left arm. Acute dermatitis with bacterial infection is diagnosed; biopsy shows several neutrophils, necrotic debris and tissue oedema. Which statement describes this <b>tissue oedema</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":12,"c":"<b>Exudate vs transudate</b> — one of the highest-yield two-column tables in L10. <b>Exudate</b>: <i>increased vascular permeability</i> (inflammation) · protein-<b>rich</b> · specific gravity &gt;1.020 · cells and debris present · cloudy · examples = pus, empyema, inflammatory effusion. <b>Transudate</b>: <i>altered Starling forces</i> (↑hydrostatic or ↓oncotic pressure) · protein-<b>poor</b> · SG &lt;1.012 · few cells · clear · examples = heart failure oedema, nephrotic syndrome, ascites in cirrhosis.","o":{"A":"This fluid is called exudate","B":"This fluid is called transudate","C":"It is loss of intravascular fluid without plasma protein into the tissue","D":"It is caused by increased hydrostatic pressure and decreased colloid osmotic pressure","E":"It is caused by decreased hydrostatic pressure and increased colloid osmotic pressure"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — inflammation increases <b>vascular permeability</b>, so <b>protein-rich fluid with cells (neutrophils) and debris</b> escapes. That is an <b>exudate</b>: high protein, high specific gravity (&gt;1.020), high LDH, cellular.","B":"A transudate is the <i>non</i>-inflammatory fluid: protein-poor, clear, low specific gravity.","C":"'Without plasma protein' describes a <b>transudate</b>, not this inflammatory fluid.","D":"Increased hydrostatic pressure with decreased oncotic pressure is the mechanism of a <b>transudate</b> (heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis) — a Starling-forces problem, not a permeability problem.","E":"This combination would actually reduce fluid movement out of the vessel."},"also":"BM30 Q13, BM29 Q23","img":"slides/L10_12.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q14","t":"Resident cell initiating inflammation","s":"Following on from the previous item, which <b>resident cell</b> initiates this inflammation?","lec":"L10","sl":8,"c":"<b>Recognition is step one of inflammation</b>, and it belongs to <b>resident macrophages, dendritic cells and mast cells</b>. They sense microbes and necrosis through <b>TLRs</b> (bacterial/viral products), <b>NLRs/inflammasome</b> (→ IL-1β), <b>CLRs</b> (fungal glucans, e.g. Dectin-1) and <b>RLRs</b> (viral RNA). Output = TNF, IL-1, IL-6, chemokines → endothelial activation → leukocyte recruitment. The L10 deck's '5 R's' framing: <b>Recognition, Recruitment, Removal, Regulation, Repair</b>.","o":{"A":"Fibroblast","B":"Macrophage","C":"Muscle cell","D":"Epidermal cell","E":"Endothelial cell"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Fibroblasts are structural and act later, in the repair phase.","B":"Correct — the <b>tissue-resident macrophage</b> is the sentinel cell. It carries <b>TLRs and other PRRs</b>, recognises PAMPs/DAMPs first, and releases <b>TNF, IL-1, IL-6 and chemokines</b> that trigger the whole vascular and cellular cascade. (Mast cells are the other resident initiator, releasing histamine.)","C":"Muscle cells have no initiating immune role here.","D":"Keratinocytes contribute cytokines but are not the primary sentinel.","E":"Endothelial cells <b>respond</b> to the mediators by expressing selectins and ICAM-1; they do not initiate the recognition step."},"also":"BM30 Q14","img":"slides/L10_8.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q15","t":"Chronic cholecystitis","s":"A 50-year-old woman has had right upper quadrant pain for 3 months; ultrasound shows a gallbladder stone. Histology shows many <b>lymphocytes and plasma cells</b> infiltrating the wall with <b>foci of fibrosis</b> and a few reactive lymphoid follicles with germinal centres. Which process is occurring?","lec":"L10","sl":36,"c":"<b>Chronic inflammation</b>: prolonged (weeks–months), with <b>simultaneous</b> active inflammation, tissue injury and healing. Cells: <b>macrophages (dominant), lymphocytes, plasma cells</b> ± eosinophils. Features: <b>angiogenesis and fibrosis</b>. Causes: <b>persistent infection</b> (TB, syphilis, fungi), <b>autoimmune disease</b>, prolonged exposure to a toxic agent (silica), and <b>persistent physical irritant</b> — a gallstone, a splinter, a suture.","o":{"A":"Healing and repair","B":"Chronic inflammation","C":"Fibrinous inflammation","D":"Suppurative inflammation","E":"Granulomatous inflammation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Healing and repair would show granulation tissue and organised scar, not an active lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with germinal centres.","B":"Correct — <b>3 months</b> of symptoms plus <b>lymphocytes, plasma cells, fibrosis and lymphoid follicles</b> is the definition of <b>chronic inflammation</b> — here chronic cholecystitis secondary to a persistent stone.","C":"Fibrinous inflammation is fibrin deposition on serosal surfaces, an acute pattern.","D":"Suppurative (purulent) inflammation means pus and neutrophils — none are described.","E":"Granulomatous inflammation requires epithelioid macrophages and giant cells."},"also":"BM30 Q15","img":"slides/L10_36.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q16","t":"Foreign body granuloma","s":"A 45-year-old man has a small mass on his left foot; he got a <b>splinter</b> there and never removed it. Grossly there is no heat, redness or swelling. Which histological picture is correct?","lec":"L10","sl":42,"c":"<b>Two granuloma types.</b> <b>Foreign-body granuloma</b> — inert material too large to phagocytose (splinter, suture, talc); <b>foreign-body giant cells</b> with haphazard nuclei; <b>no</b> T-cell response needed, <b>no</b> caseation. <b>Immune granuloma</b> — persistent T-cell-mediated (Th1/IFN-γ) response to a poorly degradable microbe; <b>epithelioid macrophages</b> and <b>Langhans giant cells</b> (nuclei in a horseshoe at the periphery); TB shows <b>central caseous necrosis</b>, sarcoidosis is <b>non-caseating</b>.","o":{"A":"Cluster of many neutrophils with necrotic and destructive tissue","B":"Many neutrophils and some lymphoid follicles","C":"Cluster of lymphocytes and plasma cells with foci of follicular necrosis","D":"Cluster of epithelioid cells and multinucleated giant cells with central necrosis","E":"Many foreign-body giant cells with some lymphocytes and plasma cells"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Neutrophils with destruction is acute suppurative inflammation — but there is no heat, redness or swelling, so the acute phase has passed.","B":"Same objection: neutrophils indicate an active acute process.","C":"A pure lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate is non-specific chronic inflammation and misses the defining giant-cell reaction to the splinter.","D":"Epithelioid cells and giant cells with <b>central (caseous) necrosis</b> describes an <b>infectious</b> granuloma, classically <b>TB</b> — but a splinter causes no caseation.","E":"Correct — a retained splinter causes a <b>foreign-body granuloma</b>: <b>foreign-body-type multinucleated giant cells</b> (nuclei scattered randomly) surrounding the indigestible material, with lymphocytes and plasma cells, and <b>no caseous necrosis</b>."},"also":"BM32 Q3, BM30 Q16","img":"slides/L10_42.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q17","t":"T-cell tolerance","s":"What is <b>correct</b> about T cell tolerance?","lec":"L13A","sl":26,"c":"<b>Central vs peripheral tolerance — the master table.</b> <b>Central (T)</b>: thymus · deletion of high-affinity self-reactive thymocytes (<b>AIRE</b>-driven, medulla) · diversion to <b>nTreg</b>. <b>Central (B)</b>: bone marrow · deletion · <b>receptor editing</b> (unique to B cells). <b>Peripheral (T)</b>: <b>anergy</b> (signal 1 without CD28 co-stimulation) · <b>suppression by Tregs</b> · <b>deletion</b> by Fas–FasL apoptosis · ignorance at <b>immunologically privileged sites</b>. <b>Peripheral (B)</b>: anergy or apoptosis.","o":{"A":"Peripheral tolerance mechanisms for T cells exist in the thymus","B":"AIRE is a transcription factor necessary for positive selection of T cells","C":"Some T cells may change their receptor during central tolerance","D":"Some self-reactive CD4⁺ T cells may differentiate into regulatory T cells during central tolerance","E":"Education and elimination of T cells is the same as for B cells"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"By definition <b>peripheral</b> tolerance occurs <b>outside</b> the generative organs — in lymph nodes, spleen and peripheral tissues. Tolerance in the thymus is <b>central</b>.","B":"<b>AIRE drives negative selection</b>, not positive selection — it makes mTECs express peripheral tissue antigens so autoreactive thymocytes can be deleted.","C":"<b>Receptor editing</b> is a <b>B-cell</b> mechanism in the bone marrow; T cells do not edit their TCR.","D":"Correct — during central tolerance in the thymus, some strongly self-reactive CD4⁺ thymocytes are not deleted but instead diverted into the <b>natural regulatory T cell (nTreg)</b> lineage, expressing FOXP3.","E":"They differ in site and in mechanism: T cells are educated in the <b>thymus</b> (deletion, Treg diversion), B cells in the <b>bone marrow</b> (deletion and <b>receptor editing</b>)."},"also":"BM30 Q17, BM29 Q30, BM28 Q10","img":"slides/L13A_26.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q18","t":"Treg function in rheumatoid arthritis","s":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has fewer <b>CD3⁺CD4⁺FOXP3⁺</b> cells than normal. What is the function of this cell?","lec":"L13A","sl":27,"c":"Treg deficiency or dysfunction underlies autoimmunity — reduced Treg number/function is described in <b>RA, SLE and type 1 diabetes</b>, and complete <b>FOXP3</b> loss causes <b>IPEX</b> (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked). Remember the full phenotype: <b>CD4⁺ CD25<sup>high</sup> CD127<sup>low</sup> FOXP3⁺</b>, suppressing via <b>IL-10, TGF-β, CTLA-4</b> and <b>IL-2 consumption</b>.","o":{"A":"Secretes IL-12","B":"Produces IFN-α","C":"Acts as an innate lymphocyte","D":"Binds self antigen and causes tissue damage","E":"Expresses large numbers of IL-2 receptors on its surface"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"IL-12 is produced by <b>dendritic cells and macrophages</b> to drive Th1 differentiation — a pro-inflammatory function.","B":"IFN-α is a type I interferon from plasmacytoid dendritic cells and virus-infected cells.","C":"Tregs are CD3⁺ — they carry a rearranged TCR and are therefore <b>adaptive</b> lymphocytes, not innate.","D":"That describes an autoreactive effector T cell — the very cell Tregs are supposed to suppress.","E":"Correct — CD3⁺CD4⁺<b>FOXP3</b>⁺ identifies a <b>regulatory T cell</b>, whose defining surface phenotype is <b>CD25<sup>high</sup> = high-affinity IL-2 receptor α chain</b> (with low CD127). They use that receptor both for their own IL-2-dependent survival and to <b>consume IL-2</b>, starving effector T cells."},"also":"BM32 Q18, BM30 Q18, BM28 Q9","img":"slides/L13A_27.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q19","t":"Reducing T-cell activation after allograft","s":"Which agent <b>decreases</b> T-cell activation and benefits a patient receiving an <b>allograft</b>?","lec":"L13A","sl":22,"c":"This is the single most reliable trap in the block: <b>the same checkpoint appears with opposite intent in transplant and in cancer.</b> Transplant/autoimmunity → you want <b>less</b> T-cell activity → <b>CTLA-4-<u>Ig</u></b> (adds a brake). Cancer → you want <b>more</b> T-cell activity → <b>anti-CTLA-4, anti-PD-1, anti-PD-L1</b> (cut the brake). Read whether the stem is a graft or a tumour before you choose.","o":{"A":"CTLA-4-Ig","B":"Anti-CTLA-4","C":"Anti-PD-1","D":"Anti-PD-L1","E":"IL-12"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>CTLA-4-Ig (abatacept/belatacept)</b> is a soluble fusion protein that <b>binds B7 (CD80/CD86)</b> on APCs, denying <b>CD28</b> its co-stimulatory signal 2. T cells receive signal 1 alone → <b>anergy</b>. This is <b>costimulatory blockade</b>, used to prevent allograft rejection.","B":"<b>Anti</b>-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab) <b>removes</b> the brake and <b>activates</b> T cells — the cancer strategy, and exactly what you must not do to a transplant patient.","C":"Anti-PD-1 also releases a brake and boosts T cells; giving it to a transplant recipient can precipitate rejection.","D":"Anti-PD-L1 has the same activating effect as anti-PD-1.","E":"IL-12 drives Th1 differentiation — strongly pro-inflammatory."},"also":"BM32 Q53, BM30 Q20, AX 60 Q63","img":"slides/L13A_22.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q20","t":"Recurrent fungal and bacterial infection","s":"A man has <b>recurrent fungal and bacterial infections</b>. What should be checked?","lec":"L16","sl":12,"c":"<b>Map the organism to the defect</b> — the single most useful table in L16. <b>B cell/antibody</b> → encapsulated bacteria (<i>S. pneumoniae</i>, <i>H. influenzae</i>), <i>Giardia</i>, enterovirus; onset 7–9 months. <b>T cell</b> → <b>viruses, fungi (<i>Candida</i>), <i>Pneumocystis</i>, intracellular bacteria (mycobacteria)</b>; onset in early infancy. <b>Phagocyte</b> → catalase-positive bacteria (<i>S. aureus</i>, <i>Serratia</i>, <i>Burkholderia</i>), <i>Aspergillus</i>; skin abscesses, poor wound healing. <b>Complement</b> → <b><i>Neisseria</i></b> (C5–C9), pyogenic infection (C3), immune-complex/lupus-like disease (C1–C4).","o":{"A":"Low CD4 and CD3","B":"Low CD19 and CD20","C":"Low C3 and C4","D":"Low FOXP3","E":"Low CD34"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the combination of <b>fungal</b> (including mucocutaneous candidiasis) <b>and</b> bacterial infection points to a <b>T-cell defect</b>. CD3 marks all T cells and CD4 the helper subset; Th1 and Th17 responses are required to control fungi, and T-cell help is also needed for good antibody responses.","B":"CD19/CD20 are <b>B-cell</b> markers — a pure B-cell defect gives recurrent <b>encapsulated bacterial</b> sinopulmonary infection, not prominent fungal disease.","C":"Low C3/C4 indicates complement deficiency, which classically presents with <b>Neisseria</b> infection (terminal pathway) or immune-complex disease.","D":"Low FOXP3 → IPEX, which presents with <b>autoimmunity</b> (enteropathy, endocrinopathy, dermatitis), not this infection pattern.","E":"CD34 marks haematopoietic stem cells — not a diagnostic immunodeficiency screen."},"also":"BM30 Q24, BM29 Q45","img":"slides/L16_12.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q21","t":"Acute rheumatic fever mechanism","s":"What causes <b>acute rheumatic fever</b>?","lec":"L13B","sl":14,"c":"<b>How infection triggers autoimmunity — three named mechanisms:</b> <b>Molecular mimicry</b> (cross-reactivity between microbial and self epitopes — <b>ARF</b>, rheumatic heart disease, Guillain–Barré after <i>Campylobacter</i>); <b>Bystander activation</b> (infection provides inflammation and co-stimulation that wakes up nearby autoreactive T cells); <b>Epitope spreading</b> (tissue damage releases new self antigens, broadening the response). Add <b>superantigens</b> for polyclonal activation. ARF diagnosis = <b>modified Jones criteria</b> + evidence of preceding strep (<b>ASO titre</b>).","o":{"A":"Receptor editing","B":"Cancer tolerance","C":"Epitope spreading","D":"Molecular mimicry","E":"Bystander activation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Receptor editing is a B-cell central tolerance mechanism in the bone marrow.","B":"'Cancer tolerance' is not a mechanism of autoimmunity.","C":"<b>Epitope spreading</b> is the <i>progression</i> of an established autoimmune response to new self antigens released by ongoing tissue damage — it broadens autoimmunity but does not initiate ARF.","D":"Correct — <b>molecular mimicry</b>: antibodies raised against <b>group A <i>Streptococcus pyogenes</i></b> cell-wall M protein <b>cross-react</b> with structurally similar self antigens in <b>cardiac myosin, valve, joint, skin and brain</b> tissue, causing carditis, migratory polyarthritis, chorea and erythema marginatum 2–3 weeks after a pharyngitis.","E":"<b>Bystander activation</b> is non-specific activation of nearby autoreactive lymphocytes by infection-induced inflammation — a real mechanism, but mimicry is the specific ARF answer."},"also":"BM30 Q22, AX 59f Q2, AX 59f Q12","img":"slides/L13B_14.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q22","t":"Graves' disease","s":"What causes <b>Graves' disease</b>?","lec":"L15P2","sl":14,"c":"<b>Type II, mechanism III — antibody against a receptor, causing dysfunction rather than destruction.</b> <b>Graves'</b>: <b>stimulating</b> antibody to the TSH receptor → hyperthyroidism. <b>Myasthenia gravis</b>: <b>blocking</b> antibody to the acetylcholine receptor → weakness. Both are antibody-mediated <b>without</b> cell lysis — the reason they sit in their own mechanism. Compare Graves' with <b>Hashimoto's</b> (destructive, anti-TPO/anti-thyroglobulin, hypothyroid) — the AX bank asks what they share: <b>thyroid enlargement (goitre)</b>.","o":{"A":"Viral infection","B":"Deficient maturation of B and T cells","C":"Autoantibody","D":"Immune complex deposition","E":"Cytotoxic T-cell killing of thyrocytes"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Infection can be a trigger for autoimmunity in general, but it is not the effector mechanism of Graves'.","B":"Defective lymphocyte maturation causes immunodeficiency, not hyperthyroidism.","C":"Correct — Graves' disease is caused by a <b>stimulating autoantibody</b>: <b>thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin (TSI)</b>, an IgG against the <b>TSH receptor</b>. It mimics TSH, driving continuous hormone production → <b>high T3/T4 with suppressed TSH</b>. This is <b>type II hypersensitivity, mechanism III — antibody-mediated cellular dysfunction</b>.","D":"Immune-complex deposition is type III (SLE, post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, serum sickness).","E":"CTL-mediated destruction is the mechanism of type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto's, not Graves'."},"also":"BM30 Q33, BM28 Q50, AX 59f Q31","img":"slides/L15P2_14.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q23","t":"Definition of primary immunodeficiency","s":"Which statement best describes a <b>primary</b> immunodeficiency disorder?","lec":"L16","sl":14,"c":"<b>Primary</b> = genetic, congenital, ~1 in 500 people carry one; hallmark is <b>increased susceptibility to infection</b> (recurrent, persistent, unusual organism, severe). <b>Secondary</b> = acquired: <b>HIV</b>, malignancy (especially haematological), <b>chemotherapy/immunosuppressants/corticosteroids</b>, <b>malnutrition</b> (commonest worldwide), splenectomy, diabetes, renal failure, burns, extremes of age.","o":{"A":"Inherited genetic defect affecting the immune system","B":"Autoimmune disorder affecting immune cells","C":"Caused by nutrient deficiency","D":"Acquired during life","E":"Caused by environmental factors"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>primary immunodeficiency (PID)</b>, now also called an <b>inborn error of immunity</b>, is a <b>genetic/inherited</b> defect present from birth, though it may manifest later in life.","B":"Autoimmunity is loss of self-tolerance — a different category, although some PIDs (CVID, ALPS) do feature autoimmunity.","C":"Malnutrition is the world's commonest cause of <b>secondary</b> immunodeficiency.","D":"'Acquired' is the definition of <b>secondary</b> immunodeficiency.","E":"Environmental causes (drugs, radiation, infection) again give <b>secondary</b> immunodeficiency."},"also":"BM28 Q56, AX 60 5f","img":"slides/L16_14.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q24","t":"Severe combined immunodeficiency","s":"A 2-year-old girl has chronic diarrhoea, recurrent pneumonia and failure to thrive. Laboratory testing shows very low T <b>and</b> B cells; genetic testing reveals an <b>IL2RG</b> mutation. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L16","sl":38,"c":"<b>SCID</b> = absence of all adaptive immune function; onset in the <b>first few months</b>. Genetics: <b>X-linked (IL2RG / common γ chain) is the commonest</b>, then autosomal recessive <b>ADA deficiency</b>, <b>RAG1/2</b>, JAK3, IL7Rα. Clues: failure to thrive, persistent diarrhoea, <b>oral thrush</b>, <i>Pneumocystis</i> pneumonia, absent thymic shadow, lymphopenia. Treatment: <b>haematopoietic stem cell transplantation</b>, enzyme replacement (PEG-ADA), gene therapy, IVIG — and <b>strictly avoid live vaccines</b> and use irradiated CMV-negative blood.","o":{"A":"Complement C3 deficiency","B":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","C":"Chronic granulomatous disease","D":"Severe combined immunodeficiency","E":"Common variable immunodeficiency"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"C3 deficiency causes recurrent pyogenic infection and immune-complex disease, with normal lymphocyte counts.","B":"XLA (BTK) affects <b>B cells only</b> — T cells are normal, and it occurs in boys.","C":"CGD is a <b>phagocyte</b> defect (NADPH oxidase) presenting with abscesses and granulomas; lymphocytes are normal.","D":"Correct — <b>IL2RG</b> encodes the <b>common γ chain</b> shared by the receptors for IL-2, 4, 7, 9, 15 and 21. Losing it blocks <b>IL-7</b> signalling (essential for T-cell development) and <b>IL-15</b> (NK development) → <b>X-linked SCID: T⁻ B⁺ NK⁻</b>. Antibody function fails too because B cells get no T-cell help — hence 'combined'. Presentation is exactly this: <b>failure to thrive, chronic diarrhoea, recurrent pneumonia and thrush</b> in the first year.","E":"CVID presents in older children/adults with low IgG and normal B-cell numbers."},"also":"BM30 Q26","img":"slides/L16_38.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q25","t":"Lepromatous leprosy","s":"A 30-year-old with <b>tuberculoid leprosy</b> progresses to <b>lepromatous leprosy</b>. What would be found?","lec":"L16","sl":7,"c":"<b>Leprosy is the textbook Th1/Th2 spectrum disease.</b> <b>Tuberculoid</b> (paucibacillary): Th1, IFN-γ + TNF, activated macrophages, <b>granulomas</b>, few organisms, positive lepromin test, localised nerve/skin lesions. <b>Lepromatous</b> (multibacillary): Th2, IL-4/IL-10, <b>high antibody but useless</b>, foamy macrophages packed with bacilli, negative lepromin test, disseminated disfiguring disease. Same organism, opposite outcome, determined entirely by the T-helper polarisation.","o":{"A":"Increased Th1","B":"Decreased TNF production","C":"Decreased leukocyte adhesion","D":"Decreased mast cell degranulation","E":"Increased IFN-γ"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The opposite — the shift to lepromatous disease is a shift <b>away</b> from Th1 towards Th2.","B":"Correct — <b>tuberculoid leprosy is Th1-dominant</b> (strong cell-mediated immunity, IFN-γ, TNF, granulomas, few bacilli, localised disease). <b>Lepromatous leprosy is Th2-dominant</b> (IL-4, IL-10, high antibody, weak cell-mediated immunity, abundant bacilli, disseminated disease). Losing Th1 means <b>less IFN-γ and less TNF</b> — hence poor macrophage activation and uncontrolled bacillary growth.","C":"Leukocyte adhesion defects are a separate genetic disorder (LAD), unrelated to leprosy type.","D":"Mast cell degranulation is a type I hypersensitivity phenomenon and irrelevant here.","E":"IFN-γ falls, not rises, in lepromatous disease."},"also":"BM30 Q6, BM29 Q18","img":"slides/L16_7.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q26","t":"Investigating recurrent bacterial infection in a toddler","s":"A 2-year-old girl has recurrent ear infections, pneumonia and sinusitis, with multiple bacterial infections since infancy. Which investigation should be ordered?","lec":"L16","sl":54,"c":"<b>Investigation by suspected arm</b> — memorise as four rows. <b>B cell</b>: immunoglobulin levels, <b>CD19/CD20</b> count, specific antibody titres post-vaccine, isohaemagglutinins. <b>T cell</b>: CBC with <b>absolute lymphocyte count</b>, CD3/CD4/CD8 flow cytometry, <b>DTH skin test</b>, lymphocyte proliferation assay, chest X-ray for thymic shadow. <b>Phagocyte</b>: CBC/neutrophil count, <b>NBT or DHR</b>, adhesion molecule (CD18) expression. <b>Complement</b>: <b>CH50</b>, C3, C4, AH50.","o":{"A":"Nitroblue tetrazolium dye test","B":"Complete blood count","C":"Allergen-specific IgE testing","D":"Skin patch test","E":"Immunoglobulin levels and B-cell count"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"NBT tests the <b>neutrophil respiratory burst</b> — appropriate for suspected CGD, which presents with <b>abscesses and granulomas</b>, not recurrent sinopulmonary infection.","B":"A CBC is a reasonable first screen but will not identify the defect; it does not measure immunoglobulins.","C":"Specific IgE investigates allergy, which does not cause recurrent bacterial infection.","D":"Patch testing investigates type IV contact allergy.","E":"Correct — <b>recurrent sinopulmonary bacterial infection</b> (otitis media, sinusitis, pneumonia) beginning after 6–9 months of age is the classic <b>antibody (B-cell) deficiency</b> pattern. The right tests are <b>quantitative immunoglobulins (IgG, IgA, IgM, IgE)</b> plus <b>B-cell enumeration (CD19/CD20)</b>, and ideally specific antibody responses to vaccines."},"also":"BM29 Q45, BM28 Q59","img":"slides/L16_54.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q27","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q27","t":"DiGeorge syndrome","s":"A 2-year-old girl has recurrent <b>viral and fungal</b> infections, <b>congenital heart defects</b> and characteristic facies with a small jaw and low-set ears. What is deficient?","lec":"L16","sl":33,"c":"<b>DiGeorge = CATCH-22</b>: <b>C</b>ardiac anomalies (tetralogy of Fallot, interrupted aortic arch, truncus arteriosus), <b>A</b>bnormal facies (micrognathia, low-set ears, hypertelorism), <b>T</b>hymic hypoplasia → T-cell deficiency, <b>C</b>left palate, <b>H</b>ypocalcaemia/hypoparathyroidism, deletion on chromosome <b>22</b>q11.2. Treatment is symptomatic (calcium + vitamin D, cardiac surgery), with thymic transplantation in complete DiGeorge; <b>avoid live vaccines</b> and use irradiated blood products.","o":{"A":"B-cell immunity","B":"T-cell immunity","C":"Innate phagocytic immunity","D":"Complement system","E":"NK cell function"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Immunoglobulin levels may be secondarily reduced from lack of T-cell help, but the primary lesion is not B-cell.","B":"Correct — recurrent <b>viral and fungal</b> infection + <b>congenital heart disease</b> + <b>dysmorphic facies (micrognathia, low-set ears)</b> is <b>DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion)</b>: failure of the <b>3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches</b> → <b>thymic hypoplasia/aplasia</b> → <b>T-cell deficiency</b>. Add hypoparathyroidism with <b>hypocalcaemic tetany</b> to complete the picture.","C":"Phagocyte defects give abscesses with catalase-positive bacteria.","D":"Complement deficiency gives <i>Neisseria</i> or pyogenic infection, with no dysmorphism.","E":"Isolated NK deficiency is rare and would not produce cardiac and facial anomalies."},"also":"BM28 Q57, BM28 Q58, BM28 Q60","img":"slides/L16_33.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q28","t":"Chronic granulomatous disease","s":"A 3-month-old boy has recurrent infections and an <b>abnormal DHR test</b>. What is the primary defect?","lec":"L16","sl":45,"c":"<b>CGD</b>: defective <b>NADPH oxidase</b> → no superoxide → cannot kill <b>catalase-positive</b> organisms (<i>S. aureus</i>, <i>Serratia</i>, <i>Burkholderia</i>, <i>Nocardia</i>, <i>Aspergillus</i>). Presents with <b>recurrent abscesses</b> (skin, liver, perianal), lymphadenitis and <b>granuloma</b> formation. Tests: <b>NBT</b> (yellow dye → deep purple formazan when the burst works; stays yellow in CGD) and the more sensitive <b>DHR flow cytometry</b>. Treatment: prophylactic <b>co-trimoxazole + itraconazole</b>, IFN-γ, HSCT.","o":{"A":"T cell activation","B":"Susceptibility to viruses","C":"Recruitment of neutrophils","D":"Ability to kill pathogens via the respiratory burst","E":"Susceptibility to intracellular pathogens such as mycobacteria"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The DHR assay tests neutrophils, not T cells.","B":"Viral susceptibility indicates a T-cell or interferon-pathway defect.","C":"A failure of neutrophil <b>recruitment</b> is <b>leukocyte adhesion deficiency</b> — the clue there is delayed umbilical cord separation, omphalitis and a <b>high</b> neutrophil count with no pus.","D":"Correct — the <b>dihydrorhodamine (DHR) flow-cytometric test</b> measures the <b>neutrophil respiratory (oxidative) burst</b>. An abnormal DHR means defective <b>NADPH oxidase</b> → <b>chronic granulomatous disease</b>: neutrophils phagocytose normally but cannot generate superoxide to kill ingested organisms.","E":"Mycobacterial susceptibility points to the <b>IL-12/IFN-γ axis</b> (Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease), not CGD's oxidative burst."},"also":"BM30 Q27, BM29 Q44, AX 58 Q21, AX 59f Q26","img":"slides/L16_45.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q29","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q29","t":"Peanut allergy — type and test","s":"A patient is allergic to <b>peanuts</b>. (1) Which type of hypersensitivity is this? (2) Which method is used to test for it?","lec":"L15P1","sl":22,"c":"<b>Sensitisation ≠ allergy.</b> A positive SPT or specific IgE proves only that IgE exists; the diagnosis of allergy needs that plus a matching clinical history. Also know the <b>anaphylactic vs anaphylactoid</b> distinction: anaphylactic = <b>IgE-dependent</b>, requires prior sensitisation; anaphylactoid = direct mast-cell degranulation, <b>no prior exposure needed</b> (radiocontrast, opioids, vancomycin) — clinically identical, treated identically with adrenaline.","ans":"<b>(1) Type of hypersensitivity — Type I (immediate, IgE-mediated).</b><br><ul><li>First exposure <b>sensitises</b>: peanut allergen is taken up by dendritic cells, drives a <b>Th2</b> response (IL-4, IL-13) and B-cell <b>class switching to IgE</b>. That IgE binds <b>FcεRI</b> on mast cells and basophils.</li><li>On <b>re-exposure</b> the allergen <b>cross-links surface IgE</b>, triggering degranulation within <b>minutes</b>.</li><li>Mediators: <b>preformed</b> — histamine, tryptase, heparin; <b>newly synthesised</b> — leukotrienes (LTC₄/D₄/E₄), prostaglandin D₂, PAF; then <b>cytokines</b> (IL-4, IL-5, TNF) that drive the <b>late-phase</b> reaction at 6–12 h with eosinophils.</li><li>Clinically: urticaria, angioedema, vomiting, wheeze, and in the worst case <b>anaphylaxis</b> — peanut is one of the commonest causes of fatal food anaphylaxis.</li></ul><b>(2) Investigations — any of these three, in order of usual practice:</b><ul><li><b>Skin prick test (SPT)</b> — allergen pricked into the skin, read at <b>15–20 minutes</b>; positive = <b>wheal ≥3 mm larger than the negative control</b>, with surrounding flare. Antihistamines must be stopped beforehand.</li><li><b>Allergen-specific serum IgE</b> (ImmunoCAP/immunoblot) — quantifies peanut-specific IgE; useful when the skin cannot be tested (extensive eczema, dermographism, cannot stop antihistamines) or when anaphylaxis makes skin testing risky. Reported as <b>class 0–6</b>; ≥ class 3 is a significant positive.</li><li><b>Oral food challenge</b> — the gold standard for confirming clinical allergy, but performed only under close supervision with resuscitation available.</li></ul><b>Management:</b> strict avoidance, written action plan, and an <b>adrenaline auto-injector</b> — adrenaline is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis, not antihistamine.<br><br><b>Do not confuse with:</b> the <b>patch test</b>, which is read at 48–72 h and tests <b>type IV</b> contact hypersensitivity (nickel, poison ivy) — a distractor placed in this paper's Q31.","also":"BM32 Q23","img":"slides/L15P1_22.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q30","t":"Earring dermatitis — mechanism","s":"A patient complains of <b>redness of the ear every time she wears earrings</b>. What causes this?","lec":"L15P1","sl":37,"c":"<b>Type IV hypersensitivity has two mechanisms</b>, both antibody-independent: <b>(I) cytokine-mediated inflammation</b> — CD4⁺ T cells (Th1/Th17) release IFN-γ/IL-17, activating macrophages: contact dermatitis, tuberculin reaction, TB granuloma, chronic rejection; <b>(II) T cell-mediated cytotoxicity</b> — CD8⁺ CTLs kill target cells directly: type 1 diabetes, some drug reactions. The uniting clue in a stem is the <b>delay of 24–72 hours</b>.","o":{"A":"T cell-mediated inflammation","B":"Complement-mediated immune response","C":"Immune complex response","D":"B cell-mediated response","E":"Antibody-mediated response"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — nickel in the earring acts as a <b>hapten</b>, binding skin proteins to become immunogenic. Langerhans cells present it, and sensitised <b>CD4⁺ T cells</b> release IFN-γ and recruit macrophages → <b>allergic contact dermatitis</b>, a <b>type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity</b> peaking at 48–72 hours.","B":"Complement is central to types II and III, not contact dermatitis.","C":"Immune complexes (type III) cause vasculitis, serum sickness and glomerulonephritis.","D":"B cells are not the effector in type IV.","E":"No antibody is required — this is the defining feature separating type IV from types I, II and III."},"also":"BM32 Q25, BM30 Q30, BM29 Q35, BM28 Q54","img":"slides/L15P1_37.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q31","t":"Earring dermatitis — investigation","s":"Following on from the previous item, which test should be used?","lec":"L15P1","sl":42,"c":"<b>Patch test vs prick test</b> is asked in essentially every cohort, usually as a two-part question with the mechanism. <b>Patch</b> = type IV, read at <b>48–72 h</b>, contact allergens. <b>Prick</b> = type I, read at <b>15–20 min</b>, inhalant/food allergens. If the stem says the rash appeared <b>a day or two after</b> contact with metal, jewellery, cosmetics or plants, the pair of answers is always <b>T-cell mediated + patch test</b>.","o":{"A":"Skin prick test","B":"Skin patch test","C":"Serum IgE","D":"DTH (tuberculin) test","E":"DHR test"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The prick test is read at 15–20 minutes and detects <b>IgE-mediated type I</b> allergy.","B":"Correct — the <b>patch test</b> applies suspected contact allergens (a standard series including <b>nickel sulfate</b>) under occlusive patches on the back, removed at 48 h and read at <b>48 and 72–96 hours</b>. It is the diagnostic test for allergic contact dermatitis (type IV).","C":"Serum IgE relates to type I atopy.","D":"The tuberculin/Mantoux test is also a type IV reaction, but it tests for mycobacterial sensitisation — not for a contact allergen.","E":"DHR assesses the neutrophil oxidative burst (CGD)."},"also":"BM32 Q26, BM30 Q31, BM29 Q36, BM28 Q55","img":"slides/L15P1_42.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q32","t":"SLE pathogenesis","s":"A 30-year-old patient has fatigue, weight loss, arthritis and a <b>malar rash</b>, with autoantibodies to self antigens. What is the mechanism?","lec":"L15P2","sl":21,"c":"<b>SLE:</b> young women (peak 15–45 y, F:M ≈ 9:1), multisystem. Key manifestations: <b>malar (butterfly) rash</b>, discoid lesions, photosensitivity, oral ulcers, alopecia, non-erosive <b>arthritis</b>, serositis, <b>lupus nephritis</b> (foamy urine, oedema, proteinuria), cytopenias, neuropsychiatric disease. Labs: <b>ANA</b> (sensitive screen, by <b>IFA</b>), <b>anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm</b> (specific), <b>low C3/C4</b> in active disease. ⚠ Nuance the AX bank flags: SLE is <b>type III</b>, but if the stem adds <b>autoimmune haemolytic anaemia</b> or thrombocytopenia, that component is <b>type II</b> — such a patient has both.","o":{"A":"Type III immune complex-mediated T cell activation","B":"Type III immune complex-mediated mast cell activation","C":"Type II cross-reactive antibody-mediated inflammation","D":"Type III immune complex-mediated vascular endothelial cell death","E":"Type I IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Immune complexes act through <b>complement and Fc receptors on neutrophils</b>, not by activating T cells.","B":"Mast cells are the type I effector; they are not the type III mechanism.","C":"Cross-reactive antibody against a cell-surface antigen is type II — the mechanism of acute rheumatic fever, not SLE's systemic picture.","D":"Correct — <b>SLE is the prototype type III</b> disease. Antibodies against nuclear antigens (<b>ANA, anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm</b>) form <b>circulating immune complexes</b> that deposit in vessel walls, glomeruli, skin and joints, activating <b>complement</b> and recruiting neutrophils → <b>vascular endothelial injury and cell death</b>, vasculitis and lupus nephritis.","E":"Type I is the allergy mechanism and has nothing to do with ANA-positive multisystem disease."},"also":"BM30 Q32, AX 59f 2fง, AX 57f Q50","img":"slides/L15P2_21.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q33","t":"Acute rheumatic fever — hypersensitivity type","s":"A 12-year-old boy has fever, breathlessness and tachycardia, with a history of <b>bacterial throat infection 2 weeks ago</b>. Which immune mechanism best describes this?","lec":"L15P2","sl":13,"c":"<b>Two post-streptococcal diseases — do not merge them.</b> <b>Acute rheumatic fever</b>: after <b>pharyngitis</b> only; <b>type II</b>, molecular mimicry against heart/joint/brain/skin; carditis, migratory polyarthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules; <b>Jones criteria</b> + <b>ASO titre</b>; long-term benzathine penicillin prophylaxis. <b>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</b>: after <b>pharyngitis or skin</b> infection; <b>type III</b>, immune complexes in glomeruli; haematuria, periorbital oedema, hypertension, <b>low C3</b>; usually self-limiting.","o":{"A":"Type II — antibody-enhanced receptor activity","B":"Type III — immune complex-suppressed receptor activity","C":"Type II — cross-reactive antibody-mediated tissue damage","D":"Type III — immune complex-mediated complement activation","E":"Type III — immune complex-mediated vascular endothelial cell death"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Antibody <b>enhancing</b> receptor activity is Graves' disease (stimulating anti-TSHR antibody), not carditis after pharyngitis.","B":"Antibody <b>blocking</b> a receptor is myasthenia gravis — and that is type II, not type III.","C":"Correct — <b>acute rheumatic fever</b>: 2–3 weeks after group A streptococcal <b>pharyngitis</b>, antibodies against streptococcal M protein <b>cross-react</b> with cardiac myosin and valve tissue (<b>molecular mimicry</b>) → <b>type II hypersensitivity</b> with antibody-mediated inflammation. Fever + breathlessness + tachycardia = <b>carditis</b>, a major Jones criterion.","D":"Immune complex + complement is <b>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</b> — the <i>other</i> post-strep sequela, which follows skin or throat infection by 1–3 weeks and presents with haematuria, oedema and hypertension.","E":"Also type III, and again the glomerulonephritis pattern, not carditis."},"also":"BM32 Q22-related, AX 59f Q2, AX 59f 2fง","img":"slides/L15P2_13.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q34","t":"Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia in SLE","s":"A patient with a history of <b>SLE</b> develops jaundice and <b>immune haemolytic anaemia</b>. Which hypersensitivity mechanism is responsible?","lec":"L15P2","sl":8,"c":"<b>SLE is a two-mechanism disease and the exam exploits it.</b> Most of SLE — nephritis, vasculitis, arthritis, serositis — is <b>type III</b> (immune complexes). But its <b>cytopenias</b> — autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, immune thrombocytopenia, leucopenia — are <b>type II</b> (antibody directly against the blood cell surface). Read which manifestation the stem describes. Diagnosis of AIHA: <b>direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test</b>, plus spherocytes and autoagglutination on the film.","o":{"A":"IgG-mediated complement activation and phagocytosis","B":"IgM-mediated complement activation only","C":"Antibody-mediated inflammation","D":"Immune complex-mediated inflammation and cell death","E":"Immune complex-mediated mast cell activation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia</b>, the type seen in SLE, is mediated by <b>IgG</b> autoantibodies coating red cells at 37 °C. The coated RBCs are <b>opsonised and phagocytosed by splenic macrophages</b> (extravascular haemolysis) with some complement contribution. This is <b>type II, mechanism I — antibody-mediated cell destruction</b>.","B":"IgM-driven complement lysis describes <b>cold agglutinin disease</b> and <b>acute ABO-incompatible transfusion reaction</b> — intravascular haemolysis, not the SLE pattern.","C":"'Antibody-mediated inflammation' is type II mechanism II (Goodpasture's, rheumatic fever) — inflammation at a deposition site, not red-cell clearance.","D":"Immune complex disease is type III — that is the mechanism of SLE's <b>nephritis</b>, but not of its haemolytic anaemia.","E":"Mast cells belong to type I."},"also":"BM29 Q34, AX 59f 2fง","img":"slides/L15P2_8.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q35","t":"Graft from a brother","s":"A man receives a kidney transplant from his <b>brother</b>. What type of graft is this?","lec":"L14","sl":7,"c":"⚠ <b>Read the relationship word</b>: this paper asks about a <b>brother</b> (→ allograft) while BM32 and BM30 ask about an <b>identical twin</b> (→ isograft). Same five options, different answer. Pre-transplant workup for an allograft: <b>ABO compatibility</b>, <b>HLA typing</b> (class I: B &gt; A &gt;&gt; C; class II: DR &gt; DQ &gt; DP for strength of rejection), and a <b>crossmatch</b> (lymphocyte microcytotoxicity or flow cytometry) to detect recipient pre-formed antibodies.","o":{"A":"Isograft","B":"Allograft","C":"Xenograft","D":"Autograft","E":"Twinograft"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"An isograft requires <b>genetic identity</b> — only an identical (monozygotic) twin qualifies. A brother shares on average half his genome, not all of it.","B":"Correct — a non-twin sibling is <b>genetically different but the same species</b>, so this is an <b>allograft</b>. It will be rejected without HLA matching and immunosuppression, even though a sibling has a 25% chance of being a 6/6 HLA match.","C":"Xenograft = across species.","D":"Autograft = from the patient to themselves.","E":"'Twinograft' does not exist."},"also":"BM32 Q31, BM30 Q35, BM28 Q40, AX 59f 6f","img":"slides/L14_7.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q36","t":"Hyperacute rejection after ABO mismatch","s":"A blood group <b>B</b> recipient mistakenly receives a kidney from a blood group <b>A</b> donor. The graft is rejected <b>within an hour</b> by thrombotic occlusion. Which mechanism is responsible?","lec":"L14","sl":28,"c":"<b>Why ABO matters so much in transplantation:</b> anti-A/anti-B antibodies are <b>naturally occurring IgM</b> present without prior sensitisation, and ABO antigens are expressed on <b>vascular endothelium</b>. That is why <b>ABO compatibility is checked before HLA</b> and why hyperacute rejection is now almost extinct — the crossmatch catches it. Other sources of recipient pre-formed antibody: <b>previous transfusion, pregnancy, previous transplant</b>.","o":{"A":"Acute rejection mediated by donor T and B cells","B":"Acute rejection mediated by recipient T and B cells","C":"Hyperacute rejection mediated by donor pre-formed antibodies","D":"Hyperacute rejection mediated by recipient pre-formed antibodies","E":"Graft-versus-host rejection mediated by donor pre-formed antibodies"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Donor lymphocytes attacking the host is GVHD, and acute rejection takes days to weeks — not one hour.","B":"Acute rejection is indeed recipient T- and B-cell mediated, but its time frame is <b>days to months</b>, not minutes to hours.","C":"The direction is wrong: it is the <b>recipient's</b> antibodies that attack the graft, not the donor's.","D":"Correct — a group <b>B</b> recipient has naturally occurring <b>anti-A IgM isohaemagglutinins</b> already in circulation. These <b>pre-formed recipient antibodies</b> bind ABO antigens on the donor graft endothelium the moment blood flows in → complement activation → neutrophil margination → <b>thrombotic occlusion within minutes to hours</b> = hyperacute rejection.","E":"GVHD is a haematopoietic-transplant phenomenon and is T-cell, not antibody, mediated."},"also":"BM32 Q32, BM30 Q37, BM28 Q42","img":"slides/L14_28.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q37","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q37","t":"Chronic allograft rejection","s":"A 50-year-old woman had a kidney transplant <b>10 months ago</b>. Everything was normal until she began losing graft function from a <b>vascular</b> process. This reduction in allograft function is most likely mediated by which immunological process?","lec":"L14","sl":31,"c":"<b>Chronic rejection is a DTH reaction in slow motion.</b> CD4⁺ T cells recognise alloantigen (mostly by the <b>indirect</b> pathway, recipient APCs presenting processed donor MHC), release IFN-γ and TNF, activate macrophages, and drive <b>graft arteriosclerosis</b>. It is the leading cause of late graft loss and responds poorly to current immunosuppression. Compare with the CD8⁺-dominated cytotoxicity of <b>acute cellular</b> rejection.","o":{"A":"Antigen–antibody complex deposition","B":"Complement activation","C":"Delayed-type hypersensitivity","D":"Graft-versus-host disease","E":"Pre-formed antibody"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Immune-complex deposition is the type III mechanism of SLE and serum sickness, not of graft vasculopathy.","B":"Complement activation is the hyperacute mechanism, occurring within minutes of implantation.","C":"Correct — <b>chronic rejection</b> is driven by <b>CD4⁺ T cells and their cytokines</b> — mechanistically a <b>delayed-type hypersensitivity (type IV)</b> reaction against graft alloantigens. Chronic IFN-γ and growth-factor release stimulates smooth-muscle proliferation → <b>concentric intimal hyperplasia</b>, luminal narrowing, ischaemia and fibrosis over months to years.","D":"GVHD occurs when the graft contains immunocompetent donor T cells — a kidney does not.","E":"Pre-formed antibody causes hyperacute rejection in the first hours."},"also":"BM32 Q33","img":"slides/L14_31.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q38","t":"GVHD effector cells","s":"A leukaemic patient develops <b>graft-versus-host disease</b> after haematopoietic stem cell transplantation. Which cells react with the alloantigens of the recipient?","lec":"L14","sl":37,"c":"State the direction out loud every time: <b>rejection = host attacks graft</b>; <b>GVHD = graft attacks host</b>. GVHD needs three conditions: (1) the graft contains <b>immunocompetent T cells</b>; (2) the host is <b>immunocompromised</b> and cannot destroy them; (3) the host expresses <b>alloantigens absent in the donor</b>. Hence it follows <b>HSC/bone marrow</b> transplant, non-irradiated blood products, and rarely intestinal/liver grafts — but not a kidney.","o":{"A":"Donor T cells against recipient antigens","B":"Recipient T cells against donor antigens","C":"Recipient antibody against recipient antigens","D":"Donor antibody against recipient antigens","E":"NK cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — GVHD is caused by <b>mature donor T lymphocytes</b> carried in the graft recognising <b>recipient (host) alloantigens</b> as foreign, and attacking host tissue — principally <b>skin, gut and liver</b>.","B":"Recipient T cells attacking the donor graft is <b>graft rejection</b> (host-versus-graft) — the opposite direction.","C":"Recipient antibody against recipient antigen would be autoimmunity, not GVHD.","D":"GVHD is a T-cell disease; donor antibody plays no primary role.","E":"NK cells can contribute to the graft-versus-leukaemia effect but are not the cells that define GVHD."},"also":"BM32 Q34, BM30 Q38, BM29 Q42","img":"slides/L14_37.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q39","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q39","t":"Live attenuated JE vaccine response","s":"What protective immune response follows <b>live attenuated Japanese encephalitis</b> vaccination?","lec":"L17","sl":19,"c":"<b>Live attenuated vaccines — advantages:</b> self-replicating (small dose, often single dose), <b>authentic antigen presentation</b> on both MHC I and II, <b>both cellular and humoral immunity</b>, <b>long-lasting</b> memory, can induce mucosal IgA if given orally/nasally. <b>Disadvantages:</b> risk of reversion to virulence (vaccine-derived polio), disseminated disease in the immunocompromised, contraindicated in <b>pregnancy</b>, requires cold chain.","o":{"A":"Stimulation of NK cells","B":"Stimulation of B cells","C":"Stimulation of T cells","D":"Stimulation of both B and T cells","E":"Stimulation of vascular endothelial cells"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"NK cells act in the innate response and generate no lasting protective memory.","B":"B cells alone would be the answer for a pure polysaccharide vaccine, which is T-independent.","C":"T-cell stimulation alone would leave no antibody, which is a major part of protection against a viraemic infection.","D":"Correct — a <b>live attenuated</b> vaccine replicates in the host and its antigens are presented on <b>both MHC class I</b> (endogenously synthesised → CD8⁺ CTL) <b>and MHC class II</b> (→ CD4⁺ helper T cells → B cell help). It therefore induces <b>both humoral and cell-mediated immunity</b> with long-lasting memory — the defining advantage of live vaccines.","E":"Endothelial cells are not the target of vaccination."},"also":"BM30 Q39-related","img":"slides/L17_19.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q40","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q40","t":"Vaccine contraindicated in pregnancy","s":"Which vaccine <b>cannot</b> be given to a pregnant woman?","lec":"L17","sl":48,"c":"<b>Pregnancy rule: no live vaccines.</b> Contraindicated: <b>MMR, varicella, OPV, BCG, yellow fever, live attenuated influenza</b>. Recommended or safe: <b>Tdap</b> (every pregnancy, 27–36 weeks), <b>inactivated influenza</b>, hepatitis A/B, rabies and tetanus post-exposure. The same logic answers the sibling question — 'which vaccine should an AIDS/SCID patient avoid?' — again the <b>live</b> one.","o":{"A":"Rabies","B":"Hepatitis A","C":"Hepatitis B","D":"Tetanus–diphtheria (Td)","E":"Measles, mumps, rubella (MMR)"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Rabies vaccine is <b>inactivated</b> and, given the fatality of rabies, is always administered when indicated — pregnancy is not a contraindication.","B":"Hepatitis A vaccine is <b>inactivated</b> and safe.","C":"Hepatitis B vaccine is a <b>recombinant protein subunit</b> — safe and in fact recommended in pregnancy for at-risk women.","D":"<b>Td/Tdap is a toxoid</b> and is actively <b>recommended</b> in pregnancy to protect the newborn against tetanus and pertussis through transplacental IgG.","E":"Correct — <b>MMR is live attenuated</b> and is <b>contraindicated in pregnancy</b> because of the theoretical risk of fetal infection (particularly congenital rubella syndrome). Women should avoid conception for 1 month after MMR."},"also":"BM30 Q40, BM29 Q51, BM32 Q41","img":"slides/L17_48.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q41","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q41","t":"Adjuvant for non-protein vaccines","s":"A <b>non-protein vaccine</b> stimulates immunity poorly. To achieve long-lasting immunity, what must be added to increase its immunogenicity?","lec":"L17","sl":28,"c":"⚠ <b>Carrier vs adjuvant</b> — both appear as options and the distinction is the point of the question. <b>Carrier protein</b>: covalently <b>conjugated</b> to a polysaccharide (Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal) to convert a <b>T-independent</b> antigen into a <b>T-dependent</b> one, giving class switching, memory, and efficacy in infants under 2. <b>Adjuvant</b>: mixed into the vaccine, not conjugated; <b>non-specifically amplifies</b> the response to whatever antigen is present. If the stem says 'polysaccharide' and offers both, prefer <b>carrier</b>; if it says 'increase immunogenicity' generally, choose <b>adjuvant</b>.","o":{"A":"Carrier protein","B":"Diluent","C":"Adjuvant","D":"Cytokine","E":"Chemokine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A <b>carrier protein</b> is a genuinely defensible answer for a <b>polysaccharide</b> vaccine specifically — conjugating the polysaccharide to a protein makes it T-dependent and generates memory. The BM32 key marked carrier. However, the stem asks generally about <b>increasing immunogenicity</b>, and the BM33 deck's term for that is <b>adjuvant</b>.","B":"A diluent merely reconstitutes the vaccine and adds no immunological effect.","C":"Correct per the BM33 deck — <b>adjuvants</b> (aluminium salts/alum, MF59 oil-in-water emulsion, TLR agonists such as AS04) are added to <b>inactivated, subunit and conjugate</b> vaccines to <b>enhance and modulate the immune response</b>: depot effect, better APC uptake, innate activation.","D":"Cytokines are used experimentally as molecular adjuvants but are not standard vaccine components.","E":"Chemokines recruit cells but are not used as vaccine additives."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM32 Q44, BM30 Q41","img":"slides/L17_28.webp"},{"id":"BM31|Q42","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q42","t":"Allergen immunotherapy outcome","s":"After successfully completing <b>allergen immunotherapy</b>, what would you most expect to find?","lec":"L15P1","sl":25,"c":"<b>Allergen-specific immunotherapy (desensitisation):</b> repeated administration of gradually increasing allergen doses (subcutaneous or sublingual) over <b>3–5 years</b>. Immunological signature of success: <b>↑ Treg, ↑ IL-10 and TGF-β, ↑ blocking IgG4, ↓ IL-4/IL-5, ↓ specific IgE (late), ↓ mast cell and eosinophil recruitment</b>. It is the only treatment that alters the natural history of allergic disease. Distinguish it from <b>omalizumab (anti-IgE)</b>, which mops up free IgE but does not induce tolerance.","o":{"A":"Increased IL-4","B":"Decreased IL-10","C":"Increased regulatory T cells","D":"Decreased serum IgG","E":"Increased serum IgE"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"IL-4 is the Th2/IgE-driving cytokine; successful therapy shifts <b>away</b> from Th2, so IL-4 falls.","B":"IL-10 <b>rises</b> — it is one of the key tolerogenic mediators induced by immunotherapy.","C":"Correct — allergen-specific immunotherapy works by inducing <b>allergen-specific regulatory T cells</b>, which secrete <b>IL-10 and TGF-β</b>. This drives an <b>immune deviation from Th2 to Th1/Treg</b> and a class switch from IgE to <b>blocking IgG4</b> antibodies.","D":"Serum IgG (specifically <b>IgG4 'blocking antibody'</b>) <b>increases</b> — it intercepts allergen before it can cross-link mast-cell IgE.","E":"Specific IgE transiently rises then falls with prolonged treatment; a sustained rise is not the marker of success."},"also":"BM30 Q42","img":"slides/L15P1_25.webp"},{"id":"BM31|W9","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"W9","t":"Allergy immunoblot report — identify the cause","s":"You are given an allergy report (specific IgE immunoblot). <b>From the result, what is the cause of this patient's allergy, and why?</b>","lec":"L15P1","sl":24,"c":"<b>The grading scale (classes 0–6)</b> — 0 negative · 1 borderline · 2 low positive · <b>3 positive/clinically significant</b> · 4–6 strongly positive. The <b>indicator must be ≥ class 3</b> before you read anything else. This item, in some variant, appears in the written section of BM29, BM30, BM31 and BM32 — it is the single most repeated written question in the Sum II bank, and it is entirely mechanical marks.","ans":"<b>Answer using the standard three-step reading.</b><br><br><b>Step 1 — validate the strip using the indicator.</b> Check the <b>standard indicator band</b> first. It must be <b>class 3 or above</b> for the run to be valid. In this paper's version the indicator was <b>positive (class 3+)</b>, so the result <b>can</b> be interpreted. (If the indicator were class 0–2, the correct answer would be that the test is <b>uninterpretable</b> and must be repeated — the examiners set exactly that trap in the paired question.)<br><br><b>Step 2 — identify every allergen band at class 3 or higher.</b> In the BM31 report the positive band was <b>rabbit epithelium at class 3</b>. In the closely related BM29/BM30 versions the positives were <b>mixed grass class 3, <i>Dermatophagoides pteronyssinus</i> (house dust mite) class 4 and <i>D. farinae</i> class 5</b>.<br><br><b>Step 3 — state the conclusion and the reason.</b><br>'The patient has <b>allergen-specific IgE at class 3 or above</b> against [the named allergen(s)]. Since the standard indicator is positive, the result is valid. A class ≥3 result indicates a <b>clinically significant level of specific IgE</b>, so this patient's allergic symptoms are caused by sensitisation to [that allergen] — a <b>type I, IgE-mediated hypersensitivity</b>. On re-exposure the allergen cross-links IgE bound to FcεRI on mast cells, triggering degranulation with histamine and leukotriene release.'<br><br><b>Always add the management line for the marks:</b> avoid the identified allergen, treat symptomatically (antihistamine, intranasal or inhaled corticosteroid), and consider <b>allergen-specific immunotherapy</b> for persistent disease.","f":["image"],"also":"BM32 Q60, BM30 written Q9, BM29 SG Q12","img":"slides/L15P1_24.webp"},{"id":"BM31|W10","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"W10","t":"Allergy immunoblot report — read the result","s":"You are given a second allergy report. <b>Read and interpret the result.</b>","lec":"L15P1","sl":24,"c":"The examiners deliberately pair a <b>valid</b> report with an <b>invalid</b> one to see whether students check the control. The BM29 key states it explicitly: <i>'อาจารย์อาจจะหลอกให้ indicator เป็นผล negative มา (class ต่ำกว่า 3) กรณีนี้จะอ่านผลทดสอบไม่ได้'</i> — if the indicator is below class 3, you cannot read the test. The same control-first logic applies to the <b>immunochromatography</b> written items: no control line = <b>invalid</b>, never 'negative'.","ans":"<b>This is the paired 'trap' version of the previous question — read the indicator before anything else.</b><br><br><b>If the indicator is class 0–2 (negative):</b><br>'The <b>standard indicator band is negative (below class 3)</b>, which means the assay has <b>not developed correctly</b> — the test kit may be faulty, expired or improperly run. Therefore <b>the result cannot be read or interpreted</b>, regardless of what the patient bands appear to show. The test must be <b>repeated</b> with a valid kit before any conclusion about the patient's allergies is drawn.'<br><br><b>If the indicator is class 3 or above (valid):</b><br>'The indicator is positive, so the strip is valid. The patient has specific IgE at <b>class ___ against ___</b>. Classes ≥3 are clinically significant, indicating <b>type I IgE-mediated sensitisation</b> to those allergens; the patient should avoid them.'<br><br><b>Model closing sentence either way:</b> 'Specific IgE demonstrates <b>sensitisation</b>; a diagnosis of clinical allergy requires that these results <b>correlate with the patient's exposure history and symptoms</b>.'","f":["image"],"also":"BM32 Q60, BM30 written Q10, BM29 SG Q12","img":"slides/L15P1_24.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q6","t":"Lepromatous leprosy","s":"A 30-year-old with <b>tuberculoid leprosy</b> progresses to <b>lepromatous leprosy</b>. What would be found in this patient?","lec":"L16","sl":7,"c":"<b>Tuberculoid = Th1</b> (IFN-γ, TNF, granuloma, few bacilli, localised, lepromin-positive). <b>Lepromatous = Th2</b> (IL-4/IL-10, high useless antibody, foamy macrophages full of bacilli, disseminated, lepromin-negative). Same organism, opposite outcome, determined by T-helper polarisation.","o":{"A":"Increased Th1","B":"Decreased TNF production","C":"Decreased leukocyte adhesion","D":"Decreased mast cell degranulation","E":"Increased granuloma formation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Wrong direction — progression to lepromatous disease means Th1 immunity has <b>failed</b>, replaced by a Th2 response.","B":"Correct — the Th1 → Th2 shift means less <b>IFN-γ and TNF</b>, so macrophages are not activated, bacilli multiply freely and the disease disseminates.","C":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency is a distinct inherited disorder (CD18/integrin defect), not part of the leprosy spectrum.","D":"Mast cell degranulation is a type I hypersensitivity process, irrelevant to leprosy.","E":"Granulomas are the hallmark of the <b>tuberculoid</b> (Th1) pole; they are poorly formed or absent in lepromatous disease."},"also":"BM31 Q25, BM29 Q18","img":"slides/L16_7.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q7","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q7","t":"Inflammation phase of tissue repair","s":"Which finding indicates the <b>inflammation phase</b> of tissue repair?","lec":"L10","sl":70,"c":"Phase signatures: haemostasis → platelet/fibrin · <b>inflammation → neutrophil then macrophage</b> · proliferation → angiogenesis, granulation tissue, fibroplasia, epithelialisation · maturation → MMPs, collagen III→I remodelling.","o":{"A":"Epithelialisation","B":"Neovascularisation","C":"Collagen deposition","D":"Macrophage infiltration","E":"Endothelial cell activation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Re-epithelialisation is a <b>proliferative</b>-phase event.","B":"Angiogenesis/neovascularisation is proliferative phase.","C":"Collagen deposition spans proliferation into maturation.","D":"Correct — <b>macrophage infiltration</b> marks the inflammatory phase: neutrophils first (~24 h), then macrophages (2–3 days) which clear debris and then switch to the reparative M2 phenotype.","E":"Endothelial activation is part of the immediate vascular response; the cellular hallmark the deck names is macrophage infiltration."},"also":"BM31 Q8","img":"slides/L10_70.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q8","t":"Angiogenesis factor","s":"Which factor is <b>most crucial for angiogenesis</b> in tissue repair?","lec":"L10","sl":67,"c":"One growth factor, one job: <b>VEGF → angiogenesis</b> · <b>PDGF → fibroblast/smooth-muscle recruitment</b> · <b>TGF-β → fibrosis and collagen</b> · <b>EGF/KGF → epithelialisation</b> · <b>FGF-2 → fibroblasts and supporting angiogenesis</b>. This exact set is recycled as options every cohort.","o":{"A":"EGF","B":"IGF","C":"PDGF","D":"VEGF","E":"TGF-β"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"EGF drives <b>re-epithelialisation</b> — keratinocyte proliferation and migration.","B":"IGF supports general cell growth and protein synthesis, not vessel formation.","C":"PDGF recruits <b>fibroblasts, smooth muscle and pericytes</b>; it matures new vessels but does not initiate sprouting.","D":"Correct — <b>VEGF</b> is the master angiogenic factor: it drives endothelial proliferation, migration and permeability, sprouting new capillaries from existing venules.","E":"TGF-β is the main <b>fibrogenic</b> cytokine — it drives fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition, i.e. scar formation."},"also":"BM32 Q8","img":"slides/L10_67.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q9","t":"Inflammatory signs and causes","s":"Which pairing of pathogenesis with cause is <b>correct</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":11,"c":"<b>Redness = vasodilation · Heat = vasodilation · Swelling = ↑permeability (exudate) · Pain = bradykinin + PGE₂ · Loss of function = the sum.</b>","o":{"A":"Redness — vasodilation","B":"Loss of function — increased vascular permeability","C":"Oedema — leukocyte transmigration","D":"Heat — leukocyte transmigration","E":"Pain — increased vascular permeability"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>redness (rubor) = vasodilation</b> with increased blood flow, driven by histamine, NO and prostaglandins.","B":"Loss of function follows from pain plus swelling combined.","C":"Oedema is caused by <b>increased vascular permeability</b>, not by transmigration of leukocytes.","D":"Heat, like redness, comes from <b>vasodilation</b> and increased blood flow.","E":"Pain is produced by <b>bradykinin and PGE₂</b> stimulating nerve endings."},"also":"BM31 Q9","img":"slides/L10_11.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q10","t":"Acute pneumonia histology","s":"A man has coughed for 2 days and has pneumonia. What pathology would a cross-section of lung tissue show?","lec":"L10","sl":10,"c":"<b>Neutrophil = acute.</b> Lymphocyte/plasma cell/macrophage = chronic. Epithelioid + giant cell = granulomatous. Duration in the stem tells you which to pick before you even read the options.","o":{"A":"Fibrosis","B":"Pulmonary haemorrhage","C":"Neutrophil infiltration","D":"Granulomatous inflammation","E":"Dense collagen deposition"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Fibrosis takes weeks to months of chronic injury.","B":"Haemorrhage is not the defining feature of acute bacterial pneumonia.","C":"Correct — 2 days of illness is <b>acute inflammation</b>: alveolar <b>neutrophil infiltration</b> with vascular congestion and exudate.","D":"Granulomas mean chronic infection such as TB or fungal disease.","E":"Collagen deposition is a repair/remodelling feature."},"also":"BM31 Q10, BM29 Q20","img":"slides/L10_10.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q11","t":"Acute appendicitis","s":"Several <b>neutrophils</b> and an <b>oedematous appendix</b> are found. What is the abnormality?","lec":"L10","sl":37,"c":"The single most repeated L10 concept in the bank: identify the infiltrating cell, then name the process.","o":{"A":"Scar formation","B":"No inflammation","C":"Acute inflammation","D":"Chronic inflammation","E":"Granulomatous inflammation"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Scar is fibroblasts and collagen, the end point of repair.","B":"Neutrophils plus oedema is inflammation by definition.","C":"Correct — <b>neutrophils + oedema = acute inflammation</b> (acute appendicitis).","D":"Chronic inflammation would show lymphocytes, plasma cells and fibrosis.","E":"Granulomatous inflammation requires epithelioid macrophages and multinucleated giant cells."},"also":"BM32 Q1, BM31 Q11, BM29 Q21","img":"slides/L10_37.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q12","t":"Cause of fever","s":"Following on from the previous item, which mediator causes the <b>fever</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":31,"c":"Fever chain: PAMP → macrophage <b>TNF/IL-1/IL-6</b> → hypothalamus → <b>COX-2 → PGE₂</b> → ↑set-point. NSAIDs are antipyretic because they block COX.","o":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Complement","C":"Leukotriene","D":"Lysosomal enzyme","E":"TNF"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Histamine → vasodilation, permeability, itch — not fever.","B":"Complement fragments are anaphylatoxins/opsonins/chemoattractants.","C":"Leukotrienes → bronchoconstriction, permeability, chemotaxis.","D":"Lysosomal enzymes → tissue destruction.","E":"Correct — <b>TNF, IL-1 and IL-6 are the endogenous pyrogens</b>; they induce hypothalamic <b>COX-2 → PGE₂</b>, raising the thermoregulatory set-point."},"also":"BM31 Q12, BM29 Q22, AX 57m Q39","img":"slides/L10_31.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q13","t":"Exudate","s":"A 50-year-old man with acute dermatitis and bacterial infection has a biopsy showing neutrophils, necrotic debris and tissue oedema. Which statement describes this <b>tissue oedema</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":12,"c":"<b>Exudate</b>: ↑permeability, protein-rich, SG &gt;1.020, cellular, cloudy (pus, empyema). <b>Transudate</b>: altered Starling forces, protein-poor, SG &lt;1.012, acellular, clear (heart failure, nephrotic syndrome).","o":{"A":"This fluid is called exudate","B":"This fluid is called transudate","C":"It is loss of intravascular fluid without plasma protein into the tissue","D":"It is caused by increased hydrostatic pressure and decreased colloid osmotic pressure","E":"It is caused by decreased hydrostatic pressure and increased colloid osmotic pressure"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — inflammation increases <b>vascular permeability</b>, so protein-rich, cell-containing fluid escapes: an <b>exudate</b>.","B":"A transudate is protein-poor and non-inflammatory.","C":"Protein-free fluid movement describes a transudate.","D":"That is the Starling-forces mechanism of a <b>transudate</b> (heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis).","E":"This combination would reduce, not increase, fluid escape."},"also":"BM31 Q13, BM29 Q23","img":"slides/L10_12.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q14","t":"Resident initiating cell","s":"Following on from the previous item, which resident cell initiates this inflammation?","lec":"L10","sl":8,"c":"Resident initiators = <b>macrophages, dendritic cells, mast cells</b>. The L10 deck's framing: <b>Recognition → Recruitment → Removal → Regulation → Repair</b>.","o":{"A":"Fibroblast","B":"Macrophage","C":"Muscle cell","D":"Epidermal cell","E":"Endothelial cell"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Fibroblasts act in the repair phase.","B":"Correct — the tissue-resident <b>macrophage</b> is the sentinel: TLR/PRR recognition of PAMPs and DAMPs → TNF, IL-1, IL-6, chemokines → the whole cascade.","C":"Muscle cells have no sentinel role.","D":"Keratinocytes contribute cytokines but are not the primary initiator.","E":"Endothelium <b>responds</b> (selectins, ICAM-1) rather than initiating."},"also":"BM31 Q14","img":"slides/L10_8.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q15","t":"Chronic cholecystitis","s":"A 50-year-old woman with 3 months of right upper quadrant pain has a gallbladder stone. Histology shows many lymphocytes and plasma cells with foci of fibrosis and reactive lymphoid follicles. Which process is occurring?","lec":"L10","sl":36,"c":"Chronic inflammation = prolonged, with <b>simultaneous</b> inflammation, tissue injury and repair; macrophages, lymphocytes and plasma cells, with angiogenesis and fibrosis. A <b>persistent irritant</b> — stone, splinter, suture — is a classic cause.","o":{"A":"Healing and repair","B":"Chronic inflammation","C":"Fibrinous inflammation","D":"Suppurative inflammation","E":"Granulomatous inflammation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Healing/repair shows granulation tissue and organised scar without an active lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate.","B":"Correct — <b>3 months + lymphocytes + plasma cells + fibrosis + lymphoid follicles = chronic inflammation</b> (chronic cholecystitis from a persistent stone).","C":"Fibrinous inflammation is an acute serosal pattern.","D":"Suppurative means pus and neutrophils — absent here.","E":"No epithelioid cells or giant cells are described."},"also":"BM31 Q15","img":"slides/L10_36.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q16","t":"Foreign body granuloma","s":"A 45-year-old man has a small mass on his foot from a retained <b>splinter</b>. There is no heat, redness or swelling. Which histological picture is correct?","lec":"L10","sl":42,"c":"<b>Foreign-body granuloma</b> (inert material, foreign-body giant cells, no caseation, no T-cell response required) vs <b>immune granuloma</b> (persistent microbe, Th1/IFN-γ, epithelioid + <b>Langhans</b> giant cells, TB caseates, sarcoid does not).","o":{"A":"Cluster of many neutrophils with necrotic and destructive tissue","B":"Many neutrophils and some lymphoid follicles","C":"Cluster of lymphocytes and plasma cells with foci of follicular necrosis","D":"Cluster of epithelioid cells and multinucleated giant cells with central necrosis","E":"Many foreign-body giant cells with some lymphocytes and plasma cells"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Neutrophils with destruction = active acute suppurative inflammation, excluded by the absence of heat, redness and swelling.","B":"Again, neutrophils indicate an acute process.","C":"A non-specific lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate misses the defining giant-cell reaction.","D":"Epithelioid cells and giant cells with <b>central caseous necrosis</b> is the <b>infectious (TB)</b> granuloma — a splinter causes no caseation.","E":"Correct — a retained splinter produces a <b>foreign-body granuloma</b> with <b>foreign-body-type giant cells</b> (randomly scattered nuclei) around the material, plus lymphocytes and plasma cells, and <b>no caseation</b>."},"also":"BM32 Q3, BM31 Q16","img":"slides/L10_42.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q17","t":"T-cell tolerance","s":"What is correct about T-cell tolerance?","lec":"L13A","sl":26,"c":"Central T tolerance = deletion (AIRE-driven, medulla) + nTreg diversion. Central B tolerance = deletion + <b>receptor editing</b>. Peripheral T tolerance = <b>anergy, Treg suppression, deletion (Fas–FasL), ignorance</b>.","o":{"A":"Peripheral tolerance mechanisms for T cells exist in the thymus","B":"AIRE is a transcription factor necessary for positive selection of T cells","C":"Some T cells may change their receptor during central tolerance","D":"Some self-activated CD4⁺ T cells may differentiate into regulatory T cells during central tolerance","E":"Education and elimination of T cells is similar to that of B cells"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Tolerance occurring in the thymus is <b>central</b> by definition; peripheral tolerance happens outside the generative organs.","B":"AIRE drives <b>negative</b> selection, not positive selection.","C":"<b>Receptor editing</b> is a B-cell mechanism in the bone marrow; T cells cannot edit their TCR.","D":"Correct — strongly self-reactive CD4⁺ thymocytes can be diverted into the <b>natural Treg (FOXP3⁺)</b> lineage instead of being deleted.","E":"They differ in both site and mechanism (thymus with Treg diversion vs bone marrow with receptor editing)."},"also":"BM31 Q17, BM29 Q30, BM28 Q10","img":"slides/L13A_26.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q18","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q18","t":"Treg function","s":"A patient has a defect in <b>CD4⁺ FOXP3⁺ IL-2R⁺</b> cells. What is the function of this cell?","lec":"L13A","sl":29,"c":"The Treg question appears in <b>every single cohort</b> in this bank, in MCQ or written form — BM32 Q18, BM31 Q18, BM30 Q18, BM28 Q9, AX 60 Q35. Learn the phenotype and the four mechanisms once and you have those marks permanently.","ans":"This phenotype — <b>CD4⁺, CD25(IL-2Rα)<sup>high</sup>, CD127<sup>low</sup>, FOXP3⁺</b> — identifies the <b>regulatory T cell (Treg)</b>. Its function is to <b>suppress immune responses and maintain peripheral self-tolerance</b>.<br><br><b>The four suppression mechanisms (name them all for full marks):</b><ul><li><b>Secretion of immunosuppressive cytokines — IL-10 and TGF-β</b> (the model answer the key expects: 'secrete IL-10').</li><li><b>CTLA-4</b> on the Treg surface binds and strips <b>B7 (CD80/CD86)</b> off antigen-presenting cells, denying effector T cells co-stimulation.</li><li><b>IL-2 consumption</b> — the high-affinity CD25 soaks up local IL-2, starving effector T cells of their growth factor (Tregs themselves depend on IL-2 for survival).</li><li><b>Direct cytolysis</b> of effector cells via granzyme/perforin.</li></ul><b>Origin:</b> <b>natural Tregs (nTreg)</b> arise in the thymus by self-antigen recognition during T-cell maturation; <b>induced Tregs (iTreg)</b> arise in the periphery, notably in the gut under the influence of dendritic cells and TGF-β.<br><br><b>Clinical consequence of losing them:</b> <b>FOXP3</b> mutation causes <b>IPEX</b> — Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked: severe early-onset autoimmune enteropathy, type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis and eczema. Reduced Treg number or function is also described in <b>rheumatoid arthritis, SLE and type 1 diabetes</b>.","also":"BM32 Q18, BM31 Q18, BM28 Q9, AX 60 Q35","img":"slides/L13A_29.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q19","t":"T-cell anergy","s":"What is the state of a T lymphocyte that is <b>unresponsive</b> because it engaged antigen <b>without CD28/CD80/CD86</b> signalling?","lec":"L13A","sl":17,"c":"<b>Three peripheral T-tolerance mechanisms, distinguished by fate:</b> <b>Anergy</b> = alive but unresponsive (signal 1 without signal 2); <b>Deletion</b> = apoptosis (repeated stimulation, Fas–FasL, BIM); <b>Suppression</b> = actively silenced by Tregs. Add <b>ignorance</b> at immunologically privileged sites (eye, brain, testis, uterus). The '<b>no CD28/B7</b>' phrasing in a stem always means <b>anergy</b>.","o":{"A":"Anergy","B":"Suppression","C":"Deletion","D":"Negative selection","E":"Bystander activation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>anergy</b> is functional unresponsiveness produced by <b>signal 1 (TCR–peptide-MHC) without signal 2 (CD28–B7 co-stimulation)</b>. The cell survives but cannot respond, even if it later meets the antigen properly presented. This is a core <b>peripheral</b> tolerance mechanism.","B":"<b>Suppression</b> is active inhibition by <b>Tregs</b> — an extrinsic mechanism, not an intrinsic state of the cell.","C":"<b>Deletion</b> is apoptotic death (Fas–FasL, BIM-mediated), not survival in an unresponsive state.","D":"<b>Negative selection</b> is deletion of self-reactive thymocytes in the thymus — a <b>central</b> mechanism, and it kills rather than silences.","E":"<b>Bystander activation</b> is the opposite: non-specific activation of autoreactive cells by nearby inflammation."},"also":"BM29 Q32, BM28 Q11","img":"slides/L13A_17.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q20","t":"Eliminating cancer cells","s":"Which agent can help <b>eliminate cancer cells</b>?","lec":"L18","sl":24,"c":"Sort every checkpoint option by whether it <b>adds</b> or <b>removes</b> a brake. <b>Removes the brake → activates T cells → cancer</b>: anti-PD-1, anti-PD-L1, anti-CTLA-4. <b>Adds a brake → suppresses T cells → transplant/autoimmunity</b>: CTLA-4-Ig, anti-CD28, anti-B7. The word 'anti-' in front of an <b>inhibitory</b> molecule means activation; in front of an <b>activating</b> molecule it means suppression.","o":{"A":"Anti-PD-1","B":"CTLA-4-Ig","C":"Anti-CD28","D":"Anti-FOXP3","E":"Anti-CD80/86"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>anti-PD-1</b> (nivolumab, pembrolizumab) blocks an inhibitory checkpoint, <b>releasing the brake</b> on tumour-infiltrating T cells so they can kill the tumour.","B":"<b>CTLA-4-Ig</b> (abatacept) <b>adds</b> a brake by blocking B7 — it suppresses T cells, which is what you want in transplantation, not cancer.","C":"Anti-CD28 blocks the <b>activating</b> co-stimulatory receptor, again suppressing T cells.","D":"FOXP3 is an intracellular transcription factor, not a druggable surface target.","E":"Blocking B7 (CD80/86) removes co-stimulation and suppresses the T-cell response."},"also":"BM31 Q19, BM32 Q53, BM28 Q12","img":"slides/L18_24.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q21","t":"Mechanism most related to autoimmunity","s":"Which is <b>most</b> related to autoimmunity?","lec":"L13B","sl":5,"c":"<b>How self-tolerance fails</b> — the L13B list: <b>defective tolerance/regulation</b> (defective negative selection, failed <b>AIRE</b>, deficient <b>Treg</b> function, defective <b>apoptosis</b>/Fas, failed inhibitory receptors <b>CTLA-4/PD-1</b>); <b>abnormal display of self antigens</b> (release from privileged sites, altered/citrullinated self proteins); <b>inflammation and innate activation</b> supplying co-stimulation. Then genetics (<b>HLA</b>, PTPN22, CTLA4) plus environment (<b>infection</b>, drugs, hormones).","o":{"A":"Expression of MHC class I","B":"Defective apoptosis of self-reactive lymphocytes","C":"Mutation in RAG1","D":"Increased IL-17 secretion by Th17 cells","E":"Increased complement C3 production"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"MHC class I expression is normal on all nucleated cells; it is <b>MHC/HLA allele associations</b> (e.g. HLA-B27, HLA-DR) that predispose to autoimmunity, not expression per se.","B":"Correct — <b>defective apoptosis</b> of self-reactive lymphocytes is a direct failure of tolerance. <b>FAS/FASL</b> mutations cause <b>ALPS</b> (autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome) with autoimmune cytopenias; failure of deletion at either central or peripheral level lets autoreactive clones survive.","C":"RAG1 mutation blocks V(D)J recombination → <b>SCID</b>, an immuno<b>deficiency</b>, not autoimmunity.","D":"Th17/IL-17 does contribute to autoimmune tissue damage, but the AX bank explicitly notes that <b>IL-17 loss-of-function is the option <i>least</i> associated with autoimmunity</b> — its main job is antimicrobial defence.","E":"Complement <b>deficiency</b> (C1q, C2, C4) predisposes to lupus by impairing clearance of apoptotic debris; increased C3 does not."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM28 Q14, AX 59f 2f","img":"slides/L13B_5.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q22","t":"Molecular mimicry","s":"Which mechanism involves a <b>foreign molecule resembling a self antigen</b>, leading to rheumatic fever?","lec":"L13B","sl":14,"c":"Infection → autoimmunity by three routes: <b>molecular mimicry</b> (ARF, rheumatic heart disease, Guillain–Barré after <i>Campylobacter</i>), <b>bystander activation</b>, and <b>epitope spreading</b>. ARF is the flagship mimicry example in this course and appears in BM31, BM30, BM32, BM28 and the AX bank.","o":{"A":"Receptor editing","B":"Central tolerance","C":"Epitope spreading","D":"Molecular mimicry","E":"Bystander activation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Receptor editing is a B-cell tolerance mechanism in the bone marrow.","B":"Central tolerance is the normal deletion process, not a disease mechanism.","C":"Epitope spreading broadens an <i>already established</i> autoimmune response to newly exposed self antigens.","D":"Correct — <b>molecular mimicry</b>: antibodies to <b>group A streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin and valve tissue</b>, producing acute rheumatic fever 2–3 weeks after pharyngitis.","E":"Bystander activation is non-specific activation of autoreactive cells by infection-driven inflammation."},"also":"BM31 Q21, AX 59f Q2","img":"slides/L13B_14.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q23","t":"BTK mutation immunoglobulin profile","s":"How does a <b>BTK mutation</b> affect B cells and immunoglobulins?","lec":"L16","sl":25,"c":"<b>Read immunoglobulin panels as a pattern, not as individual numbers.</b> <b>All Ig low + no CD19</b> → XLA (BTK). <b>IgM high, IgG/IgA/IgE low, B cells present</b> → hyper-IgM (CD40L). <b>IgA absent alone</b> → selective IgA deficiency. <b>IgG low ± IgA/IgM, B cells present, later onset</b> → CVID. <b>Everything low + T cells low</b> → SCID.","o":{"A":"B cells absent · IgM low · IgG low · IgA low","B":"B cells absent · IgM high · IgG normal · IgA normal","C":"B cells normal · IgM high · IgG low · IgA low","D":"B cells normal · IgM high · IgG normal · IgA normal","E":"B cells normal · IgM normal · IgG normal · IgA absent"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>BTK (Bruton tyrosine kinase)</b> mutation blocks the pre-B → immature B cell transition, so <b>mature B cells never develop</b>. With no B cells there are no plasma cells, and <b>every immunoglobulin class is low</b>: IgM, IgG, IgA and IgE. Clinically this is <b>X-linked agammaglobulinaemia</b>, with absent tonsils and lymph nodes.","B":"B cells absent with high IgM is internally contradictory — no B cells means no IgM either.","C":"B cells present with high IgM and low IgG/IgA is the <b>hyper-IgM syndrome</b> profile (CD40L defect), not BTK.","D":"Normal everything with high IgM does not match any classic defect pattern.","E":"Isolated absent IgA with everything else normal is <b>selective IgA deficiency</b>."},"also":"BM32 Q40, BM29 Q47, BM28 Q59","img":"slides/L16_25.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q24","t":"Neisseria infection and complement","s":"A man is infected with <b><i>Neisseria</i></b>. What should the CD/complement results show?","lec":"L16","sl":51,"c":"<b>Complement deficiencies map onto distinct clinical pictures:</b> <b>C1q/C2/C4 (early classical)</b> → <b>SLE-like immune-complex disease</b> (poor clearance of apoptotic debris) plus pyogenic infection; <b>C3</b> → severe recurrent <b>pyogenic</b> infection (opsonisation failure); <b>C5–C9 (MAC)</b> → recurrent <b><i>Neisseria</i></b> (meningococcus, gonococcus); <b>C1 inhibitor</b> → hereditary angioedema. Screening test = <b>CH50</b> (classical) and <b>AH50</b> (alternative).","o":{"A":"Low CD4 and CD3","B":"Low CD19 and CD20","C":"Low C3 and C4","D":"Low FOXP3","E":"Low CD34"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Low CD3/CD4 indicates a T-cell defect — the pattern for viral, fungal and <i>Pneumocystis</i> infection.","B":"Low CD19/CD20 indicates a B-cell defect — recurrent encapsulated bacterial sinopulmonary infection.","C":"Correct — <b>recurrent or disseminated <i>Neisseria</i> infection is the signature of complement deficiency</b>, classically the <b>terminal components C5–C9</b> that form the membrane attack complex needed to lyse <i>Neisseria</i>. Screening measures <b>C3, C4 and CH50</b>.","D":"Low FOXP3 → IPEX, an autoimmune phenotype.","E":"CD34 marks haematopoietic stem cells."},"also":"BM31 Q20","img":"slides/L16_51.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q25","t":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency","s":"A 4-week-old infant presents with fever, <b>omphalitis</b>, delayed wound healing and a <b>markedly increased white cell count</b>. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L16","sl":48,"c":"<b>The two phagocyte defects, side by side.</b> <b>LAD</b> — defect in <b>getting there</b> (CD18/integrin adhesion): delayed cord separation, omphalitis, <b>no pus</b>, <b>high</b> neutrophil count, severe gingivitis/periodontitis, poor wound healing. <b>CGD</b> — defect in <b>killing after arrival</b> (NADPH oxidase): abscesses, granulomas, catalase-positive organisms and <i>Aspergillus</i>, abnormal <b>NBT/DHR</b>.","o":{"A":"Complement C3 deficiency","B":"X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome","C":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency","D":"Chronic granulomatous disease","E":"Hypergammaglobulinaemia"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"C3 deficiency causes pyogenic infections but not delayed cord separation with leukocytosis and absent pus.","B":"Hyper-IgM presents at several months with sinopulmonary infection and <i>Pneumocystis</i>, not neonatal omphalitis.","C":"Correct — <b>leukocyte adhesion deficiency (LAD)</b>: defective <b>CD18/β2 integrins</b> means neutrophils cannot firmly adhere to endothelium or transmigrate. The classic triad is <b>delayed umbilical cord separation (&gt;30 days) with omphalitis</b>, <b>poor wound healing</b>, and <b>persistent marked leukocytosis (&gt;15,000)</b> — the neutrophils are trapped in the bloodstream, so there is <b>no pus</b> at infection sites.","D":"CGD also causes recurrent infection, but with <b>abscesses and granulomas</b> and an abnormal NBT/DHR; the cord separation and leukocytosis picture is LAD.","E":"Hypergammaglobulinaemia is not an immunodeficiency syndrome in this sense."},"img":"slides/L16_48.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q26","t":"SCID immunological profile","s":"What is the immunological profile of <b>severe combined immunodeficiency</b>?","lec":"L16","sl":38,"c":"SCID onset in the <b>first few months</b>: failure to thrive, persistent diarrhoea, pneumonia, <b>oral thrush</b>, absent thymic shadow, lymphopenia. Genotypes: <b>X-linked IL2RG (common γ chain)</b> most common, then <b>ADA</b>, <b>RAG1/2</b>, JAK3, IL7Rα. Treatment: <b>HSCT</b>, PEG-ADA enzyme replacement, gene therapy, IVIG. Absolute rules: <b>no live vaccines</b>, irradiated CMV-negative blood products only.","o":{"A":"Humoral normal · Cellular normal","B":"Humoral deficient · Cellular deficient","C":"Humoral normal · Cellular deficient","D":"Humoral deficient · Cellular normal","E":"None of the above"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Both arms are profoundly abnormal — that is the meaning of 'combined'.","B":"Correct — <b>SCID = absence of all adaptive immune function</b>. T lymphopenia is constant; B cells may be present in number but are <b>functionally useless</b> without T-cell help, so <b>both cellular and humoral immunity fail</b>.","C":"Normal humoral with deficient cellular immunity describes a pure T-cell defect such as <b>DiGeorge</b>.","D":"Deficient humoral with normal cellular immunity describes a pure B-cell defect such as <b>XLA</b>.","E":"Option B is correct, so this is wrong."},"also":"BM31 Q24","img":"slides/L16_38.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q27","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q27","t":"Chronic granulomatous disease","s":"A 3-month-old boy has recurrent infection and an abnormal <b>DHR test</b>. What is the primary defect?","lec":"L16","sl":45,"c":"CGD: catalase-positive organisms (<i>S. aureus</i>, <i>Serratia</i>, <i>Burkholderia</i>, <i>Nocardia</i>, <i>Aspergillus</i>), recurrent abscesses and granulomas. <b>NBT</b> = yellow dye turns purple formazan when the burst is intact (stays yellow in CGD); <b>DHR flow cytometry</b> is more sensitive and is now preferred.","o":{"A":"T cell activation","B":"Susceptibility to viruses","C":"Recruitment of neutrophils","D":"Ability to kill pathogens via the respiratory burst","E":"Susceptibility to intracellular pathogens such as mycobacteria"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The DHR assay measures neutrophil function, not T-cell activation.","B":"Viral susceptibility implies a T-cell or interferon defect.","C":"Impaired neutrophil <b>recruitment</b> is <b>LAD</b> — delayed cord separation and leukocytosis without pus.","D":"Correct — an abnormal <b>DHR</b> means a failed <b>respiratory (oxidative) burst</b> from defective <b>NADPH oxidase</b> = <b>chronic granulomatous disease</b>. Neutrophils ingest organisms normally but cannot kill them.","E":"Mycobacterial susceptibility points to the <b>IL-12/IFN-γ axis</b>."},"also":"BM31 Q28, BM29 Q44, AX 58 Q21","img":"slides/L16_45.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q28","t":"T-cell deficiency presentation","s":"Which finding indicates a <b>T-cell deficiency</b>?","lec":"L16","sl":53,"c":"<b>Clinical manifestation by arm</b> — the L16 summary table, and the fastest route to the answer in any immunodeficiency vignette. <b>B cell</b>: recurrent sinopulmonary bacterial infection, onset 7–9 months, absent tonsils. <b>T cell</b>: <b>fungal (thrush), viral, <i>Pneumocystis</i></b>, failure to thrive, onset in early infancy, absent thymic shadow. <b>Phagocyte</b>: <b>skin abscesses</b>, poor wound healing, delayed cord separation, granulomas. <b>Complement</b>: <b><i>Neisseria</i></b>, pyogenic infection, lupus-like immune-complex disease.","o":{"A":"Oral thrush with absence of the thymus","B":"Recurrent otitis media from age 8 months","C":"Delayed umbilical cord separation","D":"Recurrent <i>Neisseria</i> meningitis","E":"Isolated absence of serum IgA"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>persistent oral candidiasis (thrush)</b> plus an <b>absent thymic shadow</b> is the classic T-cell deficiency presentation (DiGeorge, SCID). Fungal and viral infections require intact cell-mediated immunity.","B":"Recurrent otitis media beginning at 7–9 months, as maternal IgG wanes, is the <b>B-cell/antibody</b> pattern.","C":"Delayed cord separation is <b>leukocyte adhesion deficiency</b> (phagocyte).","D":"Recurrent <i>Neisseria</i> infection points to <b>terminal complement (C5–C9)</b> deficiency.","E":"Isolated low IgA is <b>selective IgA deficiency</b>, a B-cell/antibody defect."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 Q27, BM28 Q57","img":"slides/L16_53.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q29","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q29","t":"Allergic rhinitis type","s":"A 10-year-old boy has nasal sneezing and watery eyes in the early morning and needs antihistamine. Which type of hypersensitivity is this?","lec":"L15P1","sl":4,"c":"<b>The strongest clue in a hypersensitivity stem is the time course, and the second strongest is the treatment.</b> Minutes + <b>responds to antihistamine</b> → type I. Hours with fever/arthralgia/vasculitis → type III. <b>48–72 hours</b> → type IV. Cytopenia or organ-specific antibody → type II. The <b>allergic (atopic) march</b> — atopic dermatitis → food allergy → allergic rhinitis → asthma — is worth knowing; the AX bank asks which condition is <b>not</b> part of it (answer: allergic conjunctivitis).","o":{"A":"Type I","B":"Type II","C":"Type III","D":"Type IV","E":"Type I and IV"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — allergic rhinitis is <b>type I, IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity</b>. Allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells → histamine → sneezing, rhinorrhoea, itch, congestion. The prompt response to <b>antihistamine</b> confirms histamine as the mediator.","B":"Type II targets cell-surface antigens (haemolytic anaemia, Goodpasture's, Graves').","C":"Type III is immune-complex disease (SLE, serum sickness, post-strep glomerulonephritis).","D":"Type IV is delayed, T-cell mediated, and would not respond to antihistamine.","E":"Asthma has an immediate and a late phase, but both are IgE/Th2-driven type I — type IV is not involved."},"also":"BM32 Q24, BM28 Q51","img":"slides/L15P1_4.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q30","t":"Earring dermatitis mechanism","s":"A patient has redness of the ear each time she wears earrings. What causes this?","lec":"L15P1","sl":37,"c":"Type IV has two mechanisms: <b>cytokine-mediated inflammation</b> (CD4⁺ Th1/Th17 → macrophage activation: contact dermatitis, tuberculin reaction, TB granuloma, chronic rejection) and <b>T cell-mediated cytotoxicity</b> (CD8⁺ CTL: type 1 diabetes, drug reactions). The delay of 24–72 h is the giveaway.","o":{"A":"T cell-mediated inflammation","B":"Complement-mediated immune response","C":"Immune complex response","D":"B cell-mediated response","E":"Antibody-mediated response"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — nickel acts as a <b>hapten</b>; Langerhans cells present it and sensitised <b>CD4⁺ T cells</b> drive cytokine-mediated inflammation at 48–72 hours = <b>allergic contact dermatitis, type IV</b>.","B":"Complement mediates types II and III.","C":"Immune complexes cause vasculitis, serum sickness and glomerulonephritis.","D":"B cells are not the type IV effector.","E":"No antibody is involved — the defining feature of type IV."},"also":"BM32 Q25, BM31 Q30, BM29 Q35, BM28 Q54","img":"slides/L15P1_37.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q31","t":"Earring dermatitis investigation","s":"Following on from the previous item, which test should be used?","lec":"L15P1","sl":42,"c":"<b>Patch (48–72 h, type IV) vs prick (15–20 min, type I)</b> — asked as a mechanism-plus-test pair in BM32, BM31, BM30, BM29 and BM28. Metal jewellery + delayed rash always yields <b>T cell-mediated + patch test</b>.","o":{"A":"Skin prick test","B":"Skin patch test","C":"Serum IgE","D":"DTH (tuberculin) test","E":"DHR test"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Prick testing detects <b>type I</b> IgE sensitivity, read at 15–20 minutes.","B":"Correct — the <b>patch test</b> is the diagnostic test for <b>type IV</b> allergic contact dermatitis: allergens applied under occlusion, read at <b>48 and 72 hours</b>.","C":"Serum IgE relates to type I atopy.","D":"Tuberculin testing is also type IV but assesses mycobacterial sensitisation, not contact allergens.","E":"DHR tests the neutrophil respiratory burst (CGD)."},"also":"BM32 Q26, BM31 Q31, BM29 Q36, BM28 Q55","img":"slides/L15P1_42.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q32","t":"SLE mechanism","s":"A 30-year-old woman presents with SLE and is <b>ANA positive</b>. Which immune mechanism is the pathogenesis?","lec":"L15P2","sl":21,"c":"SLE labs: <b>ANA</b> is the sensitive screen (performed by <b>indirect immunofluorescence, IFA</b>, on HEp-2 cells); <b>anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm</b> are specific; <b>C3 and C4 fall</b> in active disease because complexes consume complement. Remember the dual mechanism: nephritis/vasculitis = <b>type III</b>, but the <b>cytopenias are type II</b>.","o":{"A":"IgE-mediated eosinophil recruitment","B":"IgM-mediated mast cell degranulation","C":"Immune complex-mediated T cell activation","D":"Cross-reactive antibody-mediated neutrophil recruitment","E":"Immune complex-mediated vascular endothelial cell death"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"IgE and eosinophils belong to type I allergic disease.","B":"Mast cell degranulation is type I; IgM does not mediate it.","C":"Immune complexes act through <b>complement and Fc receptors on neutrophils</b>, not by activating T cells.","D":"Cross-reactive antibody against a cell surface is <b>type II</b> — the acute rheumatic fever mechanism.","E":"Correct — SLE is the archetypal <b>type III</b> disease: antinuclear antibodies form <b>circulating immune complexes</b> that deposit in vessel walls, glomeruli, skin and joints, fix <b>complement</b>, recruit neutrophils, and cause <b>vascular endothelial injury and cell death</b>."},"also":"BM31 Q32, AX 59f 2fง","img":"slides/L15P2_21.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q33","t":"Graves' disease mechanism","s":"A patient has <b>low TSH but high thyroid hormone</b>. What is the cause?","lec":"L15P2","sl":14,"c":"<b>Antibodies against receptors — the type II mechanism-III pair:</b> <b>Graves'</b> = <b>stimulating</b> antibody at the TSH receptor → hyperthyroidism (low TSH, high T3/T4, goitre, exophthalmos, heat intolerance, sweating). <b>Myasthenia gravis</b> = <b>blocking</b> antibody at the acetylcholine receptor → fatigable weakness, ptosis, diplopia. Neither destroys the cell — they hijack or silence its signalling.","o":{"A":"Antibody enhancing receptor activation","B":"Immune complex deposition","C":"Cross-reaction with immune complex","D":"Immune complex-mediated inflammation and cell death","E":"Immune complex-mediated mast cell activation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>Graves' disease</b>: a <b>stimulating IgG autoantibody (TSI)</b> binds the <b>TSH receptor</b> and mimics TSH, driving continuous thyroid hormone production. High T3/T4 then suppresses pituitary TSH by negative feedback — giving exactly this <b>low TSH, high TH</b> pattern. This is <b>type II hypersensitivity, mechanism III: antibody-mediated cellular dysfunction</b>.","B":"Immune complex deposition is type III (SLE, serum sickness).","C":"Cross-reacting antibody describes rheumatic fever, which damages tissue rather than stimulating a receptor.","D":"Immune complexes causing cell death is the SLE/type III mechanism.","E":"Mast cells are type I effectors."},"also":"BM31 Q22, BM28 Q50, AX 59f Q31","img":"slides/L15P2_14.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q34","t":"ABO-incompatible transfusion","s":"An <b>ABO-incompatible blood transfusion</b> causes which type of hypersensitivity?","lec":"L15P2","sl":10,"c":"<b>Two haemolysis patterns, two antibody classes.</b> <b>IgM → intravascular</b>: fixes complement to MAC, lyses cells in the vessel → haemoglobinaemia/haemoglobinuria; seen in <b>acute ABO-incompatible transfusion</b> and cold agglutinin disease. <b>IgG → extravascular</b>: opsonises cells for <b>splenic macrophage</b> phagocytosis → jaundice, splenomegaly, spherocytes; seen in <b>warm AIHA</b>, haemolytic disease of the newborn (Rh), and drug-induced haemolysis. Both are type II.","o":{"A":"Type II — IgE mast cell degranulation","B":"Type II — IgM-mediated complement activation","C":"Type II — IgG-mediated phagocytosis","D":"Type III — immune complex-mediated inflammation and cell death","E":"Type III — immune complex-mediated mast cell activation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Type II is never IgE-mediated — that pairing is internally contradictory.","B":"Correct — naturally occurring <b>anti-A/anti-B isohaemagglutinins are IgM</b>. IgM is a highly efficient complement activator, so mismatched red cells undergo <b>complement-mediated intravascular lysis</b>: fever, chills, hypotension, back pain, <b>haemoglobinuria</b> and possible renal failure and DIC. This is <b>acute haemolytic transfusion reaction</b>, type II mechanism I.","C":"IgG-mediated opsonisation and splenic phagocytosis describes <b>extravascular</b> haemolysis — the mechanism of <b>warm AIHA</b> and delayed transfusion reactions, not the acute ABO catastrophe.","D":"Immune complexes are type III, a different disease family.","E":"Mast cells are type I."},"also":"BM29 Q34","img":"slides/L15P2_10.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q35","t":"Identical twin graft","s":"A 50-year-old man has a kidney transplant from his <b>identical twin</b>. What type of transplantation is this?","lec":"L14","sl":7,"c":"<b>Auto</b> (self) · <b>Iso</b> (identical twin) · <b>Allo</b> (same species, different genes) · <b>Xeno</b> (different species). Watch the relationship word: <b>identical twin → iso</b>, <b>brother → allo</b>. Both versions appear in this bank.","o":{"A":"Isograft","B":"Xenograft","C":"Allograft","D":"Autograft","E":"Twinograft"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — genetically identical donor and recipient = <b>isograft (syngeneic)</b>. No rejection, no immunosuppression required.","B":"Xenograft crosses species.","C":"Allograft is same species, different genotype — the usual clinical transplant.","D":"Autograft is self to self.","E":"Not a real term — a planted distractor."},"also":"BM32 Q31, BM31 Q35, BM28 Q40","img":"slides/L14_7.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q36","t":"Acute rejection","s":"A 30-year-old woman rejects a kidney graft <b>6 weeks</b> after transplantation, with epithelial damage and inflammation. What is the mechanism?","lec":"L14","sl":29,"c":"<b>Timing decides the answer in every rejection question.</b> <b>Minutes–hours</b> → hyperacute (recipient pre-formed antibody, complement, thrombosis). <b>Days–months</b> → acute (recipient T cells and antibodies; treatable with immunosuppression). <b>Months–years</b> → chronic (CD4⁺ T cells + cytokines, concentric intimal hyperplasia, graft vasculopathy).","o":{"A":"Acute rejection mediated by donor T cells and antibodies","B":"Acute rejection mediated by recipient T cells and antibodies","C":"Chronic rejection mediated by recipient T cells and cytokines","D":"Hyperacute rejection mediated by donor pre-formed antibodies","E":"Hyperacute rejection mediated by recipient pre-formed antibodies"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Wrong direction — the <b>recipient</b> attacks the graft in rejection; donor cells attacking the host is GVHD.","B":"Correct — <b>6 weeks</b> post-transplant with parenchymal (epithelial) damage and inflammation is <b>acute rejection</b>, mediated by <b>recipient T cells</b> (CD8⁺ cytotoxicity and CD4⁺ cytokine-driven inflammation) <b>and antibodies</b> against graft alloantigens.","C":"Chronic rejection takes <b>months to years</b> and shows concentric intimal hyperplasia rather than acute epithelial injury.","D":"Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours, and is caused by <b>recipient</b>, not donor, antibody.","E":"Right mechanism, wrong timing — hyperacute rejection would have destroyed the graft on the operating table, not at 6 weeks."},"also":"BM32 Q32, BM31 Q36","img":"slides/L14_29.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q37","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q37","t":"Hyperacute rejection","s":"A patient with <b>AB</b> blood type receives a kidney from a <b>B</b> donor. <b>An hour later</b> the graft is rejected. What is the mechanism?","lec":"L14","sl":27,"c":"⚠ <b>A note on this specific stem.</b> Immunologically, an <b>AB</b> recipient is the <b>universal recipient</b> — they have <b>neither</b> anti-A nor anti-B, so a B kidney should <i>not</i> trigger ABO-mediated hyperacute rejection. The examiners almost certainly intended the reverse (a B recipient receiving an A/AB organ, as BM31 Q36 correctly words it). Answer the <b>mechanism being tested</b> — hyperacute rejection by recipient pre-formed antibody — but understand the ABO logic: recipients make antibodies against the antigens they <b>lack</b> (O has anti-A and anti-B; AB has neither).","o":{"A":"Hyperacute graft rejection by donor antibodies","B":"Hyperacute graft rejection by recipient pre-formed antibodies","C":"Acute graft rejection by donor antibodies","D":"Acute graft rejection by recipient pre-formed antibodies","E":"Chronic graft rejection by a cell-mediated mechanism"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The direction is wrong — it is the recipient's antibody attacking the graft.","B":"Correct by the mechanism the examiners are testing — rejection within <b>one hour</b> is <b>hyperacute rejection caused by recipient pre-formed antibodies</b> activating complement on graft endothelium → thrombotic occlusion.","C":"Acute rejection takes days to weeks and is T-cell driven.","D":"Pre-formed antibody causes hyperacute, not acute, rejection.","E":"Chronic rejection develops over months to years."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM32 Q32, BM31 Q36, BM28 Q42","img":"slides/L14_27.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q38","t":"GVHD effector cell","s":"A leukaemic patient develops <b>graft-versus-host disease</b> after haematopoietic stem cell transplantation. Which cells react with the recipient's alloantigens?","lec":"L14","sl":37,"c":"<b>GVHD = donor T cells vs host.</b> Requires an immunocompetent graft, an immunocompromised host, and host alloantigens the donor lacks — which is why even HLA-identical sibling transplants develop GVHD, driven by <b>minor histocompatibility antigens</b>. Acute (&lt;100 days): rash, diarrhoea, jaundice. Chronic: scleroderma-like fibrosis. Prophylaxis: <b>cyclosporine + methotrexate</b>, T-cell depletion.","o":{"A":"Donor mature B cells","B":"Donor mature T cells","C":"Donor mature NK cells","D":"Recipient mature B cells","E":"Recipient mature T cells"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"GVHD is T-cell mediated; donor B cells contribute to chronic GVHD but are not the initiating cells.","B":"Correct — <b>mature donor T lymphocytes</b> carried in the graft recognise <b>host alloantigens</b> as foreign and attack <b>skin, gut and liver</b>.","C":"Donor NK cells mediate the beneficial <b>graft-versus-leukaemia</b> effect rather than GVHD.","D":"Recipient cells attacking the graft would be rejection, not GVHD.","E":"Recipient T cells attacking donor cells is <b>host-versus-graft</b> — graft rejection."},"also":"BM32 Q34, BM31 Q38, BM29 Q42","img":"slides/L14_37.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q39","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q39","t":"Herd immunity","s":"In a village, 95% of children are vaccinated, yet even unvaccinated children do not get the disease. Why?","lec":"L17","sl":15,"c":"<b>Herd immunity</b> protects those who <b>cannot</b> be vaccinated: infants too young, the immunocompromised (SCID, chemotherapy, advanced HIV), pregnant women (for live vaccines), and those with genuine contraindications. The threshold rises with transmissibility — measles ~95%, polio ~80–85%. It is the standard justification for population vaccination programmes and appears as an NL-style example question in the BM33 L17 deck itself.","o":{"A":"Herd immunity","B":"Passive immunity from maternal antibody","C":"Natural active immunity from subclinical infection","D":"Cross-protection from an unrelated vaccine","E":"Innate resistance to the pathogen"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>herd (community) immunity</b>: when a high enough proportion of the population is immune, transmission chains break down and the pathogen cannot circulate, so <b>unvaccinated individuals are protected indirectly</b>. Measles requires roughly <b>93–95%</b> coverage because it is so transmissible.","B":"Maternal antibody protects only for the first ~6 months of life, not a 2-year-old or a whole cohort of children.","C":"Subclinical infection would require the pathogen still to be circulating — the opposite of the scenario described.","D":"Vaccines do not generally cross-protect against unrelated pathogens.","E":"Innate resistance would not correlate with the village's vaccination coverage."},"also":"BM29 Q50, BM28 Q68","img":"slides/L17_15.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q40","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q40","t":"Vaccine contraindicated in pregnancy","s":"Which vaccine is <b>not recommended</b> for administration to a pregnant woman?","lec":"L17","sl":48,"c":"<b>No live vaccines in pregnancy</b>: MMR, varicella, OPV, BCG, yellow fever, live attenuated influenza. Recommended: <b>Tdap</b> (27–36 weeks, every pregnancy), inactivated influenza. The same 'pick the live one' logic answers the SCID and AIDS versions of this question.","o":{"A":"Rabies vaccine","B":"Hepatitis A vaccine","C":"Hepatitis B vaccine","D":"Td vaccine","E":"MMR vaccine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Rabies vaccine is inactivated and given whenever indicated — rabies is uniformly fatal.","B":"Hepatitis A vaccine is inactivated and safe.","C":"Hepatitis B vaccine is a recombinant protein subunit and is recommended for at-risk pregnant women.","D":"Td/Tdap is a <b>toxoid</b> and is actively recommended in pregnancy to protect the newborn.","E":"Correct — <b>MMR is live attenuated</b> and is contraindicated in pregnancy (theoretical risk of congenital rubella syndrome). Avoid conception for 1 month afterwards."},"also":"BM32 Q41, BM31 Q40, BM29 Q51","img":"slides/L17_48.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q41","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q41","t":"Adjuvant","s":"A <b>non-protein-based vaccine</b> does not last long. What must be added to increase its immunogenicity?","lec":"L17","sl":28,"c":"<b>Adjuvant</b> = non-specifically amplifies the response to whatever antigen is present (alum's <b>depot effect</b> is the classic NL exam answer). <b>Carrier protein</b> = covalently conjugated to a polysaccharide, converting a T-independent antigen into a T-dependent one so infants under 2 can respond and memory forms (Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal conjugates).","o":{"A":"Diluent","B":"Carrier","C":"Cytokine","D":"Adjuvant","E":"Chemokine"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A diluent only reconstitutes the vaccine.","B":"A <b>carrier protein</b> is the correct answer specifically for a <b>polysaccharide</b> vaccine (conjugation converts it to a T-dependent antigen) — a defensible alternative, but the deck's general term for boosting immunogenicity is adjuvant.","C":"Cytokines are experimental molecular adjuvants, not standard components.","D":"Correct — <b>adjuvants</b> (alum, MF59, TLR agonists such as AS04) are added to inactivated, subunit and conjugate vaccines to <b>enhance and modulate the immune response</b> via a depot effect, better APC uptake and innate activation.","E":"Chemokines are not vaccine additives."},"also":"BM32 Q44, BM31 Q41","img":"slides/L17_28.webp"},{"id":"BM30|Q42","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q42","t":"Allergen immunotherapy","s":"After successfully completing <b>allergen immunotherapy</b>, what would you most expect to find?","lec":"L15P1","sl":25,"c":"Success signature of allergen-specific immunotherapy: <b>↑Treg, ↑IL-10, ↑TGF-β, ↑blocking IgG4, ↓IL-4/IL-5, ↓specific IgE (late), ↓mast cell and eosinophil recruitment</b>. It is the only allergy treatment that modifies the natural history of the disease.","o":{"A":"Increased IgE","B":"Decreased IgG","C":"Increased regulatory T cells","D":"Decreased IL-10","E":"Increased IL-4"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Specific IgE rises transiently but falls with prolonged treatment; a sustained rise is not a marker of success.","B":"<b>IgG4 blocking antibody rises</b> — that is part of the therapeutic mechanism.","C":"Correct — immunotherapy induces <b>allergen-specific regulatory T cells</b> secreting <b>IL-10 and TGF-β</b>, shifting the response from Th2 towards Th1/Treg tolerance.","D":"IL-10 <b>increases</b> — it is one of the key tolerogenic cytokines.","E":"IL-4 (the Th2/IgE-driving cytokine) <b>decreases</b>."},"also":"BM31 Q42","img":"slides/L15P1_25.webp"},{"id":"BM30|W9","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"W9","t":"Allergy panel with a class-3 positive","s":"You are given an allergy panel (cat hair at class 3, with the <b>standard indicator at class 5</b>). Interpret the result: what is the patient allergic to, and why?","lec":"L15P1","sl":24,"c":"<b>Always report the indicator first.</b> Indicator ≥ class 3 → valid, proceed. Indicator &lt; class 3 → the test is <b>uninterpretable</b>, repeat it — and say so instead of reading the bands. This is the deliberate trap in the paired question, and it is the difference between full and zero marks.","ans":"<b>Step 1 — validate.</b> The <b>standard indicator is class 5</b>, which is ≥ class 3, so the assay has developed correctly and the result is <b>valid and interpretable</b>.<br><br><b>Step 2 — read the patient bands.</b> The only positive band is <b>cat hair/epithelium at class 3</b>.<br><br><b>Step 3 — state the conclusion with the reason.</b><br>'This patient is allergic to <b>cat hair (cat epithelium)</b>. The reason is that the assay shows <b>allergen-specific IgE at class 3</b> against cat epithelium, and <b>class 3 and above is a clinically significant positive</b>. The indicator at class 5 confirms the test is valid. This represents <b>type I, IgE-mediated hypersensitivity</b>: on exposure, cat allergen cross-links IgE bound to FcεRI on mast cells, triggering degranulation with histamine and leukotriene release, producing the patient's symptoms.'<br><br><b>Step 4 — management line.</b> Avoid cat exposure; symptomatic treatment with antihistamine and intranasal corticosteroid; consider <b>allergen-specific immunotherapy</b> if symptoms persist despite avoidance.<br><br><b>Common allergens on a Thai inhalation panel</b>, so you can name them confidently: <b>house dust mite (<i>D. pteronyssinus</i>, <i>D. farinae</i>)</b>, cockroach, cat and dog epithelium, mixed grass and weed pollen, <i>Aspergillus</i> and other moulds, latex.","f":["image"],"also":"BM32 Q60, BM31 W9, BM29 SG Q12","img":"slides/L15P1_24.webp"},{"id":"BM30|W10","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"W10","t":"Allergy panel with a class-0 indicator","s":"You are given a second allergy panel where the <b>indicator is class 0</b>. Interpret the result.","lec":"L15P1","sl":24,"c":"The BM29 answer key states this explicitly: <i>'อาจารย์อาจจะหลอกให้ indicator เป็นผล negative มา (class ต่ำกว่า 3) กรณีนี้จะอ่านผลทดสอบไม่ได้นะ!'</i> — the examiner may deliberately supply a negative indicator, and in that case you cannot read the test. Expect one valid panel and one invalid panel side by side.","ans":"<b>Answer: the test cannot be interpreted.</b><br><br>'The <b>standard indicator band is class 0 (negative)</b>. The indicator must reach <b>class 3 or above</b> for the assay to be considered valid. Because it has not, the strip has <b>failed to develop correctly</b> — the kit may be expired, faulty, or improperly processed.<br><br>Therefore <b>no conclusion can be drawn about the patient's allergen-specific IgE</b>, regardless of what the individual allergen bands appear to show. Reporting any of those bands as positive or negative would be unsafe. The correct action is to <b>repeat the test</b> with a valid kit, and if necessary confirm with an alternative method such as a <b>skin prick test</b> or a quantitative specific-IgE assay (ImmunoCAP).'<br><br><b>The same control-first principle applies to every rapid test in this course:</b> on an <b>immunochromatographic strip</b>, if the <b>control line does not appear</b>, the result is <b>invalid</b> — never call it 'negative'. Report it as invalid and repeat.","f":["image"],"also":"BM32 Q60, BM31 W10, BM29 SG Q12","img":"slides/L15P1_24.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q10","t":"Tumour immune evasion","s":"How do <b>tumour cells escape</b> the immune system?","lec":"L12","sl":32,"c":"<b>Tumour immune evasion — the L12 three-part scheme.</b> <b>(1) Low immunogenicity</b>: loss/low levels of tumour antigen, <b>down-regulation of MHC class I</b>, loss of co-stimulatory molecules, decreased adhesion molecules (ICAM, VCAM). <b>(2) Tumour-induced immune suppression</b>: secretion of <b>TGF-β and IL-10</b>, engagement of <b>CTLA-4 and PD-1</b>, expression of <b>FasL</b> to kill infiltrating T cells. <b>(3) Host immune system subversion</b>: recruitment of <b>M2 macrophages</b>, Tregs and MDSCs into the tumour microenvironment.","o":{"A":"Tumour expresses Fas on T cells","B":"Tumour secretes anti-VEGF","C":"Tumour secretes TGF-β to impair T-cell activation","D":"Tumour binds CD28 on T cells to suppress them","E":"Tumour induces classical (M1) macrophages to suppress T cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The mechanism is the reverse: the <b>tumour expresses FasL</b>, which engages <b>Fas on the T cell</b> and drives the <i>T cell</i> into apoptosis — 'tumour counterattack'. As written, this option has the molecules the wrong way round.","B":"Tumours secrete <b>VEGF</b> to promote angiogenesis; <b>anti</b>-VEGF (bevacizumab) is a drug used <i>against</i> tumours.","C":"Correct — tumours secrete <b>immunosuppressive cytokines, principally TGF-β</b> (and IL-10), which inhibit T-cell activation and proliferation and promote Treg differentiation in the tumour microenvironment.","D":"Tumours engage <b>co-inhibitory</b> molecules (CTLA-4, PD-1) on T cells; they do not bind CD28, which is the activating receptor.","E":"It is <b>alternative (M2)</b> macrophages that promote tumour growth and suppress immunity. <b>Classical (M1)</b> macrophages are anti-tumour."},"also":"BM28 Q36, AX 59m Q1","img":"slides/L12_32.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q18","t":"Lepromatous leprosy","s":"A patient has <b>lepromatous leprosy</b>. Which occurs?","lec":"L16","sl":7,"c":"Leprosy is the model Th1/Th2 spectrum disease: <b>tuberculoid = Th1</b> (IFN-γ/TNF, granuloma, paucibacillary, lepromin-positive) vs <b>lepromatous = Th2</b> (IL-4/IL-10, abundant but ineffective antibody, multibacillary, lepromin-negative).","o":{"A":"Decreased TNF production","B":"Strongly activated Th1","C":"Decreased leukocyte adhesion","D":"Decreased mast cell degranulation","E":"Well-formed granulomas"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — lepromatous disease sits at the <b>Th2</b> pole. Loss of the Th1 response means <b>less IFN-γ and less TNF</b>, so macrophages are not activated and bacilli proliferate unchecked.","B":"Strong Th1 activation defines the <b>tuberculoid</b> pole, the opposite end of the spectrum.","C":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency is a separate inherited disorder.","D":"Mast cell degranulation is a type I hypersensitivity phenomenon.","E":"Granulomas are the tuberculoid feature; lepromatous lesions contain foamy macrophages stuffed with bacilli."},"also":"BM31 Q25, BM30 Q6","img":"slides/L16_7.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q20","t":"Vascular congestion and neutrophils","s":"A 28-year-old man has fever and productive cough for 2 days; chest X-ray suggests pneumonia. Lung biopsy shows <b>vascular congestion (dilation) with neutrophils in the alveoli</b>. Which mechanism is most associated with this?","lec":"L10","sl":15,"c":"<b>Leukocyte recruitment — four steps, in order:</b> <b>1. Margination and rolling</b> (selectins: E- and P-selectin on endothelium, L-selectin on leukocyte) → <b>2. Adhesion</b> (leukocyte <b>integrins</b> LFA-1/Mac-1 ↔ endothelial <b>ICAM-1/VCAM-1</b>; the step that fails in <b>LAD</b>) → <b>3. Transmigration/diapedesis</b> (PECAM-1/CD31) → <b>4. Chemotaxis</b> (C5a, LTB₄, IL-8/CXCL8, bacterial N-formyl peptides). TNF and IL-1 from resident macrophages are what switch the endothelium on.","o":{"A":"Angiogenesis","B":"Tissue remodelling","C":"Increased transcytosis","D":"Leukocyte transmigration","E":"Fibrosis with collagen deposition"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Angiogenesis belongs to the proliferative phase of repair, days to weeks later.","B":"Tissue remodelling (MMPs, collagen maturation) is the final repair phase.","C":"Transcytosis is a minor route of increased vascular permeability, not the mechanism producing neutrophils in the alveoli.","D":"Correct — the neutrophils in the alveolar space got there by <b>leukocyte transmigration (diapedesis)</b>: vasodilation and stasis promote <b>margination and rolling</b> (selectins), then firm <b>adhesion</b> (integrins–ICAM-1), then <b>transmigration</b> between endothelial cells (PECAM-1), then <b>chemotaxis</b> along a gradient (C5a, LTB₄, IL-8, bacterial peptides).","E":"Fibrosis is a chronic outcome, not a 2-day event."},"also":"BM31 Q10, BM30 Q10","img":"slides/L10_15.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q21","t":"Neutrophils in muscle after surgery","s":"A 12-year-old girl presents with acute abdominal pain and high fever after surgery. Examination indicates the presence of <b>neutrophils in muscle tissue</b>. What is this?","lec":"L10","sl":37,"c":"<b>Neutrophil = acute</b> — the single highest-yield reading rule in L10. It answers this item in BM29, BM30 (Q11) and BM31 (Q11), and the accompanying 'what causes the fever' item is always <b>TNF/IL-1 → PGE₂</b>.","o":{"A":"Acute inflammation","B":"Chronic inflammation","C":"Granulomatous inflammation","D":"Scar formation","E":"Resolution"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>neutrophil infiltration with acute pain and high fever</b> is <b>acute inflammation</b> (here a post-operative infective/inflammatory process).","B":"Chronic inflammation would show lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages with fibrosis over weeks.","C":"Granulomatous inflammation needs epithelioid macrophages and giant cells.","D":"Scar formation is the end point of repair, with fibroblasts and collagen.","E":"Resolution means restoration to normal, which is inconsistent with active neutrophilic infiltration and fever."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 Q11, BM30 Q11","img":"slides/L10_37.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q22","t":"Cause of fever","s":"Following on from the previous item, which mediator causes the <b>fever</b>?","lec":"L10","sl":31,"c":"The BM29 key cites its own L13 slide 50 for this; the current BM33 equivalent is the <b>TNF, IL-1 and IL-6</b> slide in L10. Systemic effects of these three: <b>fever, leukocytosis, acute-phase proteins (CRP), and at high concentration septic shock</b>.","o":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Complement","C":"Leukotriene","D":"Lysosomal enzyme","E":"TNF"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Histamine causes vasodilation, permeability and itch.","B":"Complement fragments are anaphylatoxins, opsonins and chemoattractants.","C":"Leukotrienes cause bronchoconstriction, permeability and chemotaxis.","D":"Lysosomal enzymes damage tissue on release.","E":"Correct — <b>TNF, IL-1 and IL-6</b> are the <b>endogenous pyrogens</b>, acting on the hypothalamus via <b>COX-2 → PGE₂</b> to raise the thermoregulatory set-point."},"also":"BM31 Q12, BM30 Q12, AX 57m Q39","img":"slides/L10_31.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q23","t":"Exudate in skin biopsy","s":"A skin biopsy shows <b>neutrophils and tissue oedema</b>. Which is correct?","lec":"L10","sl":12,"c":"<b>Exudate</b> = inflammation, ↑permeability, protein-rich, cellular, SG &gt;1.020. <b>Transudate</b> = Starling forces, protein-poor, acellular, SG &lt;1.012. If the histology mentions <b>cells in the fluid</b>, it is an exudate.","o":{"A":"The fluid escaped as an exudate","B":"The fluid escaped as a transudate","C":"It results from increased hydrostatic pressure alone","D":"It results from decreased plasma oncotic pressure alone","E":"It contains no plasma protein"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — as the BM29 key puts it, it is an <b>exudate</b> because both <b>neutrophils and fluid</b> are present in the tissue. Inflammation increases vascular permeability, letting protein and cells escape together.","B":"A transudate is protein-poor and acellular, produced by altered Starling forces rather than inflammation.","C":"Increased hydrostatic pressure produces a transudate (e.g. heart failure).","D":"Decreased oncotic pressure also produces a transudate (e.g. nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis).","E":"An exudate is by definition protein-rich."},"also":"BM31 Q13, BM30 Q13","img":"slides/L10_12.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q30","t":"T-cell tolerance","s":"What is correct about T-cell tolerance?","lec":"L13A","sl":14,"c":"⚠ <b>Note the difference between cohorts.</b> BM31/BM30 word option D as '…may differentiate into <b>regulatory</b> T cells', which makes <b>D</b> the answer. BM29 words it as '<b>follicular</b> T cells' (false) and swaps option C to say '<b>B</b> cells may change their receptor' (true, receptor editing) — so in BM29 the answer is <b>C</b>. Read the exact wording; both versions appear in this trainer.","o":{"A":"Peripheral tolerance mechanisms for T cells exist in the thymus","B":"AIRE is a transcription factor necessary for positive selection of T cells","C":"Some B cells may change their receptor during central tolerance","D":"Some self-activated CD4⁺ T cells may differentiate into follicular T cells during central tolerance","E":"Education and elimination of T cells is the same as for B cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The BM29 key notes this must be <b>central</b> tolerance for T cells — tolerance in the thymus is central by definition.","B":"The key states plainly that <b>AIRE relates to negative selection</b>, so this option is wrong.","C":"Correct in this version of the item — <b>B cells</b> (not T cells) can change their receptor during central tolerance by <b>receptor editing</b> in the bone marrow, re-arranging a new light chain to escape self-reactivity.","D":"Self-reactive CD4⁺ thymocytes are diverted into <b>regulatory</b> T cells, not <b>follicular</b> helper T cells.","E":"The key notes the two occur in <b>different organs</b> (thymus vs bone marrow) with different mechanisms."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM31 Q17, BM30 Q17","img":"slides/L13A_14.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q32","t":"T-cell anergy","s":"Which is the state of T lymphocyte <b>unresponsiveness</b> resulting from TCR engagement <b>without CD28/CD80/CD86</b> signalling?","lec":"L13A","sl":17,"c":"Peripheral T tolerance by fate: <b>anergy</b> (alive, unresponsive — no co-stimulation), <b>deletion</b> (apoptosis via Fas–FasL/BIM), <b>suppression</b> (Treg), <b>ignorance</b> (immunologically privileged sites). Any stem mentioning <b>absent CD28/B7</b> means anergy.","o":{"A":"Anergy","B":"Deletion","C":"Suppression","D":"Negative selection","E":"Bystander activation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>anergy</b>: signal 1 without signal 2 leaves the T cell alive but permanently unresponsive. A key <b>peripheral</b> tolerance mechanism.","B":"Deletion is apoptotic death, not survival in an unresponsive state.","C":"Suppression is extrinsic inhibition by Tregs.","D":"Negative selection is thymic deletion — a <b>central</b> mechanism.","E":"Bystander activation is the opposite process, waking autoreactive cells up."},"also":"BM30 Q19, BM28 Q11","img":"slides/L13A_17.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q34","t":"ABO-incompatible transfusion","s":"A 40-year-old man develops fever, chills, shortness of breath and low blood pressure after transfusion of an <b>incompatible ABO blood group</b>. Laboratory testing shows <b>haemoglobinuria</b>. Which hypersensitivity type is this?","lec":"L15P2","sl":10,"c":"<b>Type II is antibody against a cell-surface or matrix antigen</b>, with three mechanisms: <b>(I) cell destruction</b> — opsonisation/phagocytosis or complement lysis (AIHA, <b>haemolytic transfusion reaction</b>, haemolytic disease of the newborn, ITP); <b>(II) inflammation</b> at the deposition site (Goodpasture's, rheumatic fever); <b>(III) cellular dysfunction</b> without destruction (Graves', myasthenia gravis).","o":{"A":"Type I","B":"Type II","C":"Type II and III","D":"Type III","E":"Type IV"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Type I is IgE/mast cell — allergic transfusion reactions (urticaria) exist but do not cause haemoglobinuria.","B":"Correct — as the BM29 key explains, the recipient has <b>antibodies against antigens on the surface of the transfused red cells</b>. This is <b>type II hypersensitivity</b>: IgM isohaemagglutinins fix complement, lysing red cells <b>intravascularly</b> → free haemoglobin in plasma and urine, fever, chills, hypotension, and risk of renal failure and DIC.","C":"There is no immune-complex component here — the antigen is fixed on a cell surface, not soluble.","D":"Type III requires soluble antigen–antibody complexes depositing in tissue.","E":"Type IV is delayed and T-cell mediated."},"also":"BM30 Q34","img":"slides/L15P2_10.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q35","t":"Bracelet contact dermatitis","s":"A 25-year-old man develops a rash with redness and small blisters on his wrists over several days, starting as erythema while wearing a <b>new bracelet</b>. Which hypersensitivity is this?","lec":"L15P1","sl":37,"c":"<b>Allergic vs irritant contact dermatitis</b> — a distinction worth holding: <b>allergic</b> requires prior <b>sensitisation</b>, is immunological (type IV, hapten + T cell), affects only sensitised individuals, and spreads slightly beyond the contact area; <b>irritant</b> is direct chemical damage, needs no sensitisation, affects anyone given enough exposure, and stays sharply confined to the contact area.","o":{"A":"Innate immune response","B":"Irritant contact response","C":"Immune complex response","D":"Cell-mediated immune response","E":"Complement-mediated immune response"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"An innate response would be immediate and non-specific; this developed over days after sensitisation to a specific metal.","B":"<b>Irritant</b> contact dermatitis is direct chemical toxicity with no immune memory — it occurs on first exposure in anyone. Here the reaction developed after wearing a <b>new</b> bracelet over several days, indicating <b>allergic</b> sensitisation.","C":"Immune complex disease (type III) causes vasculitis, serum sickness and glomerulonephritis, not localised metal dermatitis.","D":"Correct — nickel in the bracelet acts as a <b>hapten</b>, producing <b>allergic contact dermatitis</b>: a <b>cell-mediated (type IV)</b> reaction driven by CD4⁺ T cells and CD8⁺ CTLs, peaking 48–72 hours after exposure.","E":"Complement drives types II and III."},"also":"BM32 Q25, BM31 Q30, BM30 Q30, BM28 Q54","img":"slides/L15P1_37.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q36","t":"Investigating contact dermatitis","s":"Following on from the previous item, which test should be used?","lec":"L15P1","sl":42,"c":"Every cohort pairs the contact-dermatitis mechanism question with the test question. Answer set: <b>T cell-mediated / cell-mediated</b> + <b>patch test</b>.","o":{"A":"Skin patch test","B":"Skin prick test","C":"Serum total IgE","D":"Allergen-specific IgE","E":"Complement CH50"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>patch test</b> is the diagnostic test for <b>type IV</b> allergic contact dermatitis: standard allergens applied under occlusion and read at <b>48 and 72 hours</b>.","B":"Prick testing detects type I IgE sensitivity at 15–20 minutes.","C":"Total IgE is a crude atopy marker for type I disease.","D":"Specific IgE also tests type I.","E":"CH50 screens complement function, used in suspected complement deficiency."},"also":"BM32 Q26, BM31 Q31, BM30 Q31, BM28 Q55","img":"slides/L15P1_42.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q42","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q42","t":"Mechanism of GVHD","s":"A leukaemic patient is diagnosed with <b>GVHD after bone marrow transplantation</b>. What is the mechanism of GVHD in this patient?","lec":"L14","sl":37,"c":"Say the direction aloud: <b>rejection = host attacks graft; GVHD = graft attacks host</b>. GVHD follows <b>HSC/bone marrow</b> transplantation and non-irradiated blood products — not solid organs such as a kidney, which carry too few passenger T cells.","ans":"<b>Mechanism: mature donor T lymphocytes in the graft attack the recipient's tissues.</b><br><br><b>Step by step:</b><ul><li>The bone marrow inoculum contains <b>immunocompetent mature donor T cells</b>.</li><li>The recipient has been <b>immunosuppressed by conditioning</b> (chemotherapy/irradiation) and therefore <b>cannot reject</b> those donor cells.</li><li>Conditioning also damages host tissue, releasing inflammatory cytokines and up-regulating host MHC and co-stimulatory molecules — the 'cytokine storm' that primes the reaction.</li><li>Donor T cells recognise <b>host alloantigens</b> — mismatched HLA, or, in an HLA-identical sibling transplant, <b>minor histocompatibility antigens</b> — presented by host APCs.</li><li>Activated donor <b>CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T cells</b> proliferate and attack host epithelium, causing <b>epithelial cell death</b>.</li></ul><b>Three conditions required for GVHD (Billingham's criteria):</b> (1) the graft contains immunocompetent cells; (2) the host is immunocompromised; (3) the host expresses antigens foreign to the donor.<br><br><b>Target organs and clinical picture:</b><ul><li><b>Acute GVHD</b> (within ~100 days): <b>skin</b> — maculopapular rash; <b>gastrointestinal tract</b> — watery diarrhoea, abdominal pain; <b>liver</b> — jaundice, raised bilirubin and enzymes, bile duct damage.</li><li><b>Chronic GVHD</b> (later): scleroderma-like skin fibrosis, sicca syndrome, bronchiolitis obliterans.</li></ul><b>Prevention and treatment:</b> HLA matching, <b>T-cell depletion</b> of the graft, immunosuppressive prophylaxis with <b>cyclosporine plus methotrexate</b>, and corticosteroids for established disease. <b>Irradiating blood products</b> prevents transfusion-associated GVHD.<br><br><b>Note the trade-off:</b> depleting donor T cells reduces GVHD but also removes the beneficial <b>graft-versus-leukaemia (GVL)</b> effect, increasing relapse risk.","also":"BM32 Q34, BM31 Q38, BM30 Q38","img":"slides/L14_37.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q44","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q44","t":"Respiratory burst defect","s":"Which is the defective function in a patient with recurrent abscesses and an abnormal NBT/DHR test?","lec":"L16","sl":42,"c":"CGD: <b>catalase-positive</b> organisms (<i>S. aureus</i>, <i>Serratia marcescens</i>, <i>Burkholderia cepacia</i>, <i>Nocardia</i>, <i>Aspergillus</i>) cause recurrent abscesses and granulomas. Screening: <b>NBT</b> (yellow → purple formazan if the burst works) and <b>DHR flow cytometry</b> (more sensitive, now preferred).","o":{"A":"Recruitment of neutrophils","B":"Antibody-mediated clearance","C":"Delayed-type hypersensitivity response","D":"Formation of the membrane attack complex","E":"Ability to kill pathogens via the respiratory burst"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Impaired neutrophil recruitment is <b>leukocyte adhesion deficiency</b>, which shows delayed cord separation and leukocytosis without pus.","B":"Antibody-mediated clearance failure is a B-cell/antibody defect (XLA, CVID).","C":"An impaired DTH response indicates a T-cell defect.","D":"Failure to form the MAC indicates <b>terminal complement (C5–C9)</b> deficiency, presenting with <i>Neisseria</i> infection.","E":"Correct — <b>chronic granulomatous disease</b>: defective <b>NADPH oxidase</b> means neutrophils phagocytose normally but cannot generate the superoxide burst needed to kill ingested organisms."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 Q28, BM30 Q27, AX 58 Q21","img":"slides/L16_42.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q45","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q45","t":"No antibody response to vaccine","s":"A patient has recurrent bacterial infection and, despite receiving a vaccine, <b>produces no antibody</b>. What should be checked?","lec":"L16","sl":54,"c":"<b>Failure to respond to vaccination is the functional test of the antibody arm.</b> The full B-cell workup: quantitative <b>IgG, IgA, IgM, IgE</b>; <b>CD19/CD20</b> B-cell count; <b>specific antibody titres before and after vaccination</b> (tetanus toxoid for protein antigens, pneumococcal polysaccharide for T-independent antigens); and isohaemagglutinins. Recurrent <b>encapsulated bacterial</b> infection from 7–9 months of age is the clinical signature.","o":{"A":"CD56","B":"CD3, CD4, CD8","C":"CD19, CD20","D":"CD4 level only","E":"DHR (dihydrorhodamine) assay"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"CD56 marks <b>NK cells</b>, which do not make antibody.","B":"CD3/CD4/CD8 enumerate <b>T cells</b> — relevant to a T-cell defect (viral, fungal, <i>Pneumocystis</i> infection), not to isolated antibody failure.","C":"Correct — as the BM29 key reasons, failure to produce antibody after vaccination despite recurrent infection implicates <b>B cells and B-cell memory</b>. <b>CD19 and CD20</b> are the B-cell markers, so B-cell enumeration (with quantitative immunoglobulins and specific antibody titres) is the right investigation.","D":"CD4 alone assesses helper T cells, chiefly for HIV monitoring.","E":"DHR tests the neutrophil respiratory burst (CGD)."},"also":"BM31 Q26, BM28 Q59","img":"slides/L16_54.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q47","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q47","t":"Hyper-IgM / CD40L defect","s":"A 2-year-old boy has severe pneumonia (a previous episode at age one). His CBC is normal. <b>IgG, IgA and IgM are all below the reference range</b>; B and T lymphocyte numbers are normal, and he tested <b>positive for delayed hypersensitivity</b>. What is the most likely defect?","lec":"L16","sl":26,"c":"⚠ Classic hyper-IgM has <b>IgM normal or high</b> with IgG/IgA/IgE low; this reconstruction reports all three as low, which is why the numbers do not read as textbook. The reasoning the examiners want still holds: <b>B cells present + T cells present + intact DTH + poor antibody = a defect in T-cell help for class switching (CD40/CD40L)</b>. Related AX item [59f Q11] gives the cleaner version: pneumonia with <b>low IgG, low IgA, high IgM</b> then candidiasis → <b>CD40–CD40L</b>.","o":{"A":"RAG defect","B":"Common γ chain defect","C":"BTK defect","D":"CD40 / CD40L defect","E":"TCR defect"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"RAG mutation prevents V(D)J recombination → <b>SCID</b> with absent T and B cells. Here both are present in normal numbers.","B":"Common γ chain (IL2RG) mutation → X-linked SCID with T lymphopenia; T cells are normal here, and DTH would be negative.","C":"BTK → XLA, in which <b>B cells are absent</b>. This boy has normal B-cell numbers, so BTK is excluded.","D":"Correct — <b>normal B and T cell numbers with a positive DTH (intact cell-mediated immunity) but low immunoglobulins</b> points to a <b>class-switching / T-help defect</b>: the <b>CD40–CD40L</b> interaction. Without it, B cells are present but cannot receive T-cell help to class switch or form germinal centres.","E":"A TCR defect would impair cell-mediated immunity and give a negative DTH."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM32 Q35, AX 59f Q11","img":"slides/L16_26.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q50","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q50","t":"Herd immunity","s":"A 2-year-old girl has not received basic vaccinations, yet mixes normally with other children and does not become ill. Why?","lec":"L17","sl":15,"c":"The BM33 L17 deck carries this as an NL-style sample question almost verbatim: 'MMR coverage in one village is 95%, yet there are no children with measles — why?' Answer: <b>herd immunity</b>. It appears in BM30, BM29 and BM28 too.","ans":"<b>Answer: herd immunity (ภูมิคุ้มกันหมู่ / community immunity).</b><br><br><b>Explanation:</b><ul><li>Because the <b>great majority of the children around her have been vaccinated</b>, they are immune and cannot sustain transmission of the pathogen.</li><li>With enough immune individuals, each infection produces <b>fewer than one</b> secondary case, so <b>chains of transmission break down</b> and the organism cannot circulate in the community.</li><li>The unvaccinated child is therefore <b>protected indirectly</b> — not because she has immunity of her own, but because she is unlikely ever to encounter the pathogen.</li></ul><b>Threshold:</b> the coverage needed rises with how transmissible the organism is — roughly <b>93–95% for measles</b>, around 80–85% for polio.<br><br><b>Why it matters clinically:</b> herd immunity is the only protection available to people who <b>cannot</b> be vaccinated — infants below the licensed age, the <b>immunocompromised</b> (SCID, chemotherapy, advanced HIV), pregnant women (for live vaccines), and those with genuine contraindications.<br><br><b>The corollary the examiners like:</b> if coverage falls below the threshold, herd immunity collapses and outbreaks return — which is why she is protected <i>only</i> as long as the community around her remains vaccinated.","also":"BM30 Q39, BM28 Q68","img":"slides/L17_15.webp"},{"id":"BM29|Q51","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q51","t":"Vaccine to avoid in pregnancy","s":"A 20-year-old <b>pregnant</b> woman presents for vaccination. Which vaccine should be avoided?","lec":"L17","sl":20,"c":"<b>Live attenuated list to memorise: BCG, MMR, OPV, varicella, rotavirus, live attenuated influenza, yellow fever.</b> All contraindicated in <b>pregnancy</b> and in <b>severe immunodeficiency</b>. Note the polio pair: <b>OPV = live (Sabin, oral)</b>, <b>IPV = inactivated (Salk, injected)</b> — the examiners rely on students confusing them.","o":{"A":"Tetanus","B":"Pertussis","C":"OPV (oral polio vaccine)","D":"Diphtheria","E":"Hepatitis B"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Tetanus toxoid is inactivated and is actively <b>recommended</b> in pregnancy.","B":"Acellular pertussis (in Tdap) is a protein subunit and is recommended in pregnancy to protect the newborn.","C":"Correct — <b>OPV (Sabin) is a live attenuated</b> vaccine, and as the BM29 key states, <i>live</i> vaccines are contraindicated in pregnancy. The inactivated alternative (<b>IPV, Salk</b>) may be used if polio vaccination is genuinely required.","D":"Diphtheria toxoid is inactivated and safe.","E":"Hepatitis B vaccine is a recombinant protein subunit and is safe, indeed recommended for at-risk pregnant women."},"also":"BM32 Q41, BM31 Q40, BM30 Q40","img":"slides/L17_20.webp"},{"id":"BM28|S1-Q10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"S1-Q10","t":"Immunological tolerance","s":"Which statement about <b>tolerance</b> is correct?","lec":"L13A","sl":26,"c":"Tolerance master table: <b>Central T</b> — thymus, deletion (AIRE, medulla), nTreg diversion. <b>Central B</b> — bone marrow, deletion, <b>receptor editing</b>. <b>Peripheral T</b> — anergy, Treg suppression, deletion (Fas–FasL), ignorance at privileged sites. <b>Peripheral B</b> — anergy, apoptosis.","o":{"A":"Some CD4⁺ T cells differentiate into regulatory T cells","B":"T-cell education and elimination is the same as for B cells","C":"AIRE is required for positive selection","D":"Peripheral tolerance for T cells occurs in the thymus","E":"Receptor editing occurs in T cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — strongly self-reactive <b>CD4⁺ thymocytes</b> can be diverted into the <b>natural regulatory T cell (FOXP3⁺)</b> lineage rather than deleted, and additional <b>induced Tregs</b> arise in the periphery.","B":"They differ in site and mechanism: T cells are educated in the <b>thymus</b>, B cells in the <b>bone marrow</b>, and only B cells use <b>receptor editing</b>.","C":"AIRE drives <b>negative</b> selection by making mTECs express peripheral tissue antigens.","D":"Tolerance in the thymus is <b>central</b> by definition.","E":"Receptor editing is exclusive to <b>B cells</b>."},"f":["crossover"],"also":"BM31 Q17, BM30 Q17, BM29 Q30","img":"slides/L13A_26.webp"},{"id":"BM28|S1-Q11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"S1-Q11","t":"Fas–FasL engagement","s":"Engagement of <b>Fas with FasL</b> on a lymphocyte produces which outcome?","lec":"L13A","sl":31,"c":"<b>Mutations in FAS or FASL cause ALPS</b> (autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome): failed deletion → lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, expanded double-negative T cells and autoimmune cytopenias. Compare the three peripheral T-tolerance outcomes: <b>anergy</b> (alive, silent), <b>deletion</b> (apoptosis — Fas–FasL, BIM), <b>suppression</b> (Treg).","o":{"A":"Deletion","B":"Suppression","C":"Anergy","D":"Positive selection","E":"Bystander activation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>Fas (CD95) binding FasL</b> triggers the <b>extrinsic apoptotic pathway</b> (caspase-8 → caspase-3), producing <b>deletion</b> of the lymphocyte. This is activation-induced cell death, a peripheral tolerance mechanism that removes repeatedly stimulated, self-reactive cells.","B":"Suppression is mediated by <b>Tregs</b> through IL-10, TGF-β, CTLA-4 and IL-2 consumption.","C":"Anergy is functional silencing from <b>lack of co-stimulation</b> — the cell survives, unresponsive.","D":"Positive selection is the thymic cortical process that rescues thymocytes with useful TCRs.","E":"Bystander activation is non-specific activation, the opposite of deletion."},"f":["crossover"],"also":"BM30 Q19, BM29 Q32","img":"slides/L13A_31.webp"},{"id":"BM28|S1-Q13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"S1-Q13","t":"Immunologically privileged site","s":"Which of the following is an <b>immunologically privileged site</b>?","lec":"L13A","sl":33,"c":"<b>Privileged sites: eye, brain, testis, uterus (fetus)</b> — and this is why corneal grafts survive without HLA matching. The clinical corollary the exam wants: <b>damage to a privileged site can cause autoimmunity</b>, because trauma or infection breaches the barrier and exposes previously sequestered self antigens. Classic example: <b>sympathetic ophthalmia</b> after penetrating eye injury; also post-vasectomy anti-sperm antibodies.","o":{"A":"Bone marrow","B":"Thymus","C":"Heart","D":"Testis","E":"Lung"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Bone marrow is a <b>primary lymphoid organ</b> — the site of haematopoiesis and B-cell maturation, not a privileged site.","B":"The thymus is also a primary lymphoid organ, where T cells are educated.","C":"The heart is fully accessible to the immune system — which is exactly why rheumatic carditis and myocarditis occur.","D":"Correct — the <b>testis</b> is an immunologically privileged site, along with the <b>eye (anterior chamber), brain/CNS, and the uterus (fetus)</b>. Privilege arises from physical barriers (blood–testis, blood–brain barrier), absent lymphatic drainage, local immunosuppressive factors (TGF-β, FasL) and low MHC expression.","E":"The lung is a major site of active mucosal immunity."},"f":["crossover"],"also":"AX 57f Q20","img":"slides/L13A_33.webp"},{"id":"BM28|S1-Q14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"S1-Q14","t":"Least likely to cause autoimmunity","s":"Which is <b>LEAST</b> likely to be associated with autoimmunity?","lec":"L13B","sl":10,"c":"<b>Monogenic (Mendelian) tolerance genes — memorise the five:</b> <b>AIRE</b> → APS-1/APECED (central tolerance) · <b>FOXP3</b> → IPEX (Treg development) · <b>CTLA4</b> → impaired checkpoint and Treg function · <b>FAS/FASL</b> → ALPS (deletion) · <b>C1q/C2/C4</b> → SLE-like disease (defective clearance of apoptotic cells). The AX bank asks the mirror-image version and answers <b>IL-17</b>, whose job is antimicrobial defence rather than tolerance.","o":{"A":"B cell receptor defect","B":"Decreased MHC class I expression","C":"AIRE mutation","D":"FAS mutation","E":"CTLA-4 mutation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"BCR signalling defects can impair B-cell tolerance checkpoints and permit autoreactive B cells to survive.","B":"Correct — <b>decreased MHC class I expression</b> reduces antigen presentation to CD8⁺ T cells. That impairs <b>anti-viral and anti-tumour</b> responses (and is a tumour immune-evasion strategy) rather than causing autoimmunity. It is the odd one out.","C":"<b>AIRE</b> mutation blocks thymic negative selection → <b>APS-1/APECED</b>, a classic monogenic autoimmune disease.","D":"<b>FAS</b> mutation blocks apoptotic deletion → <b>ALPS</b> with autoimmune cytopenias.","E":"<b>CTLA-4</b> mutation removes an inhibitory checkpoint and impairs Treg function → immune dysregulation with autoimmunity."},"f":["crossover"],"also":"BM30 Q21, AX 59f 2f","img":"slides/L13B_10.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q36","t":"Tumour evasion in hepatocellular carcinoma","s":"A man with chronic HBV infection develops abdominal pain and rapid weight loss; blood chemistry shows high <b>AFP</b> and imaging confirms hepatocellular carcinoma. What is the tumour's immune-evasion mechanism?","lec":"L12","sl":32,"c":"<b>AFP (alpha-fetoprotein)</b> is an <b>oncofetal antigen</b> — a tumour-<i>associated</i> antigen normally expressed in fetal life and re-expressed by <b>hepatocellular carcinoma and germ cell tumours</b>. Chronic <b>HBV/HCV</b> infection and cirrhosis are its major risk factors. Immune evasion recap: low immunogenicity (↓MHC I, ↓antigen, ↓adhesion molecules), immune suppression (<b>TGF-β, IL-10, CTLA-4/PD-L1, FasL</b>), and subversion of the host (<b>M2 macrophages, Tregs, MDSCs</b>).","o":{"A":"Tumour secretes TGF-β, suppressing T-cell activation","B":"Tumour cells bind CD28 on T cells, inactivating them","C":"Tumour expresses Fas, causing T cells to undergo apoptosis","D":"Tumour activates macrophages to suppress T cells","E":"Tumour secretes anti-VEGF to promote angiogenesis"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — tumours secrete <b>immunosuppressive cytokines, chiefly TGF-β</b> (with IL-10), which inhibit T-cell activation and proliferation and expand Tregs in the tumour microenvironment.","B":"Tumours engage <b>inhibitory</b> receptors (CTLA-4, PD-1); CD28 is the <b>activating</b> co-receptor and is not the target.","C":"The molecules are reversed: the tumour expresses <b>FasL</b>, which engages <b>Fas on the T cell</b> to kill it.","D":"It is <b>M2 (alternatively activated)</b> macrophages that promote tumour growth; classical M1 macrophages are anti-tumour.","E":"Tumours secrete <b>VEGF</b> to drive angiogenesis; <b>anti</b>-VEGF is a therapeutic antibody, and this option is self-contradictory."},"also":"BM29 Q10, AX 59m Q1","img":"slides/L12_32.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q37","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q37","t":"Tumour marker — ovarian cancer","s":"A woman has right lower quadrant abdominal pain and a right inguinal mass. Blood chemistry shows high <b>CA 125</b> and slightly raised <b>β-hCG</b>. What cancer is diagnosed?","lec":"L12","sl":12,"c":"<b>Tumour markers — learn as flashcard pairs.</b> <b>AFP</b> → hepatocellular carcinoma, germ cell tumours · <b>CEA</b> → colorectal (also gastric, pancreatic, lung) · <b>CA 19-9</b> → pancreatic, biliary · <b>CA 125</b> → ovarian · <b>CA 15-3</b> → breast · <b>PSA</b> → prostate · <b>β-hCG</b> → choriocarcinoma, germ cell tumours, and the basis of the pregnancy test · <b>calcitonin</b> → medullary thyroid carcinoma. All are <b>tumour-<i>associated</i></b> antigens: useful for monitoring treatment response and recurrence, but too non-specific for screening.","o":{"A":"Ovarian cancer","B":"Hepatocellular cancer","C":"Pancreatic cancer","D":"Colorectal cancer","E":"Breast cancer"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>CA 125 is the marker of ovarian (epithelial) carcinoma</b>, and the clinical picture of a pelvic/lower-abdominal mass in a woman fits. (CA 125 also rises in endometriosis, peritonitis and cirrhosis, so it is not specific.)","B":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is marked by <b>AFP</b>.","C":"Pancreatic cancer is marked by <b>CA 19-9</b>.","D":"Colorectal cancer is marked by <b>CEA</b>.","E":"Breast cancer uses <b>CA 15-3</b> (and <b>HER2</b> as a therapeutic target)."},"also":"AX 57f Q35, AX 59m Q38","img":"slides/L12_12.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q38","t":"Tumour marker — pancreatic cancer","s":"A man has continuous abdominal pain after sigmoid colectomy. CT shows a <b>pancreatic mass</b> with multiple lesions in other organs. Which tumour marker should be evaluated?","lec":"L12","sl":12,"c":"Note the deliberate misdirection in this stem: the surgical history points at colorectal disease (CEA) while the <b>imaging finding</b> points at the pancreas (<b>CA 19-9</b>). Answer the current lesion, not the history. Caveat worth knowing: CA 19-9 is undetectable in the ~5–10% of people who are <b>Lewis antigen-negative</b>.","o":{"A":"CEA","B":"AFP","C":"CA 19-9","D":"CA 125","E":"PSA"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"CEA is the colorectal marker and would have been the relevant one before the colectomy — but the new mass is pancreatic.","B":"AFP marks hepatocellular carcinoma and germ cell tumours.","C":"Correct — <b>CA 19-9</b> is the marker for <b>pancreatic</b> and biliary tract carcinoma, and is the appropriate test for a newly identified pancreatic mass.","D":"CA 125 marks ovarian cancer.","E":"PSA marks prostate cancer."},"also":"AX 57f Q35","img":"slides/L12_12.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q39","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q39","t":"HER2-positive breast cancer","s":"A patient is diagnosed with <b>HER2-positive breast cancer</b>. What type of anti-cancer treatment is most suitable?","lec":"L12","sl":25,"c":"<b>Therapeutic monoclonal antibodies in oncology — passive specific immunotherapy.</b> <b>Trastuzumab</b> → HER2⁺ breast cancer · <b>Rituximab</b> → CD20⁺ B-cell lymphoma/leukaemia · <b>Cetuximab</b> → EGFR⁺ colorectal cancer · <b>Bevacizumab</b> → VEGF (anti-angiogenic). Mechanisms: block growth-factor signalling, <b>ADCC</b> via NK cells, complement activation, and opsonisation for phagocytosis. Distinguish these from <b>checkpoint inhibitors</b> (ipilimumab, nivolumab), which target the T cell rather than the tumour.","o":{"A":"Growth factor receptor inhibitor","B":"Dendritic cell inhibitor","C":"Toll-like receptor inhibitor","D":"Complement inhibitor","E":"Antihistamine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>HER2 (ErbB2) is a growth factor receptor</b> overexpressed on the tumour surface. It is targeted by the monoclonal antibody <b>trastuzumab</b> (and pertuzumab, T-DM1), which blocks receptor signalling and additionally recruits <b>ADCC</b>. This is <b>passive specific immunotherapy</b>.","B":"Inhibiting dendritic cells would suppress anti-tumour immunity — the wrong direction entirely.","C":"TLR <b>agonists</b> (not inhibitors) are used as immune stimulants; inhibiting TLRs would dampen immunity.","D":"Complement inhibition (eculizumab) treats PNH and atypical HUS, not breast cancer.","E":"Antihistamines treat type I hypersensitivity."},"img":"slides/L12_25.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q40","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q40","t":"Autograft","s":"A woman with severe facial burns has a portion of <b>her own skin</b> grafted to replace the burnt skin. What type of graft is this?","lec":"L14","sl":7,"c":"<b>Auto · Iso · Allo · Xeno</b> — and only <b>allo</b> and <b>xeno</b> are rejected, because only they present foreign histocompatibility antigens. Read the source of the tissue carefully: <b>'his own skin' → auto</b>, <b>'identical twin' → iso</b>, <b>'his brother' → allo</b>, <b>'a pig' → xeno</b>. Each of those four wordings appears somewhere in this bank.","o":{"A":"Xenograft","B":"Allograft","C":"Autograft","D":"Isograft","E":"Combination graft"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Xenograft is across species (e.g. porcine heart valve, the 2022 pig heart transplant).","B":"Allograft is between genetically different individuals of the same species.","C":"Correct — tissue moved from one site to another <b>within the same individual</b> is an <b>autograft</b>. Because the tissue is genetically identical to the recipient, there is <b>no rejection</b> and no immunosuppression is needed. Common examples: skin grafts for burns, saphenous vein for coronary artery bypass, bone and cartilage grafts.","D":"Isograft is between genetically identical individuals — identical twins.","E":"Not a standard classification term."},"also":"BM32 Q31, BM31 Q35, BM30 Q35, AX 59f 6f","img":"slides/L14_7.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q41","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q41","t":"Mechanisms of graft rejection","s":"Which statement about the <b>mechanism of graft rejection</b> is correct?","lec":"L14","sl":21,"c":"<b>Direct vs indirect alloantigen recognition.</b> <b>Direct</b>: recipient T cells recognise <b>intact donor MHC</b> on <b>donor</b> passenger APCs in the graft — an unusually high precursor frequency, so it dominates <b>early acute</b> rejection. <b>Indirect</b>: donor MHC is shed, processed and presented as peptide on <b>recipient</b> MHC by <b>recipient</b> APCs — this pathway persists and dominates <b>chronic</b> rejection. HLA strength for rejection: class I <b>B &gt; A &gt;&gt; C</b>; class II <b>DR &gt; DQ &gt; DP</b>.","o":{"A":"Rejection is mediated by CD8⁺ T cells using Fc receptors","B":"CD4⁺ secreted cytokines damage the graft similarly to delayed-type hypersensitivity","C":"CD8⁺ T cells can only be activated by direct presentation","D":"Indirect presentation refers to T-cell activation by alloantigen presented by donor DCs","E":"Minor histocompatibility complexes play the major role in strong graft rejection"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"CD8⁺ CTLs kill through <b>perforin/granzyme and Fas–FasL</b>, recognising peptide–MHC via the TCR. <b>Fc receptors</b> belong to NK cells (ADCC), macrophages and neutrophils.","B":"Correct — <b>CD4⁺ T cells</b> recognise alloantigen and secrete cytokines (<b>IFN-γ, TNF</b>) that recruit and activate macrophages, damaging the graft by exactly the mechanism of a <b>delayed-type hypersensitivity (type IV)</b> reaction. This is also the core of chronic rejection.","C":"CD8⁺ T cells can be activated by <b>both</b> pathways — directly by donor APCs and, through cross-presentation, indirectly as well.","D":"This reverses the definitions: <b>direct</b> presentation = intact donor MHC displayed by <b>donor</b> APCs; <b>indirect</b> = donor MHC processed and presented by <b>recipient</b> APCs.","E":"<b>Major</b> histocompatibility complex (HLA) differences drive strong rejection; minor histocompatibility antigens cause slower, weaker rejection — though they are why even HLA-identical siblings develop GVHD."},"img":"slides/L14_21.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q42","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q42","t":"Hyperacute rejection","s":"A patient with <b>A</b> blood type receives a kidney from a <b>B</b> donor. <b>An hour later</b> the graft is rejected. What is the mechanism?","lec":"L14","sl":28,"c":"This is the cleanly worded version of the ABO question (compare BM30 Q37, which mistakenly makes the recipient AB — the universal recipient, who has no anti-A or anti-B at all). <b>You make antibodies against the ABO antigens you lack</b>: group A → anti-B; group B → anti-A; group O → both; group AB → neither.","o":{"A":"Hyperacute graft rejection by donor antibodies","B":"Hyperacute graft rejection by recipient pre-formed antibodies","C":"Acute graft rejection by donor antibodies","D":"Acute graft rejection by recipient pre-formed antibodies","E":"Chronic graft rejection by a cell-mediated mechanism"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The direction is wrong — the recipient's antibodies attack the graft, not the donor's.","B":"Correct — a group <b>A</b> recipient carries naturally occurring <b>anti-B IgM isohaemagglutinins</b>. These <b>pre-formed recipient antibodies</b> bind ABO antigens on the donor endothelium immediately, activating complement → neutrophil margination → <b>thrombotic occlusion within minutes to hours</b> = hyperacute rejection.","C":"Acute rejection develops over days to weeks and is T-cell driven.","D":"Pre-formed antibody produces hyperacute, not acute, rejection.","E":"Chronic rejection develops over months to years with intimal hyperplasia."},"also":"BM32 Q32, BM31 Q36, BM30 Q37","img":"slides/L14_28.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q49","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q49","t":"Anaphylaxis","s":"A girl presents with <b>oedema and hypotension</b>. What type of hypersensitivity is this?","lec":"L15P1","sl":20,"c":"<b>Anaphylaxis: recognise it and treat it with adrenaline.</b> Onset within minutes of exposure; involves ≥2 systems — skin (urticaria, angioedema), respiratory (stridor, wheeze), cardiovascular (hypotension, shock), GI (vomiting, cramps). <b>First-line treatment is intramuscular adrenaline</b> (antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts only). <b>Anaphylactic</b> = IgE-dependent, requires prior sensitisation; <b>anaphylactoid</b> = direct mast-cell degranulation with no prior exposure (radiocontrast, opioids, vancomycin, NSAIDs) — clinically identical, same treatment.","o":{"A":"Type I","B":"Type II","C":"Type III","D":"Type IV","E":"Type I and IV"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — acute <b>oedema (angioedema) with hypotension</b> is <b>anaphylaxis</b>, the systemic form of <b>type I</b> hypersensitivity. Mast cell degranulation releases <b>histamine, leukotrienes and PAF</b>, causing widespread vasodilation and vascular leakage → hypotension and shock, plus laryngeal oedema and bronchospasm.","B":"Type II targets cell-surface antigens (haemolytic anaemia, Graves', Goodpasture's).","C":"Type III is immune-complex disease — vasculitis, serum sickness, glomerulonephritis — which develops over hours to days, not acutely with shock.","D":"Type IV is a delayed, T-cell mediated reaction over 48–72 hours.","E":"Asthma has immediate and late phases but both remain type I; type IV is not involved."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM30 Q29","img":"slides/L15P1_20.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q50","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q50","t":"Graves' disease effector mechanism","s":"What is the effector mechanism of <b>Graves' disease</b>?","lec":"L15P2","sl":14,"c":"<b>Graves' = type II, mechanism III (antibody-mediated cellular <i>dysfunction</i>)</b>: the antibody hijacks a receptor rather than destroying the cell. Clinical picture: hyperthyroidism, <b>diffuse goitre</b>, <b>exophthalmos</b>, pretibial myxoedema, heat intolerance, sweating, weight loss, tremor. Its paired opposite is <b>myasthenia gravis</b>, where a <b>blocking</b> antibody against the acetylcholine receptor causes fatigable weakness.","o":{"A":"Autoantibodies","B":"Immune complexes","C":"IgE and eosinophils","D":"Eosinophils","E":"Thyroid hormone itself"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — Graves' disease is caused by <b>stimulating autoantibodies (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin, an IgG) against the TSH receptor</b>. They mimic TSH and drive continuous hormone production → hyperthyroidism with <b>suppressed TSH and raised T3/T4</b>.","B":"Immune complexes are the type III mechanism (SLE, serum sickness, post-strep glomerulonephritis).","C":"IgE plus eosinophils is type I allergic disease.","D":"Eosinophils are involved in parasite defence and allergy.","E":"Excess thyroid hormone is the <b>consequence</b>, not the immune mechanism."},"also":"BM31 Q22, BM30 Q33, AX 59f Q31","img":"slides/L15P2_14.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q51","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q51","t":"Allergic rhinitis","s":"A boy has nasal inflammation and watery eyes. What type of hypersensitivity is this?","lec":"L15P1","sl":4,"c":"<b>Allergic (atopic) march</b> — the natural progression the AX bank asks about: <b>atopic dermatitis → food allergy → allergic rhinitis → asthma</b>. Allergic <b>conjunctivitis</b> is the option the AX key says is <b>not</b> part of the march. All are type I, all share the Th2/IL-4/IgE axis.","o":{"A":"Type I","B":"Type II","C":"Type III","D":"Type IV","E":"Type I and IV"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — allergic rhinoconjunctivitis is <b>type I, IgE-mediated</b>: allergen cross-links IgE on mucosal mast cells → histamine and leukotrienes → sneezing, rhinorrhoea, itch, congestion and watery eyes.","B":"Type II is antibody against cell-surface antigens.","C":"Type III is immune-complex mediated.","D":"Type IV is delayed and T-cell mediated.","E":"Both phases of allergic airway disease are type I; type IV plays no part."},"also":"BM30 Q29, BM32 Q24","img":"slides/L15P1_4.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q54","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q54","t":"Nickel earring hypersensitivity","s":"A patient has itchy skin whenever he wears <b>nickel earrings</b>. What is the major mechanism?","lec":"L15P1","sl":37,"c":"Nickel contact dermatitis is the most-repeated hypersensitivity item in this bank — it appears in BM32, BM31, BM30, BM29 and BM28, always paired with the investigation question. Answer set: <b>T cell-mediated (type IV)</b> + <b>patch test at 48–72 h</b>.","o":{"A":"T cell-mediated","B":"B cell-mediated","C":"Complement activation","D":"Immune complex","E":"Antibody-mediated"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — nickel acts as a <b>hapten</b>, binding skin proteins to become immunogenic; Langerhans cells present it and sensitised <b>CD4⁺ T cells</b> (with CD8⁺ CTLs) drive inflammation at 48–72 hours = <b>type IV allergic contact dermatitis</b>.","B":"B cells are not the type IV effector.","C":"Complement mediates types II and III.","D":"Immune complexes cause vasculitis, serum sickness and glomerulonephritis.","E":"No antibody is involved — the defining feature of type IV."},"also":"BM32 Q25, BM31 Q30, BM30 Q30, BM29 Q35","img":"slides/L15P1_37.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q55","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q55","t":"Test for nickel allergy","s":"For the patient in the previous item, which laboratory test is most appropriate?","lec":"L15P1","sl":42,"c":"Match test to mechanism: <b>type I</b> → prick test / specific IgE / tryptase · <b>type II</b> → direct and indirect Coombs (DAT/IAT) · <b>type III</b> → C3/C4 (low), ANA/anti-dsDNA, immune complexes · <b>type IV</b> → <b>patch test</b>, tuberculin/Mantoux, IGRA.","o":{"A":"Skin prick test","B":"Total serum IgE","C":"Skin patch test","D":"Allergen-specific IgE antibody","E":"Complement CH50"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The prick test detects <b>type I</b> IgE sensitivity, read at 15–20 minutes — the wrong mechanism.","B":"Total IgE is a crude marker of atopy (type I).","C":"Correct — the <b>patch test</b> applies allergens under occlusion and is read at <b>48 and 72 hours</b>; it is the diagnostic test for <b>type IV</b> allergic contact dermatitis.","D":"Specific IgE also assesses type I sensitisation.","E":"CH50 screens complement function."},"also":"BM32 Q26, BM31 Q31, BM30 Q31, BM29 Q36","img":"slides/L15P1_42.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q56","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q56","t":"Hallmark of primary immunodeficiency","s":"Primary immunodeficiency is associated with an increased risk of which of the following?","lec":"L16","sl":9,"c":"<b>The four warning signs of immunodeficiency — 'the 4 R's plus severity':</b> <b>Recurrent</b> infection · <b>Persistent</b> infection (does not clear with normal treatment) · <b>Unusual</b> organism (opportunists: <i>Pneumocystis</i>, <i>Serratia</i>, <i>Burkholderia</i>, disseminated BCG) · <b>Severe</b> infection. Add failure to thrive, family history, and an unusual site. Roughly <b>1 in 500</b> people carry a known primary immunodeficiency.","o":{"A":"Infections","B":"Heart disease","C":"Diabetes","D":"Renal disease","E":"Osteoporosis"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>hallmark of primary immunodeficiency is increased susceptibility to infection</b>: infections that are <b>recurrent, persistent, unusual in organism, or unusually severe</b>. Many PIDs also carry increased risk of <b>autoimmunity and malignancy</b>, but infection is the defining feature.","B":"Cardiac anomalies appear in <b>DiGeorge syndrome</b> as part of a syndrome complex, but they are not a general feature of PID.","C":"Diabetes is associated with autoimmune dysregulation (IPEX) rather than being a hallmark of PID.","D":"Renal disease is not a general PID feature.","E":"Osteoporosis is unrelated."},"also":"BM31 Q23, AX 60 5f","img":"slides/L16_9.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q58","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q58","t":"DiGeorge syndrome — affected process","s":"A 5-year-old boy has a <b>chromosome 22 deletion</b> with an abnormal thymus and few T cells. Which process is most affected?","lec":"L16","sl":33,"c":"<b>Thymic geography:</b> <b>cortex → positive selection</b> (MHC restriction — keep the useful TCRs); <b>medulla / corticomedullary junction → negative selection</b> (self-tolerance — delete the dangerous ones, using <b>AIRE</b> to display peripheral tissue antigens), plus <b>nTreg</b> generation. <b>DiGeorge = CATCH-22</b>: Cardiac anomaly, Abnormal facies, Thymic hypoplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcaemia, chromosome 22q11.2.","o":{"A":"Heavy and light chain synthesis","B":"Lymphopoiesis","C":"Positive and negative selection","D":"Plasma cell differentiation","E":"Receptor editing"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Immunoglobulin heavy and light chain synthesis is a <b>B-cell</b> process occurring in the bone marrow — unaffected by thymic aplasia.","B":"Lymphopoiesis (generation of lymphoid precursors) occurs in the <b>bone marrow</b> and is intact; the precursors simply have nowhere to mature.","C":"Correct — the thymus is where <b>T-cell education</b> takes place: <b>positive selection</b> in the cortex (retain thymocytes whose TCR binds self-MHC with low affinity) and <b>negative selection</b> in the medulla (delete strongly self-reactive thymocytes, AIRE-driven). Thymic hypoplasia in <b>DiGeorge (22q11.2 deletion)</b> means this selection cannot occur, so few mature T cells emerge.","D":"Plasma cell differentiation occurs in secondary lymphoid organs from B cells.","E":"Receptor editing is a B-cell mechanism in the bone marrow."},"also":"BM31 Q27, BM28 Q57","img":"slides/L16_33.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q59","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q59","t":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","s":"An 8-year-old boy has recurrent bacterial infections including many episodes of <b>pneumococcal sepsis</b>. He received a pneumococcal vaccine but <b>produced no antibodies</b> against it, and his <b>tonsils are underdeveloped</b>. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L16","sl":25,"c":"<b>Absent tonsils and lymph nodes is the physical sign unique to XLA</b> — no B cells means no follicles and no germinal centres. Encapsulated organisms (<i>S. pneumoniae</i>, <i>H. influenzae</i>, <i>N. meningitidis</i>) require <b>opsonising antibody</b> for clearance, which is why they dominate. Treatment: lifelong <b>IVIG</b> (400–600 mg/kg every 3–4 weeks) plus prophylactic antibiotics; live vaccines, especially <b>OPV</b>, are avoided.","o":{"A":"DiGeorge syndrome","B":"SCID","C":"Chronic granulomatous disease","D":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","E":"Hyper-IgM syndrome"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"DiGeorge gives a <b>T-cell</b> defect with viral and fungal infections, cardiac anomalies, dysmorphic facies and hypocalcaemia.","B":"SCID presents in the <b>first months</b> of life with overwhelming infection and failure to thrive, not as recurrent pneumococcal sepsis in an 8-year-old.","C":"CGD presents with <b>abscesses and granulomas</b> caused by catalase-positive organisms, with an abnormal NBT/DHR test.","D":"Correct — a <b>boy</b> with recurrent <b>encapsulated bacterial</b> infection, <b>no antibody response to vaccination</b>, and <b>absent/underdeveloped tonsils and lymph nodes</b> has <b>X-linked agammaglobulinaemia (Bruton)</b> from a <b>BTK</b> mutation: no mature B cells (CD19⁻), all Ig classes low, and no germinal centres — hence no lymphoid tissue to palpate.","E":"Hyper-IgM would show <b>IgM normal or high</b> with B cells present, and often neutropenia and <i>Pneumocystis</i> infection."},"also":"BM32 Q40, BM30 Q23, BM29 Q45, AX 59f/60 1f","img":"slides/L16_25.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q60","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q60","t":"SCID in an infant","s":"A 7-month-old infant has severe diarrhoea and failure to thrive, with a second episode of ear infection 2 months ago requiring antibiotics. Examination shows <b>small tonsils and oral thrush</b>. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L16","sl":39,"c":"<b>Oral thrush + failure to thrive + chronic diarrhoea in an infant = think SCID.</b> Persistent candidiasis signals a <b>T-cell</b> defect, and adding failure to thrive with hypogammaglobulinaemia makes it combined. SCID is a paediatric emergency: <b>no live vaccines</b> (disseminated BCG/rotavirus), <b>irradiated CMV-negative</b> blood products only, and urgent referral for <b>haematopoietic stem cell transplantation</b> — outcomes are far better if transplanted before 3.5 months of age.","o":{"A":"DiGeorge syndrome","B":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","C":"Severe combined immunodeficiency","D":"Chronic granulomatous disease","E":"Selective IgA deficiency"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"DiGeorge would also show <b>cardiac anomalies, dysmorphic facies and hypocalcaemia</b>, none of which are described.","B":"XLA affects B cells only, so <b>cell-mediated immunity is intact</b> — persistent <b>oral thrush</b> would not be expected, and diarrhoea with failure to thrive is not typical.","C":"Correct — <b>failure to thrive + chronic diarrhoea + recurrent infection + oral thrush + hypoplastic lymphoid tissue</b> in the first year is the classic presentation of <b>SCID</b>: both T- and B-cell function absent, so bacterial, viral and <b>fungal</b> infections all occur.","D":"CGD presents with abscesses and granulomas from catalase-positive organisms.","E":"Selective IgA deficiency is usually asymptomatic or causes mild mucosal infection, not failure to thrive with thrush."},"also":"BM31 Q24, BM30 Q26","img":"slides/L16_39.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q67","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q67","t":"Vaccine giving the best T-cell response","s":"Which gives the <b>best T-cell response</b>?","lec":"L17","sl":19,"c":"<b>Why live vaccines are immunologically superior:</b> replication → endogenous antigen → <b>MHC class I → CD8⁺ CTL</b> plus MHC class II → CD4⁺ help → <b>both cellular and humoral immunity</b>, often from a single dose, with prolonged memory and (if mucosally administered) secretory IgA. The price: reversion risk, cold-chain dependence, and contraindication in <b>pregnancy and immunodeficiency</b>. mRNA and viral-vector vaccines were designed to reproduce this MHC-I advantage without a live organism.","o":{"A":"Live attenuated vaccine","B":"Purified polysaccharide vaccine","C":"Toxoid vaccine","D":"Killed whole-cell vaccine","E":"Passive immunoglobulin"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — a <b>live attenuated</b> vaccine <b>replicates inside host cells</b>, so its antigens enter the <b>cytosolic pathway and are presented on MHC class I</b> to <b>CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells</b>, as well as on MHC class II to CD4⁺ cells. That is why live vaccines give the strongest <b>cell-mediated</b> immunity, with authentic antigen presentation and long-lasting memory.","B":"Pure polysaccharide is <b>T-independent</b>: it cross-links the BCR and produces IgM with <b>no T-cell involvement, no class switching and no memory</b> — the very reason conjugate vaccines were invented.","C":"Toxoids are protein and therefore T-dependent, but being non-replicating they are processed mainly through the <b>exogenous pathway onto MHC II</b>, generating antibody rather than strong CTL responses.","D":"Killed whole-cell vaccines are also non-replicating and predominantly MHC II-restricted.","E":"Passive immunoglobulin gives ready-made antibody and provokes <b>no T-cell response and no memory</b> at all."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 Q39","img":"slides/L17_19.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q69","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q69","t":"Passive immunisation","s":"Which statement is correct about <b>passive immunisation</b>?","lec":"L17","sl":5,"c":"<b>The four-box immunity grid — memorise it, it is asked in several forms.</b> <b>Natural active</b>: infection → memory, long-lasting. <b>Artificial active</b>: <b>vaccination</b> → memory, long-lasting. <b>Natural passive</b>: <b>maternal IgG across the placenta, IgA in breast milk</b> → immediate, no memory. <b>Artificial passive</b>: <b>IVIG, antivenom, rabies/tetanus immunoglobulin, monoclonal antibodies</b> → immediate, no memory. The AX bank asks which is longest-lasting → <b>natural active</b>.","o":{"A":"It gives an immediate effect but no memory","B":"It gives a delayed effect with long-lasting memory","C":"It requires the recipient's own B cells to respond","D":"It is achieved by administering a killed vaccine","E":"It generates class-switched IgG over 2 weeks"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — passive immunisation supplies <b>pre-formed antibody</b>, so protection is <b>immediate</b>. But because the recipient's own lymphocytes are never engaged, there is <b>no memory</b> and protection lasts only weeks to months as the transferred IgG is catabolised.","B":"Delayed onset with lasting memory describes <b>active</b> immunisation.","C":"Passive immunisation works even in patients with no functional B cells — which is precisely why IVIG is used in XLA and CVID.","D":"Administering a killed vaccine is <b>artificial active</b> immunisation.","E":"Class switching over two weeks describes a primary <b>active</b> response."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM32 Q39, AX 58/60 11f, AX 57f Q12, AX 57f Q13","img":"slides/L17_5.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q70","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q70","t":"Snake bite treatment","s":"A patient is bitten by a <b>snake</b>. What should be given?","lec":"L17","sl":6,"c":"<b>When speed matters, give antibody (passive); when durability matters, give antigen (active).</b> Passive agents in clinical use: <b>antivenom</b> (snake, scorpion), <b>tetanus immunoglobulin</b>, <b>rabies immunoglobulin</b> (infiltrated around the wound — the L17 deck's ฉีดรอบแผล note), hepatitis B immunoglobulin, <b>IVIG</b> for antibody-deficient patients, and therapeutic monoclonals. A classic combination in rabies post-exposure prophylaxis is <b>both at once</b>: immunoglobulin for immediate cover plus vaccine for lasting immunity. ⚠ Antivenom is a foreign (usually equine) protein — it can itself cause <b>serum sickness (type III)</b> a week or so later.","o":{"A":"Antivenom (pre-formed antibody)","B":"Snake venom toxoid vaccine","C":"Live attenuated vaccine","D":"Adjuvanted subunit vaccine","E":"Corticosteroid alone"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — snake envenomation requires <b>immediate neutralisation of circulating venom</b>, so the treatment is <b>antivenom</b>: pre-formed antibody (usually equine or ovine immunoglobulin) = <b>artificial passive immunisation</b>. It works within minutes, which active immunisation never could.","B":"A toxoid would take weeks to generate antibody — far too slow for an acute bite, and no such vaccine is in routine use.","C":"A live vaccine is irrelevant to a toxin exposure.","D":"Any active vaccine is too slow for an emergency.","E":"Corticosteroids treat an allergic reaction to the antivenom, not the envenomation itself."},"f":["completed"],"also":"AX 58/60 11f","img":"slides/L17_6.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q71-74","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q71-74","t":"ANA testing in SLE — interpretation set","s":"You are shown an <b>ANA</b> result. (71) Interpret the result. (72) What specimen is sent? (73) What principle is used, and what is the second antibody tagged with? (74) Which hypersensitivity type is involved?","lec":"L19","sl":36,"c":"<b>ANA is the screening test for SLE</b> — highly sensitive (over 95%) but poorly specific, since low titres occur in healthy people, in other connective tissue diseases and with age. Confirm with the specific antibodies: <b>anti-dsDNA</b> (correlates with disease activity and lupus nephritis) and <b>anti-Sm</b> (most specific). Add <b>low C3/C4</b> as a marker of active complement-consuming disease.","ans":"<b>(71) Interpretation.</b> A <b>positive ANA</b> means the patient has <b>autoantibodies directed against nuclear antigens</b> of their own cells. Report both the <b>titre</b> (e.g. 1:160, 1:320 — higher is more significant) and the <b>staining pattern</b>, since the pattern hints at the antibody specificity:<ul><li><b>Homogeneous</b> (diffuse nuclear) → anti-dsDNA, anti-histone → <b>SLE</b>, drug-induced lupus</li><li><b>Speckled</b> → anti-Sm, anti-RNP, anti-Ro/La → SLE, mixed connective tissue disease, Sjögren's</li><li><b>Peripheral/rim</b> → anti-dsDNA → <b>SLE</b> (most specific pattern)</li><li><b>Nucleolar</b> → systemic sclerosis</li><li><b>Centromere</b> → limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (CREST)</li></ul><b>(72) Specimen.</b> <b>Patient serum</b> (a clotted blood sample). The serum is diluted serially and layered onto the substrate.<br><br><b>(73) Principle and label.</b> <b>Indirect immunofluorescence assay (IFA)</b> on <b>HEp-2 cells</b> as substrate:<ul><li>Patient serum is incubated with the fixed HEp-2 cells; any ANA present binds nuclear antigens.</li><li>Unbound antibody is washed away.</li><li>A <b>second (secondary) antibody — anti-human IgG — labelled with a fluorescent dye, most commonly FITC (fluorescein isothiocyanate)</b>, is added and binds the patient's bound antibody.</li><li>The slide is read under a <b>fluorescence microscope</b>; apple-green nuclear fluorescence = positive, and the distribution gives the pattern.</li></ul>It is called <b>indirect</b> because the label is on the <b>second</b> antibody, not on the patient's own antibody.<br><br><b>(74) Hypersensitivity type.</b> <b>Type III — immune complex-mediated.</b> ANA and anti-dsDNA form circulating immune complexes that deposit in glomeruli, vessel walls, skin and joints, activating complement and recruiting neutrophils → vasculitis and lupus nephritis. <b>Caveat:</b> if the patient also has <b>autoimmune haemolytic anaemia or thrombocytopenia</b>, those specific manifestations are <b>type II</b> — such a patient has both.","f":["image"],"also":"BM31 written Q7/Q8, BM30 written Q5/Q6","img":"slides/L19_36.webp"},{"id":"BM28|Q75-78","k":"written","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q75-78","t":"Allergy immunoblot — three-part reading","s":"You are shown immunoblot (specific IgE) results. (75) Which allergen is the patient allergic to? (76) Which substance causes symptoms, and why? (77) Which substances should the patient avoid? (78) Interpret the result.","lec":"L15P1","sl":24,"c":"This immunoblot reading appears in the written section of <b>every cohort</b> — BM28, BM29, BM30, BM31 and BM32 — sometimes three or four sub-questions in a row. It is the most reliably repeated written material in the whole Sum II bank, and the marking scheme is mechanical: validate, grade, name, explain the type I mechanism, advise avoidance.","ans":"<b>Use the same disciplined three-step reading every time.</b><br><br><b>Step 1 — validate with the indicator.</b> The <b>standard indicator must be class 3 or above</b>. If it is, the strip is valid and you may read on. If it is class 0–2, stop: the test is <b>uninterpretable</b> and must be repeated.<br><br><b>Step 2 — grade every band (classes 0–6).</b> 0 = negative · 1 = borderline · 2 = low positive · <b>3 = positive, clinically significant</b> · 4–6 = strongly positive.<br><br><b>Step 3 — answer each sub-question directly.</b><ul><li><b>(75) Which allergen?</b> Name every allergen scoring <b>class 3 or above</b>.</li><li><b>(76) Which causes symptoms, and why?</b> 'The allergen at class ___, because specific IgE at class 3 or above indicates clinically significant sensitisation. On exposure, the allergen cross-links IgE bound to <b>FcεRI</b> on mast cells, causing degranulation and release of <b>histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes and prostaglandin D₂</b> — a <b>type I hypersensitivity</b> reaction producing the patient's symptoms.'</li><li><b>(77) Which to avoid?</b> All allergens at class 3 or above. Avoidance is the first-line management of any confirmed IgE-mediated allergy.</li><li><b>(78) Overall interpretation.</b> 'Valid test (indicator ≥ class 3). Positive specific IgE to [allergens] at class [n]. Conclusion: <b>type I IgE-mediated allergic sensitisation</b> to these allergens. Manage with allergen avoidance, symptomatic treatment (antihistamine, intranasal or inhaled corticosteroid), and consider <b>allergen-specific immunotherapy</b> if symptoms persist.'</li></ul><b>Always finish with the caveat that earns the last mark:</b> a positive specific IgE demonstrates <b>sensitisation</b>; clinical allergy requires that the laboratory result <b>correlate with the patient's exposure history and symptoms</b>.","f":["image"],"also":"BM32 Q60, BM31 W9/W10, BM30 W9/W10, BM29 SG Q12","img":"slides/L15P1_24.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 12f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 12f","t":"Inductive site of gut mucosal immunity","s":"Which is the <b>inductive site</b> where specific T and B cells are activated in gut mucosal immunity?","lec":"L11","sl":9,"c":"<b>Inductive vs effector sites</b> — the organising idea of mucosal immunity. <b>Inductive</b> (where lymphocytes are activated): <b>Peyer's patches</b>, mesenteric lymph nodes, tonsils and adenoids, isolated lymphoid follicles — collectively <b>MALT</b> (GALT in gut, NALT in nose/throat, BALT in bronchi). <b>Effector</b> (where they act): <b>lamina propria</b> (IgA plasma cells) and the <b>intraepithelial</b> compartment. Primed lymphocytes leave the Peyer's patch, travel via lymph and blood, and <b>home back</b> to the mucosa using α4β7 integrin and CCR9.","o":{"A":"Peyer's patch","B":"Intestinal lumen","C":"Intraepithelial layer","D":"Lamina propria","E":"Basement membrane of the epithelial layer"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>Peyer's patches</b> are the organised lymphoid follicles of the small intestine and the classic <b>inductive site</b>: overlying <b>M cells</b> sample luminal antigen and hand it to dendritic cells in the subepithelial dome, which then prime naive <b>T and B cells</b> in the follicle.","B":"The intestinal lumen is outside the body's tissues — antigen must first be transported across the epithelium.","C":"The intraepithelial compartment houses <b>intraepithelial lymphocytes</b> (mostly CD8⁺ and γδ T cells) acting as effectors, not as a priming site.","D":"The <b>lamina propria</b> is the <b>effector site</b>, where IgA-secreting plasma cells and effector T cells do their work after being primed elsewhere.","E":"The basement membrane is a structural layer with no lymphoid organisation."},"img":"slides/L11_9.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 45","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 45","t":"IgA function in intestinal mucosa","s":"Which is <b>NOT</b> a function of IgA in the intestinal mucosa?","lec":"L11","sl":22,"c":"<b>Secretory IgA</b>: made as a <b>dimer</b> joined by <b>J chain</b>, transported across the epithelium by the <b>polymeric immunoglobulin receptor (pIgR)</b>, whose cleaved remnant becomes the <b>secretory component</b> — this protects IgA from gut proteases. It is produced in <b>greater quantity than any other isotype</b>. Functions: neutralise toxins and viruses, block adherence, intracellular neutralisation, excretion of antigen — all <b>without complement activation or inflammation</b>. Class switching to IgA is driven by <b>TGF-β</b> (plus APRIL/BAFF and retinoic acid).","o":{"A":"Complement induction","B":"Neutralisation of pathogens/toxins in the gut lumen","C":"Neutralisation of antigen in the endosomes of enterocytes","D":"Export of toxin from the lamina propria to the lumen","E":"Blocking microbial adherence to the epithelium"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct (the exception) — <b>secretory IgA does not activate complement</b>. It is a poor complement activator by the classical pathway, and that is deliberate: the gut faces trillions of commensal organisms, so a <b>non-inflammatory</b> antibody is exactly what is needed. IgA works by <b>immune exclusion</b>, not destruction.","B":"IgA does neutralise pathogens and toxins in the lumen — its primary job.","C":"During transcytosis, IgA can intercept and neutralise antigen <b>inside enterocyte endosomes</b>.","D":"IgA binds antigen in the lamina propria and carries it back out to the lumen during pIgR-mediated transport — genuine 'export'.","E":"Blocking adherence to epithelium is a core part of immune exclusion."},"f":["completed"],"img":"slides/L11_22.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 35","t":"Properties of regulatory T cells","s":"What is correct about <b>Treg</b>?","lec":"L13A","sl":27,"c":"<b>Treg mechanisms — four, and each is a potential answer option:</b> (1) secrete <b>IL-10 and TGF-β</b>; (2) <b>CTLA-4</b> removes B7 from APCs; (3) <b>CD25-mediated IL-2 consumption</b> starves effector T cells; (4) direct cytolysis. Populations: <b>nTreg</b> (thymus, self-antigen driven) vs <b>iTreg</b> (periphery, TGF-β driven, prominent in the gut). ⚠ Note how this question separates <b>what Tregs consume</b> (IL-2) from <b>what they secrete</b> (IL-10, TGF-β) — options are written to blur exactly that line.","o":{"A":"Treg use IL-2 for survival","B":"Natural Treg are induced by TGF-β and IL-2","C":"Immature T cells develop into Treg when the TCR binds self antigen","D":"Treg use CD25 to block B7","E":"Treg secrete IL-12 to suppress effector cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — Tregs express <b>CD25 (IL-2Rα)</b> at high levels and are <b>critically dependent on IL-2 for their survival</b> and maintenance, even though they make very little themselves. This is why IL-2 blockade or CD25 deficiency causes autoimmunity.","B":"The key notes this is wrong as written: <b>induced (peripheral) Tregs</b> are the ones generated by <b>TGF-β</b>; <b>natural</b> Tregs arise in the thymus. Tregs <i>produce</i> TGF-β and IL-10 as effector molecules.","C":"The senior students' note is worth reading: this is largely true — self-antigen recognition drives nTreg development — but it is not 100% correct because <b>mature</b> T cells in the thymus and lymph nodes can also convert to Treg, so it is not exclusively an 'immature T cell' event.","D":"Wrong molecule: Tregs use <b>CTLA-4</b> to strip <b>B7</b> off APCs. They use <b>CD25</b> to <b>capture IL-2</b> from the environment, depriving effector T cells of it.","E":"IL-12 is a pro-inflammatory Th1-inducing cytokine made by dendritic cells and macrophages, not a Treg product."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM32 Q18, BM31 Q18, BM30 Q18","img":"slides/L13A_27.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 14f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 14f","t":"Immunological event leading to autoimmunity","s":"Which immunological event can lead to <b>autoimmunity</b>?","lec":"L13A","sl":34,"c":"<b>Immunologically privileged sites: eye, brain, testis, uterus (fetus).</b> Privilege comes from physical barriers (blood–brain, blood–testis), absent lymphatic drainage, local <b>TGF-β and FasL</b> expression and low MHC. It explains why <b>corneal grafts survive without HLA matching</b>. The exam's follow-through: trauma or infection breaching that barrier releases sequestered antigen → autoimmunity.","o":{"A":"Expression of co-stimulatory molecules on APCs","B":"Damage of immunologically privileged sites","C":"Sufficiency of regulatory T cell function","D":"Deficiency of IL-2 in the environment","E":"Engagement of Fas–FasL receptors on T cells"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Co-stimulator expression is normally induced only by <b>pathogens</b>, not by self antigen — so on its own it is not the mechanism. (Note that in the context of infection this <i>is</i> how bystander activation works, which is why the option is tempting.)","B":"Correct — <b>damage to an immunologically privileged site</b> (eye, brain, testis, uterus) breaches the barrier and <b>exposes sequestered self antigens</b> that were never presented during thymic education. The immune system has no tolerance to them, so an autoimmune response follows — the classic example being <b>sympathetic ophthalmia</b> after penetrating eye injury.","C":"<b>Sufficient</b> Treg function <b>prevents</b> autoimmunity; it is Treg <b>deficiency</b> that causes it.","D":"IL-2 deficiency reduces T-cell proliferation overall — though note the nuance that IL-2/CD25 loss also cripples Tregs, which is why <b>CD25 deficiency</b> does cause autoimmunity.","E":"Fas–FasL engagement <b>deletes</b> T cells by apoptosis; it is loss of this pathway (<b>ALPS</b>) that causes autoimmunity."},"also":"BM28 S1-Q13","img":"slides/L13A_34.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59m] 23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59m] 23","t":"T cell without co-stimulation","s":"A T cell receives antigen signal but has <b>no co-stimulators</b>. What results?","lec":"L13A","sl":17,"c":"<b>Two-signal model.</b> <b>Signal 1</b> = TCR binding peptide–MHC. <b>Signal 2</b> = <b>CD28 on the T cell binding B7 (CD80/CD86)</b> on the APC. Signal 1 alone → <b>anergy</b>. Both → activation, IL-2 production, proliferation. This is precisely why <b>CTLA-4-Ig</b> works as an immunosuppressant in transplantation: it removes signal 2 and drives alloreactive T cells into anergy.","o":{"A":"Clonal deletion","B":"Clonal proliferation","C":"Clonal anergy","D":"Clonal differentiation","E":"Clonal expansion into memory cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Clonal deletion is apoptotic death, driven by repeated stimulation or Fas–FasL — not by absent co-stimulation.","B":"Proliferation requires <b>both</b> signal 1 and signal 2; without co-stimulation it cannot occur.","C":"Correct — <b>signal 1 without signal 2 produces clonal anergy</b>: the T cell survives but becomes functionally unresponsive, and remains so even if it later encounters properly presented antigen.","D":"Differentiation into effector subsets needs full activation plus cytokines.","E":"Memory formation also requires complete activation."},"also":"BM30 Q19, BM29 Q32","img":"slides/L13A_17.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 15","t":"Roles of immune regulation","s":"Which is a role of <b>immune regulation</b>?","lec":"L13A","sl":4,"c":"<b>Immunological equilibrium</b>: effector T cells (activation) balanced against regulatory T cells (tolerance). Tip too far towards activation → <b>autoimmunity, allergy, chronic inflammation, transplant rejection</b>. Tip too far towards tolerance → <b>infection and tumour escape</b>. Every therapy in L13A, L14 and L18 is an attempt to move this balance deliberately in one direction.","o":{"A":"Prevent tissue damage","B":"Prevent chronic inflammation","C":"Inhibit breakthrough of self-tolerance","D":"All of the above","E":"None of the above"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"True but incomplete — limiting collateral tissue damage from an immune response is one purpose of regulation.","B":"Also true — regulation terminates responses once the antigen is cleared, preventing them from becoming chronic.","C":"Also true — maintaining self-tolerance and preventing autoimmunity is the central role.","D":"Correct — <b>all three</b> are functions of immune regulation. The BM33 L13A deck lists them together: prevent inappropriate reactions against <b>self antigens (self-tolerance)</b>, prevent immune responses causing <b>tissue damage</b>, and control the <b>magnitude and duration</b> of responses.","E":"Incorrect, since all three statements are valid."},"img":"slides/L13A_4.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 16","t":"Inhibitory receptor on cytotoxic T lymphocytes","s":"Which molecule inhibits <b>cytotoxic T lymphocytes</b> in peripheral tissue?","lec":"L13A","sl":21,"c":"<b>CTLA-4 vs PD-1 — where and when.</b> <b>CTLA-4</b>: expressed on activated T cells and constitutively on Tregs; acts <b>early</b>, in <b>lymphoid organs</b>, during <b>priming</b>; competes with CD28 for B7 with higher affinity; blocked by <b>ipilimumab</b>. <b>PD-1</b>: acts <b>late</b>, in <b>peripheral tissues and the tumour microenvironment</b>, on <b>effector</b> T cells; limits chronic stimulation and prevents tissue damage; blocked by <b>nivolumab/pembrolizumab</b>. Both are 'brakes' — and both are removed to treat cancer, or reinforced to protect a graft.","o":{"A":"CTLA-4","B":"PD-1","C":"CD28","D":"ICOS","E":"CD40L"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"CTLA-4 is also inhibitory, but it acts <b>early, during priming in lymphoid organs</b>, competing with CD28 for B7. It is chiefly a brake on CD4⁺ activation and a Treg effector molecule.","B":"Correct — <b>PD-1</b> attenuates TCR signalling in <b>already-activated effector T cells in peripheral tissue</b>, including CTLs. Its ligands PD-L1/PD-L2 are expressed on tissue cells and, importantly, are <b>inducibly expressed on tumour cells</b> — the basis of tumour immune escape and of anti-PD-1 therapy.","C":"CD28 is the <b>activating</b> co-stimulatory receptor.","D":"ICOS is activating — it provides T-cell help in the germinal centre reaction.","E":"CD40L is activating — it licenses dendritic cells and enables B-cell class switching."},"also":"BM32 Q53, BM31 Q19, BM30 Q20","img":"slides/L13A_21.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 18","t":"Site of central tolerance","s":"In which organ does <b>central tolerance</b> occur?","lec":"L13A","sl":8,"c":"<b>Central</b> = immature lymphocytes, in the <b>generative organs</b> (thymus for T, bone marrow for B). <b>Peripheral</b> = mature lymphocytes, in <b>secondary lymphoid organs and tissues</b>. Both are needed, because central tolerance is imperfect: not every self antigen reaches the thymus even with AIRE's help, so autoreactive cells inevitably escape and must be managed peripherally.","o":{"A":"Thymus and bone marrow","B":"Lymph node and spleen","C":"Spleen only","D":"Liver","E":"Peripheral tissues"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>central tolerance occurs in the generative (primary) lymphoid organs</b>: the <b>thymus</b> for T cells and the <b>bone marrow</b> for B cells. Immature lymphocytes that recognise self antigen there are deleted, edited (B cells) or diverted into the Treg lineage (T cells).","B":"Lymph node and spleen are <b>secondary</b> lymphoid organs, where <b>peripheral</b> tolerance operates.","C":"The spleen is secondary lymphoid tissue.","D":"The liver has tolerogenic properties but is not a site of central tolerance.","E":"Peripheral tissues host peripheral tolerance mechanisms — anergy, Treg suppression, deletion, ignorance."},"also":"BM31 Q17, BM30 Q17","img":"slides/L13A_8.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 19","t":"Mechanism of intestinal homeostasis","s":"What is the mechanism of <b>intestinal homeostasis</b>?","lec":"L11","sl":23,"c":"<b>Oral tolerance</b> is the gut's central problem: it must ignore food and commensals while still attacking pathogens. Solution: CD103⁺ dendritic cells + <b>TGF-β and retinoic acid</b> → <b>iTreg</b> → <b>IL-10 and TGF-β</b>. This also supports IgA class switching (TGF-β again). Failure of this system underlies <b>food allergy, coeliac disease and inflammatory bowel disease</b>.","o":{"A":"Dendritic cells secrete IL-6 to convert naive T cells into Treg","B":"Clonal deletion of B cells","C":"Reduction of co-stimulatory substances","D":"Dendritic cells stimulate naive T cells to become Treg","E":"Intraepithelial lymphocytes kill commensal bacteria"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Wrong cytokine — <b>IL-6 opposes</b> Treg induction and, with TGF-β, drives <b>Th17</b> differentiation instead. Treg induction needs <b>TGF-β</b> (plus retinoic acid from gut dendritic cells).","B":"Clonal deletion of B cells is a bone-marrow central tolerance mechanism, not a gut homeostatic one.","C":"Reduced co-stimulation does contribute to a tolerogenic environment, but the key mechanism the deck names is active Treg induction.","D":"Correct — tolerogenic <b>intestinal dendritic cells</b> (CD103⁺, producing <b>TGF-β and retinoic acid</b>) convert naive CD4⁺ T cells into <b>induced regulatory T cells (iTreg)</b>. These Tregs then secrete <b>IL-10 and TGF-β</b> to suppress responses against food antigens and commensal flora — the basis of <b>oral tolerance</b>.","E":"Killing commensals would destroy the microbiota, which is essential for normal GALT development and homeostasis."},"img":"slides/L11_23.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 38","t":"Definition of immunotolerance","s":"Which describes <b>immunological tolerance</b>?","lec":"L13A","sl":6,"c":"The formal definition worth quoting: <b>tolerance = unresponsiveness to an antigen induced by exposure of specific lymphocytes to that antigen</b>. Note that tolerance is <b>antigen-specific and actively induced</b> — it is not the same as generalised immunosuppression. Breaking it produces <b>autoimmunity</b> (self antigen), <b>allergy</b> (innocuous antigen) or <b>graft rejection</b> (transplant antigen) — the three failures the L13A/L15P1 decks put on a single slide.","o":{"A":"Unresponsiveness to foreign antigen","B":"Unresponsiveness to self antigen","C":"Enhanced response to self antigen","D":"Enhanced response to foreign antigen","E":"Absence of all lymphocytes"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Unresponsiveness to a foreign pathogen would be immunodeficiency, or in the case of a tumour, immune escape.","B":"Correct — <b>immunological tolerance is unresponsiveness to an antigen induced by prior exposure of lymphocytes to that antigen</b>, and its most important expression is <b>self-tolerance</b>: unresponsiveness to one's own antigens.","C":"Enhanced response to self antigen is <b>autoimmunity</b> — the failure of tolerance.","D":"An enhanced response to foreign antigen is normal, protective immunity.","E":"Absence of lymphocytes is immunodeficiency (e.g. SCID), not tolerance."},"img":"slides/L13A_6.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f,60] 1f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f,60] 1f","t":"BTK defect","s":"A 12-month-old boy has had recurrent infections since 6 months of age — six ear infections and one pneumonia requiring hospitalisation. Height and weight are at the 50th percentile, and he has <b>absent tonsils</b>. Which is likely to be defective?","lec":"L16","sl":25,"c":"<b>Normal growth is a discriminating clue.</b> Pure antibody defects (XLA, CVID) usually have <b>normal growth</b> because cell-mediated immunity is intact. <b>Failure to thrive</b> points instead to a T-cell or combined defect (SCID, DiGeorge). Combine that with the age of onset: <b>&lt;3 months</b> → T-cell/combined; <b>7–9 months</b> → antibody.","o":{"A":"RAG","B":"ZAP-70","C":"BTK","D":"Lck","E":"WASP"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"RAG1/2 defects block V(D)J recombination in both lineages → <b>SCID</b>, which would present far earlier and with failure to thrive — but this child's growth is <b>normal at the 50th percentile</b>.","B":"ZAP-70 is a TCR signalling kinase; its deficiency causes a <b>T-cell</b> defect with viral and fungal infection.","C":"Correct — <b>onset at ~6 months</b> (as maternal IgG wanes), <b>recurrent sinopulmonary bacterial infection</b>, <b>normal growth</b>, and <b>absent tonsils</b> in a <b>boy</b> = <b>X-linked agammaglobulinaemia</b> from a <b>BTK</b> mutation.","D":"Lck is another proximal TCR kinase — again a T-cell defect.","E":"WASP mutation causes <b>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome</b>: the triad of <b>eczema, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, and recurrent infection</b>, none of which is described."},"also":"BM32 Q40, BM28 Q59","img":"slides/L16_25.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 5f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 5f","t":"Hallmark of primary immunodeficiency","s":"Which is the <b>hallmark</b> of primary immunodeficiency diseases?","lec":"L16","sl":9,"c":"PIDs cause three problems, not one: <b>infection</b> (the hallmark), <b>autoimmunity/immune dysregulation</b>, and <b>malignancy</b>. Prevalence is about <b>1 in 500</b> for all known primary immunodeficiencies combined — far commoner than most students assume.","o":{"A":"Increased susceptibility to infection","B":"Increased risk of heart disease","C":"Decreased chance of cancer","D":"Decreased risk of diabetes","E":"Never part of a syndrome complex"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>increased susceptibility to infection</b> is the defining hallmark: infections that are recurrent, persistent, caused by unusual organisms, or unusually severe.","B":"Cardiac anomalies appear in specific syndromes (DiGeorge) but are not a hallmark of PID as a group.","C":"The opposite — many PIDs carry an <b>increased</b> cancer risk, because immune surveillance and DNA-repair pathways are impaired (e.g. ataxia-telangiectasia, WAS, CVID).","D":"Autoimmune endocrinopathy including <b>diabetes</b> is <b>increased</b> in several PIDs (IPEX, APS-1).","E":"Many PIDs are explicitly part of syndrome complexes — DiGeorge, Wiskott–Aldrich, ataxia-telangiectasia."},"also":"BM31 Q23, BM28 Q56","img":"slides/L16_9.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 11","t":"Hyper-IgM syndrome","s":"A patient with pneumonia has <b>low IgG, low IgA and high IgM</b>, and subsequently develops candidiasis. What is defective?","lec":"L16","sl":26,"c":"<b>Read the immunoglobulin pattern as a fingerprint:</b> <b>all low + no B cells</b> → XLA (BTK) · <b>IgM high, IgG/IgA low, B cells present</b> → <b>hyper-IgM (CD40L)</b> · <b>IgA alone absent</b> → selective IgA deficiency · <b>IgG low ± others, later onset, B cells present</b> → CVID · <b>all low + T cells low</b> → SCID. Hyper-IgM is the one that also brings <b>neutropenia</b> and opportunistic infection, because CD40L failure cripples cell-mediated immunity too.","o":{"A":"RAG","B":"BTK","C":"CD40–CD40 ligand","D":"TCR","E":"NADPH oxidase"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"RAG defects cause SCID with absent T and B cells and no immunoglobulin at all.","B":"BTK causes XLA, in which <b>every</b> immunoglobulin class including IgM is low and B cells are absent.","C":"Correct — the pattern <b>IgG low, IgA low, IgM high</b> is diagnostic of <b>hyper-IgM syndrome</b>, caused by loss of <b>CD40L (on T cells) or CD40 (on B cells)</b>. Without that interaction B cells cannot <b>class switch</b> beyond IgM, and CD40L is also needed to activate macrophages and dendritic cells — hence the added susceptibility to <b><i>Candida</i>, <i>Pneumocystis</i> and <i>Cryptosporidium</i></b>.","D":"A TCR defect would impair cell-mediated immunity broadly.","E":"NADPH oxidase deficiency is CGD, a phagocyte killing defect with normal immunoglobulins."},"also":"BM32 Q35, BM29 Q47","img":"slides/L16_26.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 26","t":"Recurrent abscess investigation","s":"A patient has <b>recurrent gluteal abscesses</b>. Which investigation is appropriate?","lec":"L16","sl":45,"c":"<b>Abscess → phagocyte.</b> Recurrent <b>skin, perianal, gluteal or liver abscesses</b>, poor wound healing and granulomas all point at the phagocyte arm. Tests: <b>NBT</b> or the more sensitive <b>DHR</b> for CGD; <b>CD18/integrin flow cytometry</b> for LAD; neutrophil count for congenital neutropenia. The organisms are <b>catalase-positive</b>: <i>S. aureus</i>, <i>Serratia</i>, <i>Burkholderia</i>, <i>Nocardia</i>, <i>Aspergillus</i>.","o":{"A":"FISH","B":"DHR (dihydrorhodamine) assay","C":"Serum immunoglobulins","D":"CD4 count","E":"CH50"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"FISH detects chromosomal deletions — used for <b>22q11.2 (DiGeorge)</b>, which presents with cardiac and facial anomalies rather than abscesses.","B":"Correct — as the key reasons, <b>recurrent abscesses indicate a phagocyte defect</b>. The <b>DHR flow-cytometric assay</b> tests the <b>neutrophil respiratory burst</b> and diagnoses <b>chronic granulomatous disease</b>.","C":"Immunoglobulins screen the antibody arm — appropriate for recurrent sinopulmonary bacterial infection, not skin abscesses.","D":"CD4 count screens T-cell immunity/HIV.","E":"CH50 screens the complement pathway, relevant for <i>Neisseria</i> and immune-complex disease."},"also":"BM31 Q28, BM30 Q27","img":"slides/L16_45.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 28","t":"HIV and Pneumocystis pneumonia","s":"A patient with <b>HIV</b> develops <b><i>Pneumocystis jirovecii</i> pneumonia (PJP)</b>. Which is correct?","lec":"L16","sl":63,"c":"<b>CD4 thresholds worth memorising for HIV:</b> <b>&lt;200</b> → <i>Pneumocystis jirovecii</i> pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis (AIDS-defining); <b>&lt;100</b> → cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcal meningitis; <b>&lt;50</b> → CMV retinitis, disseminated <i>Mycobacterium avium</i> complex. HIV is the archetypal <b>secondary</b> immunodeficiency: gp120 binds <b>CD4 plus CCR5/CXCR4</b>, progressively destroying helper T cells. Also know <b>IRIS</b> — paradoxical worsening as immunity recovers after starting ART.","o":{"A":"CD4 count is below 200 cells/mm³","B":"CD4 count is above 500 cells/mm³","C":"CD8 count is below 200 cells/mm³","D":"The patient has a B-cell defect","E":"The patient has complement deficiency"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>PJP is an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection</b> that occurs almost entirely when the <b>CD4 count falls below 200 cells/mm³</b> (about 90% of cases). This is also the threshold at which <b>co-trimoxazole prophylaxis</b> is started.","B":"A CD4 count above 500 indicates preserved immunity; opportunistic infections are not expected.","C":"HIV depletes <b>CD4⁺</b> helper T cells by binding CD4 with gp120; CD8 counts are not the marker.","D":"HIV causes a <b>T-cell (CD4)</b> defect. B-cell dysfunction occurs secondarily but is not the mechanism here.","E":"Complement deficiency predisposes to <i>Neisseria</i> and pyogenic infection, not PJP."},"img":"slides/L16_63.webp"},{"id":"AX|[58] 21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] 21","t":"NADPH oxidase defect","s":"Which disease is caused by a defect in the <b>neutrophil NADPH oxidase</b> system?","lec":"L16","sl":42,"c":"CGD in one line: <b>they can eat but they cannot kill.</b> Consequence — recurrent abscesses and granulomas caused by <b>catalase-positive</b> organisms (catalase-negative organisms obligingly supply their own hydrogen peroxide and are still killed). Tests: <b>NBT</b> (yellow → purple formazan when the burst works) and <b>DHR</b> flow cytometry. Treatment: prophylactic <b>co-trimoxazole and itraconazole</b>, IFN-γ, and HSCT for cure.","o":{"A":"Chronic granulomatous disease","B":"SCID","C":"Acquired immunodeficiency","D":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency","E":"Hyper-IgM syndrome"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>chronic granulomatous disease</b> is defined by defective <b>NADPH oxidase</b>: neutrophils phagocytose normally but cannot produce the superoxide burst needed to kill ingested organisms.","B":"SCID is a lymphocyte development defect (IL2RG, ADA, RAG).","C":"Acquired immunodeficiency (HIV, chemotherapy, malnutrition) is secondary and unrelated to NADPH oxidase.","D":"LAD is a defect of <b>CD18/β2 integrin</b> adhesion — neutrophils cannot reach the tissue at all.","E":"Hyper-IgM is a class-switching defect (CD40L)."},"also":"BM31 Q28, BM30 Q27, BM29 Q44","img":"slides/L16_42.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 47","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 47","t":"Consequence of splenectomy","s":"What happens after <b>splenectomy</b>?","lec":"L16","sl":67,"c":"⚠ The AX key originally read '<b>reduced response to polysaccharide vaccine</b>', and the senior student's annotation suggests it should be <b>encapsulated bacteria</b> — both express the same underlying truth: the spleen is where responses to <b>polysaccharide capsular antigens</b> are made. Practical management, which the BM33 deck spells out: give <b>pneumococcal, Hib and meningococcal vaccines <u>before</u> elective splenectomy</b> (ideally 2 weeks prior), plus prophylactic penicillin and a clear plan for febrile illness.","o":{"A":"Increased susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria","B":"Loss of T-cell maturation","C":"Failure of haematopoiesis","D":"Loss of delayed-type hypersensitivity","E":"Complement deficiency"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the spleen filters <b>blood-borne</b> organisms and houses the marginal-zone B cells that mount rapid <b>T-independent antibody responses to polysaccharide capsules</b>. Without it, patients are at risk of <b>overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI)</b> caused by <b>encapsulated bacteria</b>: <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>, <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> type b, and <i>Neisseria meningitidis</i>.","B":"T cells mature in the <b>thymus</b>, not the spleen.","C":"Haematopoiesis takes place in the bone marrow after infancy.","D":"DTH depends on T cells and is unaffected.","E":"Complement proteins are made mainly by the liver."},"f":["keydiff"],"img":"slides/L16_67.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 2","t":"Acute rheumatic fever","s":"A patient has <b>sore throat, polyarthralgia and a diastolic murmur</b>. What is the mechanism?","lec":"L19","sl":64,"c":"<b>Acute rheumatic fever</b> — the L19 Case 3. A <b>delayed, non-suppurative sequel of group A streptococcal pharyngitis</b>, onset 2–3 weeks later, peak age 5–15 years. <b>Modified Jones criteria — major</b>: carditis, polyarthritis, chorea (Sydenham's), erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules; <b>minor</b>: fever, arthralgia, raised ESR/CRP, prolonged PR interval — plus evidence of preceding strep infection (<b>ASO titre</b>, anti-DNase B). Treatment: benzathine penicillin, aspirin (± prednisone for severe carditis), then long-term secondary prophylaxis.","o":{"A":"Delayed-type hypersensitivity","B":"Transient hypersensitivity","C":"Antibody-mediated (type II) hypersensitivity","D":"IgE-mediated hypersensitivity","E":"Immune complex hypersensitivity"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"DTH (type IV) is T-cell mediated and does not explain valvular damage after streptococcal pharyngitis.","B":"'Transient hypersensitivity' is not a recognised category — a filler distractor.","C":"Correct — this is <b>acute rheumatic fever</b>: sore throat (preceding group A streptococcal pharyngitis) → migratory <b>polyarthralgia</b> and <b>carditis</b> (the diastolic murmur of mitral regurgitation). The mechanism is <b>antibody-mediated (type II)</b> damage through <b>molecular mimicry</b> — anti-streptococcal M-protein antibodies cross-reacting with cardiac myosin and valve tissue. The key labels this 'antibody complex hypersensitivity'.","D":"IgE-mediated disease is allergy, unrelated.","E":"Immune complex (type III) disease is the mechanism of <b>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</b> — the other post-strep sequela — not of rheumatic carditis."},"also":"BM31 Q33, BM31 Q21, BM30 Q22","img":"slides/L19_64.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 6","t":"Drug target in rheumatoid arthritis","s":"A patient has fever and polyarthralgia with a <b>positive rheumatoid factor</b>. What is the target of the drug used in this disease?","lec":"L19","sl":57,"c":"<b>RA treatment ladder:</b> NSAIDs and low-dose corticosteroid for symptoms → <b>conventional DMARDs</b> (<b>methotrexate</b> first line, plus sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine, leflunomide) → <b>biologic DMARDs</b> when those fail: <b>anti-TNF</b>, <b>anti-IL-6R (tocilizumab)</b>, <b>anti-CD20 (rituximab)</b>, <b>CTLA-4-Ig (abatacept)</b> → JAK inhibitors. Note how many of these molecules reappear across L13A, L14 and L18 with different intent.","o":{"A":"TNF","B":"IL-4","C":"IL-5","D":"IL-13","E":"TGF-β"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — positive RF indicates <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>, in which <b>TNF-α</b> is the dominant pro-inflammatory cytokine driving synovitis and joint erosion. <b>Anti-TNF biologics</b> (infliximab, etanercept, adalimumab) are a mainstay of treatment.","B":"IL-4 is the Th2 cytokine driving IgE — targeted in atopic disease (dupilumab), not RA.","C":"IL-5 drives eosinophils — targeted in eosinophilic asthma (mepolizumab).","D":"IL-13 drives mucus and airway hyperreactivity — again an allergic target.","E":"TGF-β is immunosuppressive and fibrogenic; blocking it would worsen, not help, autoimmunity."},"also":"BM32 Q22, BM32 Q6","img":"slides/L19_57.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 12","t":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis investigation","s":"A boy develops <b>glomerulonephritis</b> after infection with <b>gram-positive cocci in chains</b>. What further test should be done?","lec":"L15P2","sl":23,"c":"<b>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</b>: an immune-complex (<b>type III</b>) sequela of nephritogenic <i>S. pyogenes</i>, 1–3 weeks after <b>throat or skin</b> infection. Presentation: <b>haematuria (cola-coloured urine), periorbital oedema, hypertension, proteinuria</b>. Labs: <b>ASO/anti-DNase B</b> positive, <b>low C3</b> (consumed by complexes). Usually self-limiting in children. Contrast with <b>acute rheumatic fever</b> — after pharyngitis only, <b>type II</b> molecular mimicry, targeting heart/joints/brain/skin.","o":{"A":"Direct antiglobulin test (DAT)","B":"Indirect antiglobulin test (IAT)","C":"Cold agglutinin titre","D":"ASO titre","E":"ANA"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The DAT (direct Coombs) detects antibody coating red cells — used for autoimmune haemolytic anaemia.","B":"The IAT detects antibody in serum — used in crossmatching and antibody screening.","C":"Cold agglutinins are relevant to cold AIHA and <i>Mycoplasma</i> infection.","D":"Correct — gram-positive cocci in chains = <b><i>Streptococcus pyogenes</i></b>. <b>Anti-streptolysin O (ASO) titre</b> measures antibody against streptolysin O and confirms recent group A streptococcal infection, supporting a diagnosis of <b>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</b>. (After a <i>skin</i> infection, <b>anti-DNase B</b> is more reliable than ASO.)","E":"ANA screens for SLE, another cause of glomerulonephritis but not the one indicated by a preceding streptococcal infection."},"also":"BM31 Q33","img":"slides/L15P2_23.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 31","t":"Hyperthyroidism mechanism","s":"A patient presents with <b>sweating and exophthalmos</b>; examination confirms hyperthyroidism. What is the cause?","lec":"L13B","sl":19,"c":"<b>Graves' vs Hashimoto's</b> — the AX bank asks what they have <b>in common</b> (answer: <b>thyroid enlargement/goitre</b>) as well as how they differ. <b>Graves'</b>: stimulating anti-TSHR antibody, <b>hyper</b>thyroid, low TSH/high T3-T4, exophthalmos, pretibial myxoedema, heat intolerance. <b>Hashimoto's</b>: anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies with lymphocytic infiltration and CTL destruction, <b>hypo</b>thyroid, high TSH/low T4, cold intolerance, weight gain. Both are <b>organ-specific</b> autoimmune diseases.","o":{"A":"Autoantibody binding to a normal cell receptor","B":"Immune complex deposition","C":"Cytotoxic T-cell destruction of thyrocytes","D":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","E":"Granulomatous inflammation of the thyroid"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>Graves' disease</b>: a <b>stimulating IgG autoantibody</b> binds the <b>TSH receptor</b> on normal thyroid cells and mimics TSH, driving unregulated hormone production. Exophthalmos results from retro-orbital fibroblast activation and glycosaminoglycan deposition.","B":"Immune complex deposition is type III (SLE, post-strep glomerulonephritis).","C":"CTL destruction of thyrocytes contributes to <b>Hashimoto's thyroiditis</b>, which causes hypo-, not hyper-, thyroidism.","D":"Mast cells and IgE mediate allergy.","E":"Granulomatous thyroiditis (de Quervain's) is post-viral and painful, with a different clinical course."},"also":"BM31 Q22, BM30 Q33, BM28 Q50","img":"slides/L13B_19.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 2f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 2f","t":"Mutation least associated with autoimmunity","s":"Which <b>loss-of-function</b> mutation is <b>LEAST</b> likely to be associated with autoimmunity?","lec":"L13B","sl":10,"c":"The trick here is the direction of the mutation. <b>Loss of a tolerance gene → autoimmunity</b> (AIRE, FOXP3, CTLA4, FAS, CD25). <b>Loss of an effector gene → infection</b> (IL-17 → candidiasis; IL-12/IFN-γ axis → mycobacteria; NADPH oxidase → CGD). Sort the options into 'brake' and 'weapon' and the answer falls out.","o":{"A":"IL-17","B":"AIRE","C":"Fas","D":"CD25","E":"CTLA-4"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — as the key explains, <b>IL-17</b> (secreted by Th17 cells) recruits neutrophils and induces antimicrobial peptides. Its function is <b>antimicrobial defence</b>, so losing it causes <b>chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis</b>, not autoimmunity. (Excess IL-17 contributes to autoimmune tissue damage — but this question asks about loss of function.)","B":"<b>AIRE</b> loss blocks thymic negative selection → <b>APS-1/APECED</b>, a monogenic autoimmune syndrome.","C":"<b>Fas</b> loss blocks apoptotic deletion of lymphocytes → <b>ALPS</b> with autoimmune cytopenias.","D":"<b>CD25 (IL-2Rα)</b> loss cripples Treg survival → severe autoimmunity, an IPEX-like syndrome.","E":"<b>CTLA-4</b> loss removes an inhibitory checkpoint and impairs Treg function → immune dysregulation with autoimmunity."},"also":"BM30 Q21, BM28 S1-Q14","img":"slides/L13B_10.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 3f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 3f","t":"The allergic (atopic) march","s":"Which does <b>NOT</b> form part of the <b>allergic march</b>?","lec":"L15P1","sl":4,"c":"<b>Atopic march</b>: the typical age-ordered progression of allergic disease — <b>atopic dermatitis (infancy) → food allergy → allergic rhinitis → asthma (childhood)</b>. It reflects one shared underlying mechanism: <b>Th2 polarisation with IL-4/IL-5/IL-13 and IgE class switching</b>, with epithelial barrier dysfunction (filaggrin mutations) allowing early sensitisation through the skin.","o":{"A":"Allergic urticaria","B":"Allergic asthma","C":"Allergic conjunctivitis","D":"Atopic dermatitis","E":"Allergic rhinitis"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Urticaria/angioedema is listed among allergic skin diseases in the BM33 L15P1 deck.","B":"<b>Asthma</b> is the classic late stage of the march.","C":"Correct (the exception) — the key cites the AAAAI definition: the <b>atopic march</b> comprises <b>atopic dermatitis (eczema), food allergy, allergic rhinitis (hay fever) and asthma</b>. <b>Allergic conjunctivitis</b> is not named as one of its stages, although it commonly accompanies rhinitis.","D":"<b>Atopic dermatitis</b> is the first step of the march, typically appearing in infancy.","E":"<b>Allergic rhinitis</b> is a defining stage."},"also":"BM30 Q29, BM28 Q51","img":"slides/L15P1_4.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 7f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 7f","t":"Clinical presentation of autoimmune disease","s":"Which is likely to be the clinical presentation of an <b>autoimmune disease</b>?","lec":"L13B","sl":17,"c":"<b>Systemic vs organ-specific autoimmune disease</b> — the L13B classification. <b>Organ-specific</b>: type 1 diabetes (pancreatic β cells), Hashimoto's and Graves' (thyroid), myasthenia gravis (AChR), Goodpasture's (type IV collagen), pernicious anaemia, multiple sclerosis. <b>Systemic</b>: <b>SLE</b>, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis, Sjögren's — the autoantigen is ubiquitous, so the disease is multi-organ. ⚠ The key itself marks this item '??' because several options can occur in autoimmune disease; answer on the <b>most characteristic</b> feature.","o":{"A":"Acute fever","B":"Pitting oedema","C":"Polyarthritis","D":"Pleural effusion","E":"Multiple organ involvement"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Acute fever is non-specific and far more suggestive of infection.","B":"Pitting oedema suggests cardiac, renal or hepatic disease (though it does occur in lupus nephritis).","C":"Polyarthritis is a very good clue and occurs in both RA and SLE — but taken alone it does not distinguish autoimmune disease from reactive or infective arthritis.","D":"Pleural effusion is one possible manifestation among many.","E":"Correct — the feature that most characterises <b>systemic</b> autoimmune disease is <b>multiple organ involvement</b>, since the autoantigen (nuclear material, IgG Fc) is present throughout the body. SLE is the exemplar, affecting skin, joints, kidney, blood, serosa and CNS simultaneously."},"f":["keydiff"],"img":"slides/L13B_17.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 2fง","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 2fง","t":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","s":"A 9-year-old boy has puffiness around the eyes; examination reveals hypertension, haematuria and proteinuria. He had a <b>skin infection 3 weeks ago</b>. What is the immunopathogenic reaction?","lec":"L15P2","sl":24,"c":"⚠ The AX annotation carries a genuinely useful nuance about SLE that applies here by contrast: <b>SLE is type III</b>, but if the stem also describes <b>autoimmune haemolytic anaemia</b>, that component is <b>type II</b> — so such a patient has both. Where the stem gives you <b>only</b> the immune-complex manifestation, answer <b>type III only</b>.","o":{"A":"Delayed-type hypersensitivity","B":"Transient hypersensitivity","C":"Antibody complex (type II) hypersensitivity","D":"IgE-mediated hypersensitivity","E":"Immune complex hypersensitivity"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"DTH is T-cell mediated and does not produce acute nephritic syndrome after a skin infection.","B":"Not a recognised category.","C":"Type II antibody targets a fixed cell-surface antigen — the mechanism of rheumatic carditis, not of this glomerulonephritis.","D":"IgE mediates allergy.","E":"Correct — <b>periorbital oedema, hypertension, haematuria and proteinuria 1–3 weeks after a streptococcal skin infection</b> is <b>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</b>: <b>type III, immune complex-mediated</b>. Antibody–streptococcal antigen complexes deposit in glomeruli, activate <b>complement</b> (so <b>C3 falls</b>) and recruit neutrophils, damaging the glomerular basement membrane."},"also":"BM31 Q32, BM31 Q33","img":"slides/L15P2_24.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 25","t":"Main antibody of type II hypersensitivity","s":"Which is the main antibody of <b>type II</b> hypersensitivity?","lec":"L15P2","sl":5,"c":"<b>Antibody class by hypersensitivity type:</b> <b>Type I → IgE</b> (mast cells, FcεRI). <b>Type II → IgG and/or IgM</b> against a <b>cell-bound</b> antigen. <b>Type III → IgG (and IgM)</b> against a <b>soluble</b> antigen, forming circulating complexes. <b>Type IV → no antibody</b>, T cells only. The II/III distinction hinges entirely on whether the antigen is <b>fixed on a cell</b> or <b>free in solution</b>.","o":{"A":"IgM","B":"IgG","C":"IgM and IgG","D":"IgA","E":"IgE"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"IgM alone is incomplete — it is the dominant antibody in ABO transfusion reactions and cold agglutinin disease, but not in every type II disease.","B":"IgG alone is likewise incomplete — it dominates warm AIHA, Graves' and myasthenia gravis, but not all.","C":"Correct — <b>type II hypersensitivity is mediated by IgG and/or IgM</b> directed against <b>cell-surface or extracellular matrix antigens</b>. Both classes fix complement and engage Fc receptors.","D":"IgA does not activate complement well and is not a type II mediator.","E":"IgE mediates <b>type I</b> hypersensitivity."},"also":"BM30 Q34, BM29 Q34","img":"slides/L15P2_5.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 27","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 27","t":"Contact dermatitis classification","s":"Under the <b>Gell and Coombs</b> classification, contact dermatitis is which type?","lec":"L15P1","sl":7,"c":"<b>Gell and Coombs, in one table.</b> <b>I</b> — immediate, <b>IgE</b>, mast cells, minutes (anaphylaxis, asthma, rhinitis, food allergy). <b>II</b> — <b>IgG/IgM</b> against <b>cell-surface</b> antigen (AIHA, transfusion reaction, Goodpasture's, Graves', myasthenia). <b>III</b> — <b>immune complexes</b> of soluble antigen (SLE, serum sickness, Arthus, post-strep GN). <b>IV</b> — <b>T cell</b> mediated, <b>48–72 h</b> (contact dermatitis, tuberculin, TB granuloma, type 1 diabetes, chronic rejection).","o":{"A":"Type I","B":"Type II","C":"Type III","D":"Type IV","E":"Type V"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Type I is IgE/mast cell immediate hypersensitivity — minutes, not days.","B":"Type II is antibody against cell-surface antigens.","C":"Type III is immune complex-mediated.","D":"Correct — <b>allergic contact dermatitis is type IV</b>, delayed and <b>T cell-mediated</b>: a hapten binds skin protein, Langerhans cells present it, and sensitised CD4⁺ T cells (with CD8⁺ CTLs) drive inflammation peaking at 48–72 hours.","E":"The original Gell and Coombs scheme has <b>four</b> types; some texts add a 'type V' for stimulatory antibody (Graves'), but the standard classification used here stops at IV."},"also":"BM32 Q25, BM31 Q30, BM30 Q30, BM29 Q35, BM28 Q54","img":"slides/L15P1_7.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 50","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 50","t":"Lupus nephritis","s":"<b>Lupus nephritis</b> is mediated by which mechanism?","lec":"L15P2","sl":21,"c":"<b>Goodpasture's vs lupus nephritis — an immunofluorescence pair worth remembering.</b> <b>Goodpasture's</b>: type II, antibody against <b>type IV collagen</b> in glomerular and alveolar basement membrane, <b>linear</b> IF, presenting with haemoptysis plus glomerulonephritis. <b>Lupus nephritis</b>: type III, immune complexes, <b>granular/lumpy-bumpy</b> IF, with low C3/C4 and positive anti-dsDNA.","o":{"A":"Immune complex-mediated","B":"Antibody against glomerular basement membrane","C":"T cell-mediated cytotoxicity","D":"IgE-mediated","E":"Complement deficiency alone"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — lupus nephritis is <b>type III, immune complex-mediated</b>. Anti-dsDNA and other antinuclear antibodies form complexes with nuclear antigens that <b>deposit in the glomeruli</b>, activate complement and recruit neutrophils, causing glomerular inflammation and injury.","B":"Antibody directly against the <b>glomerular basement membrane</b> (type IV collagen) is <b>Goodpasture's syndrome</b> — a <b>type II</b> disease with a linear immunofluorescence pattern, unlike lupus nephritis's granular 'lumpy-bumpy' deposits.","C":"T cell-mediated cytotoxicity is type IV.","D":"IgE mediates allergy.","E":"Early complement deficiency (C1q, C2, C4) <b>predisposes</b> to lupus by impairing clearance of apoptotic debris, but the nephritis itself is complex-mediated."},"also":"BM31 Q32, BM30 Q32","img":"slides/L15P2_21.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 54","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 54","t":"Hymenoptera sting","s":"Which is correct about a <b>hymenoptera (bee/wasp) sting</b> reaction?","lec":"L15P1","sl":20,"c":"<b>Anaphylaxis essentials.</b> Onset within minutes; two or more systems involved — skin (urticaria, angioedema), airway (stridor, wheeze), circulation (hypotension, shock), gut (vomiting, cramps). <b>First-line treatment: intramuscular adrenaline</b>; antihistamines and corticosteroids are adjuncts only. Sensitised patients should carry an <b>adrenaline auto-injector</b>. <b>Anaphylactic</b> (IgE, needs prior exposure) vs <b>anaphylactoid</b> (direct degranulation — radiocontrast, opioids, vancomycin, NSAIDs — no prior exposure needed): identical clinically, identical treatment.","o":{"A":"Systemic anaphylaxis such as bronchospasm can occur","B":"Hypotension occurs by a non-IgE-mediated mechanism","C":"It is a type IV delayed reaction","D":"It is mediated by immune complexes","E":"It requires no prior exposure"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — bee sting allergy is a classic <b>type I</b> hypersensitivity. In sensitised individuals a severe reaction develops within minutes: <b>acute asthma/bronchospasm, laryngeal oedema, urticaria, diarrhoea and shock</b> — i.e. <b>systemic anaphylaxis</b>. Penicillin allergy is the other classic example.","B":"The hypotension of true anaphylaxis <b>is</b> IgE-mediated. (Non-IgE direct mast cell degranulation exists and is called <b>anaphylactoid</b>, but that is a different mechanism from a hymenoptera sting in a sensitised patient.)","C":"Type IV would take 48–72 hours; anaphylaxis occurs within minutes.","D":"Immune complexes are type III.","E":"Type I anaphylaxis <b>requires prior sensitisation</b> — a first sting sensitises, a subsequent sting triggers."},"also":"BM28 Q49","img":"slides/L15P1_20.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 56","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 56","t":"Autoimmune disease with least CTL involvement","s":"In which autoimmune disease are <b>cytotoxic T cells least likely</b> to take part?","lec":"L15P2","sl":12,"c":"⚠ The key's own annotation admits hesitation between SLE and Goodpasture's. Resolve it by mechanism purity: <b>Goodpasture's is a single-mechanism antibody disease</b> (type II, linear IF, anti-type IV collagen), whereas SLE, though antibody-driven, still involves substantial T-cell help and tissue T-cell infiltration. Answer <b>Goodpasture's</b>.","o":{"A":"Type 1 diabetes","B":"Multiple sclerosis","C":"Rheumatoid arthritis","D":"SLE","E":"Goodpasture's syndrome"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Type 1 diabetes is a textbook <b>CD8⁺ CTL</b> disease — cytotoxic T cells destroy pancreatic β cells.","B":"Multiple sclerosis involves both CD4⁺ Th1/Th17 and CD8⁺ T cells attacking myelin.","C":"Rheumatoid arthritis is largely CD4⁺ Th1/Th17-driven with a substantial B-cell/autoantibody component, but T cells are central.","D":"SLE is predominantly antibody/immune-complex driven, though T-cell help is essential — the senior student's note records genuine uncertainty between SLE and Goodpasture's here.","E":"Correct per the key — <b>Goodpasture's syndrome</b> is purely <b>antibody-mediated (type II)</b>: anti-GBM IgG binds <b>type IV collagen</b> in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, activating complement and recruiting neutrophils. There is no significant cytotoxic T-cell component."},"f":["keydiff"],"img":"slides/L15P2_12.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 57","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 57","t":"Least relevant to controlling autoimmunity","s":"Which is <b>least</b> related to the control of autoimmune disease?","lec":"L19","sl":57,"c":"<b>Symptomatic vs disease-modifying</b> is the distinction being tested. <b>Symptomatic</b>: NSAIDs, analgesics. <b>Disease-modifying</b>: corticosteroids, conventional DMARDs (<b>methotrexate</b>, sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine), biologics (<b>anti-TNF</b>, anti-IL-6R, <b>rituximab</b>, <b>abatacept/CTLA-4-Ig</b>), and JAK inhibitors. Only the second group changes the natural history of the disease.","o":{"A":"Anti-TCR therapy","B":"Anti-BCR therapy","C":"Anti-TNF therapy","D":"NSAID","E":"Corticosteroid"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Targeting the T-cell receptor interferes directly with autoreactive T-cell activation — a disease-modifying strategy.","B":"Targeting B cells (in practice <b>anti-CD20, rituximab</b>) depletes autoantibody-producing B cells and is genuinely disease-modifying in RA and vasculitis.","C":"<b>Anti-TNF</b> biologics are among the most effective disease-modifying treatments in RA and inflammatory bowel disease.","D":"Correct — <b>NSAIDs</b> inhibit cyclo-oxygenase and relieve <b>pain, fever and inflammation symptomatically</b>. They do <b>not</b> modify the underlying autoimmune process, do not prevent joint erosion, and are therefore the option least related to <i>controlling</i> autoimmunity.","E":"Corticosteroids are broadly immunosuppressive — they inhibit NF-κB, cytokine transcription and lymphocyte function — and genuinely suppress autoimmune activity."},"img":"slides/L19_57.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 58","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 58","t":"Hashimoto's and Graves' — shared feature","s":"What is a <b>similarity</b> between Hashimoto's thyroiditis and Graves' disease?","lec":"L13B","sl":19,"c":"<b>Autoimmune thyroid disease — the comparison table.</b> <b>Graves'</b>: <b>stimulating</b> anti-TSHR antibody · hyperthyroid · <b>TSH low, T3/T4 high</b> · exophthalmos, pretibial myxoedema · heat intolerance, weight loss, tremor · type II mechanism III. <b>Hashimoto's</b>: <b>anti-TPO, anti-thyroglobulin</b> · hypothyroid · <b>TSH high, T4 low</b> · lymphocytic infiltration with germinal centres, CTL destruction · cold intolerance, weight gain, fatigue. <b>Shared: goitre, female predominance, autoimmune, organ-specific.</b>","o":{"A":"High T3","B":"High T4","C":"Heat intolerance","D":"High TSH","E":"Thyroid gland enlargement"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"T3 is <b>high</b> in Graves' but <b>low</b> in Hashimoto's.","B":"T4 likewise: high in Graves', low in Hashimoto's.","C":"Heat intolerance is a hyperthyroid symptom (Graves'); Hashimoto's patients have <b>cold</b> intolerance.","D":"TSH is <b>suppressed</b> in Graves' by negative feedback and <b>elevated</b> in Hashimoto's as the pituitary tries to stimulate a failing gland.","E":"Correct — <b>both cause thyroid enlargement (goitre)</b>. In Hashimoto's the goitre results from lymphocytic infiltration and fibrosis; in Graves' from antibody-driven stimulation and hyperplasia. Both are also <b>organ-specific autoimmune thyroid diseases</b> occurring predominantly in women."},"also":"BM31 Q22, BM30 Q33","img":"slides/L13B_19.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 13f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 13f","t":"Characteristics of PD-1","s":"Which is a characteristic of <b>programmed death protein 1 (PD-1)</b>?","lec":"L18","sl":24,"c":"<b>Adaptive immune resistance</b> — the concept behind this item and worth knowing by name: T cells infiltrate the tumour and release IFN-γ; IFN-γ <b>induces PD-L1</b> on the tumour; PD-L1 then engages PD-1 and shuts those very T cells down. The tumour uses the immune attack to build its own defence. Blocking PD-1 or PD-L1 breaks the loop — which is why PD-L1 expression is used as a predictive biomarker.","o":{"A":"Limits T-cell responses primarily in lymphoid tissue","B":"Expressed by activated T cells only","C":"Its ligand can be inducibly expressed on tumour cells","D":"Activates the immune response","E":"Controls regulatory T cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The key notes this is wrong: PD-1 acts mainly in <b>peripheral tissues</b> and the tumour microenvironment. It is <b>CTLA-4</b> that works primarily in lymphoid organs during priming.","B":"PD-1 is expressed on activated T cells but <b>not only</b> on them — it also appears on B cells, NK cells and myeloid cells.","C":"Correct — <b>PD-L1</b>, the ligand of PD-1, is <b>inducibly expressed on tumour cells</b> (largely in response to IFN-γ from infiltrating T cells). By displaying PD-L1 the tumour switches off attacking T cells — the mechanism that anti-PD-1/anti-PD-L1 antibodies reverse.","D":"PD-1 is <b>inhibitory</b> — it attenuates TCR signalling, the opposite of activating.","E":"PD-1 acts on effector T cells; Treg control is more the province of CTLA-4 and IL-2."},"also":"BM32 Q53, BM30 Q20","img":"slides/L18_24.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59m] 1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59m] 1","t":"Tumour immune evasion mechanisms","s":"Which of the following represents <b>tumour immune evasion</b>?","lec":"L12","sl":31,"c":"⚠ The original key accepts <b>options 2, 4 and 5</b> (FasL, decreased ICAM-1, increased TGF-β) — this was a multiple-response item. All three appear on the BM33 L12 evasion slides. Use it as revision of the full list rather than a single-answer question: <b>low immunogenicity</b> (↓antigen, ↓MHC I, ↓adhesion molecules, ↑anti-adhesion molecules), <b>tumour-induced suppression</b> (CTLA-4/PD-L1, <b>FasL</b>, <b>TGF-β</b>, IL-10), <b>host subversion</b> (M2 macrophages, Tregs, MDSCs).","o":{"A":"Expression of MHC class I","B":"Expression of FasL","C":"Increased mucin production","D":"Decreased ICAM-1 expression","E":"Increased TGF-β secretion"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Expressing MHC class I makes the tumour <b>more</b> visible to CD8⁺ CTLs; it is the <b>loss</b> of MHC class I that constitutes evasion (and that in turn exposes the tumour to NK cells).","B":"Correct — tumours express <b>FasL</b>, which engages <b>Fas on infiltrating T cells</b> and drives them into apoptosis: the 'tumour counterattack'. The key marks options B, D and E as all being valid evasion mechanisms; FasL is the clearest single answer.","C":"Mucin changes relate to the tumour surface but are not one of the deck's named evasion mechanisms.","D":"Also a genuine evasion mechanism — <b>decreased adhesion molecules (ICAM-1, VCAM)</b> prevent effective immune-cell contact with the tumour. The key includes it.","E":"Also genuine — <b>TGF-β</b> secretion suppresses T-cell activation and expands Tregs. The key includes it too."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM29 Q10, BM28 Q36","img":"slides/L12_31.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59m] 19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59m] 19","t":"Characteristic of malignancy","s":"What characterises a <b>malignant</b> tumour?","lec":"L12","sl":6,"c":"<b>Benign vs malignant</b>, per the L12 deck: a benign tumour <b>is not capable of indefinite growth and the host survives</b>; a malignant tumour <b>continues to grow indefinitely and spreads (metastasis)</b>. Normal cells differ from tumour cells in growth and death control, appearance, function, <b>invasion</b> and <b>metastasis</b>. Invasion and metastasis are the two that define malignancy.","o":{"A":"Invasiveness","B":"Dysplasia","C":"Necrosis","D":"Atypia","E":"Increased mitotic figures"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the defining property of malignancy is the capacity to <b>invade</b> surrounding tissue and to <b>metastasise</b>. A benign tumour grows locally and remains encapsulated; a malignant one breaches the basement membrane and spreads.","B":"Dysplasia is disordered growth and maturation — a <b>pre</b>-malignant change that may or may not progress.","C":"Necrosis is common in rapidly growing tumours that outgrow their blood supply, but it is neither specific nor defining.","D":"Atypia (abnormal cellular morphology) is a cytological feature of malignancy but also occurs in reactive and dysplastic conditions.","E":"Increased mitoses suggest rapid proliferation but occur in benign proliferative lesions too."},"img":"slides/L12_6.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59m] 38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59m] 38","t":"Tumour marker for germ cell tumours","s":"What is the tumour marker of a <b>germ cell tumour</b>?","lec":"L12","sl":12,"c":"<b>Oncofetal antigens</b> — normally expressed in fetal life, re-expressed by tumours: <b>AFP</b> (hepatocellular carcinoma, germ cell tumours) and <b>CEA</b> (colorectal, gastric, pancreatic, lung). Both are <b>tumour-associated</b>, not tumour-specific, so they are used for <b>monitoring and follow-up</b> rather than screening.","o":{"A":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)","B":"CEA","C":"CA 19-9","D":"PSA","E":"CA 15-3"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>AFP</b> is an <b>oncofetal antigen</b> and the marker for <b>germ cell tumours</b> (yolk sac tumour, embryonal carcinoma) as well as <b>hepatocellular carcinoma</b>. In testicular tumours it is measured with <b>β-hCG</b> and LDH.","B":"CEA marks colorectal carcinoma.","C":"CA 19-9 marks pancreatic and biliary carcinoma.","D":"PSA marks prostate carcinoma.","E":"CA 15-3 marks breast carcinoma."},"also":"BM28 Q37, BM28 Q38","img":"slides/L12_12.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 62","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 62","t":"Most common side effect of high-dose IL-2","s":"A 42-year-old woman with metastatic melanoma receives immunotherapy with <b>high-dose IL-2</b>. What is the <b>most common</b> side effect?","lec":"L18","sl":5,"c":"⚠ <b>The paired trap.</b> AX [60] 62 asks for the <b>most common</b> side effect → <b>flu-like syndrome</b>. AX [59m] 5 asks the same clinical stem for the <b>serious</b> side effect → <b>capillary leak syndrome</b>. Same patient, same drug, different adjective, different answer. Always read whether the question wants <i>commonest</i> or <i>most severe</i>.","o":{"A":"Hand-foot syndrome","B":"Injection site reaction","C":"Flu-like syndrome","D":"Capillary leak syndrome","E":"Hypothyroidism"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Hand-foot syndrome is characteristic of certain chemotherapies and tyrosine kinase inhibitors (capecitabine, sorafenib).","B":"Injection site reactions occur but are not the dominant toxicity of systemic high-dose IL-2.","C":"Correct — the <b>most common</b> adverse effect of cytokine therapy is a <b>flu-like syndrome</b>: fever, chills, myalgia, fatigue, headache.","D":"<b>Capillary leak syndrome</b> is the <b>most serious</b> toxicity of high-dose IL-2 — hypotension, oedema and shock — but it is far less common than the flu-like syndrome. Read the adjective in the stem.","E":"Hypothyroidism is a characteristic <b>irAE of checkpoint inhibitors</b>, not of IL-2."},"also":"AX [59m] 5, BM32 Q52","img":"slides/L18_5.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59m] 5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59m] 5","t":"Most serious side effect of high-dose IL-2","s":"A 42-year-old woman with metastatic melanoma receives immunotherapy with <b>high-dose IL-2</b>. What is the <b>serious</b> side effect?","lec":"L18","sl":5,"c":"<b>High-dose IL-2</b> defined the <b>cytokine era</b> of cancer immunotherapy: durable complete remissions in a small subset of <b>metastatic melanoma and renal cell carcinoma</b> patients, at the cost of severe toxicity requiring intensive-care-level monitoring. It has largely been displaced by <b>checkpoint inhibitors</b>, which are far better tolerated and benefit more patients.","o":{"A":"Hand-foot syndrome","B":"Capillary leak syndrome","C":"Injection site reaction","D":"Flu-like syndrome","E":"Alopecia"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Hand-foot syndrome belongs to capecitabine and some kinase inhibitors.","B":"Correct — as the key stresses, read the word 'serious': <b>capillary leak syndrome</b> is the life-threatening toxicity of high-dose IL-2. Endothelial injury causes fluid to leak from the vasculature into tissues → hypotension, pulmonary oedema, oliguria and <b>shock</b>, which can be fatal.","C":"Injection site reactions are minor.","D":"Flu-like syndrome is the <b>commonest</b> effect, but it is not serious — this is the paired distractor.","E":"Alopecia is a chemotherapy effect, not an IL-2 effect."},"also":"AX [60] 62, BM32 Q52","img":"slides/L18_5.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 59","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 59","t":"Immunotherapy for refractory prostate cancer","s":"A 55-year-old man is diagnosed with asymptomatic refractory <b>prostate cancer</b>. Which is the best treatment?","lec":"L18","sl":7,"c":"<b>Classification of cancer immunotherapy — sort every option before answering.</b> <b>Active non-specific</b>: BCG, cytokines (IL-2, IFN-α). <b>Active specific</b>: tumour vaccines, <b>dendritic cell vaccines</b>, personalised <b>mRNA neoantigen</b> vaccines. <b>Passive specific</b>: monoclonal antibodies (trastuzumab, rituximab, cetuximab). <b>Adoptive cellular ('living drugs')</b>: <b>CAR-T</b>, TILs. <b>Checkpoint blockade</b>: anti-CTLA-4, anti-PD-1/PD-L1.","o":{"A":"IL-2","B":"IFN-α","C":"BCG vaccine","D":"Dendritic cell vaccine","E":"Anti-HER2 monoclonal antibody"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The key notes IL-2 is <b>non-specific</b> cytokine immunotherapy, now little used because of its toxicity (flu-like syndrome, capillary leak).","B":"IFN-α is likewise non-specific cytokine therapy with the same drawbacks.","C":"The key states BCG is used for <b>cutaneous melanoma and superficial (non-muscle-invasive) bladder cancer</b> — not prostate cancer.","D":"Correct — a <b>dendritic cell vaccine</b> is the answer: <b>sipuleucel-T</b>, an autologous dendritic-cell vaccine loaded with prostatic acid phosphatase, is licensed for asymptomatic metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer. This is <b>specific active</b> immunotherapy.","E":"The key notes anti-HER2 (trastuzumab) is for <b>breast</b> cancer."},"also":"BM32 Q49, BM32 Q50","img":"slides/L18_7.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 56","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 56","t":"Mechanism of immune checkpoint inhibitors","s":"What is correct about the mechanism of an <b>immune checkpoint inhibitor</b> drug?","lec":"L18","sl":25,"c":"⚠ The AX key's original wording ('MAb that targets cancer cells with high CTLA-4 expression') is <b>mechanistically wrong</b> and the BM33 L18 deck contradicts it: <b>CTLA-4 is expressed on T cells, not on tumour cells</b>. The antibody acts on the <b>T cell</b> (or on tumour <b>PD-L1</b>), not on tumour CTLA-4. This item is corrected here rather than reproduced with its error.","o":{"A":"A monoclonal antibody that down-regulates T-cell function","B":"A monoclonal antibody that targets cancer cells with high mutational burden","C":"A monoclonal antibody that blocks CTLA-4 or PD-1/PD-L1 signalling","D":"A cytokine that blocks immune cell function","E":"A cytokine targeting cancer cells with oncogenic viral infection"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Backwards — checkpoint inhibitors <b>up</b>-regulate T-cell function by removing inhibition.","B":"High mutational burden <b>predicts</b> response (more neoantigens), but it is not the target of the antibody.","C":"Correct — checkpoint inhibitors are <b>monoclonal antibodies that block inhibitory receptor–ligand pairs</b>: <b>anti-CTLA-4</b> (ipilimumab) and <b>anti-PD-1/anti-PD-L1</b> (nivolumab, pembrolizumab, atezolizumab). Blocking the checkpoint releases the brake so tumour-specific T cells stay active.","D":"They are antibodies, not cytokines, and they enhance rather than block immune function.","E":"Also incorrect on both counts."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM32 Q51, BM32 Q53","img":"slides/L18_25.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 6f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 6f","t":"Definition of allograft","s":"Tissue transferred between <b>genetically different members of the same species</b> is called which of the following?","lec":"L14","sl":7,"c":"The four graft types, by genetic relationship: <b>Auto</b> (self) → no rejection · <b>Iso/syn</b> (identical twin) → no rejection · <b>Allo</b> (same species, different genotype) → rejected, needs HLA matching + immunosuppression · <b>Xeno</b> (different species) → violently rejected, the hardest problem in transplantation.","o":{"A":"Autograft","B":"Isograft","C":"Allograft","D":"Xenograft","E":"Paragraft"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"An autograft moves tissue within the <b>same individual</b> (skin graft, CABG vein graft).","B":"An isograft is between <b>genetically identical</b> individuals — identical twins.","C":"Correct — an <b>allograft</b> is between <b>genetically different individuals of the same species</b>. This is the standard clinical transplant, and it is rejected unless HLA-matched and immunosuppressed.","D":"A xenograft crosses <b>species</b> boundaries.","E":"'Paragraft' is not a real term."},"also":"BM32 Q31, BM31 Q35, BM30 Q35, BM28 Q40","img":"slides/L14_7.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f,60] 18f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f,60] 18f","t":"Mechanism of cyclosporine","s":"A 35-year-old woman receives <b>cyclosporine</b> after a kidney transplant. What is the mechanism of this drug?","lec":"L14","sl":44,"c":"<b>Immunosuppressive drug classes for transplantation — know one mechanism each.</b> <b>Calcineurin inhibitors</b>: <b>cyclosporine</b> (binds cyclophilin), <b>tacrolimus/FK506</b> (binds FKBP) → block <b>NFAT → IL-2</b>. <b>mTOR inhibitors</b>: sirolimus → block IL-2-driven proliferation. <b>Antimetabolites</b>: <b>mycophenolate mofetil</b> (blocks IMPDH → purine synthesis in lymphocytes), azathioprine, methotrexate. <b>Anti-lymphocyte antibodies</b>: ATG, anti-CD3, anti-CD25. <b>Costimulatory blockade</b>: <b>CTLA-4-Ig (belatacept)</b>. <b>Anti-inflammatory</b>: corticosteroids. <b>Anti-B cell/antibody</b>: rituximab, IVIG, plasmapheresis.","o":{"A":"Reduces plasma cell secretion of IgG antibodies","B":"Inhibits macrophage release of IFN-γ","C":"Induces involution of the thymus","D":"Destroys stem cells in bone marrow","E":"Inhibits T-cell signalling pathways"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Cyclosporine's target is the T cell; effects on antibody production are indirect, through loss of T-cell help.","B":"IFN-γ is produced principally by <b>T cells and NK cells</b>, not macrophages, and cyclosporine acts upstream on T-cell signalling.","C":"Cyclosporine does not act on the thymus.","D":"Bone marrow stem cell destruction describes cytotoxic conditioning regimens, not cyclosporine.","E":"Correct — <b>cyclosporine is a calcineurin inhibitor</b>. It binds cyclophilin; the complex inhibits <b>calcineurin</b>, preventing dephosphorylation of <b>NFAT</b> and thus blocking transcription of <b>IL-2</b> and other T-cell activation genes. Without IL-2, T cells cannot proliferate — so the graft is protected."},"also":"AX [57f,59f] 7fง","img":"slides/L14_44.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f,59f] 7fง","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f,59f] 7fง","t":"Molecular target of cyclosporine","s":"A 30-year-old man receives a liver transplant and is prescribed <b>cyclosporine</b>. What is the molecular target of this drug?","lec":"L14","sl":44,"c":"<b>Cyclosporine and tacrolimus reach the same endpoint by different immunophilins:</b> cyclosporine + <b>cyclophilin</b>, tacrolimus + <b>FKBP-12</b>, both inhibiting <b>calcineurin → NFAT → IL-2</b>. Key toxicities to remember: <b>nephrotoxicity</b> (both), hypertension, gum hypertrophy and hirsutism (cyclosporine), neurotoxicity and new-onset diabetes (tacrolimus).","o":{"A":"Calcineurin","B":"mTOR","C":"IMPDH (inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase)","D":"Dihydrofolate reductase","E":"CD20"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — cyclosporine binds <b>cyclophilin</b>, and the drug–immunophilin complex inhibits <b>calcineurin</b>, the phosphatase that activates <b>NFAT</b>. Blocking it prevents IL-2 gene transcription and therefore T-cell proliferation.","B":"<b>mTOR</b> is the target of <b>sirolimus (rapamycin)</b> and everolimus.","C":"<b>IMPDH</b> is the target of <b>mycophenolate mofetil</b>, blocking de novo purine synthesis on which lymphocytes uniquely depend.","D":"Dihydrofolate reductase is the target of <b>methotrexate</b>.","E":"CD20 is the target of <b>rituximab</b>, which depletes B cells."},"also":"AX [59f,60] 18f","img":"slides/L14_44.webp"},{"id":"AX|[59f] 6fง","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[59f] 6fง","t":"Mycophenolate mofetil target","s":"Which cells are affected by <b>mycophenolate mofetil</b>?","lec":"L14","sl":45,"c":"⚠ The AX annotation questions whether the answer should be 'T cells only'. The accurate answer is <b>lymphocytes — both T and B</b>, which is exactly why mycophenolate suppresses cellular and antibody-mediated rejection. Its selectivity is elegant: other cells can recycle purines via the salvage pathway, but lymphocytes cannot.","o":{"A":"Lymphocytes","B":"Neutrophils only","C":"Erythrocytes","D":"Hepatocytes","E":"Platelets"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — mycophenolate mofetil inhibits <b>inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH)</b>, blocking <b>de novo purine synthesis</b>. <b>Lymphocytes (both T and B)</b> are uniquely dependent on the de novo pathway because they lack an effective salvage pathway, so the drug selectively suppresses proliferating lymphocytes.","B":"Neutrophils are affected only as part of general marrow suppression at higher doses; they are not the intended target.","C":"Erythrocytes are anucleate and do not proliferate.","D":"Hepatocytes are not the target (though hepatotoxicity can occur).","E":"Platelets are affected only through marrow toxicity."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"AX [59f,60] 18f","img":"slides/L14_45.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 63","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 63","t":"CTLA-4-Ig after kidney transplant","s":"Why is <b>CTLA-4-Ig</b> given after kidney transplantation?","lec":"L14","sl":47,"c":"<b>CTLA-4-Ig exploits natural biology.</b> CTLA-4 normally out-competes CD28 for B7 because it binds with far higher affinity; the drug is that binding domain fused to an immunoglobulin Fc. Note the mirror image once more: in <b>transplantation you give CTLA-4-Ig to suppress</b>; in <b>cancer you give anti-CTLA-4 to activate</b>. Same molecule, opposite therapeutic intent.","o":{"A":"To increase thymic involution","B":"To kill bone marrow cells","C":"To block the co-stimulatory pathway","D":"To neutralise pre-formed antibodies","E":"To deplete circulating B cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"CTLA-4-Ig has no effect on the thymus.","B":"It does not kill marrow cells — that is the province of cytotoxic conditioning.","C":"Correct — <b>CTLA-4-Ig (abatacept/belatacept)</b> is a soluble fusion protein that binds <b>B7 (CD80/CD86)</b> on antigen-presenting cells, preventing them from engaging <b>CD28</b> on T cells. The alloreactive T cell then gets <b>signal 1 without signal 2</b> and becomes <b>anergic</b> — this is <b>costimulatory blockade</b>.","D":"Neutralising pre-formed antibodies is the role of <b>IVIG and plasmapheresis</b>.","E":"B-cell depletion is achieved with <b>rituximab</b> (anti-CD20)."},"also":"BM31 Q19, BM30 Q20","img":"slides/L14_47.webp"},{"id":"AX|[58,60] 11f","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58,60] 11f","t":"Rabies immunoglobulin","s":"A 7-year-old boy comes to hospital with a dog bite wound. The doctor gives him <b>rabies immunoglobulin</b>. What kind of immunisation is this?","lec":"L17","sl":6,"c":"<b>The four-box grid, with an example each:</b> <b>Natural active</b> = infection. <b>Artificial active</b> = vaccination. <b>Natural passive</b> = maternal IgG (placenta) / IgA (breast milk). <b>Artificial passive</b> = immunoglobulin, antivenom, monoclonal antibodies. <b>Rabies post-exposure prophylaxis uses both at once</b>: immunoglobulin infiltrated around the wound for immediate cover, plus vaccine for lasting active immunity — a favourite exam scenario precisely because it combines the two.","o":{"A":"Artificially acquired active immunity","B":"Artificially acquired passive immunity","C":"Naturally acquired active immunity","D":"Naturally acquired passive immunity","E":"All of the above"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Active immunity requires the recipient to <b>make</b> their own response to an antigen — that is the <b>rabies vaccine</b>, which is given alongside.","B":"Correct — rabies immunoglobulin is <b>pre-formed antibody</b> given by a medical intervention, so it is <b>artificially acquired passive immunity</b>: immediate protection, no memory, short duration.","C":"Naturally acquired active immunity comes from surviving an infection.","D":"Naturally acquired passive immunity is <b>maternal IgG across the placenta</b> or IgA in breast milk.","E":"Only one category applies."},"also":"BM28 Q69, BM28 Q70, BM32 Q39","img":"slides/L17_6.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 12","t":"Artificial active immunity","s":"Which is an example of <b>artificial active immunity</b>?","lec":"L17","sl":5,"c":"Two questions resolve every item in this family: <b>(1) Did the person receive ANTIGEN or ANTIBODY?</b> Antigen → active (memory, slow onset, long-lasting). Antibody → passive (no memory, immediate, short-lived). <b>(2) Did it happen naturally or by medical intervention?</b> Natural → infection or maternal transfer. Artificial → vaccine or immunoglobulin.","o":{"A":"Administration of a killed vaccine","B":"Transfer of maternal IgG across the placenta","C":"Administration of antivenom","D":"Recovery from natural measles infection","E":"IgA received in breast milk"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — giving a <b>killed (or any) vaccine</b> is a deliberate medical intervention (<b>artificial</b>) that supplies <b>antigen</b>, prompting the recipient's own immune system to respond and form memory (<b>active</b>).","B":"Placental IgG transfer is <b>natural passive</b>.","C":"Antivenom is pre-formed antibody given medically = <b>artificial passive</b>.","D":"Recovering from infection is <b>natural active</b>.","E":"Breast-milk IgA is <b>natural passive</b>."},"also":"BM28 Q69","img":"slides/L17_5.webp"},{"id":"AX|[57f] 13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57f] 13","t":"Longest-lasting immunity","s":"Which type of immunity is <b>long-lasting</b>?","lec":"L17","sl":5,"c":"<b>Memory is the whole point.</b> Only <b>active</b> immunisation engages the recipient's lymphocytes and generates <b>memory B and T cells</b> — hence a faster, larger, higher-affinity, class-switched secondary response on re-exposure. Passive immunity is borrowed antibody: instant, but it decays with the half-life of IgG (~3 weeks) and leaves nothing behind.","o":{"A":"Natural active","B":"Natural passive","C":"Artificial passive","D":"Both passive types equally","E":"None are long-lasting"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — as the key notes, <b>natural and artificial <u>active</u> immunity both generate memory cells</b>, and natural active immunity (recovery from infection) typically produces the strongest and most durable response, often lifelong.","B":"Natural passive immunity (maternal IgG) wanes over the first <b>6 months</b> of life as transferred antibody is catabolised.","C":"Artificial passive immunity (immunoglobulin, antivenom) lasts only <b>weeks</b>.","D":"Neither passive form is long-lasting, because no memory cells are made.","E":"Active immunity is genuinely long-lasting, so this is wrong."},"also":"BM28 Q69, AX [57f] 12","img":"slides/L17_5.webp"},{"id":"AX|[60] 66","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[60] 66","t":"Choosing a vaccine for future travel","s":"A student's cousin will travel in <b>one year's time</b> to a country where hepatitis A is endemic. Two options exist: (1) a killed microbe vaccine, or (2) serum globulin containing antibody to hepatitis A. Which should be recommended, and why?","lec":"L17","sl":5,"c":"<b>Timing decides between active and passive.</b> <b>Plenty of time before exposure</b> → <b>active</b> immunisation (vaccine): slow to develop but durable, with memory. <b>Exposure imminent or already happened</b> → <b>passive</b> (immunoglobulin): immediate but temporary. <b>Post-exposure prophylaxis often uses both</b> — rabies, hepatitis B and tetanus are the standard examples.","o":{"A":"Killed microbe, because it produces several types of antibody","B":"Killed microbe, because it gives long-lasting immunity","C":"Serum globulin, because it gives short-term immunity","D":"Serum globulin, because it gives long-lasting immunity","E":"Neither, because hepatitis A is treatable"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The number of antibody specificities is not the deciding factor.","B":"Correct — as the key reasons, she is travelling in <b>a year's time</b>, so there is no urgency. Choose <b>active immunisation with the killed vaccine</b>, which generates <b>memory cells and long-lasting protection</b>. Serum globulin would have worn off long before she travels.","C":"Serum globulin does indeed give only short-term protection — which is exactly why it is the <b>wrong</b> choice here. It is reserved for imminent or already-occurred exposure.","D":"Serum globulin gives <b>no</b> memory and therefore no long-lasting immunity — the statement is false.","E":"Prevention is preferable, and hepatitis A can cause significant illness."},"also":"BM28 Q69","img":"slides/L17_5.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L10-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L10-1","t":"Serous vs fibrinous inflammation","s":"A 60-year-old man with uraemia develops sharp chest pain and a friction rub. At autopsy the pericardial surface is covered in a shaggy, dull grey exudate that pulls apart in strands. Which morphological pattern of inflammation is this?","lec":"L10","sl":22,"c":"<b>The five morphological patterns of acute inflammation</b> — a favourite source of one-liners: <b>serous</b> (protein-poor fluid: blister, mild effusion) · <b>fibrinous</b> (fibrinogen leakage onto serosa: <b>pericarditis, pleuritis</b>) · <b>purulent/suppurative</b> (pus, abscess: pyogenic bacteria) · <b>ulcerative</b> (surface defect: peptic ulcer, oral ulcer) · <b>pseudomembranous</b> (<i>C. difficile</i>, diphtheria).","o":{"A":"Serous inflammation","B":"Fibrinous inflammation","C":"Purulent inflammation","D":"Ulcerative inflammation","E":"Granulomatous inflammation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Serous inflammation is a <b>protein-poor, watery</b> effusion — a skin blister or a mild pleural effusion. It would not form strands.","B":"Correct — a <b>shaggy, strand-forming exudate on a serosal surface</b> is <b>fibrinous inflammation</b>. Greater vascular permeability lets large molecules including <b>fibrinogen</b> escape; it polymerises to fibrin. Uraemic pericarditis ('bread-and-butter' pericarditis) is the classic example, along with fibrinous pleuritis.","C":"Purulent inflammation means <b>pus</b> — neutrophils, liquefied necrotic cells and fluid — typically from pyogenic bacteria.","D":"Ulceration is a surface defect from shedding necrotic tissue, seen on mucosa and skin, not a serosal exudate.","E":"Granulomatous inflammation is chronic, with epithelioid macrophages and giant cells."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L10_22.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L10-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L10-2","t":"Tissue proliferative capacity","s":"A patient survives a large anterior myocardial infarction. Six months later, echocardiography shows a thinned, akinetic anterior wall. Why did the heart form a scar instead of regenerating?","lec":"L10","sl":55,"c":"<b>Three tissue types by proliferative activity:</b> <b>Labile</b> — continuously dividing, high stem-cell activity (epidermis, GI and urinary epithelium, bone marrow) → full regeneration. <b>Stable</b> — quiescent in G₀ but can re-enter the cycle on demand (liver, kidney, pancreas, fibroblasts, endothelium) → regeneration if the framework survives; the liver is the standout. <b>Permanent</b> — terminally differentiated, no division (<b>neurons, cardiac muscle, skeletal muscle</b>) → <b>scar only</b>.","o":{"A":"Cardiac myocytes are permanent cells with no proliferative capacity","B":"Cardiac myocytes are labile cells that were exhausted by the infarct","C":"The infarct destroyed the basement membrane scaffold only","D":"Fibroblast proliferation actively suppressed myocyte regeneration","E":"VEGF was not released after the infarct"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>cardiac myocytes are permanent (non-dividing) cells</b>, terminally differentiated with essentially no proliferative capacity. When they die, the tissue cannot regenerate, so healing occurs by <b>replacement with connective tissue — a scar</b>. Neurons and skeletal muscle behave the same way.","B":"Labile cells divide continuously (surface epithelium, gut mucosa, bone marrow) — the opposite of cardiac muscle.","C":"An intact basement membrane scaffold matters for regeneration in labile and stable tissues, but the decisive factor here is that myocytes cannot divide at all.","D":"Fibroblasts fill the defect because regeneration is impossible; they do not suppress it.","E":"VEGF is released and angiogenesis does occur — new vessels are part of the granulation tissue that becomes the scar."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L10_55.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L10-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L10-3","t":"Inflammasome and IL-1β","s":"A child has recurrent unexplained fevers, urticarial rash and raised inflammatory markers from infancy, with no infection found. Genetic testing shows a gain-of-function mutation in NLRP3. Which mediator is most directly responsible for his fever?","lec":"L10","sl":8,"c":"<b>Pattern recognition receptors of the innate system:</b> <b>TLRs</b> (bacterial and viral products, at the cell surface and in endosomes) · <b>NLRs → inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1β</b> (responding to cytosolic danger signals: uric acid crystals in gout, cholesterol crystals, ATP, potassium efflux) · <b>CLRs</b> (fungal β-glucans — Dectin-1) · <b>RLRs</b> (cytosolic viral RNA). The IL-1 pathway is druggable: <b>anakinra, canakinumab</b>.","o":{"A":"IL-1β","B":"Histamine","C":"Bradykinin","D":"C5a","E":"Leukotriene B₄"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>NLRP3</b> is the sensor of the <b>inflammasome</b>, the multiprotein complex that activates <b>caspase-1</b>, which cleaves pro-IL-1β into active <b>IL-1β</b>. A gain-of-function mutation causes constitutive IL-1β release → recurrent fever and urticarial rash without infection (a cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome). IL-1β, with TNF and IL-6, is an <b>endogenous pyrogen</b> acting via hypothalamic COX-2 → PGE₂.","B":"Histamine causes vasodilation, permeability and itch, but it is not pyrogenic.","C":"Bradykinin causes pain and vasodilation.","D":"C5a is an anaphylatoxin and a potent neutrophil chemoattractant.","E":"LTB₄ is chemotactic for neutrophils, not pyrogenic."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L10_8.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L10-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L10-4","t":"TGF-β and pathological fibrosis","s":"A patient with chronic hepatitis C develops cirrhosis. Which cytokine is the principal driver of the fibrosis, and which cell does it act on?","lec":"L10","sl":56,"c":"<b>Fibrosis = chronic inflammation + TGF-β + a persistent stimulus.</b> The same mechanism runs across organs: liver (stellate cells → cirrhosis), lung (fibroblasts → pulmonary fibrosis), kidney (→ chronic allograft nephropathy), heart (→ post-MI scar), skin (→ keloid, systemic sclerosis). Balance is set by <b>collagen synthesis (TGF-β) versus degradation (MMPs, opposed by TIMPs)</b>.","o":{"A":"TGF-β acting on hepatic stellate cells","B":"VEGF acting on endothelial cells","C":"IL-1β acting on hepatocytes","D":"IFN-γ acting on Kupffer cells","E":"PDGF acting on cholangiocytes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>TGF-β is the master fibrogenic cytokine</b>. In the liver it activates <b>hepatic stellate cells</b>, converting them into collagen-producing myofibroblasts. Persistent injury means persistent TGF-β, so collagen deposition outpaces degradation and cirrhosis follows.","B":"VEGF drives angiogenesis; new vessels accompany fibrosis but do not cause it.","C":"IL-1β is pro-inflammatory rather than fibrogenic.","D":"IFN-γ activates macrophages and is in fact <b>anti</b>-fibrotic, opposing TGF-β.","E":"PDGF does recruit and activate myofibroblasts and contributes, but TGF-β is the principal driver, and the target cell in the liver is the stellate cell."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L10_56.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L10-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L10-5","t":"Outcome of acute inflammation","s":"A patient has lobar pneumonia. After appropriate antibiotics the alveolar exudate is cleared and lung architecture returns to normal on imaging. Which outcome of acute inflammation is this, and why was it possible here but not after a lung abscess?","lec":"L10","sl":19,"c":"<b>Four outcomes of acute inflammation:</b> <b>Resolution</b> (limited injury, intact framework, debris cleared → normal) · <b>Fibrosis/scarring</b> (substantial destruction, non-regenerable tissue, or persistent exudate → organisation) · <b>Abscess formation</b> (walled-off pus, pyogenic organisms) · <b>Progression to chronic inflammation</b> (persistent stimulus). Whether the <b>stromal framework survives</b> is usually the deciding variable.","o":{"A":"Resolution, because the alveolar tissue framework remained intact","B":"Organisation, because granulation tissue replaced the exudate","C":"Progression to chronic inflammation, because macrophages persisted","D":"Regeneration, because alveolar cells are permanent cells","E":"Scarring, because collagen replaced the exudate"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>resolution</b> (complete restoration to normal) requires that the injury be limited and short-lived, that the exudate and debris be cleared by macrophages and lymphatics, and crucially that the <b>tissue framework — the basement membrane and stroma — survive</b>. Lobar pneumonia typically spares the alveolar walls, so the lung returns to normal. A lung <b>abscess</b> destroys the framework, so healing must occur by <b>fibrosis and scarring</b>.","B":"Organisation is exactly what happens when the exudate is <b>not</b> cleared — it is replaced by granulation tissue and then scar.","C":"Chronic inflammation would mean persisting infiltrate, not restored architecture.","D":"Alveolar type II pneumocytes are <b>stable</b> cells that can regenerate; the term 'permanent' is wrong, and resolution is the better description.","E":"Scarring is the opposite of what the imaging shows."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L10_19.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L11-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L11-1","t":"Paneth cells","s":"A 32-year-old man has a small-bowel biopsy showing specialised epithelial cells at the base of the intestinal crypts containing prominent eosinophilic granules. What is the principal function of these cells?","lec":"L11","sl":14,"c":"<b>Four specialised intestinal epithelial cells — one function each:</b> <b>M cells</b> → antigen sampling and transcytosis (over Peyer's patches) · <b>Goblet cells</b> → mucus · <b>Paneth cells</b> → antimicrobial peptides, at the <b>crypt base</b> · <b>Enterocytes</b> → barrier, absorption and <b>pIgR-mediated IgA transport</b>. This is asked directly as a QUIZ slide in the BM33 L11 deck.","o":{"A":"Secretion of antimicrobial peptides","B":"Transcytosis of luminal antigen to dendritic cells","C":"Secretion of mucus to form the barrier layer","D":"Transport of dimeric IgA into the lumen","E":"Absorption of nutrients across the brush border"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — cells at the <b>base of the crypts</b> with eosinophilic granules are <b>Paneth cells</b>. They secrete <b>antimicrobial peptides</b>: <b>α-defensins (cryptdins)</b>, <b>lysozyme</b>, phospholipase A2 and RegIIIγ, which disrupt microbial membranes and shape the composition of the microbiota.","B":"Antigen transcytosis is the job of <b>M cells</b>, which overlie Peyer's patches — not crypt-base cells.","C":"Mucus is secreted by <b>goblet cells</b>, which are scattered along the villi and crypts.","D":"IgA transport across the epithelium is performed by <b>enterocytes</b> using the <b>polymeric immunoglobulin receptor (pIgR)</b>.","E":"Nutrient absorption is the enterocyte's function."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L11_14.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L11-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L11-2","t":"Secretory component and pIgR","s":"Dimeric IgA made by plasma cells in the lamina propria must reach the gut lumen and survive there. Which structure protects it from luminal proteases, and where does that structure come from?","lec":"L11","sl":21,"c":"<b>Secretory IgA, assembled step by step:</b> plasma cell in the <b>lamina propria</b> secretes <b>two IgA monomers joined by J chain</b> → binds <b>pIgR</b> on the enterocyte basolateral membrane → <b>transcytosis</b> → apical cleavage leaves the <b>secretory component</b> attached → <b>sIgA</b> in the lumen, protease-resistant. Class switching to IgA is driven by <b>TGF-β</b>. It is the <b>most abundantly produced antibody isotype</b> in the body, and it works by <b>immune exclusion</b> without inflammation.","o":{"A":"Secretory component, derived from the cleaved polymeric immunoglobulin receptor","B":"J chain, synthesised by the plasma cell","C":"Secretory component, synthesised by Paneth cells","D":"The Fc region of IgA itself","E":"Mucin bound to the IgA hinge region"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — enterocytes express the <b>polymeric immunoglobulin receptor (pIgR)</b> on their basolateral surface. It binds dimeric IgA, transcytoses it to the apical surface, and is then <b>proteolytically cleaved</b>; the retained extracellular fragment stays bound to the IgA as the <b>secretory component</b>, protecting it from luminal proteolysis.","B":"The <b>J chain</b> is made by the plasma cell and joins the two IgA monomers into a dimer — necessary for pIgR binding, but it is not the protective element.","C":"Paneth cells make antimicrobial peptides, not secretory component.","D":"The Fc region is what pIgR and J chain engage; alone it confers no protease resistance.","E":"Mucin traps IgA in the mucus layer but does not shield it structurally."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L11_21.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L11-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L11-3","t":"Selective IgA deficiency at mucosal surfaces","s":"A 22-year-old woman with recurrent giardiasis and sinusitis is found to have undetectable serum IgA with normal IgG and IgM. She later develops a severe anaphylactic reaction during a blood transfusion. What explains the transfusion reaction?","lec":"L11","sl":21,"c":"<b>Selective IgA deficiency</b> is the <b>commonest primary immunodeficiency</b> (~1 in 300–700) and is often asymptomatic. When symptomatic: recurrent <b>mucosal</b> infections (sinopulmonary, <b><i>Giardia</i></b>), and associations with <b>coeliac disease, autoimmunity and atopy</b>. The examinable trap is the <b>anti-IgA anaphylactic transfusion reaction</b> — remember to ask for <b>washed or IgA-deficient blood products</b>.","o":{"A":"Anti-IgA antibodies reacting with IgA in the donor plasma","B":"ABO incompatibility causing complement-mediated haemolysis","C":"Bacterial contamination of the blood product","D":"Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","E":"Febrile non-haemolytic reaction from donor leukocytes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — patients with <b>selective IgA deficiency</b> may recognise IgA as foreign and develop <b>anti-IgA antibodies</b>. Transfusing plasma-containing products then triggers a severe <b>anaphylactic reaction</b>. Such patients need <b>washed red cells or IgA-deficient products</b>.","B":"ABO incompatibility causes fever, chills, back pain and haemoglobinuria — an intravascular haemolytic reaction, not anaphylaxis without haemolysis.","C":"Bacterial contamination causes septic shock with high fever and rigors.","D":"Transfusion-associated GVHD presents days to weeks later with rash, diarrhoea and pancytopenia, and is prevented by irradiation.","E":"Febrile non-haemolytic reactions cause fever and chills, not anaphylaxis."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L11_21.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L11-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L11-4","t":"Gut microbiota and immune education","s":"Germ-free mice raised in sterile isolators have poorly developed Peyer's patches, few IgA-secreting plasma cells and reduced intestinal Tregs. What does this experiment demonstrate?","lec":"L11","sl":18,"c":"<b>The microbiota is an immunological organ.</b> It drives GALT development, induces <b>secretory IgA</b> and <b>iTregs</b>, produces short-chain fatty acids (butyrate) that support the barrier and Treg induction, and provides <b>colonisation resistance</b> against pathogens. Disruption (broad-spectrum antibiotics) causes <b><i>C. difficile</i> colitis and candidiasis</b> — the L11 deck's own QUIZ scenario.","o":{"A":"The commensal microbiome is required for normal development and education of GALT","B":"Commensal bacteria are the principal cause of intestinal inflammation","C":"IgA is produced only in response to pathogenic organisms","D":"Peyer's patches develop independently of any external stimulus","E":"Tregs in the gut are derived exclusively from the thymus"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>commensal microbiome is essential for the normal development and immune education of GALT</b>. Germ-free animals show hypoplastic Peyer's patches and mesenteric nodes, low secretory IgA and reduced intestinal Tregs — and they are more susceptible to infection and to inflammatory disease.","B":"Commensals normally <b>maintain homeostasis</b>; it is loss of tolerance to them (dysbiosis plus a barrier defect) that produces inflammatory bowel disease.","C":"Most intestinal IgA is directed at <b>commensals</b>, keeping them contained within the lumen.","D":"The experiment shows the opposite — development depends on microbial stimulus.","E":"Intestinal Tregs are largely <b>induced (iTreg)</b> in the periphery under the influence of CD103⁺ dendritic cells, TGF-β and retinoic acid, plus microbial products such as short-chain fatty acids."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L11_18.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L11-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L11-5","t":"Respiratory mucosal defence","s":"A premature neonate develops severe respiratory distress. Which innate molecules secreted into the alveolar space bind carbohydrates on pathogen surfaces and promote their clearance?","lec":"L11","sl":27,"c":"<b>Respiratory mucosal defence, innate to adaptive:</b> <b>ciliated columnar epithelium</b> with the mucociliary escalator (physical) → <b>antimicrobial peptides, lysozyme, lactoferrin</b> → <b>SP-A and SP-D</b> collectins opsonising in the alveoli → <b>alveolar macrophages</b> → adaptive: <b>secretory IgA</b> in the upper airway, <b>IgG</b> in the lower airway, plus resident T cells. <b>IgE</b> responses here underlie allergic asthma.","o":{"A":"Surfactant proteins A and D","B":"Secretory IgA","C":"α-defensins from Paneth cells","D":"C-reactive protein","E":"Mannose-binding lectin from alveolar macrophages"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>SP-A and SP-D</b> are collectins secreted into the alveolar space. They bind <b>carbohydrate structures on the surface of many pathogens</b>, acting as opsonins that promote clearance by alveolar macrophages, and they also modulate inflammation.","B":"Secretory IgA dominates the <b>upper</b> airway; it is adaptive, and the alveolar innate collectins are what the question describes.","C":"α-defensins from Paneth cells belong to the <b>gut</b> crypts.","D":"CRP is a hepatic acute-phase protein in plasma, not an alveolar secretion.","E":"MBL is a genuine collectin with a similar mechanism, but it is made by the <b>liver</b> and circulates in plasma — the alveolar ones named in the deck are SP-A and SP-D."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L11_27.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L12-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L12-1","t":"MHC class I loss and NK cells","s":"A melanoma is biopsied twice. The first specimen expresses MHC class I and is heavily infiltrated by CD8⁺ T cells. A recurrence one year later has lost MHC class I expression entirely. Which effector cell is now best placed to kill the tumour?","lec":"L12","sl":21,"c":"<b>The CTL/NK complementarity is the single most elegant idea in tumour immunology</b>, and a guaranteed exam pairing. Express MHC I → the <b>CTL</b> sees you. Delete MHC I to hide → the <b>NK cell</b> kills you for missing self. The tumour is caught either way — which is exactly why it must instead resort to <b>PD-L1, TGF-β, FasL and Tregs</b> to survive.","o":{"A":"NK cell","B":"CD8⁺ cytotoxic T lymphocyte","C":"CD4⁺ Th1 cell","D":"Plasma cell","E":"Neutrophil"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>NK cells kill by 'missing-self' recognition</b>. MHC class I normally delivers an <b>inhibitory</b> signal through KIR; a tumour that deletes MHC class I to escape CTLs loses that inhibition and becomes an <b>NK target</b>. NK cells kill via perforin/granzyme, Fas–FasL and ADCC.","B":"CTLs are now blind — they require <b>peptide presented on MHC class I</b> to recognise the target. This is precisely the escape mechanism the tumour has adopted.","C":"CD4⁺ Th1 cells help by licensing DCs and supplying IL-2/IFN-γ but do not directly kill MHC-I-negative tumour cells.","D":"Antibody can mediate complement lysis and ADCC, but the question asks which effector the MHC-I loss specifically favours.","E":"Neutrophils are not significant anti-tumour effectors."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L12_21.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L12-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L12-2","t":"M1 versus M2 macrophages in tumours","s":"Immunohistochemistry of a breast carcinoma shows abundant tumour-associated macrophages expressing CD163 and arginase-1, secreting IL-10 and VEGF. What effect do these cells have on the tumour?","lec":"L12","sl":33,"c":"<b>M1 vs M2 — memorise as opposites.</b> <b>M1 (classical)</b>: induced by IFN-γ and LPS; secretes IL-1, IL-12, TNF, ROS, NO; <b>anti-tumour</b>, pro-inflammatory, microbicidal. <b>M2 (alternative)</b>: induced by IL-4 and IL-13; secretes IL-10, TGF-β, VEGF, arginase; <b>pro-tumour</b>, anti-inflammatory, pro-fibrotic and pro-repair. The same polarity explains the M1→M2 switch in wound healing (L10) — inflammation to repair.","o":{"A":"They promote tumour growth, angiogenesis and invasion","B":"They directly kill tumour cells through ROS and nitric oxide","C":"They present tumour antigen to CD8⁺ T cells on MHC class I","D":"They mediate antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity","E":"They induce tumour cell apoptosis via TNF-α"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — CD163, arginase-1, IL-10 and VEGF define the <b>alternatively activated (M2)</b> phenotype. <b>M2 tumour-associated macrophages promote tumour growth and invasiveness</b>: they remodel the microenvironment, drive <b>angiogenesis (VEGF)</b>, deposit matrix, and <b>suppress T-cell responses (IL-10, TGF-β, arginase depleting arginine)</b>. High M2 infiltration predicts poor prognosis.","B":"ROS and NO killing is the <b>classical M1</b> function — the anti-tumour phenotype.","C":"Cross-presentation to CD8⁺ cells is chiefly a dendritic-cell function.","D":"ADCC is mediated by NK cells (and macrophages via FcγR), but that is not what this phenotype is doing.","E":"TNF-α-mediated tumour killing is again the M1 function."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L12_33.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L12-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L12-3","t":"Neoantigens and mutational burden","s":"Two patients with the same cancer type receive anti-PD-1 therapy. The one whose tumour has microsatellite instability and a very high mutational burden responds dramatically; the other does not respond at all. What best explains the difference?","lec":"L12","sl":13,"c":"<b>Neoantigen (TSA) vs tumour-associated antigen (TAA).</b> <b>TSA/neoantigen</b>: arises from mutation, expressed <b>only</b> on tumour cells, never tolerised — the ideal target for CTLs and for <b>personalised mRNA vaccines</b>; individual to each patient. <b>TAA</b>: a normal protein <b>overexpressed</b> or aberrantly expressed (AFP, CEA, PSA, HER2, tyrosinase) — useful as a marker, but tolerance and on-target off-tumour toxicity limit it as a target.","o":{"A":"High mutational burden generates more neoantigens for T cells to recognise","B":"High mutational burden increases tumour PD-1 expression","C":"Microsatellite instability makes tumour cells intrinsically sensitive to antibodies","D":"High mutational burden reduces MHC class II expression on the tumour","E":"Microsatellite instability activates the complement cascade"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — every somatic mutation can create a <b>neoantigen</b>, a genuinely tumour-specific peptide the immune system has never been tolerised to. A high mutational burden therefore means many neoantigens, more tumour-specific T cells, and a stronger response once the <b>PD-1 brake is released</b>. This is why <b>MSI-high/mismatch-repair-deficient</b> tumours are a licensed indication for checkpoint inhibitors regardless of tissue of origin.","B":"PD-1 is expressed on <b>T cells</b>; tumours express the ligand PD-L1, and mutational burden does not directly drive it.","C":"Anti-PD-1 does not kill tumour cells directly — it acts on the T cell.","D":"MHC class II is not the relevant variable for CD8⁺ recognition.","E":"Complement is not the mechanism of checkpoint blockade."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L12_13.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L12-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L12-4","t":"Immunoediting","s":"A patient on long-term immunosuppression after renal transplantation develops an aggressive squamous cell carcinoma of the skin. Which concept best explains the increased cancer incidence in this population?","lec":"L12","sl":16,"c":"<b>Cancer immunoediting — three E's:</b> <b>Elimination</b> (immunosurveillance destroys transformed cells) → <b>Equilibrium</b> (immune pressure holds a dormant population in check while selecting less immunogenic variants) → <b>Escape</b> (variants with low immunogenicity or active suppression grow out clinically). Evidence for elimination: <b>increased malignancy in immunosuppressed patients</b>, and better prognosis when tumours are heavily infiltrated by lymphocytes.","o":{"A":"Loss of immunosurveillance, the elimination phase of immunoediting","B":"Direct carcinogenic effect of calcineurin inhibitors on keratinocyte DNA","C":"Chronic inflammation from the graft driving malignant transformation","D":"Failure of DNA mismatch repair caused by immunosuppression","E":"Increased angiogenesis secondary to immunosuppression"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the increased incidence of malignancy in transplant recipients and in HIV/AIDS is the strongest clinical evidence for <b>immunosurveillance</b>: the immune system normally detects and eliminates transformed cells. Suppressing T-cell function removes the <b>elimination</b> phase of <b>immunoediting</b>, so tumours (especially virus-driven ones — HPV skin/cervical cancer, EBV lymphoma, HHV-8 Kaposi's sarcoma) emerge.","B":"Some immunosuppressants have secondary effects on DNA repair, but the dominant, examinable mechanism is loss of immune surveillance.","C":"Chronic inflammation does contribute to carcinogenesis generally, but it does not explain the pattern seen in transplant recipients.","D":"Mismatch repair defects are genetic (Lynch syndrome, MSI), not caused by immunosuppression.","E":"Angiogenesis is a downstream tumour property, not a consequence of immunosuppression."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L12_16.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L12-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L12-5","t":"Antibody-mediated tumour killing","s":"Rituximab is given for CD20⁺ B-cell lymphoma. Through which mechanisms does this antibody destroy tumour cells?","lec":"L12","sl":25,"c":"<b>How antibodies kill tumour cells</b> (L12 slide 25): <b>complement activation</b>, <b>ADCC</b> (NK cells via FcγR), <b>opsonisation and phagocytosis</b>, and <b>blockade of growth-factor signalling</b> where the target is a receptor. Therapeutic examples to keep straight: <b>rituximab</b> (CD20, lymphoma) · <b>trastuzumab</b> (HER2, breast) · <b>cetuximab</b> (EGFR, colorectal) · <b>bevacizumab</b> (VEGF, anti-angiogenic).","o":{"A":"Complement activation, ADCC and opsonisation for phagocytosis","B":"Direct blockade of a growth factor receptor only","C":"Release of the PD-1 brake on cytotoxic T cells","D":"Delivery of a radioactive payload to the tumour","E":"Induction of tumour-specific memory T cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — an antibody bound to a tumour surface antigen kills through its <b>Fc region</b>: <b>complement activation</b> (classical pathway → MAC and C3b opsonisation), <b>antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC)</b> by <b>NK cells</b> engaging FcγRIII, and <b>opsonisation for phagocytosis</b> by macrophages. Rituximab also signals directly to induce apoptosis.","B":"CD20 is not a growth factor receptor — that mechanism describes <b>trastuzumab (HER2)</b> or <b>cetuximab (EGFR)</b>.","C":"Releasing the PD-1 brake describes <b>checkpoint inhibitors</b>, a different class.","D":"Radioimmunoconjugates exist but are not how standard rituximab works.","E":"Passive antibody therapy provides no memory — it is <b>passive specific</b> immunotherapy."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L12_25.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13A-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13A-1","t":"IPEX syndrome","s":"A male infant develops severe watery diarrhoea, type 1 diabetes at 2 months, eczema and thyroiditis. Family history reveals two maternal uncles who died in infancy of similar illness. Which gene is mutated?","lec":"L13A","sl":27,"c":"<b>Two monogenic tolerance diseases, easily confused:</b> <b>IPEX</b> = <b>FOXP3</b>, X-linked, <b>peripheral</b> tolerance (no Tregs) → enteropathy + T1DM + dermatitis in infancy. <b>APS-1/APECED</b> = <b>AIRE</b>, autosomal recessive, <b>central</b> tolerance (failed negative selection) → candidiasis + hypoparathyroidism + adrenal failure. Both are the clinical proof that their respective tolerance mechanisms are indispensable.","o":{"A":"FOXP3","B":"AIRE","C":"BTK","D":"IL2RG","E":"CD40LG"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>IPEX</b> (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked) is caused by <b>FOXP3</b> mutation. FOXP3 is the master transcription factor of <b>regulatory T cells</b>; without functional Tregs, peripheral tolerance collapses and multi-organ autoimmunity appears in infancy — the triad of <b>autoimmune enteropathy, early-onset type 1 diabetes and dermatitis</b>, often with thyroiditis and cytopenias. X-linked inheritance fits the affected maternal uncles.","B":"<b>AIRE</b> mutation causes <b>APS-1/APECED</b> — autosomal recessive, with the classic triad of <b>chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism and adrenal insufficiency</b>, usually presenting in childhood rather than the neonatal period.","C":"BTK causes XLA — recurrent bacterial infection, not autoimmunity.","D":"IL2RG causes X-linked SCID — infection and failure to thrive, not autoimmune endocrinopathy.","E":"CD40LG causes hyper-IgM syndrome — infection with a characteristic immunoglobulin pattern."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13A_27.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13A-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13A-2","t":"ICOS versus CD28","s":"A patient has a mutation abolishing ICOS function. He has normal T-cell numbers and normal early T-cell activation, but very poor antibody responses with low IgG and absent germinal centres. Why?","lec":"L13A","sl":19,"c":"<b>The B7:CD28 family — activation versus inhibition.</b> <b>Activating</b>: <b>CD28–B7 (CD80/CD86)</b> initiates the response; <b>ICOS–ICOS-L</b> supports Tfh help in germinal centres. <b>Inhibiting</b>: <b>CTLA-4</b> (competes with CD28 for B7, acts early in lymphoid tissue, also the Treg effector) and <b>PD-1–PD-L1/L2</b> (acts late, in peripheral tissue, limits chronic stimulation). Every therapeutic in L13A, L14 and L18 is a manipulation of one of these four.","o":{"A":"ICOS is required for T follicular helper function in the germinal centre reaction","B":"ICOS is required for the initiation of all T-cell responses","C":"ICOS is the inhibitory receptor that terminates B-cell responses","D":"ICOS mediates thymic negative selection","E":"ICOS is the receptor for IL-21 on B cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the B7:CD28 family divides its labour. <b>CD28–B7 initiates</b> the immune response, while <b>ICOS–ICOS-L provides T-cell help in the germinal centre reaction</b>, supporting <b>T follicular helper (Tfh)</b> cells. Losing ICOS therefore spares early activation but abolishes germinal centres, class switching and affinity maturation — presenting as a <b>CVID-like</b> antibody deficiency.","B":"Initiation depends on <b>CD28</b>, which is intact here — hence the normal early activation.","C":"The inhibitory members of the family are <b>CTLA-4 and PD-1</b>.","D":"Negative selection depends on TCR affinity and AIRE-driven self-antigen display, not ICOS.","E":"IL-21 acts through the IL-21 receptor; ICOS is a co-stimulatory molecule, not a cytokine receptor."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13A_19.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13A-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13A-3","t":"Why central tolerance alone is insufficient","s":"AIRE drives thymic expression of a wide range of peripheral tissue antigens, yet autoreactive T cells still reach the periphery in healthy people. What is the principal safeguard against them there?","lec":"L13A","sl":16,"c":"<b>Peripheral T-cell tolerance — four mechanisms:</b> <b>Anergy</b> (signal 1 without signal 2, prolonged) · <b>Suppression</b> (Treg: IL-10, TGF-β, CTLA-4, IL-2 consumption) · <b>Deletion</b> (repeated stimulation → Fas–FasL and BIM-mediated apoptosis) · <b>Ignorance</b> (antigen sequestered at privileged sites, or present below the threshold for activation). Failure of any one is a recognised route to autoimmune disease.","o":{"A":"Regulatory T cells and anergy in peripheral tissues","B":"Continued negative selection in lymph nodes","C":"Receptor editing of the TCR in the spleen","D":"Complement-mediated lysis of autoreactive clones","E":"Physical exclusion of T cells from all self tissues"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — central tolerance is necessarily incomplete, so <b>peripheral tolerance</b> must catch the escapees. Its mechanisms are <b>anergy</b> (self antigen presented without co-stimulation or inflammation), <b>suppression by regulatory T cells</b>, <b>deletion</b> by Fas–FasL-mediated apoptosis, and <b>ignorance</b> at immunologically privileged sites.","B":"Negative selection is a <b>thymic</b> process; lymph nodes are secondary lymphoid organs where peripheral, not central, tolerance operates.","C":"<b>Receptor editing</b> is exclusive to <b>B cells</b> in the bone marrow — T cells cannot edit their TCR.","D":"Complement does not police autoreactive lymphocytes.","E":"T cells recirculate through virtually all tissues; only a handful of privileged sites are shielded."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13A_16.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13A-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13A-4","t":"CTLA-4 versus PD-1 in irAE prediction","s":"A patient on ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) develops severe colitis and hypophysitis, whereas a patient on nivolumab (anti-PD-1) develops thyroiditis and pneumonitis. Which difference in the two checkpoints best explains their distinct toxicity profiles?","lec":"L13A","sl":21,"c":"<b>irAEs (immune-related adverse events)</b> are the predictable price of checkpoint blockade: <b>colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis and other endocrinopathy, pneumonitis, dermatitis, nephritis</b>. They differ from chemotherapy toxicity — <b>delayed and variable in onset (even after treatment ends), autoimmune in character, and treated with corticosteroids</b> rather than dose reduction. Anti-CTLA-4 causes more frequent and severe irAEs than anti-PD-1.","o":{"A":"CTLA-4 acts early during priming in lymphoid organs; PD-1 acts on effector T cells in peripheral tissues","B":"CTLA-4 is expressed on tumour cells; PD-1 is expressed on T cells","C":"CTLA-4 blockade is antibody-mediated; PD-1 blockade is cell-mediated","D":"CTLA-4 controls B cells; PD-1 controls T cells","E":"CTLA-4 acts only on regulatory T cells; PD-1 acts only on cytotoxic T cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>CTLA-4</b> restrains <b>priming in secondary lymphoid organs</b>, so blocking it unleashes a broad new wave of T-cell activation, giving more frequent and more severe systemic autoimmunity (colitis, hypophysitis). <b>PD-1</b> restrains <b>already-activated effector T cells in peripheral tissue</b>, so blocking it produces more organ-localised toxicity and is generally better tolerated.","B":"CTLA-4 is on <b>T cells</b> (and constitutively on Tregs); the tumour expresses <b>PD-L1</b>, not CTLA-4.","C":"Both drugs are monoclonal antibodies.","D":"Both checkpoints act on T cells.","E":"CTLA-4 acts on conventional T cells as well as being a Treg effector; PD-1 acts on multiple T-cell subsets."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13A_21.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13A-4b","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13A-4b","t":"Fetal tolerance","s":"A pregnant woman carries a fetus expressing paternal HLA antigens, yet does not reject it. Which mechanism contributes most to this tolerance?","lec":"L13A","sl":33,"c":"<b>Immunologically privileged sites: eye, brain, testis, uterus/fetus.</b> Shared features: physical barriers, absent or limited lymphatic drainage, local immunosuppressive mediators (<b>TGF-β, IL-10, FasL, IDO</b>) and reduced MHC expression. Two clinical consequences worth knowing: <b>corneal grafts survive without HLA matching</b>, and <b>breaching a privileged site can trigger autoimmunity</b> (sympathetic ophthalmia).","o":{"A":"The uterus is an immunologically privileged site with local immune regulation","B":"The fetus expresses no MHC molecules at any stage","C":"Maternal T cells are physically absent from the uterus","D":"The placenta destroys all maternal lymphocytes it contacts","E":"Maternal immunity is globally suppressed throughout pregnancy"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>uterus (fetus) is one of the immunologically privileged sites</b> named in the BM33 deck, alongside eye, brain and testis. Tolerance is actively maintained: the trophoblast expresses non-classical <b>HLA-G</b> instead of classical class I, the decidua is rich in <b>regulatory T cells</b> and tolerogenic cytokines (IL-10, TGF-β), and local <b>FasL</b> and enzymatic tryptophan depletion (IDO) suppress maternal effector cells.","B":"Trophoblast does express non-classical MHC (HLA-G), which is part of the mechanism rather than an absence of MHC.","C":"Maternal immune cells, including uterine NK cells and T cells, are abundantly present in the decidua — they are regulated, not excluded.","D":"There is no wholesale destruction of maternal lymphocytes.","E":"Maternal immunity is <b>modulated</b>, not globally suppressed — pregnant women still mount responses to infection (though live vaccines are avoided)."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13A_33.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13B-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13B-1","t":"HLA association","s":"A 24-year-old man has chronic inflammatory low back pain with morning stiffness improving on exercise, and sacroiliitis on imaging. Which HLA allele is most strongly associated, and what is the proposed mechanism of HLA association with autoimmunity?","lec":"L13B","sl":8,"c":"<b>High-yield HLA associations:</b> <b>B27</b> → ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis · <b>DR3</b> → SLE, type 1 diabetes, Graves', Sjögren's · <b>DR4</b> → <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b> (the DRB1 'shared epitope'), type 1 diabetes · <b>DQ2/DQ8</b> → coeliac disease · <b>DR2</b> → multiple sclerosis, Goodpasture's. HLA is the <b>strongest genetic risk factor</b> for most autoimmune disease, but it is only a susceptibility factor: most carriers never develop the disease, so environment matters too.","o":{"A":"HLA-B27; certain HLA alleles present self peptides to autoreactive T cells more effectively","B":"HLA-DR3; HLA alleles directly damage joint tissue","C":"HLA-B27; the allele causes a defect in AIRE function","D":"HLA-DQ2; the allele prevents thymic positive selection","E":"HLA-DR4; the allele encodes a complement component"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — this is <b>ankylosing spondylitis</b>, strongly associated with <b>HLA-B27</b>. The BM33 L13B deck lists the proposed mechanisms of HLA association: certain alleles <b>present self peptides to autoreactive T cells</b> more effectively, <b>present self peptides inefficiently in the thymus</b> so negative selection fails, or shape the T-cell repertoire in a way that favours autoreactivity.","B":"HLA-DR3 associates with SLE, type 1 diabetes and Graves', not ankylosing spondylitis — and HLA molecules do not damage tissue directly.","C":"HLA and AIRE are separate genes with separate mechanisms.","D":"HLA-DQ2/DQ8 associate with <b>coeliac disease</b>.","E":"HLA genes encode antigen-presenting molecules; complement components C4 and C2 lie within the MHC region but are distinct genes."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13B_8.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13B-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13B-2","t":"Epitope spreading","s":"A patient with early SLE initially has antibodies only against Ro (SSA). Two years later, anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm and anti-histone antibodies have all appeared and the disease is more severe. Which mechanism best explains the widening autoantibody profile?","lec":"L13B","sl":18,"c":"<b>Three infection-related mechanisms, distinguished by role:</b> <b>Molecular mimicry</b> — cross-reactivity initiates (acute rheumatic fever, Guillain–Barré after <i>Campylobacter</i>). <b>Bystander activation</b> — inflammation supplies co-stimulation and wakes autoreactive cells. <b>Epitope spreading</b> — established damage releases new self antigens, <b>broadening</b> the response. Mimicry and bystander start it; spreading amplifies it.","o":{"A":"Epitope spreading","B":"Molecular mimicry","C":"Bystander activation","D":"Receptor editing","E":"Superantigen stimulation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>epitope spreading</b>: the <b>constant presence of autoantigen drives chronic inflammation, which causes tissue damage and releases further self antigens</b>, broadening the autoimmune response to new epitopes and new molecules over time. It explains why autoantibody profiles widen and disease worsens as autoimmunity becomes established.","B":"<b>Molecular mimicry</b> initiates autoimmunity through cross-reactivity with a microbial epitope — it explains the <i>start</i>, not the progressive broadening.","C":"<b>Bystander activation</b> is non-specific activation of autoreactive lymphocytes by infection-driven inflammation — again an initiating mechanism.","D":"Receptor editing is a B-cell <b>tolerance</b> mechanism in the bone marrow.","E":"Superantigens cause massive polyclonal T-cell activation (toxic shock syndrome), not a progressive autoantibody cascade."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13B_18.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13B-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13B-3","t":"Sex hormones and autoimmunity","s":"Systemic lupus erythematosus affects women roughly nine times as often as men, with peak incidence in the reproductive years. Which hormonal factor contributes to this?","lec":"L13B","sl":16,"c":"<b>Postulated factors in autoimmunity</b> — the L13B framework, worth reproducing whole: <b>Genetic susceptibility</b> (MHC/HLA alleles, non-HLA polymorphisms such as PTPN22 and CTLA4, and inherited single-gene defects — AIRE, FOXP3, CTLA4, FAS, complement) plus <b>Environmental triggers</b> (<b>infection</b> via mimicry/bystander/spreading, <b>drugs</b> such as procainamide and hydralazine causing drug-induced lupus, and <b>hormones</b>). Neither alone is sufficient.","o":{"A":"Oestrogen has an immunoenhancing effect while testosterone is immunosuppressive","B":"Progesterone directly stimulates autoantibody production by plasma cells","C":"Testosterone increases MHC class II expression on B cells","D":"Oestrogen inhibits regulatory T cell development exclusively","E":"Female X-inactivation removes all tolerance genes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the BM33 L13B deck states it plainly: <b>oestrogen has an immunoenhancing effect on the immune system, whereas testosterone is immunosuppressive</b>. This helps explain the striking female predominance of most systemic autoimmune diseases and the peak in the reproductive years.","B":"Progesterone is broadly immunomodulatory (important in pregnancy) but is not the mechanism described.","C":"Testosterone is immunosuppressive, so this reverses the effect.","D":"Oestrogen has multiple immune effects; 'exclusively inhibits Treg development' is too narrow and not what the deck teaches.","E":"X-inactivation does not delete tolerance genes, though incomplete X-inactivation of immune genes such as TLR7 is an area of research."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13B_16.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13B-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13B-4","t":"Organ-specific versus systemic","s":"Which pairing correctly classifies the disease?","lec":"L13B","sl":17,"c":"<b>The classification follows the distribution of the autoantigen.</b> <b>Organ-specific</b> (antigen confined to one tissue): type 1 diabetes, Hashimoto's, Graves', myasthenia gravis, pernicious anaemia, multiple sclerosis, Goodpasture's. <b>Systemic</b> (ubiquitous antigen — nuclear material, IgG Fc): <b>SLE</b>, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis, Sjögren's, vasculitides. Ask yourself where the target antigen lives and the answer follows.","o":{"A":"Type 1 diabetes — organ-specific; systemic lupus erythematosus — systemic","B":"Multiple sclerosis — systemic; rheumatoid arthritis — organ-specific","C":"Graves' disease — systemic; Sjögren's syndrome — organ-specific","D":"Myasthenia gravis — systemic; systemic sclerosis — organ-specific","E":"Goodpasture's syndrome — systemic; SLE — organ-specific"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>type 1 diabetes</b> targets a single tissue (pancreatic β cells) and is <b>organ-specific</b>; <b>SLE</b> targets ubiquitous nuclear antigens and is therefore <b>systemic</b>, affecting skin, joints, kidney, blood, serosa and CNS.","B":"Reversed — multiple sclerosis is organ-specific (CNS myelin) and rheumatoid arthritis is systemic.","C":"Reversed — Graves' is organ-specific (thyroid) and Sjögren's is systemic.","D":"Reversed — myasthenia gravis is organ-specific (AChR at the neuromuscular junction) and systemic sclerosis is systemic.","E":"Reversed — Goodpasture's targets type IV collagen in kidney and lung (organ-restricted) and SLE is systemic."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13B_17.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L13B-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L13B-5","t":"Drug-induced lupus","s":"A 62-year-old man on procainamide for arrhythmia develops arthralgia, pleuritic chest pain and a positive ANA. Anti-dsDNA is negative but anti-histone antibodies are strongly positive. What is the diagnosis and the correct management?","lec":"L13B","sl":15,"c":"<b>Drug-induced lupus — the classic culprits:</b> <b>procainamide</b> (highest risk), <b>hydralazine</b>, isoniazid, minocycline, quinidine, and anti-TNF biologics. Distinguishing features from idiopathic SLE: <b>anti-histone positive, anti-dsDNA negative</b>, normal complement, older patients, equal sex distribution, and <b>renal and CNS involvement is rare</b>. It resolves on withdrawing the drug — which makes recognising it high-value clinically.","o":{"A":"Drug-induced lupus; stop procainamide","B":"Idiopathic SLE; start high-dose corticosteroid and hydroxychloroquine","C":"Rheumatoid arthritis; start methotrexate","D":"Acute rheumatic fever; start benzathine penicillin","E":"Mixed connective tissue disease; start immunosuppression"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>drug-induced lupus</b>: the BM33 L13B deck names <b>procainamide</b> (an antiarrhythmic) as notable for inducing autoantibodies. The signature is <b>positive ANA with anti-histone antibodies and negative anti-dsDNA</b>, with arthralgia and serositis but characteristically <b>sparing the kidneys and CNS</b>. Treatment is simply to <b>withdraw the drug</b>; symptoms resolve over weeks to months.","B":"Idiopathic SLE would typically show <b>anti-dsDNA and/or anti-Sm</b> and can involve kidney and CNS. Committing this patient to long-term immunosuppression when stopping a drug would cure him is the error being tested.","C":"Anti-CCP and erosive symmetrical small-joint disease would be needed for RA.","D":"Acute rheumatic fever follows streptococcal pharyngitis in children and shows carditis, migratory polyarthritis and chorea.","E":"MCTD is defined by high-titre anti-U1-RNP with overlapping features."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L13B_15.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L14-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L14-1","t":"Direct versus indirect allorecognition","s":"Six years after a kidney transplant a patient develops slowly worsening graft function with concentric intimal thickening on biopsy. Donor passenger dendritic cells disappeared from the graft within weeks of transplantation. Which pathway of allorecognition is sustaining this late rejection?","lec":"L14","sl":22,"c":"<b>Direct vs indirect allorecognition — timing is the discriminator.</b> <b>Direct</b>: recipient T cells see <b>intact donor MHC on donor APCs</b>; unusually high precursor frequency (1–10% of T cells) → powerful, but self-limiting as passenger cells die → <b>early acute rejection</b>. <b>Indirect</b>: <b>recipient APCs present processed donor MHC peptides</b>; lower frequency but <b>persistent</b> → <b>chronic rejection</b>. Chronic rejection is CD4⁺/cytokine-driven, mechanistically a slow DTH reaction.","o":{"A":"Indirect presentation — recipient APCs present processed donor MHC peptides","B":"Direct presentation — donor APCs present intact donor MHC","C":"Semi-direct presentation by donor endothelium only","D":"NK cell missing-self recognition","E":"Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity by donor B cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>direct</b> presentation depends on <b>donor passenger APCs</b>, which are lost within weeks, so it drives <b>early acute</b> rejection and then fades. <b>Indirect</b> presentation — <b>recipient APCs taking up shed donor MHC, processing it and presenting the peptides on recipient MHC class II to CD4⁺ T cells</b> — continues indefinitely and therefore sustains <b>chronic rejection</b> with its characteristic intimal hyperplasia.","B":"Direct presentation cannot be responsible once the donor APCs are gone.","C":"Semi-direct presentation (recipient APCs acquiring intact donor MHC by trogocytosis) is a real phenomenon but not the deck's answer for chronic rejection.","D":"Missing-self NK recognition is not the mechanism of graft vasculopathy.","E":"Donor B cells attacking the host would be a GVHD-type mechanism, inapplicable to a kidney graft."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L14_22.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L14-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L14-2","t":"Graft-versus-leukaemia effect","s":"A patient with AML undergoes allogeneic HSCT. The transplant team elects not to deplete donor T cells from the graft, accepting a higher risk of GVHD. What is the clinical rationale?","lec":"L14","sl":41,"c":"<b>The GVHD/GVL trade-off</b> is the central clinical tension of allogeneic HSCT. <b>More donor T cells</b> → more GVHD but less relapse. <b>T-cell depletion</b> → less GVHD but more relapse and slower immune reconstitution. Management therefore aims to <b>separate the two</b> — HLA matching, calibrated immunosuppression (<b>cyclosporine + methotrexate</b>), and donor lymphocyte infusion timed after relapse.","o":{"A":"Donor T cells also mediate a graft-versus-leukaemia effect that reduces relapse","B":"Donor T cells are required for engraftment of the stem cells","C":"T-cell depletion causes hyperacute rejection of the graft","D":"Donor T cells prevent CMV reactivation exclusively","E":"T-cell depletion would abolish the recipient's antibody responses"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the same donor T cells that cause GVHD also recognise residual leukaemic cells as foreign and destroy them: the <b>graft-versus-leukaemia (GVL)</b> effect. Depleting T cells reduces GVHD but <b>increases relapse</b>, so the team accepts controlled GVHD risk to keep the anti-leukaemic benefit. Donor lymphocyte infusion exploits the same principle after relapse.","B":"Engraftment depends on haematopoietic stem cells; donor T cells assist by suppressing residual host immunity but are not the reason given here.","C":"Hyperacute rejection is an antibody-mediated solid-organ phenomenon.","D":"Donor T cells do help control CMV, but 'exclusively' is wrong and GVL is the principal rationale.","E":"Antibody responses derive from the engrafted donor immune system over time."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L14_41.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L14-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L14-3","t":"Pre-transplant crossmatch","s":"Before a kidney transplant, recipient serum is incubated with donor lymphocytes and complement; the donor cells die. What does this positive crossmatch indicate and what should be done?","lec":"L14","sl":19,"c":"<b>Pre-transplant evaluation, in order:</b> medical history → <b>ABO blood group compatibility</b> → <b>HLA typing</b> (a sibling has a 25% chance of a 6/6 match) → <b>crossmatch</b> (lymphocyte microcytotoxicity or the more sensitive flow cytometry crossmatch) to exclude donor-specific antibody. Recipients become sensitised through <b>previous transfusion, pregnancy or a previous transplant</b> — always ask about all three.","o":{"A":"Recipient has pre-formed donor-specific antibodies; do not proceed — hyperacute rejection would occur","B":"Recipient has donor-specific T cells; proceed with intensified immunosuppression","C":"Donor has antibodies against the recipient; proceed and monitor","D":"The donor and recipient are HLA-identical; proceed without immunosuppression","E":"The recipient is immunodeficient; proceed with IVIG cover"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>lymphocyte microcytotoxicity crossmatch</b> detects <b>pre-formed recipient antibodies against donor HLA</b>. A positive crossmatch predicts <b>hyperacute rejection</b> — antibody plus complement destroying the graft within minutes to hours — so the transplant must <b>not</b> proceed with that donor. This test is precisely why hyperacute rejection is now rare.","B":"The crossmatch detects <b>antibody</b>, not T cells; and no amount of immunosuppression prevents hyperacute rejection.","C":"The direction is wrong — recipient serum is the source of the antibody being tested.","D":"HLA identity would produce a <b>negative</b> crossmatch.","E":"A positive crossmatch indicates sensitisation, the opposite of immunodeficiency."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L14_19.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L14-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L14-4","t":"Acute versus chronic GVHD","s":"A patient 14 months after allogeneic HSCT develops thickened sclerotic skin, dry eyes and dry mouth, and progressive dyspnoea with obstructive spirometry. Which complication is this?","lec":"L14","sl":38,"c":"<b>Acute vs chronic GVHD.</b> <b>Acute</b> (&lt;100 days): epithelial cell death in <b>skin</b> (maculopapular rash), <b>gut</b> (watery diarrhoea, abdominal pain), <b>liver</b> (jaundice, bile duct damage). <b>Chronic</b> (&gt;100 days): <b>autoimmune-like</b> — scleroderma-like skin, sicca syndrome, bronchiolitis obliterans, hepatic dysfunction. Both are driven by donor T cells against host alloantigens; the chronic form additionally involves B cells and fibrosis.","o":{"A":"Chronic GVHD","B":"Acute GVHD","C":"Chronic graft rejection","D":"Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome","E":"Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — beyond ~100 days, with <b>scleroderma-like skin fibrosis, sicca syndrome (dry eyes and mouth) and bronchiolitis obliterans</b>, this is <b>chronic GVHD</b>. It resembles an autoimmune connective tissue disease rather than the acute epithelial injury pattern.","B":"<b>Acute GVHD</b> occurs within roughly 100 days and shows <b>maculopapular rash, watery diarrhoea and cholestatic jaundice</b> — epithelial cell death rather than fibrosis.","C":"Graft rejection is host-versus-graft; in HSCT it presents as graft failure with cytopenias, not multi-organ fibrosis.","D":"IRIS is a phenomenon of immune recovery in HIV after starting antiretroviral therapy.","E":"PTLD is an EBV-driven lymphoproliferation presenting with lymphadenopathy and masses."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L14_38.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L14-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L14-5","t":"Immunosuppression and infection risk","s":"A renal transplant recipient two months into tacrolimus, mycophenolate and prednisolone develops fever, dry cough and marked hypoxaemia out of proportion to a diffuse interstitial chest radiograph. Which organism is most likely, and why?","lec":"L14","sl":43,"c":"<b>Match the immunosuppressive target to the infection risk</b> — the same logic as the primary immunodeficiency table, applied iatrogenically. <b>T-cell suppression</b> (calcineurin inhibitors, steroids, ATG) → viruses (<b>CMV, EBV, VZV</b>), fungi, <b><i>Pneumocystis</i></b>, mycobacteria. <b>B-cell depletion</b> (rituximab) → encapsulated bacteria, hepatitis B reactivation. <b>Neutropenia</b> (antimetabolites) → bacterial and fungal sepsis. Also remember the <b>malignancy</b> risk from lost immunosurveillance: <b>PTLD (EBV), skin cancer (HPV), Kaposi's sarcoma (HHV-8)</b>.","o":{"A":"<i>Pneumocystis jirovecii</i>, because the drugs suppress T-cell immunity","B":"<i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>, because the drugs suppress antibody production","C":"<i>Neisseria meningitidis</i>, because the drugs deplete complement","D":"<i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>, because the drugs impair the neutrophil respiratory burst","E":"<i>Giardia lamblia</i>, because the drugs abolish secretory IgA"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — this regimen suppresses <b>T-cell mediated immunity</b> (calcineurin inhibitor blocking IL-2, antimetabolite blocking lymphocyte proliferation, corticosteroid broadly suppressing). The resulting infections are those normally controlled by T cells: <b><i>Pneumocystis jirovecii</i> pneumonia</b> (dry cough, hypoxia disproportionate to the film), <b>CMV</b>, <b>fungi</b>, <b>mycobacteria</b> and reactivated viruses. Co-trimoxazole prophylaxis is routine for exactly this reason.","B":"Encapsulated bacterial infection is the pattern of <b>antibody</b> deficiency (XLA, CVID) or asplenia.","C":"<i>Neisseria</i> infection indicates <b>terminal complement</b> deficiency.","D":"<i>S. aureus</i> abscesses indicate a <b>phagocyte</b> defect such as CGD.","E":"<i>Giardia</i> is associated with IgA deficiency and CVID."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L14_43.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P1-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P1-1","t":"Early versus late phase","s":"A patient with allergic asthma inhales allergen. She wheezes within 15 minutes, improves with salbutamol, then deteriorates again 8 hours later with more persistent airflow obstruction that responds poorly to bronchodilator. What accounts for the second deterioration?","lec":"L15P1","sl":16,"c":"<b>The biphasic allergic response — and why treatment differs between the phases.</b> <b>Immediate (minutes)</b>: preformed <b>histamine, tryptase</b> → vasodilation, permeability, bronchoconstriction → treated with <b>antihistamine and bronchodilator</b>. <b>Late (6–12 h)</b>: newly synthesised <b>leukotrienes, PGD₂, PAF</b> plus <b>IL-4/IL-5/TNF</b> recruiting <b>eosinophils</b> → sustained inflammation → treated with <b>corticosteroids</b> (and leukotriene antagonists). This is why inhaled steroids, not bronchodilators, are the controller therapy in asthma.","o":{"A":"Late-phase reaction with eosinophil and neutrophil recruitment driven by Th2 cytokines","B":"A second wave of histamine release from mast cells","C":"Type III immune complex deposition in the airway","D":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity to the allergen","E":"Anaphylactoid reaction to salbutamol"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>immediate phase</b> (minutes) is driven by preformed mediators, chiefly <b>histamine</b>, causing reversible bronchoconstriction. The <b>late-phase reaction</b> at <b>6–12 hours</b> is driven by newly synthesised <b>leukotrienes and cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, TNF)</b> recruiting <b>eosinophils, neutrophils and Th2 cells</b> into the airway — producing sustained inflammation that responds to corticosteroids rather than bronchodilators.","B":"Histamine is preformed and released once, within minutes; it does not account for an 8-hour recurrence.","C":"Immune complex disease is not the mechanism of allergic asthma.","D":"Type IV takes 48–72 hours and is not IgE-driven.","E":"Salbutamol reactions are rare and would not follow this stereotyped biphasic pattern."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P1_16.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P1-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P1-2","t":"Anaphylactic versus anaphylactoid","s":"A patient receives intravenous radiocontrast for the first time in her life and immediately develops urticaria, wheeze and hypotension. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L15P1","sl":21,"c":"<b>Anaphylactic vs anaphylactoid</b> — a distinction the BM33 deck gives its own slide. <b>Anaphylactic</b>: <b>IgE-dependent</b>, requires <b>re-exposure</b> after sensitisation (foods, drugs, venom, latex). <b>Anaphylactoid</b>: <b>IgE-independent</b>, direct mast-cell/complement-mediated degranulation, <b>can occur on first exposure</b> (radiocontrast, opioids, vancomycin 'red man syndrome', NSAIDs, exercise). <b>Same clinical picture, same treatment: adrenaline IM first.</b>","o":{"A":"This is an anaphylactoid reaction — direct mast cell degranulation without prior sensitisation","B":"This is IgE-mediated anaphylaxis from an undetected previous exposure","C":"This is a type III immune complex reaction to contrast","D":"This is a type IV delayed reaction presenting unusually early","E":"This is a vasovagal episode rather than an immune reaction"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — a severe reaction on <b>first exposure</b> cannot be IgE-mediated, because type I hypersensitivity <b>requires prior sensitisation</b>. Radiocontrast, opioids, vancomycin and NSAIDs can trigger <b>direct, non-IgE mast cell degranulation</b> — an <b>anaphylactoid</b> reaction. Clinically it is indistinguishable from anaphylaxis and is treated identically, with <b>intramuscular adrenaline</b>.","B":"Possible in principle, but the defining teaching point is that anaphylactoid reactions occur <b>without</b> prior exposure — which is what makes contrast reactions distinctive.","C":"Type III takes hours to days and presents as serum sickness or vasculitis.","D":"Type IV peaks at 48–72 hours.","E":"A vasovagal episode causes bradycardia and pallor, not urticaria and wheeze."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P1_21.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P1-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P1-3","t":"Omalizumab mechanism","s":"A patient with severe allergic asthma uncontrolled on high-dose inhaled corticosteroid is started on omalizumab. What is its mechanism, and how does it differ from allergen immunotherapy?","lec":"L15P1","sl":24,"c":"<b>Biologics in allergic disease, by target:</b> <b>Omalizumab</b> → anti-IgE (severe allergic asthma, chronic urticaria) · <b>Mepolizumab/reslizumab</b> → anti-IL-5 (eosinophilic asthma) · <b>Benralizumab</b> → anti-IL-5Rα · <b>Dupilumab</b> → anti-IL-4Rα, blocking both IL-4 and IL-13 (atopic dermatitis, asthma). Set against these, <b>allergen-specific immunotherapy</b> remains the only treatment that modifies the natural history of allergic disease.","o":{"A":"Omalizumab binds free IgE preventing FcεRI engagement; immunotherapy induces Tregs and blocking IgG4","B":"Omalizumab depletes mast cells; immunotherapy depletes eosinophils","C":"Omalizumab blocks IL-5; immunotherapy blocks IL-4","D":"Omalizumab induces tolerance; immunotherapy provides passive antibody","E":"Both act identically by reducing total serum IgE"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>omalizumab is an anti-IgE monoclonal antibody</b> that binds <b>free IgE</b> at the site that would engage <b>FcεRI</b>, preventing IgE from arming mast cells and basophils (and down-regulating FcεRI expression). It works while it is being given but <b>induces no tolerance</b>. <b>Allergen-specific immunotherapy</b>, by contrast, is disease-modifying: repeated allergen exposure induces <b>allergen-specific Tregs, IL-10 and TGF-β, and blocking IgG4</b>, with benefit persisting after treatment stops.","B":"Neither drug depletes mast cells; anti-IL-5 agents (mepolizumab) target eosinophils.","C":"Omalizumab targets IgE, not IL-5.","D":"The roles are reversed — immunotherapy induces tolerance, omalizumab does not, and neither supplies passive antibody in the protective sense.","E":"Their mechanisms are fundamentally different."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P1_24.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P1-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P1-4","t":"Tuberculin skin test","s":"A nurse has a Mantoux test placed. At 48–72 hours there is 15 mm of firm induration. What is the immunological basis of this induration?","lec":"L15P1","sl":34,"c":"<b>Reading a skin test by its timing tells you the mechanism.</b> <b>15–20 minutes, soft wheal and flare</b> → type I (prick test). <b>4–12 hours, oedema and haemorrhage</b> → type III (Arthus). <b>48–72 hours, firm induration</b> → <b>type IV</b> (tuberculin, patch test). Type IV has two arms: <b>cytokine-mediated inflammation</b> (CD4⁺ Th1/Th17 → macrophages: tuberculin, contact dermatitis, TB granuloma) and <b>T cell-mediated cytotoxicity</b> (CD8⁺ CTL: type 1 diabetes, severe drug eruptions).","o":{"A":"CD4⁺ Th1 cells recruiting and activating macrophages via IFN-γ","B":"IgE cross-linking on dermal mast cells releasing histamine","C":"Immune complex deposition activating complement","D":"IgG opsonising dermal cells for phagocytosis","E":"Direct cytotoxicity of NK cells against dermal fibroblasts"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the tuberculin (Mantoux) reaction is the prototype <b>delayed-type hypersensitivity (type IV)</b> reaction. Previously sensitised <b>CD4⁺ Th1 cells</b> recognise mycobacterial antigen presented by dermal APCs and release <b>IFN-γ and TNF</b>, recruiting and activating <b>macrophages</b>. The resulting mononuclear infiltrate and oedema produce the firm <b>induration</b>, peaking at <b>48–72 hours</b>.","B":"Histamine from mast cells gives a soft <b>wheal and flare within minutes</b>, not induration at two days.","C":"Immune complex reactions (Arthus) peak at 4–12 hours with oedema, haemorrhage and neutrophils.","D":"Opsonisation is a type II mechanism against cell-surface antigens.","E":"NK cells are not the effectors of DTH."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P1_34.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P1-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P1-5","t":"Type IV drug reaction","s":"Ten days after starting allopurinol a patient develops widespread painful skin detachment with mucosal erosions and a positive Nikolsky sign. Which immunological mechanism underlies the keratinocyte death?","lec":"L15P1","sl":40,"c":"<b>Type IV has two mechanisms, and severe drug eruptions belong to the second.</b> <b>Mechanism I — cytokine-mediated inflammation</b>: CD4⁺ Th1/Th17 → macrophage activation → contact dermatitis, tuberculin reaction, TB granuloma, chronic rejection. <b>Mechanism II — T cell-mediated cytotoxicity</b>: CD8⁺ CTLs kill target cells directly → <b>type 1 diabetes</b>, <b>SJS/TEN</b>, DRESS. Note the HLA link that makes this clinically actionable: <b>HLA-B*58:01 with allopurinol</b> and <b>HLA-B*15:02 with carbamazepine</b> — both especially relevant in Southeast Asian populations.","o":{"A":"CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells killing keratinocytes — type IV mechanism II","B":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation — type I","C":"IgG against desmoglein — type II","D":"Immune complex deposition in dermal vessels — type III","E":"Complement-mediated lysis of keratinocytes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — this is <b>toxic epidermal necrolysis / Stevens–Johnson syndrome</b>, a severe <b>type IV</b> drug reaction. The mechanism is the deck's <b>Mechanism II: T cell-mediated cytotoxicity</b> — drug-reactive <b>CD8⁺ CTLs</b> kill keratinocytes via <b>perforin/granzyme, granulysin and Fas–FasL</b>, causing full-thickness epidermal necrosis. The delay of days to weeks after starting the drug fits type IV, not type I.","B":"An IgE reaction to a drug produces urticaria or anaphylaxis <b>within minutes to an hour</b>, not epidermal detachment ten days later.","C":"IgG against desmoglein causes <b>pemphigus vulgaris</b> — also Nikolsky-positive, but an autoimmune type II disease unrelated to a new drug.","D":"Type III drug reactions present as serum sickness or vasculitis with palpable purpura.","E":"Complement is not the effector in TEN."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P1_40.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P2-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P2-1","t":"Haemolytic disease of the newborn","s":"An Rh-negative woman had an uncomplicated first pregnancy with an Rh-positive baby. During her second pregnancy, also with an Rh-positive fetus, the fetus develops severe anaemia and hydrops. Why was the first pregnancy unaffected?","lec":"L15P2","sl":9,"c":"<b>Haemolytic disease of the newborn — type II hypersensitivity, mechanism I.</b> Sequence: Rh⁻ mother + Rh⁺ fetus → fetomaternal haemorrhage at delivery → maternal <b>anti-D IgM then IgG</b> → next Rh⁺ pregnancy → <b>IgG crosses the placenta</b> → fetal red cell destruction → anaemia, jaundice, kernicterus, hydrops fetalis. <b>Prevention: anti-D immunoglobulin (RhoGAM)</b> given to the mother at 28 weeks and within 72 hours of delivery, which clears fetal cells before she can be sensitised. This is a beautiful applied example of primary versus secondary antibody responses.","o":{"A":"The first pregnancy produced only IgM, which does not cross the placenta; the secondary response produced IgG, which does","B":"The first fetus was protected by maternal complement inhibitors","C":"Rh antigen is not expressed until the second pregnancy","D":"The mother developed tolerance during the first pregnancy that was lost afterwards","E":"The first pregnancy induced IgA, which is neutralising"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — fetal Rh⁺ red cells enter the maternal circulation mainly <b>at delivery</b>. The <b>primary response produces IgM</b>, which is pentameric and <b>cannot cross the placenta</b>, so the first baby escapes. On re-exposure in a subsequent pregnancy, the <b>secondary response produces IgG</b>, which <b>does cross the placenta</b>, coats fetal red cells and causes <b>haemolytic disease of the newborn</b>.","B":"Complement regulators do not explain the first/second pregnancy difference.","C":"Rh antigen is expressed on fetal red cells from early gestation.","D":"Sensitisation, not tolerance, occurs during the first pregnancy.","E":"IgA is not the relevant isotype and does not cross the placenta."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P2_9.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P2-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P2-2","t":"Serum sickness","s":"A patient bitten by a snake receives equine antivenom. Nine days later he develops fever, an urticarial rash, arthralgia and lymphadenopathy, with low C3 and C4. What is the mechanism and the expected course?","lec":"L15P2","sl":25,"c":"<b>Type III: the two classic clinical forms.</b> <b>Serum sickness</b> — <b>systemic</b>, 7–14 days after exposure to a <b>foreign protein</b> (antivenom, ATG, chimeric monoclonal antibodies, some drugs): fever, urticaria, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, low complement, self-limiting. <b>Arthus reaction</b> — <b>local</b>, 4–12 hours after a booster in someone with high pre-existing antibody (vaccines, toxoids): painful oedematous swelling with haemorrhage and necrosis at the injection site.","o":{"A":"Type III serum sickness from immune complexes of foreign protein; self-limiting as the antigen clears","B":"Type I anaphylaxis to horse protein; requires immediate adrenaline","C":"Type II antibody against red cells; requires transfusion","D":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity; requires topical corticosteroid","E":"Direct toxic effect of the venom; requires more antivenom"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>serum sickness</b> is the systemic form of <b>type III hypersensitivity</b>. Antibody made against the <b>foreign (equine) protein</b> forms circulating <b>immune complexes</b> roughly <b>7–14 days</b> after exposure. These deposit in vessels, joints, skin and glomeruli, activating complement (hence the <b>low C3/C4</b>) and recruiting neutrophils. It is <b>self-limiting</b> as the foreign antigen is cleared; treatment is symptomatic with antihistamines, NSAIDs and, if severe, corticosteroids.","B":"Anaphylaxis occurs within <b>minutes</b> of infusion, not after nine days.","C":"There is no evidence of haemolysis here.","D":"Type IV is a localised cell-mediated reaction, not this systemic complex-mediated syndrome.","E":"The venom has long since been neutralised; more antivenom would worsen the problem."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P2_25.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P2-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P2-3","t":"Goodpasture's syndrome","s":"A 25-year-old man presents with haemoptysis and acute kidney injury with haematuria. Renal biopsy immunofluorescence shows smooth linear deposition of IgG along the glomerular basement membrane. What is the target antigen?","lec":"L15P2","sl":12,"c":"<b>Linear versus granular immunofluorescence is a diagnosis in one image.</b> <b>Linear</b> → antibody bound uniformly along a <b>fixed structural antigen</b> in the basement membrane = <b>Goodpasture's, type II</b>. <b>Granular ('lumpy-bumpy')</b> → discrete <b>immune complex</b> deposits = <b>type III</b> (lupus nephritis, post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis). Treatment of Goodpasture's follows the mechanism: <b>plasmapheresis</b> to remove the antibody plus immunosuppression to stop making it.","o":{"A":"The α3 chain of type IV collagen","B":"Double-stranded DNA","C":"The TSH receptor","D":"Streptococcal M protein","E":"Citrullinated peptides"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>Goodpasture's (anti-GBM) syndrome</b>: autoantibody against the <b>non-collagenous domain of the α3 chain of type IV collagen</b>, found in the basement membranes of both <b>renal glomeruli and pulmonary alveoli</b> — which is why the disease attacks kidney and lung together. It is <b>type II, mechanism II (antibody-mediated inflammation)</b>, producing <b>linear</b> immunofluorescence.","B":"Anti-dsDNA is the SLE antibody, and lupus nephritis shows <b>granular</b>, not linear, immunofluorescence.","C":"Anti-TSHR antibody causes Graves' disease.","D":"Anti-M protein cross-reactivity causes acute rheumatic fever.","E":"Anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies (anti-CCP) occur in rheumatoid arthritis."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P2_12.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P2-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P2-4","t":"Myasthenia gravis","s":"A 30-year-old woman has ptosis and diplopia that worsen through the day and improve with rest. Antibodies against the acetylcholine receptor are detected. How does this differ mechanistically from Graves' disease, and what type of hypersensitivity are both?","lec":"L15P2","sl":14,"c":"<b>Type II has three mechanisms — learn one disease pair for each.</b> <b>I. Cell destruction</b> (opsonisation or complement lysis): AIHA, ITP, haemolytic transfusion reaction, haemolytic disease of the newborn. <b>II. Inflammation at the deposition site</b> (complement and Fc receptor recruitment of neutrophils): <b>Goodpasture's</b>, acute rheumatic fever. <b>III. Cellular dysfunction</b> (no destruction): <b>Graves' (stimulating)</b> and <b>myasthenia gravis (blocking)</b>.","o":{"A":"Both are type II mechanism III; the antibody blocks the receptor in myasthenia but stimulates it in Graves'","B":"Both are type III; the antibody forms complexes in both","C":"Myasthenia is type II and Graves' is type III","D":"Both are type IV; T cells attack the receptor in each","E":"Myasthenia is type I and Graves' is type II"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — both are <b>type II hypersensitivity, mechanism III: antibody-mediated cellular dysfunction</b>. Neither destroys the target cell; each antibody hijacks a receptor. In <b>myasthenia gravis</b> the antibody <b>blocks and internalises the acetylcholine receptor</b> → fatigable weakness, ptosis, diplopia. In <b>Graves'</b> the antibody <b>stimulates the TSH receptor</b> → hyperthyroidism.","B":"Neither involves circulating immune complexes.","C":"Both are type II.","D":"Both are antibody-mediated, not T cell-mediated.","E":"Neither involves IgE."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P2_14.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L15P2-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L15P2-5","t":"Type II versus type III in one patient","s":"A patient with SLE has both lupus nephritis (proteinuria, low C3, granular immunofluorescence) and autoimmune haemolytic anaemia with a positive direct Coombs test. Which hypersensitivity types are operating?","lec":"L15P2","sl":20,"c":"<b>The AX bank makes this exact point:</b> SLE is type III, but a patient described as having <b>autoimmune haemolytic anaemia</b> has <b>both type II and type III</b>. Read the manifestation, not just the disease name. The distinguishing question is always whether the antigen is <b>soluble</b> (forming complexes → type III) or <b>fixed on a cell surface</b> (→ type II).","o":{"A":"Type III for the nephritis and type II for the haemolytic anaemia","B":"Type III for both","C":"Type II for both","D":"Type III for the nephritis and type IV for the anaemia","E":"Type I for the nephritis and type II for the anaemia"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — SLE is a <b>two-mechanism disease</b>. Its immune-complex manifestations (<b>nephritis, vasculitis, arthritis, serositis</b>) are <b>type III</b>: soluble antinuclear antigen–antibody complexes depositing in tissue, consuming complement and showing <b>granular</b> immunofluorescence. Its cytopenias (<b>autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia</b>) are <b>type II</b>: antibody directed at antigens <b>on the surface of the blood cells themselves</b>, confirmed by a <b>positive direct Coombs test</b>.","B":"That would miss the direct anti-red-cell antibody, which is unambiguously type II.","C":"Granular deposits with complement consumption in the glomerulus are type III.","D":"AIHA is antibody-mediated, not T cell-mediated.","E":"There is no IgE component in SLE."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L15P2_20.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L16-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L16-1","t":"Timing of presentation","s":"Two infants are referred. Infant A presented at 6 weeks with thrush, chronic diarrhoea and failure to thrive. Infant B presented at 9 months with a third episode of otitis media and normal growth. What does the difference in timing indicate?","lec":"L16","sl":18,"c":"<b>Age of onset is a diagnostic tool.</b> <b>Birth to 3 months</b> → T-cell/combined (SCID, DiGeorge) — maternal IgG cannot help; look for thrush, failure to thrive, absent thymic shadow. <b>7–9 months</b> → antibody defect (XLA) — maternal IgG has gone; look for encapsulated bacteria, otitis, sinusitis, pneumonia, with normal growth. <b>Neonatal</b> with delayed cord separation and no pus → <b>LAD</b>. <b>Any age</b> with abscesses → CGD; with <i>Neisseria</i> → complement.","o":{"A":"A has a T-cell or combined defect; B has an antibody defect unmasked as maternal IgG wanes","B":"A has an antibody defect; B has a complement defect","C":"Both have phagocyte defects presenting at different ages","D":"A has a complement defect; B has a T-cell defect","E":"Timing carries no diagnostic information"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>very early presentation (first weeks to 3 months) with thrush, diarrhoea and failure to thrive</b> indicates a <b>T-cell or combined defect</b> (SCID, DiGeorge), because maternal IgG cannot substitute for cell-mediated immunity. <b>Presentation at 7–9 months with recurrent sinopulmonary bacterial infection and normal growth</b> indicates an <b>antibody defect</b>, unmasked exactly as <b>transplacental maternal IgG</b> falls away.","B":"Reversed, and complement defects typically present with <i>Neisseria</i> infection rather than otitis media.","C":"Phagocyte defects present with abscesses, granulomas and delayed cord separation.","D":"Reversed.","E":"Timing is one of the most useful discriminators in the entire immunodeficiency workup."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L16_18.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L16-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L16-2","t":"Anti-IFN-γ autoantibody immunodeficiency","s":"A previously healthy 45-year-old Thai woman develops disseminated non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection and recurrent <i>Salmonella</i> bacteraemia. HIV testing is negative and lymphocyte subsets are normal. Which mechanism should be considered?","lec":"L16","sl":23,"c":"<b>The IFN-γ/IL-12 axis controls intracellular organisms</b>, and failure anywhere along it produces <b>Mendelian (or acquired) susceptibility to mycobacterial disease</b>. Genetic causes: IFN-γR1/R2, IL-12p40 and IL-12Rβ1, STAT1 defects. Acquired cause: <b>neutralising anti-IFN-γ autoantibodies</b> — adult onset, strongly represented in Southeast Asia, and specifically taught in the BM33 deck. Suspect it in an HIV-negative adult with disseminated NTM.","o":{"A":"Neutralising anti-IFN-γ autoantibodies causing adult-onset immunodeficiency","B":"Late-onset X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","C":"Chronic granulomatous disease presenting in adulthood","D":"Terminal complement deficiency","E":"Selective IgA deficiency"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the BM33 L16 deck devotes a slide to this: <b>anti-cytokine autoantibody-associated immunodeficiency</b>, specifically <b>neutralising anti-IFN-γ autoantibodies</b>, reported in over 130 cases across <b>Asia since 2004</b>. It causes <b>adult-onset</b> susceptibility to <b>disseminated non-tuberculous mycobacteria</b>, <i>Salmonella</i>, and other intracellular organisms, in patients with otherwise normal immune testing. It is a locally important diagnosis in Thailand.","B":"XLA affects boys from infancy and would show absent B cells and low immunoglobulins.","C":"CGD presents with abscesses and catalase-positive organisms, usually in childhood, with an abnormal DHR.","D":"Terminal complement deficiency predisposes to <b><i>Neisseria</i></b>, not mycobacteria.","E":"IgA deficiency causes mucosal bacterial infection and giardiasis."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L16_23.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L16-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L16-3","t":"Secondary immunodeficiency after splenectomy","s":"A patient who had a splenectomy after trauma five years ago presents with rapidly progressive septic shock and <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i> bacteraemia. Which preventive measures should have been taken?","lec":"L16","sl":67,"c":"<b>Causes of secondary immunodeficiency</b> — far commoner than primary: <b>HIV</b>, malignancy (especially haematological), <b>chemotherapy, corticosteroids and other immunosuppressants</b>, <b>malnutrition</b> (the commonest cause worldwide), <b>splenectomy/asplenia</b>, diabetes, chronic kidney or liver disease, burns, and extremes of age. Each carries its own characteristic infection pattern — asplenia's is <b>encapsulated bacteria and OPSI</b>.","o":{"A":"Pneumococcal, Hib and meningococcal vaccination before splenectomy, plus prophylactic penicillin","B":"Lifelong IVIG replacement therapy","C":"Prophylactic co-trimoxazole for Pneumocystis","D":"Annual DHR testing","E":"Avoidance of all live vaccines for life"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — asplenic patients are at risk of <b>overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI)</b> from <b>encapsulated organisms</b>, because the spleen filters blood-borne bacteria and hosts the marginal-zone B cells that respond to <b>polysaccharide capsules</b>. Prevention: vaccinate against <b><i>S. pneumoniae</i>, <i>H. influenzae</i> type b and <i>N. meningitidis</i></b> — ideally about 2 weeks <b>before</b> elective splenectomy — plus <b>prophylactic penicillin</b>, annual influenza vaccination, a medical alert card, and a clear plan for any febrile illness.","B":"IVIG is for primary antibody deficiencies, not routine asplenia.","C":"<i>Pneumocystis</i> prophylaxis is for T-cell deficiency and transplant recipients.","D":"DHR tests neutrophil oxidative burst — irrelevant here.","E":"Live vaccines are not contraindicated in asplenia; it is the encapsulated-organism vaccines that are essential."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L16_67.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L16-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L16-4","t":"Interpreting a complement screen","s":"A 19-year-old has had two episodes of meningococcal meningitis. CH50 is undetectable, but C3 and C4 are normal. Where is the defect?","lec":"L16","sl":51,"c":"<b>Reading a complement screen.</b> <b>CH50</b> = classical pathway function end-to-end; <b>AH50</b> = alternative pathway. Undetectable CH50 with <b>normal C3 and C4</b> → <b>terminal (C5–C9)</b> defect → recurrent <b><i>Neisseria</i></b>. <b>Low C3</b> → severe pyogenic infection. <b>Low C4 ± C2</b> → early classical defect → <b>lupus-like immune-complex disease</b> from impaired clearance of apoptotic debris. Management of terminal deficiency: <b>meningococcal vaccination</b> (including serogroup B) and a low threshold for antibiotics.","o":{"A":"Terminal complement components C5–C9","B":"C3 deficiency","C":"Early classical pathway (C1, C2, C4)","D":"C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency","E":"Mannose-binding lectin deficiency"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>CH50 tests the entire classical pathway through to the membrane attack complex</b>, so an <b>undetectable CH50 with normal C3 and C4</b> localises the defect <b>downstream</b> of C3, in the <b>terminal components C5–C9</b>. Without a functioning MAC the patient cannot lyse <i>Neisseria</i> — hence recurrent meningococcal disease.","B":"C3 deficiency would show a <b>low C3</b> and cause severe recurrent <b>pyogenic</b> infection through failed opsonisation.","C":"Early classical pathway deficiency would show <b>low C4</b> (and often C2) and typically causes an <b>SLE-like immune-complex</b> illness.","D":"C1 inhibitor deficiency causes <b>hereditary angioedema</b>, not infection.","E":"MBL deficiency affects the lectin pathway and would not abolish CH50."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L16_51.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L16-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L16-5","t":"Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome","s":"A patient with advanced HIV (CD4 30 cells/mm³) and treated cryptococcal meningitis begins antiretroviral therapy. Three weeks later he deteriorates with fever, headache and worsening meningeal inflammation, but CSF cultures are now sterile and his viral load is falling. What is happening?","lec":"L16","sl":65,"c":"<b>IRIS</b> is a paradox worth understanding rather than memorising: the patient gets <i>worse</i> because their immunity is getting <i>better</i>. Highest risk with a <b>very low starting CD4 count</b>, a <b>high organism burden</b>, and <b>early initiation of ART</b> after diagnosing the opportunistic infection. Commonly triggered by <b>mycobacteria (TB, MAC), <i>Cryptococcus</i>, CMV and PML</b>. Management: continue ART where possible, treat the underlying infection, and use <b>corticosteroids</b> for severe inflammation.","o":{"A":"Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome","B":"Antiretroviral drug hypersensitivity","C":"Failure of antifungal therapy requiring escalation","D":"A new bacterial meningitis","E":"Progression to AIDS-related lymphoma"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>IRIS</b> occurs when the immune system begins to recover after starting effective antiretroviral therapy. The returning CD4⁺ T cells mount a vigorous inflammatory response against <b>residual antigen from a treated or subclinical opportunistic infection</b>, producing <b>paradoxical clinical worsening despite improving virological and microbiological parameters</b>. The falling viral load and <b>sterile cultures</b> are the diagnostic clues.","B":"Drug hypersensitivity would present with rash and systemic features, not focal worsening of the treated infection.","C":"Treatment failure would be accompanied by <b>positive</b> cultures.","D":"A new bacterial meningitis would show organisms and neutrophils on CSF examination.","E":"Lymphoma would present as a mass lesion over a longer time course."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L16_65.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L17-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L17-1","t":"Conjugate vaccine principle","s":"The plain pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine works poorly in children under 2, whereas the conjugate version works well. What immunological change does conjugation produce?","lec":"L17","sl":25,"c":"<b>Conjugate vaccines: Hib, pneumococcal (PCV), meningococcal.</b> The carrier protein (tetanus toxoid, CRM197 diphtheria protein) provides the T-cell epitopes. This distinction — <b>carrier</b> (conjugated, converts T-independent to T-dependent) versus <b>adjuvant</b> (mixed in, non-specifically amplifies) — is asked directly in BM31 Q41 and BM30 Q41, where both appear as options.","o":{"A":"It converts a T-independent antigen into a T-dependent one, enabling class switching and memory","B":"It adds an adjuvant that creates a depot effect","C":"It increases the polysaccharide dose delivered","D":"It converts the vaccine from killed to live attenuated","E":"It allows the vaccine to be given orally"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — a plain polysaccharide is a <b>T-independent</b> antigen: it cross-links the B-cell receptor and elicits IgM with <b>no class switching, no affinity maturation and no memory</b>, and infants under 2 respond poorly because their marginal-zone B cells are immature. <b>Covalently linking the polysaccharide to a carrier protein</b> means the B cell internalises the conjugate, presents <b>carrier peptides on MHC II</b>, and recruits <b>T-cell help</b> — producing <b>class-switched IgG, affinity maturation, memory, and efficacy in infants</b>.","B":"Adjuvants amplify a response non-specifically; conjugation changes the <b>nature</b> of the response.","C":"Dose is not the issue.","D":"Conjugate vaccines contain no live organism.","E":"Route of administration is unchanged."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L17_25.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L17-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L17-2","t":"mRNA vaccine mechanism","s":"How does an mRNA vaccine generate cytotoxic T-cell immunity, and why does it not require an adjuvant?","lec":"L17","sl":31,"c":"<b>Why mRNA and viral-vector vaccines reproduce the advantage of live vaccines without a live organism:</b> both achieve <b>authentic endogenous antigen presentation</b>, giving <b>MHC class I → CD8⁺ CTL</b> plus MHC class II → antibody, so both cellular and humoral immunity with long-lasting memory — but with <b>no virulence and no reversion risk</b>, making them safe in immunocompromised patients where live vaccines are contraindicated. Their practical drawbacks are cold-chain requirements (mRNA) and anti-vector immunity.","o":{"A":"Host cells translate the mRNA, so antigen is presented endogenously on MHC class I; the lipid nanoparticle and RNA are intrinsically immunostimulatory","B":"The mRNA integrates into host DNA and is expressed permanently","C":"The mRNA is taken up by B cells and directly cross-links the BCR","D":"The vaccine contains a live attenuated virus vector","E":"The mRNA is translated by gut bacteria into protein antigen"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — mRNA encoding the pathogen protein is protected in a <b>lipid nanoparticle</b>; once absorbed, <b>host cells translate it</b>, so the antigen is synthesised <b>inside</b> the cell and enters the <b>endogenous pathway → MHC class I → CD8⁺ CTLs</b>, as well as being secreted for MHC II presentation and antibody production. The lipid nanoparticle and the RNA itself provide <b>intrinsic innate stimulation</b>, so no separate adjuvant is needed.","B":"mRNA does <b>not</b> enter the nucleus and cannot integrate into DNA; it is degraded within days.","C":"Direct BCR cross-linking is the mechanism of T-independent polysaccharide antigens.","D":"That describes a <b>viral vector</b> vaccine, a related but distinct platform.","E":"Translation occurs in the recipient's own cells."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L17_31.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L17-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L17-3","t":"Vaccine schedule design","s":"Why is the first dose of hepatitis B vaccine given at birth, whereas MMR is delayed until about 9–12 months?","lec":"L17","sl":41,"c":"<b>Immunisation schedules balance three competing factors:</b> (1) <b>produce immunity before the child is likely to meet the pathogen</b>; (2) <b>avoid interference from maternal antibody</b> (which is why live viral vaccines are delayed); (3) <b>match the child's immunological maturity</b> (which is why plain polysaccharide vaccines fail under age 2 and conjugates are used instead). Every scheduling question reduces to one of these three.","o":{"A":"Maternal antibody would neutralise the live measles virus, but hepatitis B protection is needed immediately for perinatal transmission","B":"Newborns cannot mount any immune response before 9 months","C":"MMR is more painful and is deferred for comfort","D":"Hepatitis B vaccine is live and MMR is killed","E":"The MMR antigen is unstable before 9 months of age"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — two separate principles collide. <b>Transplacental maternal IgG</b> persists for months and would <b>neutralise the live attenuated measles virus</b> before it could replicate and immunise, so MMR is deferred until maternal antibody has waned. Hepatitis B, by contrast, poses an <b>immediate risk of perinatal transmission</b>, so the birth dose is given at once — and it works because it is a non-replicating subunit vaccine unaffected in the same way.","B":"Neonates do mount immune responses, though they are less robust — the birth dose of hepatitis B vaccine proves it.","C":"Comfort has no role in schedule design.","D":"Reversed — MMR is live attenuated; hepatitis B is a recombinant protein subunit.","E":"Stability is a cold-chain issue, not the reason for scheduling."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L17_41.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L17-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L17-4","t":"Toxoid vaccines","s":"Tetanus and diphtheria vaccines contain toxoids rather than whole organisms. What is a toxoid and why does this approach work for these two diseases specifically?","lec":"L17","sl":35,"c":"<b>Match the vaccine platform to the pathogen's mechanism of disease</b> — this is the reasoning behind the whole L17 lecture. Disease caused by an <b>exotoxin</b> → <b>toxoid</b> (tetanus, diphtheria). Disease requiring <b>cell-mediated immunity</b> → <b>live attenuated</b> (BCG, MMR). Disease caused by an <b>encapsulated</b> organism in infants → <b>conjugate</b> (Hib, PCV). A pathogen too dangerous to attenuate → <b>inactivated or subunit</b> (rabies, hepatitis B, HPV). Toxoids, being non-replicating, require an <b>adjuvant</b>.","o":{"A":"A chemically inactivated toxin that remains antigenic; disease in both is caused by the exotoxin, not by invasion","B":"A live attenuated organism engineered to lose its toxin gene","C":"A purified polysaccharide capsule conjugated to a protein","D":"A killed whole organism preserved in formalin","E":"A fragment of bacterial DNA encoding the toxin"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — a <b>toxoid</b> is a <b>chemically modified toxin that is no longer toxic but is still antigenic</b>. It works for tetanus and diphtheria because in both diseases the pathology is caused almost entirely by a secreted <b>exotoxin</b>, not by bacterial invasion. Antibody that neutralises the toxin therefore prevents disease even though it does not prevent colonisation.","B":"That describes a genetically attenuated live vaccine.","C":"That describes a conjugate vaccine.","D":"A killed whole-organism vaccine contains the whole bacterium, not just its toxin.","E":"That describes a nucleic-acid vaccine."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L17_35.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L17-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L17-5","t":"Post-exposure prophylaxis strategy","s":"An unvaccinated child is bitten by a stray dog in a rabies-endemic area. Rabies immunoglobulin is infiltrated around the wound and a vaccine course is started the same day. Why are both given?","lec":"L17","sl":6,"c":"<b>Passive + active together — the standard post-exposure strategy.</b> Used for <b>rabies</b> (RIG + vaccine), <b>hepatitis B</b> (HBIG + vaccine, including for infants of HBsAg-positive mothers), and <b>tetanus</b> (TIG + toxoid for a dirty wound in an unvaccinated patient). The principle: <b>antibody buys time; antigen buys durability</b>. Note the L17 deck's own detail — rabies immunoglobulin is <b>infiltrated around the wound</b> (ฉีดรอบแผล), not just given systemically.","o":{"A":"Immunoglobulin gives immediate passive protection while the vaccine induces active immunity that takes 1–2 weeks","B":"The immunoglobulin acts as an adjuvant for the vaccine","C":"The vaccine neutralises virus at the wound and immunoglobulin provides memory","D":"Both are passive treatments, given at different sites for coverage","E":"The immunoglobulin prevents an allergic reaction to the vaccine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — rabies is essentially <b>100% fatal once symptomatic</b>, and active immunity from the vaccine takes <b>1–2 weeks</b> to develop. <b>Rabies immunoglobulin infiltrated around the wound</b> supplies <b>immediate passive</b> neutralising antibody to cover that interval, while the vaccine generates <b>long-lasting active</b> immunity with memory. The combination covers both the short and long term.","B":"Immunoglobulin is not an adjuvant; if anything, excess antibody can slightly blunt the vaccine response, which is why it is infiltrated locally rather than given at the vaccine site.","C":"The roles are exactly reversed.","D":"The vaccine is active, not passive.","E":"Immunoglobulin does not prevent vaccine allergy."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L17_6.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L18-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L18-1","t":"Cytokine release syndrome","s":"Four days after CAR-T cell infusion for refractory B-cell lymphoma, a patient develops high fever, hypotension, hypoxia and a markedly raised IL-6 and ferritin. What is the diagnosis and the targeted treatment?","lec":"L18","sl":13,"c":"<b>The two major CAR-T toxicities</b>, both on the BM33 L18 slide: <b>Cytokine release syndrome (CRS)</b> — fever, hypotension, hypoxia; mediated by <b>IL-6</b>; treated with <b>tocilizumab</b> ± corticosteroids. <b>ICANS (immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity syndrome)</b> — confusion, aphasia, seizures, cerebral oedema; treated with <b>corticosteroids</b> (tocilizumab penetrates the CNS poorly). Both reflect the same underlying truth: a living, self-amplifying drug is powerful precisely because it is hard to switch off.","o":{"A":"Cytokine release syndrome; tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor)","B":"Septic shock; broad-spectrum antibiotics alone","C":"Tumour lysis syndrome; rasburicase","D":"Graft-versus-host disease; cyclosporine","E":"Anaphylaxis to the infusion; adrenaline"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>cytokine release syndrome (CRS)</b> is the major toxicity of CAR-T therapy: a <b>systemic inflammatory response triggered by massive T-cell activation</b>, with fever, hypotension, hypoxia, and high <b>IL-6</b>, IFN-γ and ferritin. Because <b>IL-6</b> is the central mediator, the targeted treatment is <b>tocilizumab</b>, an anti-IL-6-receptor antibody, with corticosteroids for refractory or neurotoxic disease.","B":"CRS mimics sepsis and antibiotics are given empirically, but they do not treat the underlying cytokine storm.","C":"Tumour lysis syndrome causes hyperkalaemia, hyperuricaemia and hyperphosphataemia with renal failure, not this cytokine profile.","D":"GVHD requires an allogeneic graft; CAR-T cells are <b>autologous</b>.","E":"Anaphylaxis occurs within minutes of infusion, not on day 4."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L18_13.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L18-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L18-2","t":"CAR structure","s":"A chimeric antigen receptor recognises its target independently of MHC. Which component of the CAR molecule confers this property?","lec":"L18","sl":11,"c":"<b>CAR architecture, from outside in:</b> <b>scFv</b> (antibody-derived antigen recognition, <b>MHC-independent</b>) → <b>hinge/transmembrane</b> → <b>costimulatory domain</b> (CD28 or 4-1BB, signal 2, determines persistence) → <b><b>CD3ζ</b></b> (signal 1). Manufacture: <b>leukapheresis → genetic modification with a lentiviral/retroviral vector → ex vivo expansion → lymphodepleting chemotherapy → infusion</b>. Greatest success: <b>CD19-directed CAR-T in B-cell malignancies</b>; solid tumours remain much harder because of antigen heterogeneity and the suppressive microenvironment.","o":{"A":"An extracellular scFv derived from an antibody variable region","B":"The CD3ζ intracellular signalling domain","C":"The 4-1BB or CD28 costimulatory domain","D":"The transmembrane hinge region","E":"The lentiviral vector used for transduction"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the CAR's <b>ectodomain is an scFv (single-chain variable fragment) derived from an antibody</b>. Because it binds antigen the way an antibody does — recognising <b>native surface structure</b> rather than peptide presented on MHC — the CAR-T cell is <b>MHC-independent</b>. That is what allows CAR-T cells to bypass the tumour's classic escape route of MHC class I down-regulation.","B":"CD3ζ supplies <b>signal 1</b>, transmitting activation into the T cell, but does not determine antigen recognition.","C":"CD28 or 4-1BB supplies <b>signal 2</b> (costimulation), improving persistence and expansion.","D":"The hinge and transmembrane region provide structural flexibility and anchoring.","E":"The vector delivers the construct; it is not part of the receptor."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L18_11.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L18-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L18-3","t":"Immunotherapy classification","s":"Classify each intervention correctly: (i) intravesical BCG for bladder cancer, (ii) trastuzumab for HER2⁺ breast cancer, (iii) tumour-infiltrating lymphocyte therapy.","lec":"L18","sl":4,"c":"<b>Four categories, one example each — sort the options before answering any immunotherapy question.</b> <b>Active non-specific</b>: BCG, IL-2, IFN-α. <b>Active specific</b>: tumour vaccines, <b>dendritic cell vaccines (sipuleucel-T)</b>, personalised <b>mRNA neoantigen</b> vaccines. <b>Passive specific</b>: monoclonal antibodies — trastuzumab, rituximab, cetuximab. <b>Adoptive cellular ('living drugs')</b>: <b>CAR-T</b>, TILs. <b>Checkpoint blockade</b> sits slightly apart: it removes inhibition rather than adding stimulation.","o":{"A":"(i) active non-specific, (ii) passive specific, (iii) adoptive cellular","B":"(i) passive specific, (ii) active specific, (iii) active non-specific","C":"(i) adoptive cellular, (ii) active non-specific, (iii) passive specific","D":"(i) active specific, (ii) adoptive cellular, (iii) passive specific","E":"All three are forms of active specific immunotherapy"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>(i) BCG</b> stimulates the patient's own immune system without targeting a defined tumour antigen = <b>active non-specific</b>. <b>(ii) Trastuzumab</b> is a ready-made antibody against a defined target, given to the patient = <b>passive specific</b>. <b>(iii) TIL therapy</b> transfers expanded tumour-reactive lymphocytes = <b>adoptive cellular</b>.","B":"BCG supplies no antibody, so it cannot be passive.","C":"BCG is not a cellular therapy and trastuzumab is specific.","D":"BCG targets no defined antigen, so it is not specific; trastuzumab is not a cell.","E":"Only tumour vaccines are active specific."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L18_4.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L18-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L18-4","t":"irAE management","s":"A patient on pembrolizumab for melanoma develops profuse diarrhoea with blood; colonoscopy shows diffuse colitis and infection is excluded. What is the correct management principle, and how does this differ from chemotherapy toxicity?","lec":"L18","sl":28,"c":"<b>irAEs versus chemotherapy side effects</b> — a distinction the BM33 L18 deck emphasises. irAEs are <b>autoimmune in character</b>, <b>unpredictable in onset</b> (any time, even after treatment ends), <b>organ-specific</b> (colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis and other endocrinopathies, pneumonitis, dermatitis, nephritis, hypophysitis), and <b>treated with corticosteroids</b>. Anti-CTLA-4 produces more frequent and severe irAEs than anti-PD-1, consistent with CTLA-4 acting earlier and more broadly during priming.","o":{"A":"Hold the drug and give corticosteroids; irAEs are autoimmune and steroid-responsive rather than dose-related","B":"Reduce the dose and continue; the toxicity is cumulative and dose-dependent","C":"Give loperamide alone and continue at full dose","D":"Switch to ipilimumab, which does not cause colitis","E":"Give granulocyte colony-stimulating factor"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — this is an <b>immune-related adverse event (irAE)</b>: immune-mediated colitis from unleashed T cells. Management is to <b>withhold the checkpoint inhibitor and give corticosteroids</b>, escalating to infliximab if steroid-refractory. Unlike chemotherapy toxicity, irAEs are <b>not dose-related</b>, are <b>unpredictable in timing</b> (they can appear even after treatment ends), and are treated by <b>immunosuppression</b> rather than dose reduction.","B":"Dose reduction is the chemotherapy paradigm and does not apply to irAEs.","C":"Symptomatic treatment alone risks progression to perforation.","D":"<b>Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) causes more colitis</b>, not less.","E":"G-CSF treats neutropenia, an unrelated problem."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L18_28.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L18-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L18-5","t":"Cancer immunity cycle","s":"According to the cancer immunity cycle, a tumour is heavily infiltrated by T cells but they are functionally exhausted and express high PD-1, while the tumour expresses PD-L1. At which step is the cycle blocked, and what is the rational therapy?","lec":"L18","sl":21,"c":"<b>The cancer immunity cycle (Chen & Mellman, 2013)</b> — the framework the whole L18 lecture hangs on: (1) release of cancer antigens → (2) antigen presentation by DCs → (3) <b>priming and activation</b> in the lymph node → (4) <b>trafficking</b> of T cells to the tumour → (5) infiltration → (6) recognition of cancer cells → (7) <b>killing</b>. Every immunotherapy targets a specific step: vaccines at 1–2, <b>anti-CTLA-4 at 3</b>, <b>anti-PD-1/PD-L1 at 6–7</b>, CAR-T supplying ready-made effectors at 5–7. Identify the blocked step and the drug follows.","o":{"A":"The final killing step; anti-PD-1 or anti-PD-L1 blockade","B":"Antigen release; radiotherapy to release antigen","C":"Antigen presentation by dendritic cells; a dendritic cell vaccine","D":"T-cell trafficking to the tumour; a chemokine agonist","E":"T-cell priming in the lymph node; anti-CTLA-4"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the T cells have already been primed, have trafficked to the tumour and have infiltrated it, so the earlier steps are intact. The block is at the <b>final recognition and killing step</b>, where <b>PD-L1 on the tumour engages PD-1 on the exhausted T cells</b> and switches them off. The rational therapy is therefore <b>PD-1/PD-L1 blockade</b>, and a PD-L1-positive, T-cell-inflamed tumour is exactly the phenotype that responds best.","B":"Antigen release is not the limiting step when the tumour is already heavily infiltrated.","C":"Priming has clearly occurred, so a vaccine addresses the wrong step.","D":"Trafficking has succeeded — the T cells are present.","E":"Anti-CTLA-4 acts at <b>priming in the lymph node</b>, which is not where this tumour is blocked."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L18_21.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L19-1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L19-1","t":"SLE classification criteria","s":"A 24-year-old woman has a malar rash, oral ulcers, non-erosive arthritis of the small joints, proteinuria of 1.2 g/24 h and a positive ANA at 1:640. Which additional finding would be most specific for SLE rather than another connective tissue disease?","lec":"L19","sl":36,"c":"<b>SLE investigations, in order of use:</b> <b>ANA</b> — screening, by <b>indirect immunofluorescence on HEp-2 cells</b>; highly sensitive, poorly specific · <b>anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm</b> — specific, and anti-dsDNA tracks disease activity and nephritis · <b>C3 and C4</b> — <b>fall</b> in active disease as complexes consume complement · <b>UA and 24-h urine protein/UPCR</b> — renal involvement · <b>antiphospholipid antibodies</b> — thrombosis and pregnancy loss risk · <b>CBC</b> — cytopenias.","o":{"A":"Anti-double-stranded DNA or anti-Sm antibodies","B":"Raised ESR","C":"Positive rheumatoid factor","D":"Raised CRP","E":"Normochromic normocytic anaemia"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>ANA is sensitive but not specific</b> (positive in many connective tissue diseases and in healthy people at low titre). <b>Anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm are the specific antibodies for SLE</b>, and both are weighted heavily in the classification criteria. Anti-dsDNA also correlates with disease activity and with lupus nephritis.","B":"ESR rises in any inflammatory state.","C":"Rheumatoid factor is neither sensitive nor specific for SLE — it points more towards RA and Sjögren's.","D":"CRP is characteristically <b>normal or only mildly raised</b> in active SLE (a striking CRP rise should prompt a search for infection or serositis) — useful clinically but not specific for diagnosis.","E":"Anaemia of chronic inflammation is common but non-specific."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L19_36.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L19-2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L19-2","t":"Anti-CCP in rheumatoid arthritis","s":"A 45-year-old smoker has a symmetrical polyarthritis of the MCP and PIP joints with 2 hours of morning stiffness. RF is weakly positive; anti-CCP is strongly positive. What is the significance of the anti-CCP result?","lec":"L19","sl":46,"c":"<b>RA pathogenesis, as the BM33 L19 deck lays it out:</b> <b>environmental factors (smoking, <i>P. gingivalis</i>) + genetic susceptibility (HLA-DRB1 shared epitope, PADI4, PTPN22)</b> → <b>citrullination by PAD enzymes</b> → citrullinated peptides presented by dendritic cells → <b>T and B cell activation</b> → <b>RF and ACPA</b> from plasma cells → <b>Th1/Th17</b> cytokines (IFN-γ, IL-17) → macrophage <b>TNF, IL-1, IL-6</b> → synovial pannus, cartilage and bone erosion. Every treatment target sits somewhere on that chain.","o":{"A":"It is more specific for RA than RF and predicts more erosive disease","B":"It excludes RA and suggests SLE instead","C":"It indicates active infection with a citrullinating organism","D":"It is a non-specific marker of any inflammatory arthritis","E":"It correlates with the degree of morning stiffness only"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP / ACPA)</b> antibodies are considerably <b>more specific for rheumatoid arthritis than rheumatoid factor</b> (which also appears in Sjögren's, hepatitis C, endocarditis and in healthy older people). Anti-CCP can precede clinical disease by years and predicts <b>more erosive, more aggressive disease</b>, which argues for early aggressive DMARD therapy.","B":"Anti-CCP supports RA; it does not point to SLE.","C":"Citrullination is a normal post-translational modification catalysed by <b>PAD enzymes</b>, promoted by <b>smoking</b> and <b><i>P. gingivalis</i></b> — but the antibody indicates autoimmunity, not active infection.","D":"Its value lies precisely in being specific rather than non-specific.","E":"It relates to disease severity and erosion, not simply to stiffness duration."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L19_46.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L19-3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L19-3","t":"Jones criteria","s":"A 7-year-old girl has fever, migratory polyarthritis of the wrists, elbows and knees, and a new mitral murmur, 3 weeks after a sore throat. Which investigation best confirms the required evidence of preceding streptococcal infection?","lec":"L19","sl":67,"c":"<b>Modified Jones criteria.</b> <b>Major</b>: <b>carditis</b>, <b>polyarthritis</b> (migratory), <b>chorea</b> (Sydenham's), <b>erythema marginatum</b>, <b>subcutaneous nodules</b>. <b>Minor</b>: fever, arthralgia, raised ESR/CRP, prolonged PR interval. Diagnosis needs <b>2 major, or 1 major + 2 minor</b>, <b>plus evidence of preceding streptococcal infection</b> (<b>ASO</b>, anti-DNase B). Treatment: benzathine penicillin, aspirin (± prednisone for moderate/severe carditis), then <b>long-term secondary prophylaxis</b> to prevent recurrent carditis and rheumatic heart disease.","o":{"A":"Anti-streptolysin O (ASO) titre","B":"Blood culture","C":"Rheumatoid factor","D":"Antinuclear antibody","E":"Throat swab culture taken today"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the <b>modified Jones criteria</b> require <b>evidence of a preceding group A streptococcal infection</b> in addition to the major and minor criteria. The <b>ASO titre</b> (with <b>anti-DNase B</b>) provides serological evidence of infection weeks earlier, which is exactly the window in acute rheumatic fever.","B":"Blood cultures are negative — ARF is a <b>non-suppurative sequel</b>, not an active bacteraemia.","C":"RF is a marker for rheumatoid arthritis and is not part of the Jones criteria (the name is a coincidence students are expected to resist).","D":"ANA screens for SLE.","E":"A throat swab taken now is usually <b>negative</b>, because the organism has typically been cleared by the time ARF appears 2–3 weeks later — which is precisely why serology is required."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L19_67.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L19-4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L19-4","t":"Hypersensitivity type in three clinical cases","s":"Assign the dominant hypersensitivity mechanism in each of L19's three cases: (i) lupus nephritis in SLE, (ii) the synovitis of rheumatoid arthritis, (iii) the carditis of acute rheumatic fever.","lec":"L19","sl":39,"c":"<b>The BM33 L19 lecture is built around exactly this question</b> — it poses 'which type of hypersensitivity is associated with the pathogenesis of…' after each of its three cases. Memorise the set: <b>SLE → type III</b> (with <b>type II</b> for its haemolytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia) · <b>Rheumatoid arthritis → type IV</b> (with a type III component) · <b>Acute rheumatic fever → type II</b> (molecular mimicry). Expect at least one of these three in the exam.","o":{"A":"(i) type III, (ii) type IV, (iii) type II","B":"(i) type II, (ii) type III, (iii) type IV","C":"(i) type IV, (ii) type II, (iii) type III","D":"(i) type III, (ii) type III, (iii) type III","E":"(i) type I, (ii) type IV, (iii) type II"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>(i) Lupus nephritis</b> is <b>type III</b>: antinuclear immune complexes deposit in the glomerulus, activate complement and recruit neutrophils (granular immunofluorescence, low C3/C4). <b>(ii) RA synovitis</b> is predominantly <b>type IV</b>: T cell-driven (Th1/Th17) macrophage activation in the pannus, with a type III contribution from RF-containing complexes. <b>(iii) Rheumatic carditis</b> is <b>type II</b>: cross-reactive antibody against cardiac myosin and valve tissue via molecular mimicry.","B":"Lupus nephritis is complex-mediated, not directly antibody-against-cell.","C":"Reverses lupus and RA.","D":"RA and ARF are not primarily immune-complex diseases.","E":"No type I mechanism is involved in any of the three."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L19_39.webp"},{"id":"AUTHOR|A-L19-5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTHOR","n":"A-L19-5","t":"Distinguishing the causes of polyarthritis","s":"Three women present with polyarthritis. Patient 1: 32 years, symmetrical MCP/PIP swelling, morning stiffness 90 minutes, anti-CCP positive. Patient 2: 24 years, arthritis with malar rash, oral ulcers, proteinuria, ANA 1:640. Patient 3: 17 years, migratory arthritis of large joints, fever, new murmur, sore throat 3 weeks ago. What are the diagnoses in order?","lec":"L19","sl":49,"c":"<b>Read the joint pattern first — it separates these three faster than any antibody.</b> <b>RA</b>: <b>symmetrical, small joints (MCP, PIP, wrists), persistent, erosive</b>, morning stiffness &gt;1 hour, anti-CCP/RF. <b>SLE</b>: arthritis is <b>non-erosive</b> and comes with <b>multisystem</b> features (rash, ulcers, renal, cytopenias, ANA). <b>ARF</b>: <b>migratory, large joints, transient</b>, with fever, carditis and preceding pharyngitis (Jones criteria, ASO). Add <b>gout</b> (acute monoarthritis, first MTP, crystals) and <b>septic arthritis</b> (single hot joint, systemically unwell) to complete the differential.","o":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis; systemic lupus erythematosus; acute rheumatic fever","B":"Systemic lupus erythematosus; rheumatoid arthritis; septic arthritis","C":"Acute rheumatic fever; rheumatoid arthritis; systemic lupus erythematosus","D":"Rheumatoid arthritis; acute rheumatic fever; systemic lupus erythematosus","E":"Reactive arthritis; systemic lupus erythematosus; rheumatoid arthritis"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — <b>Patient 1</b>: symmetrical <b>small-joint (MCP/PIP)</b> arthritis with morning stiffness over an hour and <b>anti-CCP</b> positivity = <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>. <b>Patient 2</b>: <b>multisystem</b> disease — malar rash, oral ulcers, <b>renal</b> involvement, high-titre ANA = <b>SLE</b>. <b>Patient 3</b>: <b>migratory large-joint</b> arthritis with fever, <b>carditis</b> and antecedent pharyngitis = <b>acute rheumatic fever</b>.","B":"Misassigns the first two and ignores the post-streptococcal history in the third.","C":"Wrong order throughout.","D":"Patients 2 and 3 are swapped.","E":"Reactive arthritis follows genitourinary or enteric infection and is typically an asymmetrical oligoarthritis of the lower limbs."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides/L19_49.webp"}];
